er": "/goldens/37/remedy"}}, {"after": "none; retention DEFAULTED durable-unbounded, the account-derived installation root and backup status unknown were disclosed before first write; no policy file written", "before": "none; retention DEFAULTED durable-unbounded was disclosed before first write; no policy file written", "parent": {"bytes": 74644, "path": "docs/implementation/m1/source-selection-v2/reference/composed-owners/command-inventory.proposed.json", "sha256": "fa0d72a32f50d5f2b76b196b751a2b310448e36f8e78c728d701b7a585fb5f6a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/goldens/0/remedy"}}], "passageSupersessions": [], "selected": "docs/implementation/m2/existing-root-diagnostics-468a/successor.json", "sha256": "dae42833b0f6a204836762b273a42e36433b968a5edb81018b0825277ae17d5c"}, {"inputs": [{"bytes": 2849, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/stale-descriptions-461b/README.md", "sha256": "0e2f1ad2b62a22dabdb65e6abc0687ac85ec9cc00628ba78b3fa856674a1f96e"}, {"bytes": 2285, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/stale-descriptions-461b/evidence/verify_scratch.py", "sha256": "16948161e6bcb3c2ab2b3f7fedec1bc3fe87d9e7299da18c97a1d966bf12b715"}], "passageOverrides": [{"after": "Define the held-fence view that installation captures borrow (law 458c item 8): the held I, the actor, a charged recheck of the held fence and a sample of the fence carrier's filesystem, and the carrier name lifecycle.fence. The read session implements it for every production reader. The supplied and native installation fences (one nonblocking exclusive attempt with native root provenance and pre/post custody checks, busy distinguished from missing or invalid state) remain for supplied-root tests only; no production installation reader calls them. No descriptor escape, creation or authority.", "before": "Acquire and retain the existing installation fence with native root provenance and pre/post custody checks; distinguish busy from missing or invalid state.", "parent": {"bytes": 313526, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v80.json", "sha256": "838a7f4072b408e8c4011249c89e0974d38616031cda110267c92ab762d0d1e7"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/236/description"}}, {"after": "Check the initial creator's stage, validation and publication (law 467) over scratch account homes, with InitialCore and InitialPlatform built from signed on-disk test trees: the exact private P0 tree and its 44 barriers, the fence held through the act and free after, reads through the read session and the native trust read session, the lost race, a moved stage, a scripted indeterminate rename, scripted barrier failures, ledgers short of a reservation, validation refusals, an owner change before the rename, two concurrent creators and NotPristine. Nothing runs on the real account's chain. Compiled only for tests; confers no qualification.", "before": "Check the initial creator's stage, validation and publication (law 467) over scratch account homes, with InitialCore and InitialPlatform built from signed on-disk test trees: the exact private P0 tree and its 44 barriers, the fence held through the act and free after, reads through the existing installation fence and native trust read session, the lost race, a moved stage, a scripted indeterminate rename, scripted barrier failures, ledgers short of a reservation, validation refusals, an owner change before the rename, two concurrent creators and NotPristine. Nothing runs on the real account's chain. Compiled only for tests; confers no qualification.", "parent": {"bytes": 313526, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v80.json", "sha256": "838a7f4072b408e8c4011249c89e0974d38616031cda110267c92ab762d0d1e7"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/240/description"}}, {"after": "The read side's observation path (law 458c r5 items 5, 6, 7 and 11). One session per process on one failure-latching ledger with the owner's limits; one observation per session. It reaches I through the durable write gate's own step 0 and step 1 code, shared rather than copied, with no barrier: a charged, retained, no-follow walk from the root to I under the read receipt's sealed lending, a positive absence only when the first missing suffix component is observed absent under its retained parent, and lifecycle.fence opened through the retained I. The lock wait repeats one nonblocking attempt on that same descriptor, paced at 25 ms, for at most 5 s and 201 attempts, all reserved before the first; busy retries, any other failure stops on the host I/O row, and a lock still busy at the bound is the busy row. Under the held fence it runs the receipt's rechecks and the full recheck set, reads the required members charged before they run (the fixed members up front, each lineage node before its own read), records missing, undecodable or misbound members as structural findings, and runs the same full recheck over every captured file, also after a failed read. Every refusal takes exactly the write gate's row. Never a write capability. Its consumers are the read session behind InstallationReadFence::acquire and doctor's installation check. Library only: no CLI command is wired.", "before": "The read side's observation path (law 458c r5 items 5, 6, 7 and 11). One session per process on one failure-latching ledger with the owner's limits; one observation per session. It reaches I through the durable write gate's own step 0 and step 1 code, shared rather than copied, with no barrier: a charged, retained, no-follow walk from the root to I under the read receipt's sealed lending, a positive absence only when the first missing suffix component is observed absent under its retained parent, and lifecycle.fence opened through the retained I. The lock wait repeats one nonblocking attempt on that same descriptor, paced at 25 ms, for at most 5 s and 201 attempts, all reserved before the first; busy retries, any other failure stops on the host I/O row, and a lock still busy at the bound is the busy row. Under the held fence it runs the receipt's rechecks and the full recheck set, reads the required members charged before they run (the fixed members up front, each lineage node before its own read), records missing, undecodable or misbound members as structural findings, and runs the same full recheck over every captured file, also after a failed read. Every refusal takes exactly the write gate's row. Never a write capability. Library only: no consumer uses it yet.", "parent": {"bytes": 313526, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v80.json", "sha256": "838a7f4072b408e8c4011249c89e0974d38616031cda110267c92ab762d0d1e7"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/247/description"}}, {"after": "Produce the read side's premise receipt (law 458c r5 items 1 to 4): the process's one attempt, the actor, InitialCore and InitialPlatform, exactly the creator's producers, with no intent, storage choice, disclosure, effect or fence. The receipt is private and not Clone, holds its own attempt, and lends only the sealed ReadPremiseQualification that InitialPlatform alone implements: the check that a filesystem is H's and the omission premise with 465 item 4's scope, no barrier policy. Every refusal ends in its law 468 item 6 row through 468c's maps. InstallationReadFence::acquire and doctor's installation check pass it to the observation session. Library only: no CLI command is wired.", "before": "Produce the read side's premise receipt (law 458c r5 items 1 to 4): the process's one attempt, the actor, InitialCore and InitialPlatform, exactly the creator's producers, with no intent, storage choice, disclosure, effect or fence. The receipt is private and not Clone, holds its own attempt, and lends only the sealed ReadPremiseQualification that InitialPlatform alone implements: the check that a filesystem is H's and the omission premise with 465 item 4's scope, no barrier policy. Every refusal ends in its law 468 item 6 row through 468c's maps. Library only: no read path uses it yet.", "parent": {"bytes": 313526, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v80.json", "sha256": "838a7f4072b408e8c4011249c89e0974d38616031cda110267c92ab762d0d1e7"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/250/description"}}, {"after": "The production installation reader API (law 458c r5 items 5 and 8). InstallationReadFence::acquire produces the read receipt, runs the observation session on the account's I and requires I complete; every refusal is its law 468 item 6 row, and a development build ends at CORE.NO_EMBEDDED_RELEASE. A bounded leaf or descendant capture opens only through the held I by the read session, is charged to its one ledger, and rechecks the held fence, every retained edge and the file before its bytes are consumed; a request refused before any native step (an invalid leaf, depth or bound) has no row. Provisional observations only: no selected store, core/profile admission, current authority, durability or creation permission. Cooperative first-party exclusion and the existing kernel/loader TCB apply.", "before": "Provisional observations under a native installation fence. No selected store, core/profile admission, current authority, durability or creation permission. Cooperative first-party exclusion and the existing kernel/loader TCB apply.", "parent": {"bytes": 313526, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v80.json", "sha256": "838a7f4072b408e8c4011249c89e0974d38616031cda110267c92ab762d0d1e7"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/259/description"}}, {"after": "Capture the complete structural successor census at fixed installation-relative locations under a borrowed held-fence view and shared budget.", "before": "Capture the complete structural successor census at fixed installation-relative locations under the retained native fence and shared budget.", "parent": {"bytes": 313526, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v80.json", "sha256": "838a7f4072b408e8c4011249c89e0974d38616031cda110267c92ab762d0d1e7"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/316/description"}}, {"after": "Own all original current and successor observations of one borrowed InstallationReadFence under one cumulative failure-latching budget charged to that read session's one ledger (law 458c item 6); lend the trust captures its held-fence view, run the read session's full recheck after each capture, and recheck every consumer without exposing reset or raw handles.", "before": "Own all original current and successor observations under one cumulative failure-latching budget and borrowed native fence; recheck every consumer without exposing reset or raw handles.", "parent": {"bytes": 313526, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v80.json", "sha256": "838a7f4072b408e8c4011249c89e0974d38616031cda110267c92ab762d0d1e7"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/320/description"}}, {"after": "Resolve full typed record locations under a borrowed held-fence view, retain every prefix/file and preserve native failure diagnostics across logical decoding.", "before": "Resolve full typed record locations under the retained native fence, retain every prefix/file and preserve native failure diagnostics across logical decoding.", "parent": {"bytes": 313526, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v80.json", "sha256": "838a7f4072b408e8c4011249c89e0974d38616031cda110267c92ab762d0d1e7"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/321/description"}}], "passageSupersessions": [], "selected": "docs/implementation/m2/stale-descriptions-461b/successor.json", "sha256": "2c8a4de79e156b0f0c47e579c2121adae07f804a505e3d19d3bb2b787596fad6"}, {"inputs": [{"bytes": 2323, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/read-cli-x10b/README.md", "sha256": "8601a2da2d4721c84e4ec06fe778005f88b7eddfc3ab8c4cf3d59236b3d555d9"}, {"bytes": 2707, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/read-cli-x10b/evidence/build_x10b.py", "sha256": "0887ac070121444aada3855d5b678585d29d86a4430ff46dcc8207aac721bb75"}, {"bytes": 2275, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/read-cli-x10b/evidence/verify_scratch.py", "sha256": "5687071a410a0286fc652adc6c249a1f31412cf99d277637dda332b38e3e5472"}], "passageOverrides": [{"after": "the bounded doctor report cannot be produced: more entries than its bound, or an entry that cannot be bounded; nothing is truncated", "before": "the private store cannot be opened to produce the report", "parent": {"bytes": 66800, "path": "docs/coop/design-corrections/workflows/command-inventory.v3.json", "sha256": "d303cc640154ff2b4473452c52f6578b5ffa3f20d6ed03c3976e0ddd397a4e66"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/goldens/26/situation"}}, {"after": "Resolve the report-production failure and run doctor again.", "before": "check permissions on the private store root", "parent": {"bytes": 66800, "path": "docs/coop/design-corrections/workflows/command-inventory.v3.json", "sha256": "d303cc640154ff2b4473452c52f6578b5ffa3f20d6ed03c3976e0ddd397a4e66"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/goldens/26/remedy"}}, {"after": "the bounded doctor report cannot be produced: more entries than its bound, or an entry that cannot be bounded; nothing is truncated", "before": "the private store cannot be opened to produce the report", "parent": {"bytes": 67211, "path": "docs/implementation/m1/metadata-v2/command-inventory.v4.json", "sha256": "4cd5e4179417678e67242f1faf3e74a4f1a2833bfe8d5f35f78259e6bc1bf789"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/goldens/26/situation"}}, {"after": "Resolve the report-production failure and run doctor again.", "before": "check permissions on the private store root", "parent": {"bytes": 67211, "path": "docs/implementation/m1/metadata-v2/command-inventory.v4.json", "sha256": "4cd5e4179417678e67242f1faf3e74a4f1a2833bfe8d5f35f78259e6bc1bf789"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/goldens/26/remedy"}}, {"after": "the bounded doctor report cannot be produced: more entries than its bound, or an entry that cannot be bounded; nothing is truncated", "before": "the private store cannot be opened to produce the report", "parent": {"bytes": 74644, "path": "docs/implementation/m1/source-selection-v2/reference/composed-owners/command-inventory.proposed.json", "sha256": "fa0d72a32f50d5f2b76b196b751a2b310448e36f8e78c728d701b7a585fb5f6a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/goldens/26/situation"}}, {"after": "Resolve the report-production failure and run doctor again.", "before": "check permissions on the private store root", "parent": {"bytes": 74644, "path": "docs/implementation/m1/source-selection-v2/reference/composed-owners/command-inventory.proposed.json", "sha256": "fa0d72a32f50d5f2b76b196b751a2b310448e36f8e78c728d701b7a585fb5f6a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/goldens/26/remedy"}}], "passageSupersessions": [], "selected": "docs/implementation/m2/read-cli-x10b/successor.json", "sha256": "7bc32d79927a758c04859c8e73aea95dc30c9c69d255c5618727c2287a4c06b5"}, {"inputs": [{"bytes": 5251, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/config-remedy-x12-0/README.md", "sha256": "1674536fd41ee093d4cb5c98b9c40f9b709f174f3c12420a3710532fa0294f4f"}, {"bytes": 3374, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/config-remedy-x12-0/evidence/build_x12_0.py", "sha256": "3f6f8026a2a09905034f54448e31667b901e905f02978769f17d80c6d2e5ee93"}, {"bytes": 3399, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/config-remedy-x12-0/evidence/check_remedy.py", "sha256": "cfc94893725f7b3185b85906fd93a001bf75a4b6619c4f7bac1bd7064a73d927"}, {"bytes": 2559, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/config-remedy-x12-0/evidence/verify_scratch.py", "sha256": "e430fc6f1ce0cb9e77f23621f91e97625b727f26198ecbc28984dee9c819eb08"}], "passageOverrides": [{"after": "    \"CONFIG.INVALID\": \"the configured capability or policy selection is invalid: for capabilities, name a registered capability id from the native capability matrix, and state at most one row per (capabilityId, languageMode, workspaceRoot); for policy, name exactly one bundled policy pack id, written exactly as name:version, and supply no policy document of your own or from a third party\",", "before": "    \"CONFIG.INVALID\": \"the configured capability selection is invalid: name a registered capability id from the native capability matrix, and state at most one row per (capabilityId, languageMode, workspaceRoot)\",", "parent": {"bytes": 319376, "path": "docs/coop/design-corrections/native/native_evidence_model.v2.py", "sha256": "7d1c0acf2c7d74e52c6570bba66dcb846c03710f64cb61a2c83bd1c39abab8be"}, "selector": {"line": 1158}}, {"after": "    \"CONFIG.INVALID\": \"the configured capability or policy selection is invalid: for capabilities, name a registered capability id from the native capability matrix, and state at most one row per (capabilityId, languageMode, workspaceRoot); for policy, name exactly one bundled policy pack id, written exactly as name:version, and supply no policy document of your own or from a third party\",", "before": "    \"CONFIG.INVALID\": \"the configured capability selection is invalid: name a registered capability id from the native capability matrix, and state at most one row per (capabilityId, languageMode, workspaceRoot)\",", "parent": {"bytes": 319944, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/capability-totality-reference-selection-v1/reference/native_evidence_model.py", "sha256": "e6784aa1a595222cfd5a3da55e2beaa3d0839c878d67d6821297682089bde2b9"}, "selector": {"line": 1158}}], "passageSupersessions": [], "selected": "docs/implementation/m2/config-remedy-x12-0/successor.json", "sha256": "469317223374f351bdecf24c9054f864a3249c6d23e613a605e637b72d4fd3c4"}, {"inputs": [{"bytes": 10308, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/description-batch-d1/README.md", "sha256": "82b1636b250271d8afa8847046c1ff3f7d8ac513a20b76869c547b110075a004"}, {"bytes": 3532, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/description-batch-d1/evidence/build_d1.py", "sha256": "23fb86d2080ce1d2c7a290c2cc2f3b6e1b43ceb62ecbe3f96dddc55709795573"}, {"bytes": 113673, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/description-batch-d1/evidence/descriptions.json", "sha256": "ecd23b264dbc2fb95d8068ca386513e635b7d6ad754603dd06dfb16d4560a0eb"}, {"bytes": 3137, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/description-batch-d1/evidence/verify_scratch.py", "sha256": "907eec3a569d1b3a88bb2673b0578a068ed0c75d677f02934831cdf20239cfcf"}], "passageOverrides": [{"after": "The opensip doctor ingress (law X10 r3), beside the metadata ingress under the same process-custody request authority; doctor writes nothing durable. It makes exactly one producer call, observe_installation_for_doctor, the process's one attempt, read receipt and observation session, with the native account and the authenticated release and profile producers as its only inputs, and passes doctor's other checks as empty. A produced report or a report that cannot be produced is a kind doctor envelope carrying the assembled DoctorResult unchanged; an unreachable installation is a kind failure envelope with its law 468 item 6 row as termination and its one detail in errors (HOST.IO_FAILURE for the host I/O row). Exit codes derive from the class only. Remedies are the selected golden's where one exists and otherwise one fixed text per row. The STATE.SCHEMA_UNSUPPORTED row (law X3a item 5), which has no registered remedy, takes its own fixed text. The same kind failure envelope projects law X12 r3 item 7's policy-selection rows, whose CONFIG.INVALID and POLICY.IMPERATIVE_KEY_REFUSED details take the configuration module's remedies; a detail with no remedy is a projection error.", "before": "The opensip doctor ingress (law X10 r3), beside the metadata ingress under the same process-custody request authority; doctor writes nothing durable. It makes exactly one producer call, observe_installation_for_doctor, the process's one attempt, read receipt and observation session, with the native account and the authenticated release and profile producers as its only inputs, and passes doctor's other checks as empty. A produced report or a report that cannot be produced is a kind doctor envelope carrying the assembled DoctorResult unchanged; an unreachable installation is a kind failure envelope with its law 468 item 6 row as termination and its one detail in errors (HOST.IO_FAILURE for the host I/O row). Exit codes derive from the class only. Remedies are the selected golden's where one exists and otherwise one fixed text per row.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/107/description"}}, {"after": "Check the doctor ingress from every value the security layer produces (law X10 items 4, 5 and 7): native-shaped complete and incomplete installations with no other checks (every finding kind, law X3a item 5's core and current-store findings included), explicitly synthetic report-bound inputs (one other defect plus the note, 255 plus the note versus 256, partial 256 versus 257) and every law 468 item 6 row, with the invariant row and law X3a item 5's STATE.SCHEMA_UNSUPPORTED row. Each envelope is validated against the selected command-envelope v7 source schema, its exit derives from its class, and its human output equals the lines its JSON facts imply, the complete termination included; an unproducible envelope without or with an altered nested doctor kind fails validation.", "before": "Check the doctor ingress from every value the security layer produces (law X10 items 4, 5 and 7): native-shaped complete and incomplete installations with no other checks, explicitly synthetic report-bound inputs (one other defect plus the note, 255 plus the note versus 256, partial 256 versus 257) and every law 468 item 6 row. Each envelope is validated against the selected command-envelope v7 source schema, its exit derives from its class, and its human output equals the lines its JSON facts imply, the complete termination included; an unproducible envelope without or with an altered nested doctor kind fails validation.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/108/description"}}, {"after": "The doctor report assembler (law 458c r6 items 7, 10 and 12, owner section 5 Diagnostics). A complete installation adds the informational INSTALLATION.DURABILITY_NOT_CHECKED entry with the owner's fixed text, which takes one of the 256 slots: 255 actual defects plus the note is a report, 256 is not. A report without the note keeps the 256-entry bound. defectsFound counts actual defects only, and DOCTOR.DEFECTS_FOUND is set only when that count is positive. A report that cannot be produced is HOST.IO_FAILURE / DOCTOR.REPORT_NOT_PRODUCIBLE, exit 4, and latches the report session; nothing is truncated and no envelope member is added. Doctor's installation check projects an unreachable installation onto its law 468 item 6 row with no report, and each structural finding of a reachable incomplete installation (a missing member, the pair, marker, store id, node or chain, and law X3a item 5's core and current-store findings) onto one CONFIG.CUSTODY_REFUSED entry with its installation-incomplete subject and fixed remedy. A positive DOCTOR.DEFECTS_FOUND carries the selected golden doctor-defects-found remedy. The doctor ingress (law X10) emits it through doctor_installation.", "before": "The doctor report assembler (law 458c r6 items 7, 10 and 12, owner section 5 Diagnostics). A complete installation adds the informational INSTALLATION.DURABILITY_NOT_CHECKED entry with the owner's fixed text, which takes one of the 256 slots: 255 actual defects plus the note is a report, 256 is not. A report without the note keeps the 256-entry bound. defectsFound counts actual defects only, and DOCTOR.DEFECTS_FOUND is set only when that count is positive. A report that cannot be produced is HOST.IO_FAILURE / DOCTOR.REPORT_NOT_PRODUCIBLE, exit 4, and latches the report session; nothing is truncated and no envelope member is added. Doctor's installation check projects an unreachable installation onto its law 468 item 6 row with no report, and each structural finding of a reachable incomplete installation onto one CONFIG.CUSTODY_REFUSED entry with its installation-incomplete subject and fixed remedy. Library only: nothing emits it yet.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/109/description"}}, {"after": "Project each law 468 item 6 row onto its one public D9 termination: class, exit, error code, fault cause, domain detail and subject, exactly as the 468a diagnostic routes publish them plus law 468 r5's incomplete-installation row. It also projects the rows later units added to the closed table, each with existing codes and the class S12 fixes for it: law X3a item 5's STATE.SCHEMA_UNSUPPORTED row (request-rejected, exit 2, REQUEST.SCHEMA_MAJOR_UNSUPPORTED); the fenced trust admission's rows of law X4T r9 item 10 and the live guards' TRUST.COMPONENT_REVOKED_DURING_OPERATION and OBSERVER.FAIL_STOP rows (law X4 r7 item 8, unit X4a), where each ROOT.* chain detail projects to its own code and only ROOT.SCHEMA_UNSUPPORTED leaves the admission class; and the commit facade's LEDGER.CORRUPT, MIGRATION.CORRUPT, DURABILITY.COMMIT_FAILED and project-owner rows (law X3d r6 item 9, unit X3d-1). The invariant row is SYSTEM.OUTCOME.ILLEGAL_STATE with HOST.INVARIANT_VIOLATED, exit 4. The match is exhaustive, so a new row cannot compile without its termination. InstallationTerminationV1 is the termination the doctor ingress emits for an unreachable installation (law X10), and the shape law X12 r3 item 7's policy-selection terminations take.", "before": "Project each law 468 item 6 row onto its one public D9 termination: class, exit, error code, fault cause, domain detail and subject, exactly as the 468a diagnostic routes publish them plus law 468 r5's incomplete-installation row. The match is exhaustive, so a new row cannot compile without its termination. Nothing emits it yet: no CLI command is wired.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/118/description"}}, {"after": "Verify malformed provider joins, identity-valid but replay-invalid Runs, revoked authority and stale fences cannot produce acknowledged authoritative commits. It also holds the opaque API refusal suite's compile-fail driver (law X8 r3, unit X8a): a census of the cases under crates/host/tests/refusal/cases, each row naming its file, owning crate, type, owner unit, group and category (groups I, K and L from unit X8a, group D's AdmissionPermit, FinalGate and OperationGuard rows from unit X4a, and the session types' rows of groups D, E, F and G from unit X3d-1), checked before anything compiles; a nested cargo check of opensip-host's release surface; each case compiled twice by the pinned rustc 1.95.0, the control compiling and the misuse under --cfg x8_misuse failing with exactly its one annotated error; the must-reject self-tests under crates/host/tests/refusal/selftest each rejected for the reason it names; the annotation grammar; and the scenario-fixtures source pin over the workspace manifests, with its refusals. The behavioural cases that verify the first sentence are unit X8c's.", "before": "Verify malformed provider joins, identity-valid but replay-invalid Runs, revoked authority and stale fences cannot produce acknowledged authoritative commits.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/134/description"}}, {"after": "Non-authority bounded object/edge/byte accounting borrowed across one host operation, with permanent failure, unwind closure and precharged effect/postcheck reservations; prepaid work inside a postcheck reservation draws only from its carved allowance, failing ReservedPostcheck before the work it covers (law 467 item 6). Each instance may take one settlement reserve (law X3d r6 item 8, unit X3d-0): reserve_settlement charges it to used at once and never refunds it, and settle spends a SettlementReserve this instance issued once, even after the ledger has failed, with every charge inside drawing only from its allowance; outside settle a failed ledger still refuses everything. Retained evidence identity/cache and native authority remain with their owners; constructor alone cannot establish same-attempt discipline. Includes focused accounting regressions and the compile_fail doctests that keep SettlementReserve uncloneable, unconstructible and out of WorkScope and ReservedPostchecks.", "before": "Non-authority bounded object/edge/byte accounting borrowed across one host operation, with permanent failure, unwind closure and precharged effect/postcheck reservations. Retained evidence identity/cache and native authority remain with their owners; constructor alone cannot establish same-attempt discipline. Includes focused accounting regressions.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/304/description"}}, {"after": "Exercise the public settlement reserve of the work ledger (law X3d r6 items 8 and 13, unit X3d-0): a reserve charged to used at once and never refunded; a settlement still spendable after a nested failure, an unwind, a swallowed failure, a budget overrun or a postcheck overrun has closed the ledger, which still refuses every scope, charge, run, record and effect outside it; charges inside it, including nested run, effect, ReservedPostchecks and prepaid, drawing only from the allowance with used unchanged, even past exhausted limits; an overrun inside it refused ReservedPostcheck before the work, and an Err, a swallowed failure or an unwind inside it latching both the settlement and the ledger, so no second step runs; a successful settlement leaving an open ledger open; a second reservation, before or after the spend, and a reservation on a closed ledger or beyond the limits refused on existing BudgetFailure variants, taking nothing and latching; a reserve from another instance refused Closed before its action; another ledger unchanged; and a source pin (unit X3d-1) that, outside comments and _tests.rs files, the production sources naming reserve_settlement or SettlementReserve are exactly the end-path chain work_ledger.rs, lib.rs, initial_installation.rs, read_premise.rs, operation_handoff.rs and commit_session.rs, that apart from work_ledger.rs only initial_installation.rs calls reserve_settlement, once, and that lib.rs names SettlementReserve once and reserve_settlement never. Pure accounting and borrow tests; no journal, carrier, native effect or end-path qualification.", "before": "Exercise the public settlement reserve of the work ledger (law X3d r6 items 8 and 13, unit X3d-0): a reserve charged to used at once and never refunded; a settlement still spendable after a nested failure, an unwind, a swallowed failure, a budget overrun or a postcheck overrun has closed the ledger, which still refuses every scope, charge, run, record and effect outside it; charges inside it, including nested run, effect, ReservedPostchecks and prepaid, drawing only from the allowance with used unchanged, even past exhausted limits; an overrun inside it refused ReservedPostcheck before the work, and an Err, a swallowed failure or an unwind inside it latching both the settlement and the ledger, so no second step runs; a successful settlement leaving an open ledger open; a second reservation, before or after the spend, and a reservation on a closed ledger or beyond the limits refused on existing BudgetFailure variants, taking nothing and latching; a reserve from another instance refused Closed before its action; another ledger unchanged; and a source pin that no production source names reserve_settlement or SettlementReserve until X3d-1. Pure accounting and borrow tests; no journal, carrier, native effect or end-path qualification.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/309/description"}}, {"after": "The private final commit-admission primitive, not a CommitSession constructor: it is handed no custody, grant, generation, writer or journal guard and no replay evidence of its own. FinalGate is one SeqCst two-bit atomic (0 preparing, 1 admitted, 2 latched before admission, 3 admitted then latched) whose compare-exchange 0 to 1 is the only admission and whose fetch-OR 2 is the latch, never reset. OperationGate (law X4 r7 items 2 and 3, unit X4a) is an operation's one gate, created at the lease-free point before any prepared value, not Clone, with its StopObserver to latch and observe it; its admit binds the prepared value and mints the one AdmissionPermit, or hands the value back with the gate's state on a refusal, so there is no second permit. PreparedAttempt is the same gate created with its prepared value. An AdmissionPermit is consumed once, and a later latch records a stop without relabelling its result. It places law X9 r1 G4's x4.gate.admit.after and x4.gate.latch.after points. PreparedJournalSeal (law X3d r6 item 4 step 3.3, unit X3d-1) binds the SEAL record that seal_under_append_lock is about to append to the caller's borrowed evaluator-minted ReplayedRun by run id, refusing another record type, a missing or fixture run id and a mismatched run; it establishes no live custody, guard, generation or durable commit. CommitSession, JournalWriteTxn and JournalSealBinding are custody/commit_session.rs's, and the S6 checkpoint is custody/operation_guard.rs's. Its tests check every bounded admit, latch and observe trace exhaustively, a latch before and after admission, a dropped permit never reopening admission, a concurrent stop never lost and no effect duplicated, and the seal binding's refusals.", "before": "Mint opaque live CommitSession from actual project/store custody, operational grants and writer guard; bind exact operation/generation and perform final S6 checkpoint under the journal lock. Own the session-consuming JournalWriteTxn, opaque JournalSealBinding and atomic final commit-admission gate; accept only evaluator-minted ReplayedRun for SEAL targets.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/327/description"}}, {"after": "Check X2c on scratch homes with a creator-published P0, an ordinary writer over 462's signed test trees, and scratch projects (no real home, installation or Git): canonical UUIDv4 candidates, exact document lengths and later spellings and R0's exact re-encoding, and the S12 scope-limit and identity rows; a whole registration (one ACTIVE random row binding the root's path and incarnation, private host and projects, a namespace with exactly two empty private leases, a 0700 .opensip and the exact 0600 marker, no temporary left, the gate's and the project's rechecks passing, a read session then classifying Eligible, and a second registration refused); a reused .opensip and existing parents admitted and never changed; Eligible, RESERVED and one-sided roots refused with nothing written; a VCS change after the observation, a transition entry and a non-private host refused before any effect; eight colliding ProjectId draws, eight occupied namespace paths and failed entropy as host I/O; 4097 rows, a RESERVED document over 4 MiB and a worst later terminal spelling over 4 MiB as PROJECT.SCOPE_LIMIT before any effect; a stop at each of the eleven steps leaving its durable prefix, spending the writer and classifying as the registry owner's recovery cases; a registry rewritten or a marker replaced before ACTIVE refused without ACTIVE; a later registry change seen by the registered project's recheck; and the two namespace footprints (unit X2e): an N populated by X3b's carrier start refused as changed under the Exact footprint and admitted under Started, and a lease that is no longer empty, or missing, refused under Started.", "before": "Check X2c on scratch homes with a creator-published P0, an ordinary writer over 462's signed test trees, and scratch projects (no real home, installation or Git): canonical UUIDv4 candidates, exact document lengths and later spellings and R0's exact re-encoding, and the S12 scope-limit and identity rows; a whole registration (one ACTIVE random row binding the root's path and incarnation, private host and projects, a namespace with exactly two empty private leases, a 0700 .opensip and the exact 0600 marker, no temporary left, the gate's and the project's rechecks passing, a read session then classifying Eligible, and a second registration refused); a reused .opensip and existing parents admitted and never changed; Eligible, RESERVED and one-sided roots refused with nothing written; a VCS change after the observation, a transition entry and a non-private host refused before any effect; eight colliding ProjectId draws, eight occupied namespace paths and failed entropy as host I/O; 4097 rows, a RESERVED document over 4 MiB and a worst later terminal spelling over 4 MiB as PROJECT.SCOPE_LIMIT before any effect; a stop at each of the eleven steps leaving its durable prefix, spending the writer and classifying as the registry owner's recovery cases; a registry rewritten or a marker replaced before ACTIVE refused without ACTIVE; and a later registry change seen by the registered project's recheck.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/334/description"}}, {"after": "The owner \u00a75 durable write gate a creator invocation passes after its creator act ends (law 468 r5 items 1 to 5). One failure-latching ledger with the owner's limits, one admission per process. Step 0 is a charged, retained, no-follow walk from the root through H, Library, Application Support, OpenSIP and I with the 460 and 465 custody predicates; InitialPlatform's omission premise applies only to the root-to-H prefix, Library and Application Support, through its own fstatfs, and OpenSIP and I must be private. Step 1 opens lifecycle.fence through the retained I handle and takes one nonblocking exclusive lock; busy latches. Under the held fence it rechecks the account, the retained chain, the I-parent name and I's identity, custody of I and its parent, H's filesystem, the locked fence, and the required files of a complete I (fence, registry, pair, endpoint marker, node chain, trust current record) by owner, mode, link and ACL; then I's barrier and I's parent's barrier, receipts checked against their handles, and the recheck set again, reserved with the barriers and run even after a failed barrier. The first recheck is the session's one read of each required file (law X3a item 2, unit X3a-1): it keeps each file's full metadata sample and the decoded pair, marker, node chain (at most 64 nodes; past that, the budget row) and trust current record, whose C.store must name the pair's (S, G, K); the second recheck, and every later gate recheck, compare those samples and read nothing again, so an in-place rewrite is a change. It borrows only the sealed InitialPlatform qualification and reuses no creator handle, lock or receipt. Typed refusals follow law 468 item 6, with law X3a item 5's STATE.SCHEMA_UNSUPPORTED; the public mapping is separate. After admission the gate's own ledger charges each further step for the installation it admitted (charged, scope, recheck) and stays spent after any refusal (spend). DurableInstallation lends the retained I, H's spelling as walked, the account's UID and the endpoint values; WriterFence, its held fence as the trust readers' HeldFence (law X4 r7 item 2, unit X4a); current_trust, the trust current owner's inputs from the one state.v1 read, and observe_private_file, that owner's no-follow metadata recheck of one required file (law X4T r9 item 7). The retained registry and state.v1 samples advance only from the predecessor a confirmed publication reconfirmed (advance_registry, unit X2c; advance_current, unit X4T-b), and into_handoff moves the installation's owners into the operation handoff with the fence lock still held (unit X2e). Library only.", "before": "The owner \u00a75 durable write gate a creator invocation passes after its creator act ends (law 468 r5 items 1 to 5). One failure-latching ledger with the owner's limits, one admission per process. Step 0 is a charged, retained, no-follow walk from the root through H, Library, Application Support, OpenSIP and I with the 460 and 465 custody predicates; InitialPlatform's omission premise applies only to the root-to-H prefix, Library and Application Support, through its own fstatfs, and OpenSIP and I must be private. Step 1 opens lifecycle.fence through the retained I handle and takes one nonblocking exclusive lock; busy latches. Under the held fence it rechecks the account, the retained chain, the I-parent name and I's identity, custody of I and its parent, H's filesystem, the locked fence, and the required files of a complete I (fence, registry, pair, endpoint marker, node chain, trust current record) by owner, mode, link and ACL; then I's barrier and I's parent's barrier, receipts checked against their handles, and the same recheck reserved with the barriers and run even after a failed barrier. It borrows only the sealed InitialPlatform qualification and reuses no creator handle, lock or receipt. Typed refusals follow law 468 item 6; the public mapping is separate. Library only.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/338/description"}}, {"after": "Check the durable write gate (law 468 r5) over scratch account homes after a real scratch publication, with InitialCore and InitialPlatform built from signed on-disk test trees: the step order under a held fence, the required files, the busy fence, first and second recheck refusals, a replaced required file, scripted barrier failures with the recheck still run, short ledgers, the premise present or absent at an omitted-ACL prefix, OpenSIP or I with an omitted ACL refusing even with a premise, a deep home well inside the owner's budget, and one gate per process whose only input is the platform qualification. It also checks law X3a's one read (unit X3a-1): the endpoint values retained from step 2 with the ledger kept for one more full recheck, an in-place rewrite between the rechecks as required-files-changed, an undecodable trust current record as incomplete, a node chain past the 64-node limit or unended after 64 nodes on the budget row, and a 64-node chain on a deep home read once within the owner's caps; and that a confirmed trust publication is the only advance of the retained state.v1 sample, only from its reconfirmed predecessor and leaving the registry sample untouched, an unadvanced publication failing the recheck (unit X4T-b), with two confirmed publications of one hold followed in order and the second before the first refused as changed (unit X4B-b). Nothing runs on the real account's I. Compiled only for tests; confers no qualification.", "before": "Check the durable write gate (law 468 r5) over scratch account homes after a real scratch publication, with InitialCore and InitialPlatform built from signed on-disk test trees: the step order under a held fence, the required files, the busy fence, first and second recheck refusals, a replaced required file, scripted barrier failures with the recheck still run, short ledgers, the premise present or absent at an omitted-ACL prefix, OpenSIP or I with an omitted ACL refusing even with a premise, a deep home well inside the owner's budget, and one gate per process whose only input is the platform qualification. Nothing runs on the real account's I. Compiled only for tests; confers no qualification.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/339/description"}}, {"after": "Doctor's installation check (law 458c r6 items 7 and 12). It observes I through the same read receipt and observation session as every read command, and differs only at item 7's end: a reachable I that is incomplete or contradictory is not a refusal for doctor, and each structural finding is returned in member order, never collapsed. Every refusal before or at a recheck, and every ordinary read failure, ends on its law 468 item 6 row exactly as for any read command. The fence is released before the result is returned. Its findings include law X3a item 5's core finding (the pair names a core other than the selected core) and current-store finding (the trust current record is over its cap, does not decode, or names another store). observe_installation_for_doctor is the one producer call of the host's doctor ingress (law X10).", "before": "Doctor's installation check (law 458c r6 items 7 and 12). It observes I through the same read receipt and observation session as every read command, and differs only at item 7's end: a reachable I that is incomplete or contradictory is not a refusal for doctor, and each structural finding is returned in member order, never collapsed. Every refusal before or at a recheck, and every ordinary read failure, ends on its law 468 item 6 row exactly as for any read command. The fence is released before the result is returned. Library only.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/340/description"}}, {"after": "The read session for installation readers (law 458c r5 item 8). It keeps a complete I that the observation session admitted: the held fence, the retained chain, the account and the read receipt, with the observation session's one ledger. Every later capture and recheck is charged to that ledger before it runs (item 6). A capture opens prefixes and a leaf only through the retained I and its retained descendants: each directory judged private by the owner's predicate on a charged capture and named exactly, the file judged a private regular file, bounded, named, on H's filesystem, read, judged again and reopened by name. Its failures are values, so the full recheck (item 5 step 4) runs after every capture, over the fence, the observation's required files and every file captured since; any refusal latches the session. Between full rechecks, the held-fence check samples the carrier's name and identity, and a capture's own recheck samples its edges and file. It lends the trust readers a held-fence view and charges their retained budget to the same ledger. Captures never walk from the root again; admit_project_root alone walks from / to the launch or explicit path and the selected project root (law X2 r5, unit X2b-1), charged on the session ledger. Each of the session's admissions runs on its ledger under the held fence, then one full recheck, and any refusal latches it: store_endpoint admits the selected store endpoint from the observation's one read against the read receipt's selected core, reading no file (law X3a items 1 and 2, unit X3a-1); admit_project_root selects and admits the root with its registry classification and rechecks it; observe_project_tracking observes the root's Git tracking, reading the process environment only to refuse and the fixed system sources as refusal-only evidence (law X2 r7 item 6a, unit X2b-2); and admit_namespace makes the session the namespace fence holder for an Eligible root, for a SHARED-READ lease only (law X2 item 7, unit X2d). Never a write capability.", "before": "The read session for installation readers (law 458c r5 item 8). It keeps a complete I that the observation session admitted: the held fence, the retained chain, the account and the read receipt, with the observation session's one ledger. Every later capture and recheck is charged to that ledger before it runs (item 6). A capture opens prefixes and a leaf only through the retained I and its retained descendants: each directory judged private by the owner's predicate on a charged capture and named exactly, the file judged a private regular file, bounded, named, on H's filesystem, read, judged again and reopened by name. Its failures are values, so the full recheck (item 5 step 4) runs after every capture, over the fence, the observation's required files and every file captured since; any refusal latches the session. Between full rechecks, the held-fence check samples the carrier's name and identity, and a capture's own recheck samples its edges and file. It lends the trust readers a held-fence view and charges their retained budget to the same ledger. Nothing walks from the root again. Never a write capability.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/347/description"}}, {"after": "Test support for installation readers on scratch account homes: publish a real P0 with the creator composition under a scratch H, then hold read fences on it through the observation session with the read receipt over signed on-disk test trees, and write further files below I as private creations. It also extends the published store's node chain and reselects the pair at a given generation, or rewrites the root node to name an unread predecessor (law X3a item 3's chains); replaces the P0 trust store with the signed accepted store for the same S and K, in place or as another writer would by atomic replacement with state.v1 renamed last, giving every new trust file the zero-rights owner allow (unit X4a); and restarts a creation or session refused only for churn in a shared ancestor above the scratch parent, at most eight attempts (F4). It never opens the real account's I.", "before": "Test support for installation readers on scratch account homes: publish a real P0 with the creator composition under a scratch H, then hold read fences on it through the observation session with the read receipt over signed on-disk test trees, and write further files below I as private creations. It never opens the real account's I.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/348/description"}}, {"after": "Namespace admission and leases (law X2 r8 item 7, unit X2d) under a held installation fence, on the fence holder's one ledger: the ordinary writer's law 468 gate (APPEND-WRITE or EXCLUSIVE) or the 458c read session (SHARED-READ only). For an Eligible root with its item 6a tracking observation (N from R0's ACTIVE row) or a project the same writer has just registered (N from R2's ACTIVE row, which must still classify Eligible), never from a caller: the root admission's recheck (chain, incarnation, marker, registry owner, v1 absence) with the tracking recheck, or the registered project's recheck; then I/host, I/host/projects and N confirmed private, exactly named and on their parents' device, and writer.lease and readers.lease opened no-follow and judged private, all retained, with no project lock taken. That NamespaceTarget is the seam where X3b's floor step runs (X3b r8 items 1 and 3, composed by X3b-3): fence held, R current, no project lock. The lease rechecks the subject, the directories and both retained carriers again, then, as one effect reserved before the first lock, takes S7's locks without blocking (SHARED-READ readers.lease LOCK_SH|LOCK_NB; APPEND-WRITE writer.lease LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB; EXCLUSIVE writer.lease then readers.lease, both LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB) on the retained carriers and rechecks each locked carrier's name binding and the directories, then the holder's full recheck. A busy carrier is the busy row; a busy or failed lock or any later refusal releases every lock already taken, in reverse order, while the fence is still held, and spends the gate or latches the session. Absent namespace objects are the incomplete row, custody is 468's custody row, a change is required-files-changed, and FirstUseCandidate is a broken composition. The result, FencedNamespace (the held lease, the ACTIVE row snapshot and the root admission), borrows the fence holder, has no upgrade and never releases the fence: it can be rechecked or dropped, which releases its lease, until item 7a's handoff (X2e). For X2e it lends the lease-free point (NamespaceTarget::lease_free: the holder, the retained N and N's id, where X4a's fenced trust admission runs before X3b's floor step) and moves its owners out (FencedNamespace::into_parts to NamespaceParts, the lease still held, nothing copied). After the fence's release (law X4 r7 items 4 and 8, unit X4a), recheck_namespace_at rechecks host, projects and N by name binding and privacy under the retained I, and HeldLease::recheck_held rechecks each held carrier's name binding and that its lock is still held: a fresh nonblocking EXCLUSIVE attempt on a new descriptor must be refused, and a granted probe is dropped at once and refused as S7's busy row, never a changed file. A cfg(test) seam releases the locks while keeping each carrier's judged sample (the lost-lock row). No fence-free lease (item 7's recovery exception is X6's). Library only.", "before": "Namespace admission and leases (law X2 r8 item 7, unit X2d) under a held installation fence, on the fence holder's one ledger: the ordinary writer's law 468 gate (APPEND-WRITE or EXCLUSIVE) or the 458c read session (SHARED-READ only). For an Eligible root with its item 6a tracking observation (N from R0's ACTIVE row) or a project the same writer has just registered (N from R2's ACTIVE row, which must still classify Eligible), never from a caller: the root admission's recheck (chain, incarnation, marker, registry owner, v1 absence) with the tracking recheck, or the registered project's recheck; then I/host, I/host/projects and N confirmed private, exactly named and on their parents' device, and writer.lease and readers.lease opened no-follow and judged private, all retained, with no project lock taken. That NamespaceTarget is the seam where X3b's floor step runs (X3b r8 items 1 and 3, composed by X3b-3): fence held, R current, no project lock. The lease rechecks the subject, the directories and both retained carriers again, then, as one effect reserved before the first lock, takes S7's locks without blocking (SHARED-READ readers.lease LOCK_SH|LOCK_NB; APPEND-WRITE writer.lease LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB; EXCLUSIVE writer.lease then readers.lease, both LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB) on the retained carriers and rechecks each locked carrier's name binding and the directories, then the holder's full recheck. A busy carrier is the busy row; a busy or failed lock or any later refusal releases every lock already taken, in reverse order, while the fence is still held, and spends the gate or latches the session. Absent namespace objects are the incomplete row, custody is 468's custody row, a change is required-files-changed, and FirstUseCandidate is a broken composition. The result, FencedNamespace (the held lease, the ACTIVE row snapshot and the root admission), borrows the fence holder, has no upgrade and never releases the fence: it can be rechecked or dropped, which releases its lease, until item 7a's handoff (X2e). No fence-free lease (item 7's recovery exception is X6's). Library only.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/356/description"}}, {"after": "The live security guards of one ProjectOperation (law X4 r7 items 2 to 8; unit X4a), custody.rs's macOS module beside X2e's operation_handoff. LeaseFree creates the operation's one FinalGate (commit_authority's OperationGate, never reset) and its FreshnessMonitor first, at X2e's lease-free point under the held fence and before any lease, and runs the monitor's first read with the counter receiving that read's own opening sample (X4B r5 item 1). OperationGuard is built inside the handoff from that gate, monitor and admitted view with the trust store's retained handles, and moves into ProjectOperation, which starts its one observer thread: every 5 s (in tests, only on request) it performs one observation through the shared monitor, whose one history sits behind one mutex shared with every checkpoint so their reads serialize and a waiting read's wait is inside its bound; the observer never takes the fence, appends nothing, and charges only its own per-observation ledger; it stops when the guard is dropped. A revoking observation latches the gate with trust-revoked or policy; an unrelated update or a policy change keeping every required grant (none for any M2 writer) is recorded as drift; any other failure is a fail-stop. Checkpoint, lent by ProjectOperation::journal inside the write receipt's attempt-ledger charge and requiring a JournalAppendHeld borrow of the operation's own append lock (level 4), runs item 3's steps inside the x4.checkpoint scope: item 4's mandatory guard rechecks on the attempt ledger (the write receipt's actor, core and platform; the held lease by name binding and by a fresh nonblocking EXCLUSIVE probe on a new descriptor, a lost lock being S7's busy row; the original project root, .opensip and marker; the namespace; the store marker and lineage nodes by full sample; and the operation joins N, S, G, K and the append lock's N), the final monitored observation and S6's predicate against the immutable start epoch, the latch check, and the admission-boundary check (one clock sample, no I/O, boot unchanged, within 10 s of the final read's earliest instant), with the monitor held from the observation through admission; then FinalGate::admit binds the prepared value and mints the one AdmissionPermit (handing the value back on a refusal), or the effect's own intent append follows. Every failure latches the gate and records the first stop cause (revoked, fail-stop with its reason, or a stale guard with that guard's own row). OperationGuard::stop (law X3d r6 item 3, unit X3d-1) latches the gate for a certain refusal (the fetch-OR 2: 0 to 2 before admission, 1 to 3 after it, never relabelling the admitted outcome), recording no cause. trust_termination maps X4T r9 item 10's fenced admission rows onto new InstallationTermination variants on existing details. It places law X9 r1's points x4.observer.tick (a gate replacing the observer's timed wait when armed), x4.observer.after-observation and x4.checkpoint.before-observation, .after-observation and .before-admit. cfg(test) only: a scripted monotonic clock, a manual cadence and observer ticks on request. Library only.", "before": "The live security guards of one ProjectOperation (law X4 r7 items 2 to 8; unit X4a), custody.rs's macOS module beside X2e's operation_handoff. LeaseFree creates the operation's one FinalGate (commit_authority's OperationGate, never reset) and its FreshnessMonitor first, at X2e's lease-free point under the held fence and before any lease, and runs the monitor's first read with the counter receiving that read's own opening sample (X4B r5 item 1). OperationGuard is built inside the handoff from that gate, monitor and admitted view with the trust store's retained handles, and moves into ProjectOperation, which starts its one observer thread: every 5 s (in tests, only on request) it performs one observation through the shared monitor, whose one history sits behind one mutex shared with every checkpoint so their reads serialize and a waiting read's wait is inside its bound; the observer never takes the fence, appends nothing, and charges only its own per-observation ledger; it stops when the guard is dropped. A revoking observation latches the gate with trust-revoked or policy; an unrelated update or a policy change keeping every required grant (none for any M2 writer) is recorded as drift; any other failure is a fail-stop. Checkpoint, lent by ProjectOperation::journal inside the write receipt's attempt-ledger charge and requiring a JournalAppendHeld borrow of the operation's own append lock (level 4), runs item 3's steps inside the x4.checkpoint scope: item 4's mandatory guard rechecks on the attempt ledger (the write receipt's actor, core and platform; the held lease by name binding and by a fresh nonblocking EXCLUSIVE probe on a new descriptor, a lost lock being S7's busy row; the original project root, .opensip and marker; the namespace; the store marker and lineage nodes by full sample; and the operation joins N, S, G, K and the append lock's N), the final monitored observation and S6's predicate against the immutable start epoch, the latch check, and the admission-boundary check (one clock sample, no I/O, boot unchanged, within 10 s of the final read's earliest instant), with the monitor held from the observation through admission; then FinalGate::admit binds the prepared value and mints the one AdmissionPermit (handing the value back on a refusal), or the effect's own intent append follows. Every failure latches the gate and records the first stop cause (revoked, fail-stop with its reason, or a stale guard with that guard's own row). trust_termination maps X4T r9 item 10's fenced admission rows onto new InstallationTermination variants on existing details. It places law X9 r1's points x4.observer.tick (a gate replacing the observer's timed wait when armed), x4.observer.after-observation and x4.checkpoint.before-observation, .after-observation and .before-admit. cfg(test) only: a scripted monotonic clock, a manual cadence and observer ticks on request. Library only.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/358/description"}}, {"after": "The checked operation handoff (law X2 r8 item 7a, unit X2e) composed with the grant journal's floor step, carrier start and end step (law X3b r10 items 1, 3, 3a and 4, unit X3b-3) and with X4a's live guards (law X4 r7 items 2 and 6), on one ordinary writer's law 468 gate under one held installation fence. In order: the selected store endpoint joined from the gate's one read (law X3a r5); the subject's namespace admitted (X2d, no lock); at the lease-free point, with R current and no project lock held, first X4a's gate and monitor created and X4T's fenced admission run as the monitor's first read on the gate's ledger, with X4B-b's bootstrap acceptance (from a BootstrapSource the write receipt lends) and its one confirming admission inside that read on F absent, the gate following each confirmed publication, and the trust store's handles retained; then I/trust confirmed private and N's absolute spelling admitted only if a no-follow status finds the retained N, then X3b's floor step on the write receipt's attempt ledger (X3b's operation ledger) followed by the gate's and the receipt's rechecks; the APPEND-WRITE or EXCLUSIVE lease (X2d); the join, rechecking the subject, the namespace, each locked carrier's binding and the gate, and deriving owner \u00a78's five-field binding {schemaVersion 1, N, S, G, K} from the ACTIVE row and the admitted endpoint; the transfer of the owners out of the fenced gate, never copied; X3b's carrier start on the attempt ledger (INIT creation when the floor step found INIT, the start against the floor step's observation, the append lock), then every owner rechecked again with a registered subject's namespace held to the started footprint; OperationGuard built under the fence and moved into the operation, starting its observer; and only then the fence's release, ending the gate's ledger. Any refusal spends the gate and releases the append lock, the lease and then the fence. ProjectOperation is not Clone, exported only because the public CommitSession::open takes it (law X3d r6 item 2, unit X3d-1), and cannot be built or read outside security. It holds X4a's OperationGuard, the started carrier and start tail, the held lease, the binding and the ACTIVE row snapshot, the subject's owners, the carrier place, the endpoint values and required files' full samples with the retained chain to I (no fence), and the write receipt, whose attempt ledger charges the operation's journal steps, each lent X4's Checkpoint by journal; charge, reserve_end_path_settlement and settle_end_path serve X3d-1's commit session (a step with no checkpoint; the one settlement reserve, spent with no checkpoint even on a closed ledger). Its end path drops the guard (its observer stops) and the append lock and releases the lease; the end step is then not entered when the owner reports an uncertain journal outcome or when the attempt ledger is closed (law X3b r10 item 4): ProjectOperation::end returns NotEntered, with no fence walk, read or copy and nothing disclosed; otherwise end_entered, on the attempt ledger, retakes the fence by the shared charged walk, no-follow fence open and S7's bounded wait, requires the walked I to be the retained I and trust, host, projects and N still bound by name, runs, when the owner reports X3d's CarrierCapacityExhausted with every journal outcome certain, X3b-4's rollover (law X3b r10 item 13) under its own EXCLUSIVE lease, then the end step's probe, read and floor copy, and releases the fence; a failure is a value that never rewrites the operation's outcome, and a rollover outcome is returned for disclosure on its own row. It places x3d.finish's lease-release and end-step points. CarrierPlace, built only here, is the one input of the carrier location's production constructor. Rows: the endpoint's 468c row, X2's item 8 rows, X3b's item 8 carrier rows, the budget row, X4T's trust rows through trust_termination, X4's fail-stop row and the observer's host I/O row. No read-session handoff. Library only.", "before": "The checked operation handoff (law X2 r8 item 7a, unit X2e) composed with the grant journal's floor step, carrier start and end step (law X3b r10 items 1, 3, 3a and 4, unit X3b-3), on one ordinary writer's law 468 gate under one held installation fence. In order: the selected store endpoint joined from the gate's one read (law X3a r5); the subject's namespace admitted (X2d, no lock); at the lease-free point, with R current and no project lock held, I/trust confirmed private and N's absolute spelling admitted only if a no-follow status finds the retained N, then X3b's floor step on the write receipt's attempt ledger (X3b's operation ledger) followed by the gate's and the receipt's rechecks; the APPEND-WRITE or EXCLUSIVE lease (X2d); the join, rechecking the subject, the namespace, each locked carrier's binding and the gate, and deriving owner \u00a78's five-field binding {schemaVersion 1, N, S, G, K} from the ACTIVE row and the admitted endpoint; the transfer of the owners out of the fenced gate, never copied; X3b's carrier start on the attempt ledger (INIT creation when the floor step found INIT, the start against the floor step's observation, the append lock), then every owner rechecked again with a registered subject's namespace held to the started footprint; and only then the fence's release, ending the gate's ledger. Any refusal spends the gate and releases the append lock, the lease and then the fence. ProjectOperation is private and not Clone: the started carrier and start tail, the held lease, the binding and the ACTIVE row snapshot, the subject's owners, the carrier place, the endpoint values and required files' full samples with the retained chain to I (no fence), and the write receipt, whose attempt ledger charges the operation's journal steps. Its end path drops the append lock and releases the lease; the end step is then not entered when the owner reports an uncertain journal outcome or when the attempt ledger is closed (law X3b r10 item 4): ProjectOperation::end returns NotEntered, with no fence walk, read or copy and nothing disclosed; otherwise, on the attempt ledger, it retakes the fence by the shared charged walk, no-follow fence open and S7's bounded wait, requires the walked I to be the retained I and trust, host, projects and N still bound by name, runs, when the owner reports X3d's CarrierCapacityExhausted with every journal outcome certain, X3b-4's rollover (law X3b r10 item 13) under its own EXCLUSIVE lease, then the end step's probe, read and floor copy, and releases the fence; a failure is a value that never rewrites the operation's outcome, and a rollover outcome is returned for disclosure on its own row. CarrierPlace, built only here, is the one input of the carrier location's production constructor. Rows: the endpoint's 468c row, X2's item 8 rows, X3b's item 8 carrier rows and the budget row. OperationGuard (X4a) is a seam not built here; no read-session handoff. Library only.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/360/description"}}, {"after": "Check X2e with X3b-3 on scratch homes with a creator-published P0 whose trust store is replaced by X4T-0's signed accepted store for the same S and K, ordinary writers over 462's signed test trees and a registered scratch project (no real home, installation or Git), each handoff run through a begin_test adapter on a scripted guard seam (a scripted monitor clock, observer ticks on request, a fixed invocation), other holders simulated by nonblocking flocks on separate descriptors and S7's wait run on a scripted clock: a fresh registration's APPEND-WRITE handoff (fence released, writer.lease held and readers.lease free, the INIT floor, the created carrier and its COMMITTED 0 witness, owner \u00a78's binding equal to the ACTIVE row's N and the endpoint's S, G and K, then an end step leaving the floor unchanged and nothing locked); an Eligible root's EXCLUSIVE handoff, a certain append leaving the floor until the end step copies it forward, and the next writer's start at that tail; the floor step written before the lease when EXCLUSIVE then refuses busy, with no carrier created; a busy writer.lease skipping the floor step and refusing busy with nothing written; a floorLost quarantine refused before any lease with nothing written; a budget cut at the handoff's last charge, after the carrier start, releasing the lease and the fence, with the next writer starting on what it left; an uncertain outcome releasing the lease with no fence taken and no copy, and the next writer's floor step copying forward; a busy fence at the end waited for within S7's bound, then disclosed as the busy row with the lease already released; a closed attempt ledger not entering the end step (no walk while the fence is held elsewhere, no copy, nothing disclosed), and the next writer's floor step copying forward; a moved namespace refusing the end copy as required-files-changed with the fence released; a capacity exhaustion reported at an exhausted tail rolling the generation over under the retaken fence (TERMINAL closing generation 1, witness COMMITTED at generation 2) and copying the floor to generation 2's empty tail, with the next writer starting there; the rollover's outcome returned whatever the attempt-ledger charge returned, with the copy's result beside it: a failed reservation (Refused on the budget, no copy), a writer.lease taken by another writer before the rollover (Skipped, then the copy Skipped), a floor I/O refusal (Refused on the host I/O row, then the copy's host I/O failure), an undetermined OPEN at X3b-4's test point (Undetermined on the durability row, no copy), and a copy failure after Rolled (Rolled kept, generation 2 open, the copy's host I/O failure); and a source pin that the carrier location's only production constructor takes this module's carrier place. It also includes operation_live_tests.rs (X4a through this handoff) and commit_session_tests.rs (X3d-1) as child modules that reuse its scratch-home helpers.", "before": "Check X2e with X3b-3 on scratch homes with a creator-published P0, ordinary writers over 462's signed test trees and a registered scratch project (no real home, installation or Git), other holders simulated by nonblocking flocks on separate descriptors and S7's wait run on a scripted clock: a fresh registration's APPEND-WRITE handoff (fence released, writer.lease held and readers.lease free, the INIT floor, the created carrier and its COMMITTED 0 witness, owner \u00a78's binding equal to the ACTIVE row's N and the endpoint's S, G and K, then an end step leaving the floor unchanged and nothing locked); an Eligible root's EXCLUSIVE handoff, a certain append leaving the floor until the end step copies it forward, and the next writer's start at that tail; the floor step written before the lease when EXCLUSIVE then refuses busy, with no carrier created; a busy writer.lease skipping the floor step and refusing busy with nothing written; a floorLost quarantine refused before any lease with nothing written; a budget cut at the handoff's last charge, after the carrier start, releasing the lease and the fence, with the next writer starting on what it left; an uncertain outcome releasing the lease with no fence taken and no copy, and the next writer's floor step copying forward; a busy fence at the end waited for within S7's bound, then disclosed as the busy row with the lease already released; a closed attempt ledger not entering the end step (no walk while the fence is held elsewhere, no copy, nothing disclosed), and the next writer's floor step copying forward; a moved namespace refusing the end copy as required-files-changed with the fence released; a capacity exhaustion reported at an exhausted tail rolling the generation over under the retaken fence (TERMINAL closing generation 1, witness COMMITTED at generation 2) and copying the floor to generation 2's empty tail, with the next writer starting there; the rollover's outcome returned whatever the attempt-ledger charge returned, with the copy's result beside it: a failed reservation (Refused on the budget, no copy), a writer.lease taken by another writer before the rollover (Skipped, then the copy Skipped), a floor I/O refusal (Refused on the host I/O row, then the copy's host I/O failure), an undetermined OPEN at X3b-4's test point (Undetermined on the durability row, no copy), and a copy failure after Rolled (Rolled kept, generation 2 open, the copy's host I/O failure); and a source pin that the carrier location's only production constructor takes this module's carrier place.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/361/description"}}, {"after": "Check X4a through X2e's real handoff on scratch homes (a creator-published P0 whose trust store is replaced by X4T-0's signed accepted store for the same S and K except in the P0 acceptance case, ordinary writers over 462's signed test trees, a scripted monitor clock, observer ticks on request, other holders as flocks on separate descriptors; no real home or installation): the first read admitting the start view at the lease-free point and the write-ahead publication completing the handoff, then a tick with no drift; a P0 installation accepted inside the monitor's one first read (law X4B r5 item 1, unit X4B-b): the read's bracket opening on P0 and closing on the accepted state.v1, both under the fence and before any lease, the anchor the opening sample's projection and evalHighWater its wall time, revision 2 (the confirming admission wrote nothing) with the four carried roles ST-TRUSTED, the handoff completing and a tick reading the same store, and a next operation writing nothing; an effect checkpoint then its RA, the commit admission's one permit, a revoking tick after admission taking the gate from 1 to 3 without relabelling the admitted result, and a second admission refused; a pause beyond the bound after the first read fail-stopping before any effect and closing the attempt ledger; ticks and checkpoints sharing one history; a pause after the final observation failing the admission boundary; a boot change; another operation's level 4 refused as a broken caller; an unrelated revocation update continuing as drift while another holder keeps the fence; a revoking update latching through the observer and through the checkpoint's own observation; a newer view whose revocation body is missing failing unreadable, and a pointer back below the start view's counters failing as a rollback; a state.v1 replacement during the first attempt absorbed inside one monitored read and a second one failing as mixed; the stale guards (a lost lease lock on the busy row, a replaced lease file, a substituted marker, a replaced or missing store marker, a namespace no longer private, a substituted project root), each refusing before any effect on its own row; an unrelated registration not invalidating the operation; a repeated checkpoint after journal work latching on a guard gone stale; a read waiting on the shared monitor counting its wait; and the end path stopping the observer and releasing everything.", "before": "Check X4a through X2e's real handoff on scratch homes (a creator-published P0 whose trust store is replaced by X4T-0's signed accepted store for the same S and K, ordinary writers over 462's signed test trees, a scripted monitor clock, observer ticks on request, other holders as flocks on separate descriptors; no real home or installation): the first read admitting the start view at the lease-free point and the write-ahead publication completing the handoff, then a tick with no drift; a P0 installation refusing TRUST.NO_ADMITTED_TIME_CONTEXT before any lease with nothing written; an effect checkpoint then its RA, the commit admission's one permit, a revoking tick after admission taking the gate from 1 to 3 without relabelling the admitted result, and a second admission refused; a pause beyond the bound after the first read fail-stopping before any effect and closing the attempt ledger; ticks and checkpoints sharing one history; a pause after the final observation failing the admission boundary; a boot change; another operation's level 4 refused as a broken caller; an unrelated revocation update continuing as drift while another holder keeps the fence; a revoking update latching through the observer and through the checkpoint's own observation; a newer view whose revocation body is missing failing unreadable, and a pointer back below the start view's counters failing as a rollback; a state.v1 replacement during the first attempt absorbed inside one monitored read and a second one failing as mixed; the stale guards (a lost lease lock on the busy row, a replaced lease file, a substituted marker, a replaced or missing store marker, a namespace no longer private, a substituted project root), each refusing before any effect on its own row; an unrelated registration not invalidating the operation; a repeated checkpoint after journal work latching on a guard gone stale; a read waiting on the shared monitor counting its wait; and the end path stopping the observer and releasing everything.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/362/description"}}, {"after": "The ordinary writer's admission (law X1 r1 items 2 to 7). A writer that is not a creator produces the write receipt (the same producer composition as the read side's, typed Write), rechecks it, enters the law 468 durable write gate with the receipt's sealed DurableBarrierQualification as the gate's only input, and rechecks the receipt again while the gate's fence is held; a failed second recheck releases the fence and returns nothing. OrdinaryWriteAdmission holds the admitted DurableInstallation (the held fence and confirmed durability), the write receipt and the gate that admitted it, kept for its one ledger; it has no serialized form and no conversion into a read session. An absent I is INSTALLATION.NOT_INITIALIZED through the gate's positive absence; I is never created. The admission's refusals are their law 468 item 6 rows through 468c's maps. Further standing comes only through its steps, each under the held fence and followed by the gate's full recheck and the receipt's recheck, any refusal spending the gate: admit_store_endpoint validates the selected store endpoint from the gate's one read against the receipt's selected core and yields StoreAdmittedWriter, which grants only the endpoint (law X3a items 1 and 2, unit X3a-1; a refusal releases the fence); admit_project_root, observe_project_tracking, register_first_use and recheck_registered run on the gate's ledger with the write receipt's premise (law X2 items 2 to 6a, unit X2c); admit_namespace makes the writer the namespace fence holder for an APPEND-WRITE or EXCLUSIVE lease (law X2 item 7, unit X2d); and begin_operation consumes the writer into the checked operation handoff, taking the floor publication's TrustInvocation and carrying X4's live guard (law X2 r8 item 7a, unit X2e; law X4 r7, unit X4a). For the handoff it lends, crate-internally, join_store_endpoint, attempt_step on the write receipt's attempt ledger, gate_step on the gate's own ledger at the lease-free point (the installation lent mutably so a confirmed trust publication can advance state.v1, and the receipt's bootstrap source for the fenced first read's F-absent branch, law X4B r5 item 2, unit X4B-b), selected_closure and into_parts. Library only: no command is wired.", "before": "The ordinary writer's admission (law X1 r1 items 2 to 7). A writer that is not a creator produces the write receipt (the same producer composition as the read side's, typed Write), rechecks it, enters the law 468 durable write gate with the receipt's sealed DurableBarrierQualification as the gate's only input, and rechecks the receipt again while the gate's fence is held; a failed second recheck releases the fence and returns nothing. OrdinaryWriteAdmission grants exactly what DurableInstallation grants: the held fence and confirmed durability, with no store, trust, project, id, grant or commit standing, no serialized form and no conversion into a read session. An absent I is INSTALLATION.NOT_INITIALIZED through the gate's positive absence; nothing is created. Every refusal is its law 468 item 6 row through 468c's maps. Library only: no command is wired.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/363/description"}}, {"after": "Walk and judge the project chain (law X2 r5 items 1 to 3 and 9, and the custody half of item 4): one retained, no-follow, charged walk from / to a selected project root. The root must be strictly below H and outside H/Library/Application Support/OpenSIP, judged lexically before any open. / through the directory above the root takes the shared 468 step 0 external-ancestor judgment with the invocation's omission premise; the root takes S3 directory custody (owner the invoking user or root, no other-write, group-write and ACL write grants only for explicit trust groups, ACL readable or omitted under the premise); every directory from H down must be on H's own volume. That S3 judgment, from one bounded ACL capture with the premise applied to an omitted ACL through the object's own fstatfs, is judge_project_object, which also judges .opensip and the S3 config file (as a configuration file: regular, one link, at most 4 MiB) for project admission and first registration, and the Git evidence for tracking. The root's native birth is sampled with the existing sampler, charged first, with no mtime or ctime substitute. ProjectChain is private and not Clone, holds the retained chain, spelling and birth, rechecks names, custody, volume and birth for the held-fence recheck set, and grants nothing; it lends its retained path and H's index, and the policy its H-filesystem check, to X2b-2's tracking. Each refusal names its item 8 row and subject; selection, the registry, ProjectRootAdmission, tracking and public projection are X2b's. Nothing is created. Library only.", "before": "Walk and judge the project chain (law X2 r5 items 1 to 3 and 9, and the custody half of item 4): one retained, no-follow, charged walk from / to a selected project root. The root must be strictly below H and outside H/Library/Application Support/OpenSIP, judged lexically before any open. / through the directory above the root takes the shared 468 step 0 external-ancestor judgment with the invocation's omission premise; the root takes S3 directory custody (owner the invoking user or root, no other-write, group-write and ACL write grants only for explicit trust groups, ACL readable or omitted under the premise); every directory from H down must be on H's own volume. The root's native birth is sampled with the existing sampler, charged first, with no mtime or ctime substitute. ProjectChain is private and not Clone, holds the retained chain, spelling and birth, rechecks names, custody, volume and birth for the held-fence recheck set, and grants nothing. Each refusal names its item 8 row and subject; selection, the registry, ProjectRootAdmission, tracking and public projection are X2b's. Nothing is created. Library only.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/367/description"}}, {"after": "The closed vocabulary of law 468 item 6: one variant per public row of creator and existing-root admission refusals, the platform decision's NT-TCB detail, and an invariant row for refusals only a broken caller or a broken internal invariant reaches, such as a second F absent after a confirmed trust acceptance (unit X4B-b). Later units extend the table, each row with existing codes: law X3a item 5's STATE.SCHEMA_UNSUPPORTED row (unit X3a-1); the fenced trust admission's rows of law X4T r9 item 10, with RootDetail's nine S5 ROOT.* chain details, and the live guards' revoked-during-operation and observer fail-stop rows (law X4 r7 item 8, unit X4a); and the commit facade's journal and ledger quarantine, carrier migration, durability-undetermined and project-owner rows (law X3d r6 item 9, unit X3d-1). A diagnostic classification for the host's D9 projection, never authority.", "before": "The closed vocabulary of law 468 item 6: one variant per public row of creator and existing-root admission refusals, the platform decision's NT-TCB detail, and an invariant row for refusals only a broken caller reaches. A diagnostic classification for the host's D9 projection, never authority.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/382/description"}}, {"after": "Own grant journal append/witness/high-water admission and versioned SEAL carrier mapping; preserve append-only sequence/cap/restore rules and keep this carrier distinct from lifecycle transition journals; own grant-generation succession (law X3b r8, unit X3b-4): per-generation prefix admission in which a TERMINAL closes its generation and only cause grantGenerationClosure justifies the next, the SEAL ceiling predicate seal_fits, and, in the macOS carrier_floor child, the predecessor check, the successor rule and the rollover; re-export the carrier's operation-handoff (X3b-3 with X2e) and commit-session (X3d-1) surface and its private file protocol.", "before": "Own grant journal append/witness/high-water admission and versioned SEAL carrier mapping; preserve append-only sequence/cap/restore rules and keep this carrier distinct from lifecycle transition journals.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/383/description"}}, {"after": "The grant-journal append protocol (law X3b r6 items 5, 6, 8 and 9; unit X3b-2; X3b r8 item 5a's capacity window, unit X3b-4), as carrier_floor.rs's macOS child module append, under the operation lease the caller holds. JournalAppendLock (level 4) is one in-process mutex per carrier, made only from the CarrierStart it consumes, holding the committed tail its appends follow and two latches that are never reset: REV (after which no SEAL or RA is appended, S6) and undetermined. Level 3: begin opens the carrier read-write through the retained namespace (the same file before and after), sets the WAL and fullfsync writer pragmas with busy_timeout 0, takes BEGIN IMMEDIATE (busy is LEDGER.BUSY_TIMEOUT at once and holds nothing, F06), re-admits the exact definitions, the format row and the SHA-256(N) binding in that transaction, and confirms the tail is the lock's and the witness names it (COMMITTED at the tail, or a PENDING beyond it that a certain failure left; on a closed generation, item 4a's effective tail), refusing while level 4 is held or after an undetermined outcome. Level 4 is only tried after level 3, never waited on; the held value is the borrow X4's checkpoint receives, and dropping it releases level 4 and then rolls back any open level 3. One append, with both witness publications, their confirmations and the commit reserved before the first effect: build the record at tail + 1 (SEAL, REV, CLN and RA as closed schema-3 bodies through the existing parser, TERMINAL as the frozen recordSchema-1 body with cause grantGenerationClosure, built only through append_terminal, which only the rollover calls; the physical operationRef grammar, the domain-framed body hash, chain_law 1 from the genesis value), check item 5a's capacity table before any effect (nothing after a TERMINAL, a SEAL only while seal_fits holds, RA, REV and CLN refused as GenerationFull at tail 9007199254740990, a TERMINAL only in its window), witness PENDING by the file protocol, INSERT and COMMIT, witness COMMITTED. A SEAL keeps level 4 held after its commit until released (r5); other records release it on return. Failures before visibility are certain; a failure after the PENDING rename, a failed or lost COMMIT, and any failure after the commit are durability-undetermined: the lock latches and refuses every further effect, nothing follows in the operation, and the next writer's floor step and start reconcile (X3b r9 item 5). Refusals name item 8's rows, GenerationFull the busy row, and a broken composition (wrong carrier, lock order, after REV, capacity, the terminal slot, the SEAL ceiling, an unbuildable record) the invariant row. Nothing here takes the fence or a lease or writes trust state; grant-generation rollover itself is carrier_rollover.rs's. Crash and fault hooks exist only under cfg(test). Library only.", "before": "The grant-journal append protocol (law X3b r6 items 5, 6, 8 and 9; unit X3b-2), as carrier_floor.rs's macOS child module append, under the operation lease the caller holds. JournalAppendLock (level 4) is one in-process mutex per carrier, made only from the CarrierStart it consumes, holding the committed tail its appends follow and two latches that are never reset: REV (after which no SEAL or RA is appended, S6) and undetermined. Level 3: begin opens the carrier read-write through the retained namespace (the same file before and after), sets the WAL and fullfsync writer pragmas with busy_timeout 0, takes BEGIN IMMEDIATE (busy is LEDGER.BUSY_TIMEOUT at once and holds nothing, F06), re-admits the exact definitions, the format row and the SHA-256(N) binding in that transaction, and confirms the tail is the lock's and the witness names it (COMMITTED at the tail, or a PENDING beyond it that a certain failure left), refusing while level 4 is held or after an undetermined outcome. Level 4 is only tried after level 3, never waited on; the held value is the borrow X4's checkpoint receives, and dropping it releases level 4 and then rolls back any open level 3. One append, with both witness publications, their confirmations and the commit reserved before the first effect: build the record at tail + 1 (SEAL, REV, CLN and RA as closed schema-3 bodies through the existing parser, TERMINAL as the frozen recordSchema-1 body with cause grantGenerationClosure only at the reserved slot; the physical operationRef grammar, the domain-framed body hash, chain_law 1 from the genesis value, ordinary records refused at the reserved slot), witness PENDING by the file protocol, INSERT and COMMIT, witness COMMITTED. A SEAL keeps level 4 held after its commit until released (r5); other records release it on return. Failures before visibility are certain; a failure after the PENDING rename, a failed or lost COMMIT, and any failure after the commit are durability-undetermined: the lock latches, refuses every further effect, and reconciles through X3b-1b's reconcile_after_uncertain, writing only the witness. Refusals name item 8's rows, and a broken composition (wrong carrier, lock order, after REV, capacity, the terminal slot, an unbuildable record) the invariant row. Nothing here takes the fence or a lease or writes trust state; grant-generation rollover is not here. Crash and fault hooks exist only under cfg(test). Library only.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/386/description"}}, {"after": "Check X3b-2 and X3b-4's capacity window on scratch installations: SEAL, REV, CLN, RA and TERMINAL bodies, the domain-framed hash, the genesis and chain_law 1 previous values, and each build refusal (operationRef grammar, fixture or non-run3 Run, non-object epoch, overlong reason, malformed wall clock, a missing previous hash); every protocol step in order, the floor untouched, no temporary witness left, and the reader's own prefix admission accepting the appended RA, SEAL, REV and CLN; a SEAL holding level 4 until released, no level 3 under it and no second record, then level 4 released before level 3; a stop before the record releasing both and committing nothing; a busy journal refused within a second holding nothing; no SEAL or RA after a REV while CLN and REV follow; a crash after each step reconciling by the next floor step and start to OK, REVERT, REVERT, REVERT, ADVANCE and OK; failures before visibility certain with the next append completing, and failures after it undetermined, latching every further effect, the end step copying nothing, and the next writer's floor step and start reconciling to the stated outcome; item 5a's window at each boundary: a SEAL taking 9007199254740988 and refused above the ceiling, RA, REV and CLN taking 9007199254740990 and refused there as GenerationFull on the busy row, a TERMINAL refused below its window and on an empty tail and admitted after tails 9007199254740988 to 9007199254740990 as the frozen recordSchema-1 body, and nothing after it; a source pin that only carrier_rollover.rs calls append_terminal and RecordDraft has no TERMINAL; a moved tail, a foreign witness and another carrier refused before any write; the reservation refusing before the first effect and covering a measured witness publication; and a source pin that the module never waits, retries, writes trust state or stores a quarantine marker.", "before": "Check X3b-2 on scratch installations: SEAL, REV, CLN, RA and TERMINAL bodies, the domain-framed hash, the genesis and chain_law 1 previous values, and each build refusal (operationRef grammar, fixture or non-run3 Run, non-object epoch, overlong reason, malformed wall clock, a missing previous hash); every protocol step in order, the floor untouched, no temporary witness left, and the reader's own prefix admission accepting the appended RA, SEAL, REV and CLN; a SEAL holding level 4 until released, no level 3 under it and no second record, then level 4 released before level 3; a stop before the record releasing both and committing nothing; a busy journal refused within a second holding nothing; no SEAL or RA after a REV while CLN and REV follow; a crash after each step reconciling by the next floor step and start to OK, REVERT, REVERT, REVERT, ADVANCE and OK; failures before visibility certain with the next append completing, and failures after it undetermined, latching every further effect and reconciling to the stated tail that the end step alone copies; TERMINAL only at the reserved slot, an ordinary record refused there, and nothing after it; a moved tail, a foreign witness and another carrier refused before any write; the reservation refusing before the first effect and covering a measured witness publication; and a source pin that the module never waits, retries, writes trust state or stores a quarantine marker.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/387/description"}}, {"after": "The grant-journal carrier floor step and floor-first carrier creation (law X3b r4 items 2, 3, 3a and 5; unit X3b-1a). The private file protocol publishes a witness or floor by a fresh exclusive private temporary name, write and read-back with F_FULLFSYNC, rename, directory barrier, and a no-follow reopen confirming the same file and bytes. The floor step probes writer.lease without waiting and releases it at once (busy skips and writes nothing), observes the floor under trust/carrier-floors, the carrier and the witness read-only, classifies the carrier by format before any floor decision (inherited, footprint and project-binding refusals), decides by the floor table (INIT, resume, copy forward never down, floorLost, uncertainTailLoss, regression, and reconcile_witness as a read-only decision), and writes only the floor, with no project lock held. Creation re-observes INIT, creates the database exclusively and private, sets the WAL and fullfsync writer pragmas, applies the DDL and publishes the format row in one BEGIN IMMEDIATE transaction, takes the file and namespace barriers, and writes the witness COMMITTED 0. Generation succession (X3b r8 item 4a; unit X3b-4) is here too: the committed tail carries its TERMINAL flag, the tail query every writer open uses checks that a newer generation's predecessor is a TERMINAL of the generation before, succession applies the successor rule over one tail and one witness (OPEN on a closed generation, NoSuccessor at the last generation), and the floor follows its copy tail with the open-successor exception. Each refusal names its item 8 row, and the composition owner maps it. Its macOS child modules are start (carrier_start.rs: the carrier start and the end step), append (carrier_append.rs: the append protocol), rollover (carrier_rollover.rs: grant-generation rollover) and operation (carrier_operation.rs: the journal half of the operation handoff, X3b-3 with X2e), whose CarrierLocation::admitted, built from X2e's confirmed CarrierPlace, is CarrierLocation's only production constructor (item 11). It also exports publish_private_file, through which the trust current pointer is replaced (law X4T r9 item 7), and project_key_digest for X3d-1's commit session. Library only.", "before": "The grant-journal carrier floor step and floor-first carrier creation (law X3b r4 items 2, 3, 3a and 5; unit X3b-1a). The private file protocol publishes a witness or floor by a fresh exclusive private temporary name, write and read-back with F_FULLFSYNC, rename, directory barrier, and a no-follow reopen confirming the same file and bytes. The floor step probes writer.lease without waiting and releases it at once (busy skips and writes nothing), observes the floor under trust/carrier-floors, the carrier and the witness read-only, classifies the carrier by format before any floor decision (inherited, footprint and project-binding refusals), decides by the floor table (INIT, resume, copy forward never down, floorLost, uncertainTailLoss, regression, and reconcile_witness as a read-only decision), and writes only the floor, with no project lock held. Creation re-observes INIT, creates the database exclusively and private, sets the WAL and fullfsync writer pragmas, applies the DDL and publishes the format row in one BEGIN IMMEDIATE transaction, takes the file and namespace barriers, and writes the witness COMMITTED 0. Each refusal names its item 8 row; the public mapping, the carrier start's witness writes, the end step and the append are later units. CarrierLocation has no production constructor until X3b-3. Library only.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/389/description"}}, {"after": "The grant-journal carrier start and the end step (law X3b r6 item 4; unit X3b-1b; item 4a's successor rule, X3b r9, unit X3b-4; X3d r3 item 7 relies on them), as carrier_floor.rs's macOS child module start. The start, under the fence and the operation lease, opens and classifies the carrier (format, SHA-256(N) binding and the predecessor check), reads the tail and the witness, confirms the tail equals the floor step's observation before any write, then runs item 4a's reconciliation: OK writes nothing, REVERT, ADVANCE and INIT write only the witness COMMITTED at the committed tail by the private file protocol, OPEN on a generation a TERMINAL closed writes only the witness COMMITTED (G+1, 0, null), and QUARANTINE and OPEN at the last generation refuse writing nothing; it writes no floor and returns the effective tail (the successor's empty tail after OPEN) for X4's epoch binding. X3b r9 item 5 withdrew X3b-1b's reconciliation after an uncertain outcome: nothing follows such an outcome in the operation, and the next writer's floor step and start reconcile. The end step, under the fence with no project lock, does nothing at all after an uncertain outcome; otherwise it probes writer.lease without waiting (busy skips), reads the tail and the witness while the probe is held, releases the probe, and copies the floor forward to item 4a's copy tail only if higher, a floor already at the witnessed successor's empty tail being unchanged; a missing floor is floorLost and a floor above the copy tail is regression, each writing nothing. Step 3's rollover (carrier_rollover.rs's end_step_after_exhaustion) runs before it. Taking the fence and lease is the composition owner's (X3b-3). Library only.", "before": "The grant-journal carrier start, the end step and the post-uncertainty reconciliation (law X3b r6 item 4, item 5's uncertain outcomes; unit X3b-1b; X3d r3 item 7 relies on them). The start, under the fence and the operation lease, opens and classifies the carrier (format and SHA-256(N) binding), reads the tail and the witness, confirms the tail equals the floor step's observation before any write, then runs reconcile_witness: OK writes nothing, REVERT, ADVANCE and INIT write only the witness COMMITTED at the committed tail by the private file protocol, and QUARANTINE refuses writing nothing; it writes no floor and returns the start tail for X4's epoch binding. reconcile_after_uncertain, still under the lease, reopens and reconciles, writing only the witness on REVERT, ADVANCE or INIT, and yields a reconciled tail only on OK, REVERT or ADVANCE (INIT stays non-copyable). The end step, under the fence with no project lock, probes writer.lease without waiting (busy skips), reads the tail to copy while the probe is held (the committed tail, or after an uncertain outcome only the reconciled tail, or nothing), releases the probe, and copies the floor forward only if higher; a missing floor is floorLost and a floor above the tail is regression, each writing nothing. Taking the fence and lease is the composition owner's (X3b-3). Library only.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/395/description"}}, {"after": "Check X3b-1b, with X3b r9's uncertain-outcome rule (unit X3b-4), on scratch installations: the floor step reports the tail the start must confirm (INIT, skipped, current); a consistent start writes nothing and returns the start tail; a moved tail or a missing carrier refuses before any write; REVERT, ADVANCE and INIT write only the witness and never the floor; QUARANTINE at start (witnessless restore, malformed witness, protocol violation) refuses and writes nothing; after an uncertain outcome the end step returns before its probe and any carrier, witness or floor read, charging no work and writing nothing, in REVERT, ADVANCE, INIT and witnessless-restore states alike; the end step copies the committed tail forward and never down, releases its probe, refuses regression and a lost floor without writing; a busy writer lease skips the copy; after an uncertain outcome the next writer reconciles (the floor step copying the committed tail and the start writing COMMITTED there on REVERT and ADVANCE, the floor step resuming INIT and the start writing COMMITTED 0, and a witnessless restore refused at the floor step writing nothing); and a floor bound to another namespace refuses at end.", "before": "Check X3b-1b on scratch installations: the floor step reports the tail the start must confirm (INIT, skipped, current); a consistent start writes nothing and returns the start tail; a moved tail or a missing carrier refuses before any write; REVERT, ADVANCE and INIT write only the witness and never the floor; QUARANTINE at start (witnessless restore, malformed witness, protocol violation) refuses and writes nothing; reconciliation after an uncertain outcome is copyable only on OK, REVERT or ADVANCE, writing the witness on REVERT, ADVANCE and INIT, with INIT left non-copyable so the end step copies nothing; the end step copies the committed tail forward and never down, releases its probe, refuses regression and a lost floor without writing; a busy writer lease skips the copy; after an uncertain outcome only the reconciled tail is copied and without one nothing runs; and a floor bound to another namespace refuses at end.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/396/description"}}, {"after": "Private fail-stop freshness monitor under S6's 10-second OBSERVATION_BOUND; a caller-supplied counter reader remains a custody/authentication obligation. FreshnessMonitor, shared one per operation by law X4 r7 (unit X4a), brackets each monitored read with native clock samples (Sample: monotonic interval, boot id and wall clock) and latches the attempt's StopObserver on an unavailable or malformed clock, a boot change or regression, an unreadable counter, a stall beyond the bound or an unwind; read_sampled_with hands the counter the read's own opening sample (law X4B r5 item 1), and boundary_with is item 3 step 4's one-sample admission-boundary check with no I/O. StopReason::subject names OBSERVER.FAIL_STOP's subject (item 8). No polling thread, journal append, cancellation or operational authority is supplied here; the S6 trust-epoch predicate is root_payload's observe_revocation.", "before": "Observe trust epochs and enforce effect/SEAL checkpoints under the required journal ordering.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/408/description"}}, {"after": "Generate a signed accepted trust store for tests only (law X4T r4 item 13, X4T-0), declared solely under cfg(test). It builds a real P0 publication with the product initial_publication producer, then one retained publication at revision 2: a recorded root chain of n roots (default one; each later root rotates ROOT to the next public key set and is signed by both, and the head is root n), and a revocation list, catalog and payload manifest signed under the head with the public test-only quorum seeds; the payload metadata closure, a bootstrap operation, an S4 evaluation input as time evidence with a signed time source; ordinary root and catalog/revocation admission records and a list revocation history; an S4 clock-write event and, per role, an accepted EV-PRESENT-PAYLOAD role event plus one conditioning event for an expired, stale-revocation, quorum-lost or revoked target, with their role-change and publication-event rows; and the retained-phase capsule and descriptor. Spec also chooses the store id S and the list's revocation version (X4a), and, for X4T-a3's negative cases, substitute root or list signers and a closure rootChain that ends on another envelope of the head, is short or is empty. Every record is canonically encoded and admitted by its closed shape before it is written; files are content-addressed in the trust locators. No production path reaches it; X4B-a's trust_bootstrap is the product producer of these kinds.", "before": "Generate a signed accepted trust store for tests only (law X4T r4 item 13, X4T-0), declared solely under cfg(test). It builds a real P0 publication with the product initial_publication producer, then one retained publication at revision 2: a root, revocation, catalog and payload manifest signed with the public test-only quorum seeds; the payload metadata closure, a bootstrap operation, an S4 evaluation input as time evidence with a signed time source; ordinary root and catalog/revocation admission records and a list revocation history; an S4 clock-write event and, per role, an accepted EV-PRESENT-PAYLOAD role event plus one conditioning event for an expired, stale-revocation, quorum-lost or revoked target, with their role-change and publication-event rows; and the retained-phase capsule and descriptor. Every record is canonically encoded and admitted by its closed shape before it is written; files are content-addressed in the trust locators. It is the only constructor of these kinds and no production path reaches it; X4B owns the real producers.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/411/description"}}, {"after": "Check the signed accepted-store generator: every immutable file is canonical and addressed by its digest and each kind passes its closed shape; the root, catalog and revocation envelopes carry real ed25519 quorum signatures over their bodies by their owning role; current_record_bindings::bind, publication_events::bind_events (from P0's roles and event head) and bind_retained_head (through the capsule clock) accept the generated store for state schemas 1 and 2; a recorded rotation of two and three roots lists the whole chain with the head last, each link signed by both key sets and the list signed under root n, and the binders accept it; chosen role states publish with their accepted standing, INDEX-only catalog and revoked or quorum-lost evidence; capture_p2 accepts the written store through a supplied native fence on a scratch installation; a store with no accepted role is not offered; and a source pin shows the constructor is declared only under cfg(test) and named by no other source file except X4T-a's current_trust_admission_tests.rs and X4T-b's floor_publication_tests.rs, each checked to be included only under cfg(test).", "before": "Check the signed accepted-store generator: every immutable file is canonical and addressed by its digest and each kind passes its closed shape; the root, catalog and revocation envelopes carry real ed25519 quorum signatures over their bodies by their owning role; current_record_bindings::bind, publication_events::bind_events (from P0's roles and event head) and bind_retained_head (through the capsule clock) accept the generated store for state schemas 1 and 2; chosen role states publish with their accepted standing, INDEX-only catalog and revoked or quorum-lost evidence; capture_p2 accepts the written store through a supplied native fence on a scratch installation; a store with no accepted role is not offered; and a source pin shows the constructor is declared only under cfg(test) and named by no other source file.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/412/description"}}, {"after": "Admit the installation's current trust read-only (law X4T r5, unit X4T-a), as a child of the retained-head composition in ordinary_targets. A pre-acceptance capsule is refused as F absent (TRUST.NO_ADMITTED_TIME_CONTEXT) before any read. Otherwise it binds the capsule, descriptor and event chain with current_record_bindings::bind (capture_p2 on the native path), bounds the descriptor at 32 events, checks each accepted role's accepted.by as an accepted role event of that role, from the loaded chain or opened by its reference (one read, at most six, in this store and earlier than the current publication; history is never walked), takes the role machine's continuation standing before authentication, binds the root head with bind_retained_head, opens the root admission record, and, with heads.root as the signing root, takes the accepted root as the closure's first rootChain document (the chain must end on exactly heads.root.document) and authenticates through the existing retained-head shared authentication at a 16-link, 16 MiB chain budget. It opens the catalog and revocation heads (body, envelope, admission), history and timeEvidence itself with Budget::load, bounds the closure's stored bytes at 40 MiB outside the chain, checks the capsule's floors against the time evidence (trust-rollback), reverifies the catalog envelope and verifies the revocation list with verify_revocation over exactly those bytes, refuses an entry naming a closure component (release, signing keyId, namespace or catalog snapshot) on the continuation row, then requires the catalog's and the list's quorums with the list's keyIds excluded, merges the empty global policy (and a project policy when supplied) for Effective::digest(), evaluates S4 time on the fenced read only (report-only proposes no writes; a reread admits none). It returns AdmittedCurrentTrust (standing, S6 epoch, time outcome, revoked subjects, the capsule's floors, the closure components and admitted revocation evidence for X4's S6 predicate, and a fenced non-report read's S4 proposal for the write-ahead) or a refusal naming its law 468 item 6 row (TrustRow, including the host invariant row). The admission itself writes nothing. The file declares its macOS child modules floor_publication (X4T-b's write-ahead floor publication), live_observation (X4a's fenced operation read, retained handles and observation) and trust_bootstrap (X4B's first trust acceptance and its F-absent branch), and re-exports ConfirmedCurrent, fenced_operation_read, the live-observation types and BootstrapSource.", "before": "Admit the installation's current trust read-only (law X4T r5, unit X4T-a), as a child of the retained-head composition in ordinary_targets. A pre-acceptance capsule is refused as F absent (TRUST.NO_ADMITTED_TIME_CONTEXT) before any read. Otherwise it binds the capsule, descriptor and event chain with current_record_bindings::bind (capture_p2 on the native path), bounds the descriptor at 32 events, checks each accepted role's accepted.by against the loaded events of that role, binds the root head with bind_retained_head, opens the root admission record, and authenticates the accepted state from the accepted root through the existing retained-head shared authentication at a 16-link, 16 MiB chain budget. It opens the catalog and revocation heads (body, envelope, admission), history and timeEvidence itself with Budget::load, reverifies the catalog envelope and verifies the revocation list with verify_revocation over exactly those bytes, merges the empty global policy (and a project policy when supplied) for Effective::digest(), evaluates S4 time on the fenced read only (report-only proposes no writes; a reread admits none), and takes the role machine's continuation standing. It returns AdmittedCurrentTrust (standing, S6 epoch, time outcome, revoked subjects) or a refusal naming its law 468 item 6 row. It writes nothing; the write-ahead floor is X4T-b's and consumption is X4a's.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/435/description"}}, {"after": "Produce the attempt-bound InitialCore receipt of owner \u00a71a step 3 (law 463 r3\u2013r9). Join the kernel-validated running image to the entrypoint row of the embedded, authenticated CoreInventoryV3 through fixed no-follow opens under core-tree custody, an in-memory anchor store charged to the attempt's one ledger, the release's own revocation, the required code-signing flags and K from stateWriter. No listing, latest selection, record from I, or caller revoked set. For first trust acceptance (law X4B r5 item 2) it retains, as raw bytes with their references and unverified, the catalog pair (only when an envelope's stored digest admitted it), the component-manifest pairs in members.manifests order and the revocation pair (bootstrap_documents), and lends the anchor's embedded root binding, the bootstrap manifest and core inventory pairs and the root chain's pairs in chain order (bootstrap_release); it grants nothing for them.", "before": "Produce the attempt-bound InitialCore receipt of owner \u00a71a step 3 (law 463 r3\u2013r8). Join the kernel-validated running image to the entrypoint row of the embedded, authenticated CoreInventoryV3 through fixed no-follow opens under core-tree custody, an in-memory anchor store charged to the attempt's one ledger, the release's own revocation, the required code-signing flags and K from stateWriter. No listing, latest selection, record from I, or caller revoked set.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/447/description"}}, {"after": "The operation's trust side (law X4 r7 items 2, 4 and 5 over law X4T r9 items 7 to 9; unit X4a; with law X4B r5 item 1, unit X4B-b), as a macOS child of the current-trust admission. fenced_operation_read is the lease-free point's fenced first read: X4T-b's retained state.v1 owner bound to the write gate's one read (its bytes and sample, no new content read), X4T-b's fenced_first_read with its write-ahead and the S4 observation the caller projects from the monitor's opening sample, and on F absent only, X4B-b's acceptance from the write receipt's BootstrapSource and the one confirming admission on the same observation (trust_bootstrap's bootstrapping_first_read), returning the view and every confirmed publication in order for the gate to follow one by one with advance_current; TrustInvocation is the floor publication's invocation identity (req1_ and exec1_ with 32 lowercase hex, a step id). RetainedTrustHandles retains trust/stores/S, its events, and objects, records and publications, each bound no-follow by exact name to its retained parent and judged private and on H's filesystem under the fence. LiveObservation is one unfenced observation through those handles only, on a fresh per-observation ledger of two views at TRUST_VIEW_COST (256 objects, 4096 edges, 224 MiB): state.v1 opened by name through the store handle, X4T-a's reread over the closure with every file opened by name through its collection's retained handle (the native readers' own locator), state.v1 reopened and its identity and full sample compared, and all of it once more if they differ; a second change is mixed. A reread refusal naming a revoked closure component is the revoking observation; any other refusal, an unreadable record, a budget overrun or a view below the start view's floors or counters (never reaching the predicate) is a fail-stop with its reason. Then S6's observe_revocation runs against the immutable start epoch and closure subjects (X4T item 4's components, from the start view), with empty required permission pairs. The observation never takes the fence, writes nothing and grants nothing. Library only.", "before": "The operation's trust side (law X4 r7 items 2, 4 and 5 over law X4T r9 items 7 to 9; unit X4a), as a macOS child of the current-trust admission. fenced_operation_read is the lease-free point's fenced first read: X4T-b's retained state.v1 owner bound to the write gate's one read (its bytes and sample, no new content read), X4T-b's fenced_first_read with its write-ahead and the S4 observation the caller projects from the monitor's opening sample, returning the view and any confirmed publication for the gate's advance_current; TrustInvocation is the floor publication's invocation identity (req1_ and exec1_ with 32 lowercase hex, a step id). RetainedTrustHandles retains trust/stores/S, its events, and objects, records and publications, each bound no-follow by exact name to its retained parent and judged private and on H's filesystem under the fence. LiveObservation is one unfenced observation through those handles only, on a fresh per-observation ledger of two views at TRUST_VIEW_COST (256 objects, 4096 edges, 224 MiB): state.v1 opened by name through the store handle, X4T-a's reread over the closure with every file opened by name through its collection's retained handle (the native readers' own locator), state.v1 reopened and its identity and full sample compared, and all of it once more if they differ; a second change is mixed. A reread refusal naming a revoked closure component is the revoking observation; any other refusal, an unreadable record, a budget overrun or a view below the start view's floors or counters (never reaching the predicate) is a fail-stop with its reason. Then S6's observe_revocation runs against the immutable start epoch and closure subjects (X4T item 4's components, from the start view), with empty required permission pairs. It never takes the fence, writes nothing and grants nothing. Library only.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/452/description"}}, {"after": "Capture the original current capsule file, compare its independently parsed store claim, and retain current/dependency observations under one budget. It also owns the trust current record's own read bound, CURRENT_STATE_CAP, and decoder, decode_current_store, which returns C.store's (S, G, K) for the write gate's and the observation session's single read (law X3a r3 item 2), and parts_mut hands the capsule, its descriptor, the bindings and the capture's native store to the read-only current trust admission (law X4T r5 item 2).", "before": "Capture the original current capsule file, compare its independently parsed store claim, and retain current/dependency observations under one budget.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/457/description"}}, {"after": "Produce first trust acceptance from the running core's embedded bootstrap payload (law X4B r5 items 2 to 6, unit X4B-a; item 1, unit X4B-b), as a macOS child of the current-trust admission, under the held installation fence on the retained P0 state.v1 owner. The payload is exactly what InitialCore retains (law 463 r9): the root chain, the revocation pair, the bootstrap manifest (TR-BUNDLE), the core inventory (TR-CORE), the catalog pair (TR-INDEX) and the component-manifest pairs (TR-COMPONENT). Every signature is verified again: the chain from the embedded root binding within ChainBudget{16 links, 16 MiB}, the list under the final root, re-filtering every link, the index-0 root and its own quorum; each document's envelope at its role's threshold with the list's keyIds excluded. A bootstrap without a signed catalog, or any document that fails, refuses PAYLOAD-NOT-ADMISSIBLE before any write. S4 step 2 runs on the caller's one clock sample (F := max(W, A), L := A, the anchor, expiry from the presented documents). Each carried role's sequence goes through role_machine::decide: PresentOrdinary, then Clock when expired or stale, then Revoke when the payload's own list names its signer, namespace, release or catalog snapshot. Every record (objects, payload closure, operation, time evidence with the payload's signed time source, root and metadata admissions, revocation history, clock-write and role events with their role changes, the revision-2 descriptor and the retained capsule) is canonically encoded, admitted by its closed shape and bound by current_record_bindings, successor_record_bindings and publication_events before floor_publication's shared protocol publishes it, state.v1 last, advancing the retained owner from the confirming reread. bootstrapping_first_read is the body of the operation monitor's single first read at the lease-free point: X4T-b's fenced_first_read on the retained owner, then, only on F absent (TRUST.NO_ADMITTED_TIME_CONTEXT) and only for a fenced read that is not report-only, acceptance of the payload from BootstrapSource (the write receipt's running core, copied and charged only on that branch) on the read's own clock sample under the same fence hold, and the one confirming admission on the confirmed state.v1, whose view is returned. An acceptance refusal ends the read, and a second F absent is the host invariant row (TrustRow::Invariant); the order lives in first_read_sequence. It grants nothing: the confirming admission is X4T's own.", "before": "Produce first trust acceptance from the running core's embedded bootstrap payload (law X4B r5 items 2 to 6; unit X4B-a), as a macOS child of the current-trust admission, under the held installation fence on the retained P0 state.v1 owner. The payload is exactly what InitialCore retains (law 463 r9): the root chain, the revocation pair, the bootstrap manifest (TR-BUNDLE), the core inventory (TR-CORE), the catalog pair (TR-INDEX) and the component-manifest pairs (TR-COMPONENT). Every signature is verified again: the chain from the embedded root binding within ChainBudget{16 links, 16 MiB}, the list under the final root, re-filtering every link, the index-0 root and its own quorum; each document's envelope at its role's threshold with the list's keyIds excluded. A bootstrap without a signed catalog, or any document that fails, refuses PAYLOAD-NOT-ADMISSIBLE before any write. S4 step 2 runs on the caller's one clock sample (F := max(W, A), L := A, the anchor, expiry from the presented documents). Each carried role's sequence goes through role_machine::decide: PresentOrdinary, then Clock when expired or stale, then Revoke when the payload's own list names its signer, namespace, release or catalog snapshot. Every record (objects, payload closure, operation, time evidence with the payload's signed time source, root and metadata admissions, revocation history, clock-write and role events with their role changes, the revision-2 descriptor and the retained capsule) is canonically encoded, admitted by its closed shape and bound by current_record_bindings, successor_record_bindings and publication_events before floor_publication's shared protocol publishes it, state.v1 last, advancing the retained owner from the confirming reread. Not wired to the fenced first read (X4B-b); grants nothing.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/497/description"}}, {"after": "Check X4B-a and X4B-b (law X4B r5 item 10) on ACL-scratch installations holding 467's P0 publication under a supplied fence, with test-signed releases (public quorum62 seeds) produced into an InitialCore through 463's injected-image constructor: P0 is accepted with TR-BUNDLE, TR-COMPONENT, TR-CORE and TR-INDEX trusted from one EV-PRESENT-PAYLOAD each and the one confirming admission admits the store without writing; X4T-a's native reread and every record's canonical content address accept it; the fresh-install time rules (tEval = max(W, A), payload-future, beyond-horizon and its boundary) write nothing on refusal; an expired catalog, an expired root, a stale list, a revoked signer whose quorum survives and expired-and-revoked give their event sequences, accepted.by and revokedBy, and X4T's continuation row or ExistingOnly; inactive and rejected entries record no event; a revoked counted signer that leaves a catalog or component manifest below threshold writes nothing; a missing catalog or a document signed outside its role writes nothing; no component manifest leaves TR-COMPONENT unbootstrapped; a crash before the pointer and a rerun; a changed state.v1; a short ledger; a non-P0 predecessor and a foreign root binding; and a two-root chain recorded in full, which the confirming admission admits at root 2's epoch and floor (X4T-a3). Through the first read (X4B-b): F absent is accepted inside it and the confirming view returned with exactly one confirmed publication and F, L and the anchor from the read's own sample, and a later first read enters no branch and writes nothing; a report-only or reread mode never bootstraps; a missing catalog or a beyond-horizon sample ends the read with nothing written and no confirming admission, and a missing component manifest leaves one publication that the confirming admission refuses; a first refusal other than F absent runs no acceptance; the stub sequence accepts once, admits at most twice and makes a second F absent the host invariant row; and source pins show only the write receipt lends a BootstrapSource, only the lease-free read reaches bootstrapping_first_read, and the read side names neither entry point.", "before": "Check X4B-a (law X4B r5 item 10) on ACL-scratch installations holding 467's P0 publication under a supplied fence, with test-signed releases (public quorum62 seeds) produced into an InitialCore through 463's injected-image constructor: P0 is accepted with TR-BUNDLE, TR-COMPONENT, TR-CORE and TR-INDEX trusted from one EV-PRESENT-PAYLOAD each and the one confirming admission admits the store without writing; X4T-a's native reread and every record's canonical content address accept it; the fresh-install time rules (tEval = max(W, A), payload-future, beyond-horizon and its boundary) write nothing on refusal; an expired catalog, an expired root, a stale list, a revoked signer whose quorum survives and expired-and-revoked give their event sequences, accepted.by and revokedBy, and X4T's continuation row or ExistingOnly; inactive and rejected entries record no event; a revoked counted signer that leaves a catalog or component manifest below threshold writes nothing; a missing catalog or a document signed outside its role writes nothing; no component manifest leaves TR-COMPONENT unbootstrapped; a crash before the pointer and a rerun; a changed state.v1; a short ledger; a non-P0 predecessor and a foreign root binding; and a two-root chain recorded in full, with the reader's current FirstIdentity refusal pinned as a stated gap.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/498/description"}}, {"after": "Place, create and open the per-(S, N) evidence ledger and admit the attempt (law X3c r6 items 1 to 3, 9 and 10; unit X3c-1). Owner201's spellings: I/stores/S/projects/N/ledger.sqlite with its -wal and -shm, and objects under projects/N/objects/sha256. projects/, projects/N/, objects/ and objects/sha256/ are created or admitted through security's store custody. A new ledger is created exclusively, WAL is selected, then one BEGIN IMMEDIATE runs the selected DDL fragments in their pinned order and COMMITs under synchronous=FULL and fullfsync, the namespace directory is barriered, and the file is reopened through open_existing with a whole-schema equality against the same DDL. An empty file with no -wal is the only resumable creation state; every other stored state is LEDGER.CORRUPT, and nothing is migrated or adopted. The attempt_custody row (admitted, this namespace) is committed in its own non-waiting transaction before any object; a reused ExecutionId is the invariant row and a failed COMMIT is durability-undetermined. Every step is charged. Refusals name their item 10 row; the host projection is X3d's. admit_attempt returns a non-Clone AdmittedAttempt, which object publication requires. Object publication, the prepared ledger transaction, staging and the commit with its durability classification are the child module commit (project_commit.rs, unit X3c-2), whose ObjectDeclaration and StagingMismatch refusals take the invariant row and CommitUndetermined the durability row here. Nothing here settles an attempt or reads an outcome back. Only tests construct a location.", "before": "Place, create and open the per-(S, N) evidence ledger and admit the attempt (law X3c r6 items 1 to 3, 9 and 10; unit X3c-1). Owner201's spellings: I/stores/S/projects/N/ledger.sqlite with its -wal and -shm, and objects under projects/N/objects/sha256. projects/, projects/N/, objects/ and objects/sha256/ are created or admitted through security's store custody. A new ledger is created exclusively, WAL is selected, then one BEGIN IMMEDIATE runs the selected DDL fragments in their pinned order and COMMITs under synchronous=FULL and fullfsync, the namespace directory is barriered, and the file is reopened through open_existing with a whole-schema equality against the same DDL. An empty file with no -wal is the only resumable creation state; every other stored state is LEDGER.CORRUPT, and nothing is migrated or adopted. The attempt_custody row (admitted, this namespace) is committed in its own non-waiting transaction before any object; a reused ExecutionId is the invariant row and a failed COMMIT is durability-undetermined. Every step is charged. Refusals name their item 10 row; the host projection is X3d's. No object publication, staging, commit, settlement or read-back. Only tests construct a location.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/665/description"}}, {"after": "Read and join immutable manifest and receipt-inventory material from the recovery ledger snapshot, and stage a Run's manifest and commit inventory into commit_run_material inside an open write transaction (WriteTransaction::stage_run_material, law X3c r7 item 6, unit X3c-2) under the same schema check and body admission recovery uses, after the receipt staged in that transaction, returning the inventory's blob digests and committing nothing; signatures, live retention, pins and full replay remain separate owners.", "before": "Read and join immutable manifest and receipt-inventory material from the recovery ledger snapshot; signatures, live retention, pins and full replay remain separate owners.", "parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/668/description"}}], "passageSupersessions": [], "selected": "docs/implementation/m2/description-batch-d1/successor.json", "sha256": "2a2f94a61515c175efb3e88eb619626b0c0709682a8724a7fa119576f95a27c9"}, {"inputs": [{"bytes": 5735, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/description-batch-d2/README.md", "sha256": "c32c2d08a8a7811aaa8069022a98309211ad66d57533f04c6cd4a0e30cd88b79"}, {"bytes": 5978, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/description-batch-d2/evidence/build_d2.py", "sha256": "e1d64812679b8632d0b6c7b118b932f04e146af77962510cc103b02cb37fe186"}, {"bytes": 10001, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/description-batch-d2/evidence/descriptions.json", "sha256": "a0ebb8594817717e002a59692b83ccc5477cca1f257b7cc0ad1f92d56bab3e7d"}, {"bytes": 3845, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/description-batch-d2/evidence/verify_scratch.py", "sha256": "a6ea0b90910d02bc0e45f6d08fbae0bd9fa844bae22881babaf00a696431497d"}], "passageOverrides": [], "passageSupersessions": [{"after": "Own alloc-only full-triple lineage keys, the one relative node spelling transitions/lineage/S/G/K.node that every crate joins, closed canonical 4096-byte node decoding and bounded iterative supplied-chain inspection. Callers assemble OS paths from those components; the spelling is not resolution. SuppliedChain::into_nodes yields the inspected chain by value, selected node to root, so that the write gate's one read and the observation session's member reads keep it for the selected store endpoint (law X3a items 2 and 3, unit X3a-1). Decoded chains do not prove native existence, publication, complete operation budget or authority.", "before": "Own alloc-only full-triple lineage keys, the one relative node spelling transitions/lineage/S/G/K.node that every crate joins, closed canonical 4096-byte node decoding and bounded iterative supplied-chain inspection. Callers assemble OS paths from those components; the spelling is not resolution. Decoded chains do not prove native existence, publication, complete operation budget or authority.", "parent": {"bytes": 485705, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v122.json", "sha256": "69cf90db0ade09fc0f9536b3ef293d7de6aff68c7d3ae9a2aabfab4f6c244c7a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/273/description"}, "supersedes": {"parent": {"bytes": 297759, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v74.json", "sha256": "90ccda7e0b199ad4705661e5303581f4b6b4ab220b560a8ec93d9809dd982279"}, "record": {"bytes": 15663, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/existing-root-diagnostics-468a/successor.json", "sha256": "dae42833b0f6a204836762b273a42e36433b968a5edb81018b0825277ae17d5c"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/158/description"}}}, {"after": "The read side's observation path (law 458c r5 items 5, 6, 7 and 11). One session per process on one failure-latching ledger with the owner's limits; one observation per session. It reaches I through the durable write gate's own step 0 and step 1 code, shared rather than copied, with no barrier: a charged, retained, no-follow walk from the root to I under the read receipt's sealed lending, a positive absence only when the first missing suffix component is observed absent under its retained parent, and lifecycle.fence opened through the retained I. The lock wait repeats one nonblocking attempt on that same descriptor, paced at 25 ms, for at most 5 s and 201 attempts, all reserved before the first; busy retries, any other failure stops on the host I/O row, and a lock still busy at the bound is the busy row. Under the held fence it runs the receipt's rechecks and the full recheck set, reads the required members charged before they run (the fixed members up front, each lineage node before its own read), records missing, undecodable or misbound members as structural findings, and runs the same full recheck over every captured file, also after a failed read. The receipt's rechecks run again after that final recheck. Within the same one read (law X3a items 1 to 5, unit X3a-1): the selection pair must name the read receipt's selected core, or it is the core finding; the trust current record trust/stores/S/state.v1 is read once under CURRENT_STATE_CAP and decoded, and a record over its cap, undecodable or naming a store other than the pair's is the current-store finding; a lineage chain longer than its generation allows is a finding, while the session's own node limit is the budget row; and a complete I keeps the endpoint values (the pair, the marker, the node chain and the current record) for the selected store endpoint. retain (unit 458c-b2) requires I complete and keeps the observation, the receipt and the session's ledger in the ReadSession. Every refusal takes exactly the write gate's row. Never a write capability. Its consumers are the read session behind InstallationReadFence::acquire and doctor's installation check. Its one wired CLI path is opensip doctor (law X10), through doctor's installation check.", "before": "The read side's observation path (law 458c r5 items 5, 6, 7 and 11). One session per process on one failure-latching ledger with the owner's limits; one observation per session. It reaches I through the durable write gate's own step 0 and step 1 code, shared rather than copied, with no barrier: a charged, retained, no-follow walk from the root to I under the read receipt's sealed lending, a positive absence only when the first missing suffix component is observed absent under its retained parent, and lifecycle.fence opened through the retained I. The lock wait repeats one nonblocking attempt on that same descriptor, paced at 25 ms, for at most 5 s and 201 attempts, all reserved before the first; busy retries, any other failure stops on the host I/O row, and a lock still busy at the bound is the busy row. Under the held fence it runs the receipt's rechecks and the full recheck set, reads the required members charged before they run (the fixed members up front, each lineage node before its own read), records missing, undecodable or misbound members as structural findings, and runs the same full recheck over every captured file, also after a failed read. Every refusal takes exactly the write gate's row. Never a write capability. Its consumers are the read session behind InstallationReadFence::acquire and doctor's installation check. Library only: no CLI command is wired.", "parent": {"bytes": 485705, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v122.json", "sha256": "69cf90db0ade09fc0f9536b3ef293d7de6aff68c7d3ae9a2aabfab4f6c244c7a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/372/description"}, "supersedes": {"parent": {"bytes": 313526, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v80.json", "sha256": "838a7f4072b408e8c4011249c89e0974d38616031cda110267c92ab762d0d1e7"}, "record": {"bytes": 11909, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/stale-descriptions-461b/successor.json", "sha256": "2c8a4de79e156b0f0c47e579c2121adae07f804a505e3d19d3bb2b787596fad6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/247/description"}}}, {"after": "Produce the read side's premise receipt (law 458c r5 items 1 to 4): the process's one attempt, the actor, InitialCore and InitialPlatform, exactly the creator's producers, with no intent, storage choice, disclosure, effect or fence. Law X1 r1 items 1 and 2 type it by purpose: the one producer composition makes PlatformReceipt<Read> (ReadPremiseReceipt, produce_read_platform) or PlatformReceipt<Write> (WritePlatformReceipt, produce_write_platform); Read and Write are sealed, neither receipt converts into the other, and a receipt whose actor, core or platform belongs to another attempt is the invariant row. The read receipt is private and not Clone, holds its own attempt, and lends only the sealed ReadPremiseQualification that InitialPlatform alone implements: the check that a filesystem is H's and the omission premise with 465 item 4's scope, no barrier policy. The write receipt is likewise private, not Clone and holds its own attempt; its lending is the durable write gate's sealed DurableBarrierQualification, beside which it lends the running core's embedded bootstrap source to the fenced first read alone (law X4B r5 item 2, unit X4B-b), charge, one charged step on its attempt ledger with that lending (unit X2e), charge_guarded, the same step with the actor, core and platform rechecks lent beside it for the checkpoint's guard (law X4 r7 item 4, unit X4a), and the attempt ledger's one end-path settlement reserve and its single spend, on the commit session's way only (law X3d r6 item 8, unit X3d-1). Both purposes expose recheck, the actor, core and platform rechecks on the receipt's own attempt, and selected_core, the selected core's closure and state writer K as values only (law X3a item 1). Every refusal ends in its law 468 item 6 row through 468c's maps. InstallationReadFence::acquire and doctor's installation check pass it to the observation session. The ordinary writer's admission consumes the write receipt (law X1), and the operation handoff carries it (unit X2e). Its one wired CLI path is opensip doctor (law X10), through doctor's installation check; no CLI command reaches a write receipt.", "before": "Produce the read side's premise receipt (law 458c r5 items 1 to 4): the process's one attempt, the actor, InitialCore and InitialPlatform, exactly the creator's producers, with no intent, storage choice, disclosure, effect or fence. The receipt is private and not Clone, holds its own attempt, and lends only the sealed ReadPremiseQualification that InitialPlatform alone implements: the check that a filesystem is H's and the omission premise with 465 item 4's scope, no barrier policy. Every refusal ends in its law 468 item 6 row through 468c's maps. InstallationReadFence::acquire and doctor's installation check pass it to the observation session. Library only: no CLI command is wired.", "parent": {"bytes": 485705, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v122.json", "sha256": "69cf90db0ade09fc0f9536b3ef293d7de6aff68c7d3ae9a2aabfab4f6c244c7a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/388/description"}, "supersedes": {"parent": {"bytes": 313526, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v80.json", "sha256": "838a7f4072b408e8c4011249c89e0974d38616031cda110267c92ab762d0d1e7"}, "record": {"bytes": 11909, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/stale-descriptions-461b/successor.json", "sha256": "2c8a4de79e156b0f0c47e579c2121adae07f804a505e3d19d3bb2b787596fad6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/250/description"}}}, {"after": "Own the initial creator's pre-installation attempt: one per process, the only work ledger for the whole act and a failure latch, with private non-Clone receipts bound to the attempt. The same attempt, with its one ledger, is also the process's one attempt behind the read receipt and the ordinary writer's write receipt (laws 458c and X1). Mint the InitialActor (equal real and effective UID, a plain absolute home spelling that fits I within 4096 bytes and 256 components) and recheck it; classify storage (constant UNKNOWN), apply the first-creation backup choice, write and flush the first-write disclosure, and mint, recheck and consume the single-use CreationIntent with its request and execution ids at StepId 0. recheck_actor_in rechecks the actor on a borrowed scope (law 467 item 6; also the write receipt's guard, law X4 r7 item 4), and recheck_storage_in and recheck_handoff_storage rebuild I and re-classify a consumed intent's target (law 467 item 2). reserve_end_path_settlement takes the attempt ledger's one settlement reserve, refused Closed once the attempt is latched, and settle_end_path spends it once whether or not the attempt is latched (law X3d r6 item 8, unit X3d-1); they are the only production callers of WorkLedger::reserve_settlement and settle, reached only on the write receipt's way to the commit session's end-path step and StoppedSession::finish. It opens no installation path, creates nothing, and does not enable the creator.", "before": "Own the initial creator's pre-installation attempt: one per process, the only work ledger for the whole act and a failure latch, with private non-Clone receipts bound to the attempt. Mint the InitialActor (equal real and effective UID, a plain absolute home spelling that fits I within 4096 bytes and 256 components) and recheck it; classify storage (constant UNKNOWN), apply the first-creation backup choice, write and flush the first-write disclosure, and mint, recheck and consume the single-use CreationIntent with its request and execution ids at StepId 0. It opens no installation path, creates nothing, and does not enable the creator.", "parent": {"bytes": 485705, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v122.json", "sha256": "69cf90db0ade09fc0f9536b3ef293d7de6aff68c7d3ae9a2aabfab4f6c244c7a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/398/description"}, "supersedes": {"parent": {"bytes": 297759, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v74.json", "sha256": "90ccda7e0b199ad4705661e5303581f4b6b4ab220b560a8ec93d9809dd982279"}, "record": {"bytes": 15663, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/existing-root-diagnostics-468a/successor.json", "sha256": "dae42833b0f6a204836762b273a42e36433b968a5edb81018b0825277ae17d5c"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/242/description"}}}], "selected": "docs/implementation/m2/description-batch-d2/successor.json", "sha256": "21e1ce5141834b8df62f73ffd84b59ccbf9262c1ed58828112929315ed6d048a"}, {"inputs": [{"bytes": 9247, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/core-evaluator-closure-ec1/README.md", "sha256": "57dd9f03d0fc32f11532ee166b2f3c1225b351ce7e76c26f65b9b1d357111f2b"}, {"bytes": 8813, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/core-evaluator-closure-ec1/evidence/build_ec1.py", "sha256": "a69883e16f32b2c851498167f93e3e240a272928c38fbb47a1011365c43bcf3f"}, {"bytes": 4918, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/core-evaluator-closure-ec1/evidence/check_ec1.py", "sha256": "a4d1957e1f8dc143d31c3f2af0ce346c434d74dd3c79f8bf775708aa90e22ab9"}, {"bytes": 16729, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/core-evaluator-closure-ec1/evidence/vector.json", "sha256": "5363451b25347f26a919f989b0907bbc895ad13d5f275f67eccef2d2cdb938dc"}, {"bytes": 2556, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/core-evaluator-closure-ec1/evidence/verify_scratch.py", "sha256": "c285e84827e482bb476ff6d2ba2dd1a97a3579fc34777e2b3c3835bfbdf618a3"}], "passageOverrides": [{"after": "the admitted component manifest body bytes under the security metadata profile, excluding the signature envelope; for kind evaluator, the TR-CORE-signed core inventory body bytes (opensip.metadata.inventory.1) under the same profile, excluding its signature envelope (contract successor EC1, the core evaluator closure)", "before": "the admitted component manifest body bytes under the security metadata profile, excluding the signature envelope", "parent": {"bytes": 196987, "path": "docs/coop/design-corrections/foundation/identity-schemas.v3.json", "sha256": "a76c9e2f07e8f8e52ee611f157548f6a09061866308652e3a0f7e3c24893db21"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/$defs/closure/properties/manifestDigest/x-opensip-digest/artifact"}}, {"after": "No new closure kind is invented for producer acts: enumeration, view production, fact production and stage production are all provider acts under the existing kind. `grammar` is a genuinely new INSTALLED-COMPONENT kind, in the same family as `toolchain`, `stdlib` and `rust-dev-llvm`: the syntax-only mode's body interpreter is a bundled grammar set, and binding it to an existing kind would either claim it is a toolchain (it executes no compiler) or leave the component that interprets a body span outside the closure law, which is the one thing every other universe pins down. The evaluator kind names no separately delivered component, because the evaluator is the pure core (contract successor EC1). A core release's evaluator closure is closure2: + H(\"closure\", D), where D is the authenticated core inventory's selected-platform closure descriptor that security projects for the core closure, with kind set to \"evaluator\" and every other member unchanged. Its manifestDigest is the raw SHA-256 of the TR-CORE-signed inventory body and its tree is that platform's regular files. The core closure and the core evaluator closure therefore differ only by kind.", "before": "No new closure kind is invented for producer acts: enumeration, view production, fact production and stage production are all provider acts under the existing kind. `grammar` is a genuinely new INSTALLED-COMPONENT kind, in the same family as `toolchain`, `stdlib` and `rust-dev-llvm`: the syntax-only mode's body interpreter is a bundled grammar set, and binding it to an existing kind would either claim it is a toolchain (it executes no compiler) or leave the component that interprets a body span outside the closure law, which is the one thing every other universe pins down.", "parent": {"bytes": 196987, "path": "docs/coop/design-corrections/foundation/identity-schemas.v3.json", "sha256": "a76c9e2f07e8f8e52ee611f157548f6a09061866308652e3a0f7e3c24893db21"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/x-opensip-digest-domains/closureKinds/note"}}, {"after": "admitted component manifest body bytes (for kind `evaluator`, the TR-CORE-signed core inventory body; see the core evaluator closure below, EC1) encoded with the security metadata", "before": "admitted component manifest body bytes encoded with the security metadata", "parent": {"bytes": 135448, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/identity-and-evidence.md", "sha256": "c82404f3a0cf56fa6cc02e99cc3ebbd5356fedc3b36aeb38f9ef284077fbd31f"}, "selector": {"line": 273}}, {"after": "and digest for every selected file. A semantic version alone is not a closure. **Core evaluator closure (contract successor EC1, 2026-10-02).** The evaluator is the pure core, so a core release's evaluator closure is not a separately delivered component. It is `closure2:` + H(\"closure\", D), where D is the authenticated core inventory's selected-platform closure descriptor `{schemaVersion: 2, kind, manifestDigest, tree, semanticVersion, protocolMajor, platform}` that security projects for the core closure (`kind: \"core\"`), with `kind` set to `\"evaluator\"` and every other member unchanged. For this kind `manifestDigest` is raw SHA256 of the TR-CORE-signed core inventory body (`opensip.metadata.inventory.1`), excluding its signature envelope, and `tree` is the projection of that platform's regular files. The core closure and the core evaluator closure of one release therefore differ only by `kind`. Security derives both from the same authenticated inventory; neither is read from a Run, a worker claim or a build-time string. As for every closure, the inventory body and every tree member are retained preimages of a Run that names it. A new core release is a new evaluator closure, so the Plans and Runs it evaluates have new identities. Historical closures, Plans and Runs keep their bytes and are not re-read under this law.", "before": "and digest for every selected file. A semantic version alone is not a closure.", "parent": {"bytes": 135448, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/identity-and-evidence.md", "sha256": "c82404f3a0cf56fa6cc02e99cc3ebbd5356fedc3b36aeb38f9ef284077fbd31f"}, "selector": {"line": 278}}, {"after": "| `raw-artifact` | raw SHA256 of the **exact retained artifact bytes** \u2014 a source or closure-tree file, a committed capability-manifest artifact, a component manifest body (for kind `evaluator`, the TR-CORE-signed core inventory body, EC1), or a complete registered schema document. Not of a canonicalization of anything. |", "before": "| `raw-artifact` | raw SHA256 of the **exact retained artifact bytes** \u2014 a source or closure-tree file, a committed capability-manifest artifact, a component manifest body, or a complete registered schema document. Not of a canonicalization of anything. |", "parent": {"bytes": 135448, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/identity-and-evidence.md", "sha256": "c82404f3a0cf56fa6cc02e99cc3ebbd5356fedc3b36aeb38f9ef284077fbd31f"}, "selector": {"line": 455}}], "passageSupersessions": [], "selected": "docs/implementation/m2/core-evaluator-closure-ec1/successor.json", "sha256": "fd7d8b9791e824dc8d2c6914c383aee84ceb6124e1ceaf5670bf22e52ece8421"}, {"inputs": [{"bytes": 21898, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/description-batch-d3/README.md", "sha256": "b3db2cdf7b6c3465299d89976ef3c54d97a4be094187640ab81ea3614d6c4aec"}, {"bytes": 7066, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/description-batch-d3/evidence/build_d3.py", "sha256": "4b8b6e64d6b321e73aa646aeac9140fd80b31f75d4e21dabdc2275fa13f5da89"}, {"bytes": 214633, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/description-batch-d3/evidence/descriptions.json", "sha256": "699e9345d351f323c6d60331a84a3eb5d378e8172a7ec034d84ada96c60e134d"}, {"bytes": 5351, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/description-batch-d3/evidence/verify_scratch.py", "sha256": "cf04e2871abaf2689622221fa578fe2316d69b248a28002051742928d9398ea5"}], "passageOverrides": [{"after": "Run the real built opensip doctor with no override (law X10 items 5 and 7): a development build ends at CORE.NO_EMBEDDED_RELEASE, exit 2, with a command-envelope v7 schema-valid kind failure envelope in JSON and the matching human lines, and the account's real installation untouched; doctor takes no flags and the agent format is not applicable; help and completion list doctor. Source pins keep every environment, home, feature, test or debug selector out of the binary, the doctor ingress and the request path, require exactly one producer call in the ingress, and refuse a features table on apps/cli and reporting and, on host, security and storage, any feature but X9's crash-matrix and X8's scenario-fixtures, a default included (law X10 r4 item 5, unit X9-1).", "before": "Run the real built opensip doctor with no override (law X10 items 5 and 7): a development build ends at CORE.NO_EMBEDDED_RELEASE, exit 2, with a command-envelope v7 schema-valid kind failure envelope in JSON and the matching human lines, and the account's real installation untouched; doctor takes no flags and the agent format is not applicable; help and completion list doctor. Source pins keep every environment, home, feature, test or debug selector out of the binary, the doctor ingress and the request path, require exactly one producer call in the ingress, and refuse a features table on the crates of that path.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/12/description"}}, {"after": "Host's crash-matrix support surface (law X9 r1 item 6, unit X9-1), doc(hidden). Compiled only under the test-only crash-matrix feature, which no manifest enables from a dependency table and which the crate refuses in any build without debug assertions (law X9 r1 item 2), so it is absent from every release build. It forwards storage's surface, which forwards security's, unchanged; X3d-3's synthetic run candidate reaches it there, as storage's own item (law X3d r8 item 13, X9 r7). Host's own items (law X9 r10 and r11, unit X9-5) include two runners: finalize_commit, which reads the on-disk run candidate and calls host's finalize with the operation entry as its admission and the fixed delivery phase, so that replay runs before any custody, and reports an unreadable candidate on the host I/O row with no entry; and store_gc, which runs host's maintenance::run over the settlement_sweep entry. Each makes the process's one entry, a second runner call refusing on the invariant row without effect, and returns a value report only: FinalizeReport (the outcome with any retained RunId, remedy, namespace and requested-binding flag, the end-path, end-step and rollover disclosures, the optional failure and the delivered bytes) or GcReport (each namespace's sweep or busy skip with its row, and the ending row). Host also supplies the opensip.x9.run-candidate.v1 file's writer and reader, inputs only, and the fixed delivery phase, one fixed response with exit 0 whose failure comes only from an armed x7.delivery point. Nothing here takes a receipt, gate, guard, session, ProjectOperation, ReplayedRun or delivery phase from its caller, or sleeps, polls or waits on a timer. Apart from the forwarded driver entries, nothing here returns an authority type.", "before": "Host's crash-matrix support surface (law X9 r1 item 6, unit X9-1), doc(hidden). Compiled only under the test-only crash-matrix feature, which no manifest enables from a dependency table and which the crate refuses in any build without debug assertions (law X9 r1 item 2), so it is absent from every release build. It forwards storage's surface, which forwards security's, unchanged; item 6's synthetic run candidate for the evaluator's replay joins it with X9-5. Nothing here returns an authority type.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/108/description"}}, {"after": "Own host admission of provider candidates, Coverage and occupancy joins against the selected source/context before evaluation; protocol framing alone grants no fact authority. Its M2 slice is the replay join alone (law X5 r3 item 1, unit X5a); the M3 syntax, context, occupancy and Coverage joins arrive here. RunCandidateInputs is the inert candidate Run, exactly what RetainedInputs::new borrows plus the claimed RunId, and claims nothing. replay_candidate calls the evaluator's replay_run once and returns its opaque ReplayedRun unchanged, or a ReplayRefusal carrying the claimed RunId and the evaluator's refusal unchanged (item 2); replay is pure, with no I/O, lock or ledger charge, so host finalization (X7a), its one production caller, runs it before any write or project admission, fence, lease or attempt row (item 3). REPLAY_LIMITS holds item 5e's local replay bounds, the value the one production call passes (item 5d). replay_termination maps each refusal onto one existing operational-failed exit 4 row in two steps (item 5): a promised retained byte the inputs lack, wherever the evaluator nested it, is HOST.IO_FAILURE evidence.missing naming the reference; otherwise an exhaustive match over ReplayError with no wildcard makes a structure, capture, law, input or evaluation failure PROVIDER.PROTOCOL_VIOLATION EVALUATION.INPUT_REFUSED and a mismatch HOST.IO_FAILURE evidence.regeneration-mismatch naming the claimed RunId. replay_remedy gives each route its item 5d remedy (identity's EvidenceUnavailable and RegenerationMismatch remedies verbatim, and the provider-return route's); the doctor ingress's row_remedy takes the same three remedies for those details.", "before": "Own host admission of provider candidates, Coverage and occupancy joins against the selected source/context before evaluation; protocol framing alone grants no fact authority.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/116/description"}}, {"after": "Check X5a (law X5 r3 item 8, host side), included as fact_admission.rs's cfg(test) module, on the synthetic replay corpus with no scratch installation: all 101 cases go through replay_candidate with REPLAY_LIMITS, the lawful ones returning the claimed RunId, the forged-output ones evidence.regeneration-mismatch with the claimed RunId as subject, and the corpus missing-blob case evidence.missing with its digest although the evaluator reports it under the walk; removing each member of one lawful case's claimed closure, one at a time, is evidence.missing naming exactly that reference, and removing an ambient member still replays; schema and identity failures are EVALUATION.INPUT_REFUSED on the provider-return route; every ReplayError variant maps to its row and remedy, each rendered through the shared failure envelope valid against command-envelope v7 with no run member; the remedies equal the fault-contract routes and identity carriers byte for byte; REPLAY_LIMITS holds item 5e's values, each of its nine dimensions admits the corpus at its measured exact need, at most the constant, and refuses one below on the structural row, and the capture-entry bound holds at the constant itself with ambient padding; and source pins show the join calls replay_run exactly once, mints no ReplayedRun, RunId or verdict, wires no check_plan_pack and reaches no custody, storage, ledger or I/O, and that every production caller passes REPLAY_LIMITS and there is exactly one (finalization.rs, unit X7a), with a self-check refusing caller-chosen limits.", "before": "Check X5a (law X5 r3 item 8, host side), included as fact_admission.rs's cfg(test) module, on the synthetic replay corpus with no scratch installation: all 101 cases go through replay_candidate with REPLAY_LIMITS, the lawful ones returning the claimed RunId, the forged-output ones evidence.regeneration-mismatch with the claimed RunId as subject, and the corpus missing-blob case evidence.missing with its digest although the evaluator reports it under the walk; removing each member of one lawful case's claimed closure, one at a time, is evidence.missing naming exactly that reference, and removing an ambient member still replays; schema and identity failures are EVALUATION.INPUT_REFUSED on the provider-return route; every ReplayError variant maps to its row and remedy, each rendered through the shared failure envelope valid against command-envelope v7 with no run member; the remedies equal the fault-contract routes and identity carriers byte for byte; REPLAY_LIMITS holds item 5e's values, each of its nine dimensions admits the corpus at its measured exact need, at most the constant, and refuses one below on the structural row, and the capture-entry bound holds at the constant itself with ambient padding; and source pins show the join calls replay_run exactly once, mints no ReplayedRun, RunId or verdict, wires no check_plan_pack and reaches no custody, storage, ledger or I/O, and that every production caller passes REPLAY_LIMITS (vacuous until X7a), with a self-check refusing caller-chosen limits.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/117/description"}}, {"after": "Coordinate full evaluator replay and live security admission, then pass opaque ReplayedRun and CommitSession to guarded storage publication; project success only after PublishedCommit. finalize is the host's one commit coordinator (law X7 r5, units X7a and X7b), in this order: X5's replay_candidate with REPLAY_LIMITS before any custody, a refusal ending the invocation on the replay join's row and remedy; the caller's X1 write and X2 project admission, which yields the one ProjectOperation; CommitSession::open, prepare_commit and publish; StoppedSession::finish on every end path; and only then, for a Committed Run not latched after admission, the caller's delivery phase at X9's x7.delivery required and optional points, which reads nothing from the store and takes no lease, receipt or read session. It projects exactly one outcome through exhaustive matches with no wildcard arm (item 3): a delivered commit is authoritative with its exit 0, 1 or 3 and any optional failure beside it; a failed or latched delivery is DELIVERY.REQUIRED_FAILED with the RunId retained; CommitUndetermined is the durability row with the ExecutionId as subject and the namespace beside it; a refusal is its own row; ExistingAttempt is the invariant row with the ExecutionId as subject and the whole requested binding disclosed (law X6 r3 item 6, unit X6b); the undetermined and existing-attempt rows take a later invocation's read-only recovery remedy. Only authoritative_run, which takes &PublishedCommit and nothing else, writes the authoritative label and RunId (DR-G27); an ephemeral analysis's label is ephemeral with no RunId. A typed CarrierCapacityExhausted is finished like any other attempt: X3d's finish carries it into X3b's end step, which runs the grant-generation rollover under the fence that step holds (X3b r10 item 13); finalization runs no rollover, projects item 6a's busy row naming the namespace, and discloses beside it, through SessionEnd's accessors, the rollover's outcome and any end-step failure, as it discloses a failed end-path settlement beside any outcome, none of which rewrites the row (item 6, unit X7b); storage never calls lifecycle. Finalization charges no ledger, never recovers its own outcome or retries a commit, and takes no admission, gate or fence of its own. The retained analysis projection after publication is the caller's delivery phase. Library only: no CLI command is wired.", "before": "Coordinate full evaluator replay and live security admission, then pass opaque ReplayedRun and CommitSession to guarded storage publication; project success only after PublishedCommit. Route typed CarrierCapacityExhausted through cleanup and operation-lease release to lifecycle rollover under its fence; storage never calls lifecycle. After publication, use outcomes.rs for the retained analysis projection and separately validate delegated attempt attribution, detail and authority under their owners.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/118/description"}}, {"after": "Check X7a and X7b (law X7 r5 item 10, host side), included as finalization.rs's cfg(test) module, with no scratch installation, home or lock: every row of item 3's table through injected X3d outcomes (a delivered commit is authoritative with its RunId and the delivered exit 0, 1 or 3, with any optional failure disclosed beside it; a failed required delivery and a latch after admission are both DELIVERY.REQUIRED_FAILED, delivery-required, DELIVERY.RENDERER_FAILED_AFTER_COMMIT, exit 4, with the RunId retained; CommitUndetermined is DURABILITY.COMMIT_FAILED with the ExecutionId as subject, the namespace beside it, no RunId and the read-only recovery remedy; a refusal is its X3d item 9 row unchanged; ExistingAttempt is the invariant row with its ExecutionId as subject, the binding's namespace and its other three members (store generation digest, carrier digest and operation reference; law X6 r3 item 6, unit X6b) beside it and the later read-only recovery remedy, with no recovery in the writer's invocation; CarrierCapacityExhausted is item 6a's busy row naming the namespace); every caller-constructible CommitOutcome and NotPrepared joins one attempt, the requested binding carried through; a failed end-path settlement is disclosed beside the outcome and never rewrites it; through injected finish disclosures (unit X7b), each rollover outcome (rolled, already rolled, skipped) is disclosed beside the unchanged busy row, a rollover refusal keeps its own row (busy, host I/O, quarantine, invariant and budget) and an uncertain append is DURABILITY.COMMIT_FAILED with no subject, neither rewriting the attempt's row, and an entered end step's failure is disclosed on its own row beside the outcome and any rollover outcome; the delivery phase over a scripted renderer and sink (a latched commit starts nothing, a renderer, exit, write or flush failure fails once with no retry and no optional effect, an optional failure never changes the exit); over the synthetic replay corpus, a replay refusal ends before any admission on the replay join's row and remedy, and admission runs once after a successful replay with its refusal on its own row; an ephemeral label is never authoritative and has no RunId; and source pins show finalization is the host's only commit coordinator, its order (replay, admission, open, prepare, publish, finish, then delivery), the one authoritative literal inside authoritative_run(&PublishedCommit), no recovery, admission, gate, fence, ledger, lifecycle, retry or wildcard arm of its own, no 458c read entry, receipt or read session, X2 lease or storage reader (the delivery phase reads nothing from the store; only the commit facade's two exact use lines, the security one also naming the inert RequestedBinding and the end path's RolloverDisclosure and SessionEnd), X9's two x7.delivery points, and that it reads the end path only through SessionEnd's three accessors, once each, naming no rollover operation, end step, carrier record or lease (unit X7b). A ProjectOperation cannot be built from the host's tests before X9-1's support surface or X8b's scenario seam (law X9 r1 gap G1), so the session-level integration rows are not here. Synthetic fixtures; no compiler qualification.", "before": "Check X7a (law X7 r5 item 10, host side), included as finalization.rs's cfg(test) module, with no scratch installation, home or lock: every row of item 3's table through injected X3d outcomes (a delivered commit is authoritative with its RunId and the delivered exit 0, 1 or 3, with any optional failure disclosed beside it; a failed required delivery and a latch after admission are both DELIVERY.REQUIRED_FAILED, delivery-required, DELIVERY.RENDERER_FAILED_AFTER_COMMIT, exit 4, with the RunId retained; CommitUndetermined is DURABILITY.COMMIT_FAILED with the ExecutionId as subject, the namespace beside it, no RunId and the read-only recovery remedy; a refusal is its X3d item 9 row unchanged; ExistingAttempt is the invariant row with its ExecutionId as subject, the binding's namespace beside it and the later read-only recovery remedy, with no recovery in the writer's invocation; CarrierCapacityExhausted is item 6a's busy row naming the namespace); every caller-constructible CommitOutcome and NotPrepared joins one attempt; a failed end-path settlement is disclosed beside the outcome and never rewrites it; the delivery phase over a scripted renderer and sink (a latched commit starts nothing, a renderer, exit, write or flush failure fails once with no retry and no optional effect, an optional failure never changes the exit); over the synthetic replay corpus, a replay refusal ends before any admission on the replay join's row and remedy, and admission runs once after a successful replay with its refusal on its own row; an ephemeral label is never authoritative and has no RunId; and source pins show finalization is the host's only commit coordinator, its order (replay, admission, open, prepare, publish, finish, then delivery), the one authoritative literal inside authoritative_run(&PublishedCommit), no recovery, admission, gate, fence, ledger, lifecycle, retry or wildcard arm of its own, no 458c read entry, receipt or read session, X2 lease or storage reader (the delivery phase reads nothing from the store; only the commit facade's two exact use lines), and X9's two x7.delivery points. A ProjectOperation cannot be built from the host's tests before X9-1's support surface or X8b's scenario seam (law X9 r1 gap G1), so the session-level integration rows are not here. Synthetic fixtures; no compiler qualification.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/119/description"}}, {"after": "Coordinate store status, authorized purge/backup/recovery and pin-impact decisions through lifecycle/security/storage owners; never use cache deletion as evidence deletion. Its M2 content is store-gc's per-namespace settlement sweep step (law X6 r3 items 1, 7 and 8, unit X6c, under commit-recovery-readonly.v3.md section 4 and F53), routing only: sweep_store admits security's SettlementSweep once (X1's ordinary writer admission, the endpoint, I/stores/S and the registry, under the installation fence), and run takes each listed namespace in order under X2's EXCLUSIVE lease, runs storage's sweep_namespace under it and drops the lease before the next, skipping and retaining a busy lease and ending the sweep on a lease refusal's X2 row, then releases the fence last. Item 8's rows per namespace: an unreadable ledger is the host I/O row and the sweep continues, a refused charge the budget row, and a settle whose COMMIT reports failure DURABILITY.COMMIT_FAILED, never retried; a busy or missing ledger and a one-sided or contradictory attempt (F23) write nothing and report no row, and MIGRATION.CORRUPT is on no row. Orphan objects are left in place. No CLI command calls it; the crash matrix's store_gc runner (unit X9-5) calls run over its settlement_sweep entry.", "before": "Coordinate store status, authorized purge/backup/recovery and pin-impact decisions through lifecycle/security/storage owners; never use cache deletion as evidence deletion.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/128/description"}}, {"after": "The crash matrix's host rows (law X9 r15 item 12, unit X9-5, with r10 to r13): F01, F12's and F40's caller route, F16, F17, F32 with X3b item 13's rollover crash table, F39's delivery half and F53's store-gc step, run through host's own commit coordinator and store-gc step in fresh processes. Built only with an explicit --features crash-matrix (required-features), and whole-file under the support predicate. Its children are this binary re-executed by X9-0's driver under the scripted wall clock: fixture; candidate (r10's host order: one operation entry, CommitSession::open, X3d-3's candidate and r12's distinct variant written to disk with the session's core closure, then the session's refused end); finalize (host's finalize_commit runner, which replays before any custody); store-gc (host's store_gc runner); recover; and a publisher that revokes the session's core closure under the installation fence. The parent transcribes nothing: it runs each reviewed host required row's script step by step by blocking record reads, records r12's timing guard on the parent's monotonic clock for runs that arm the observer tick, captures the post state, runs item 8's ladder through host where a row leaves an attempt, and writes one run record per run and the run set's matrix.json with r11's three-run host census. x9_5_matrix runs the file's rows that carry no unit member; x9_6_matrix, X9-6's host run set (law X9 r16), runs every row, X9-6's four F16 and F17 kills at the x7.delivery points included, over the same census. On every child whose outcome is an authoritative commit it asserts item 8's guard order from that child's own trace (law X9 r16, unit X9-6): acquisition at the journal's level 3, then the ledger's level 3, then level 4; release at the evidence COMMIT, level 4, then level 3, then the writer lease, then the end fence, with an owed REV or CLN append's level 4 and level 3 released before the lease; a miss fails the run. Nothing sleeps, polls or waits on a timer. Synthetic; no compiler qualification.", "before": "The crash matrix's host rows (law X9 r15 item 12, unit X9-5, with r10 to r13): F01, F12's and F40's caller route, F16, F17, F32 with X3b item 13's rollover crash table, F39's delivery half and F53's store-gc step, run through host's own commit coordinator and store-gc step in fresh processes. Built only with an explicit --features crash-matrix (required-features), and whole-file under the support predicate. Its children are this binary re-executed by X9-0's driver under the scripted wall clock: fixture; candidate (r10's host order: one operation entry, CommitSession::open, X3d-3's candidate and r12's distinct variant written to disk with the session's core closure, then the session's refused end); finalize (host's finalize_commit runner, which replays before any custody); store-gc (host's store_gc runner); recover; and a publisher that revokes the session's core closure under the installation fence. The parent transcribes nothing: it runs each reviewed host required row's script step by step by blocking record reads, records r12's timing guard on the parent's monotonic clock for runs that arm the observer tick, captures the post state, runs item 8's ladder through host where a row leaves an attempt, and writes one run record per run and the run set's matrix.json with r11's three-run host census. Nothing sleeps, polls or waits on a timer. Synthetic; no compiler qualification.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/144/description"}}, {"after": "Host's reviewed required runs of the crash matrix (law X9 r10 and r16: host's rows only, a file separate from storage's), opensip.x9.required-runs.v1 canonical JSON with storage's clockEpoch. Unit X9-5 transcribes its 94 rows (F01 4, F12 2, F16 1, F17 1, F32 76, F39 1, F40 3, F53 6) from law X9 r15 before any lead run: F32's kill points and their crash-table windows from the unarmed host census's exhausted-carrier run, and every expected value from the law's row or the owning law's outcome it names, never read back from a run. Unit X9-6 appends law X9 r16's four host kill-set coverage rows before any run, for 98: process-death kills after r12's distinct R2 at x7.delivery.required.before and .after (F16) and x7.delivery.optional.before and .after (F17), each at #1, expecting the finalize child killed and the committed Run kept (R1 committed-historically:pendingSettlement, R2 authoritative:0, R3 committed, R4 committed-historically); each carries the unit member X9-6, which keeps it out of every unit's check-unit subset. Each row names its case, variant, owning units, labels, a step-by-step script (candidate variants, the reserved-slot plant, arms, runs, awaits with kill, revocation or live-writer actions, mutations, and r12's distinct R2) and expected per-step values. Host's crash-matrix target reads it; tools/check_crash_matrix.py check-unit --unit X9-5 checks run sets against it, and X9-6's check takes it beside storage's file, as does the coverage command. Synthetic; no compiler qualification.", "before": "Host's reviewed required runs of the crash matrix (law X9 r10: X9-5's rows only, a file separate from storage's), opensip.x9.required-runs.v1 canonical JSON with storage's clockEpoch. Unit X9-5 transcribes its 94 rows (F01 4, F12 2, F16 1, F17 1, F32 76, F39 1, F40 3, F53 6) from law X9 r15 before any lead run: F32's kill points and their crash-table windows from the unarmed host census's exhausted-carrier run, and every expected value from the law's row or the owning law's outcome it names, never read back from a run. Each row names its case, variant, owning units, labels, a step-by-step script (candidate variants, the reserved-slot plant, arms, runs, awaits with kill, revocation or live-writer actions, mutations, and r12's distinct R2) and expected per-step values. Host's crash-matrix target reads it; tools/check_crash_matrix.py check-unit --unit X9-5 checks run sets against it, and X9-6's check takes it beside storage's file. Synthetic; no compiler qualification.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/147/description"}}, {"after": "Expose wall and sleep-inclusive monotonic observations; do not decide trust time. Under the test-only crash-matrix feature only, observe_clock takes its wall reading from the crash barrier's scripted wall clock when the process is a matrix child with a clock script (law X9 r2 item 3, unit X9-1); its monotonic readings and boot identity stay native, and without the script or the feature the wall reading is the OS's.", "before": "Expose wall and sleep-inclusive monotonic observations; do not decide trust time.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/318/description"}}, {"after": "The crash barrier module of law X9 r1 items 1 to 5 (unit X9-0), compiled only under the test-only crash-matrix feature, which no manifest enables and which src/lib.rs refuses without debug assertions. It holds the scope registry fixed by item 5 (with a read-only flag for x4.observer and x6.recover, and two x9.selftest scopes only in this crate's test build), the point grammar <scope>[.segments][/<primitive-step>.<before|after>] with per-name 1-based occurrences, the kinds step, fallible, write, gate and draw and the actions each admits, and the OPENSIP_X9_ARMS parser (<full name>#<k|*>=hold|fail-before|fail-after|torn|inject-id:exec1_..., inject-id only at x3d.session.execution-draw). It also holds law X9 r2 item 3's scripted wall clock (unit X9-1): OPENSIP_X9_CLOCK=<E>:<k>, read once (E a whole second, k the process's ordinal, with no sign, space or leading zero), and scripted_wall, which only observe_clock's wall reading calls: in a matrix child with a script, reading n (from 0) is E + 3600\u00b7k + n seconds with zero nanoseconds, and in any other process there is none, so the OS's wall clock is read. Without OPENSIP_X9_ARMS every point is one OnceLock read and a return. In a matrix child (OPENSIP_X9_CHILD set) every reached point writes one tagged stderr record X9|pid|thread|n|name#k|event|payload in a single write(2) of at most 512 bytes, numbered under one lock; hold registers the point, writes held and blocks on a stdin read until resume (bare when exactly one thread holds) or resume <name>#<k>, one held thread reading for all and handing lines to their targets by condvar; fail-before and fail-after return EIO from a primitive or the caller's error at a protocol site without or after the effect; torn writes the first half of the bytes with the same write, then holds, and a resumed torn write fails with EIO; a gate's armed rendezvous replaces the product's timed wait; the draw records its payload or returns an injected ExecutionId. An unadmitted action, a primitive outside any scope, an unknown scope or a malformed arming is a harness-error record and exit status 86, as are a clock script outside a matrix child or malformed and a process's scripted wall reading n = 3600. It never sleeps, polls or waits on a timer. Test-only; confers no authority, qualification or production seam.", "before": "The crash barrier module of law X9 r1 items 1 to 5 (unit X9-0), compiled only under the test-only crash-matrix feature, which no manifest enables and which src/lib.rs refuses without debug assertions. It holds the scope registry fixed by item 5 (with a read-only flag for x4.observer and x6.recover, and two x9.selftest scopes only in this crate's test build), the point grammar <scope>[.segments][/<primitive-step>.<before|after>] with per-name 1-based occurrences, the kinds step, fallible, write, gate and draw and the actions each admits, and the OPENSIP_X9_ARMS parser (<full name>#<k|*>=hold|fail-before|fail-after|torn|inject-id:exec1_..., inject-id only at x3d.session.execution-draw). Without OPENSIP_X9_ARMS every point is one OnceLock read and a return. In a matrix child (OPENSIP_X9_CHILD set) every reached point writes one tagged stderr record X9|pid|thread|n|name#k|event|payload in a single write(2) of at most 512 bytes, numbered under one lock; hold registers the point, writes held and blocks on a stdin read until resume (bare when exactly one thread holds) or resume <name>#<k>, one held thread reading for all and handing lines to their targets by condvar; fail-before and fail-after return EIO from a primitive or the caller's error at a protocol site without or after the effect; torn writes the first half of the bytes with the same write, then holds, and a resumed torn write fails with EIO; a gate's armed rendezvous replaces the product's timed wait; the draw records its payload or returns an injected ExecutionId. An unadmitted action, a primitive outside any scope, an unknown scope or a malformed arming is a harness-error record and exit status 86. It never sleeps, polls or waits on a timer. Test-only; confers no authority, qualification or production seam.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/319/description"}}, {"after": "The parent driver of law X9 r1 item 3 and item 5's census (unit X9-0), under the test-only crash-matrix feature. MatrixChild re-executes the current test binary as current_exe() --exact <entry> --nocapture --test-threads=1 with a cleared environment holding only OPENSIP_X9_CHILD, OPENSIP_X9_INPUT, OPENSIP_X9_ARMS (absent for an untraced child), OPENSIP_X9_CLOCK (law X9 r2 item 3, unit X9-1: <E>:<k> from the ChildSpec's ScriptedClock, the run set's epoch and the child's ordinal; absent without one, so the child reads the OS's wall clock) and TMPDIR, validates the arming and the clock script before spawning, reads the child's stderr records on a reader thread and drains stdout, awaits a held or other event by blocking reads, writes addressed resumes on stdin, and kills with SIGKILL; dropping an unfinished child kills it. The per-run watchdog (300 s; shorter only in this crate's tests) is the one timed wait: it kills the child and the run can only be a HARNESS-ERROR. Exit verifies a finished child (exit 0, the entry ran, no harness-error, every arm reached) and item 3's death at a point (signal 9, the holding thread's last record is that held, and no later record names a durability point), and gives the outcome and the trace without the process id. Census takes a traced child with nothing armed into points with contiguous occurrences and durability, and derives the kill set: each durability point at #1 and, when repeated, at (n + 1) / 2 and n. Test-only; confers no authority.", "before": "The parent driver of law X9 r1 item 3 and item 5's census (unit X9-0), under the test-only crash-matrix feature. MatrixChild re-executes the current test binary as current_exe() --exact <entry> --nocapture --test-threads=1 with a cleared environment holding only OPENSIP_X9_CHILD, OPENSIP_X9_INPUT, OPENSIP_X9_ARMS (absent for an untraced child) and TMPDIR, validates the arming before spawning, reads the child's stderr records on a reader thread and drains stdout, awaits a held or other event by blocking reads, writes addressed resumes on stdin, and kills with SIGKILL; dropping an unfinished child kills it. The per-run watchdog (300 s; shorter only in this crate's tests) is the one timed wait: it kills the child and the run can only be a HARNESS-ERROR. Exit verifies a finished child (exit 0, the entry ran, no harness-error, every arm reached) and item 3's death at a point (signal 9, the holding thread's last record is that held, and no later record names a durability point), and gives the outcome and the trace without the process id. Census takes a traced child with nothing armed into points with contiguous occurrences and durability, and derives the kill set: each durability point at #1 and, when repeated, at (n + 1) / 2 and n. Test-only; confers no authority.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/320/description"}}, {"after": "X9-0's self-tests (law X9 r1 item 12), compiled only for this crate's tests with the crash-matrix feature. A matrix_child test entry returns at once in the parent's own run; each test re-executes it as a child over a /tmp scratch root and drives it: a kill at replace's rename.after with the new bytes visible, at write.before leaving the empty staging file no destructor removed, and at lock.after with the flock busy until the death, each verified by SIGKILL and the trace; hold then resume to the lawful outcome and tree, with the bare resume when one thread holds; fail-before and fail-after at rename, file-barrier and directory-barrier returning EIO on the primitive's own stage and visibility, with the state each leaves, and at an accounted barrier; torn writes leaving half the bytes, killed or resumed into a failed write; an untraced child writing no record and matching the featureless outcome and tree, a traced unarmed child doing the same and recording the pinned 42-point census and 44-point kill set with a reproducible trace; armed but unreached points and actions a point's kind does not admit as harness errors; a primitive outside any scope; addressed resumes across two held threads, read-only points of another thread admitted after a kill point and a durability point refused; the watchdog ending a never-resumed child as a harness error; gate, protocol fallible and draw points with inject-id; and the arming grammar, point names, durability and kill-set derivation. X9-1's clock self-tests (law X9 r2 item 3) run an untraced child that reads observe_clock: under a ScriptedClock, ordinals 0 to 2 each read E + 3600\u00b7k, + 1 and + 2 seconds with zero nanoseconds while the monotonic readings move forward, and no record is written; without a script the wall reading lies within 60 s of /bin/date +%s readings taken before and after the child (unit F7); 3600 readings finish and a 3601st is a harness error; and the clock script grammar.", "before": "X9-0's self-tests (law X9 r1 item 12), compiled only for this crate's tests with the crash-matrix feature. A matrix_child test entry returns at once in the parent's own run; each test re-executes it as a child over a /tmp scratch root and drives it: a kill at replace's rename.after with the new bytes visible, at write.before leaving the empty staging file no destructor removed, and at lock.after with the flock busy until the death, each verified by SIGKILL and the trace; hold then resume to the lawful outcome and tree, with the bare resume when one thread holds; fail-before and fail-after at rename, file-barrier and directory-barrier returning EIO on the primitive's own stage and visibility, with the state each leaves, and at an accounted barrier; torn writes leaving half the bytes, killed or resumed into a failed write; an untraced child writing no record and matching the featureless outcome and tree, a traced unarmed child doing the same and recording the pinned 42-point census and 44-point kill set with a reproducible trace; armed but unreached points and actions a point's kind does not admit as harness errors; a primitive outside any scope; addressed resumes across two held threads, read-only points of another thread admitted after a kill point and a durability point refused; the watchdog ending a never-resumed child as a harness error; gate, protocol fallible and draw points with inject-id; and the arming grammar, point names, durability and kill-set derivation.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/321/description"}}, {"after": "X9-1's pin of every source site that names a test-only feature (law X9 r1 item 6, law X8 r3 item 4b), in every security test build: it scans every cfg and cfg! in crates/*/src and crates/*/tests and requires exactly the pinned table, the shared fixture gates under the one joint predicate any(test, feature = crash-matrix, feature = scenario-fixtures) (Image::Injected with the injected image and synthetic V2 profile set, HomeSource::Fixture with the creator path on a scratch H and the gate's test constructor, X4T-0 and its accessor, and the fenced state.v1 publisher, each with the closure it needs, and the fifth gate law X3d r8 item 13 adds by name (unit X3d-3), CommitSession::core_evaluator_closure_preimages with its two hops through ProjectOperation and the write receipt), the crash-matrix-only carrier fixtures, each crate's guard and support module, the census tests, security's and storage's scenario modules under scenario-fixtures alone (law X8 r3 item 4a, unit X8b), storage's and host's crash-matrix targets (tests/commit_tests.rs, unit X9-2; tests/commit_matrix_tests.rs, unit X9-5), each guarded whole-file by the support predicate, and X9-0's and X9 r2's platform sites; with a scanner self-test, a no-sleep pin over X9-1's sources, storage's run candidate and both matrix targets, which admits in each target at most one line, the timing guard's type GuardClock = std::time::Instant (law X9 r12 item 11; units X9-3 and X9-5), and tests of the shared producers (accepted trust installed, the fenced publisher advancing the revocation version and refusing without writing while another holder has the fence, a writer admitted) and of the inherited carrier fixture.", "before": "X9-1's pin of every source site that names a test-only feature (law X9 r1 item 6, law X8 r3 item 4b), in every security test build: it scans every cfg and cfg! in crates/*/src and crates/*/tests and requires exactly the pinned table, the shared fixture gates under the one joint predicate any(test, feature = crash-matrix, feature = scenario-fixtures) (Image::Injected with the injected image and synthetic V2 profile set, HomeSource::Fixture with the creator path on a scratch H and the gate's test constructor, X4T-0 and its accessor, and the fenced state.v1 publisher, each with the closure it needs), the crash-matrix-only carrier fixtures, each crate's guard and support module, the census tests and X9-0's and X9 r2's platform sites; with a scanner self-test, a no-sleep pin over X9-1's sources, and tests of the shared producers (accepted trust installed, the fenced publisher advancing the revocation version and refusing without writing while another holder has the fence, a writer admitted) and of the inherited carrier fixture.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/373/description"}}, {"after": "Security's synthetic crash-matrix support surface (law X9 r1 item 6, unit X9-1), doc(hidden). Compiled only under the test-only crash-matrix feature, which no manifest enables from a dependency table and which the crate refuses in any build without debug assertions (law X9 r1 item 2), so it is absent from every release build. It produces inputs on disk and returns paths, values and outcomes, and apart from its three driver entries never an authority type: SyntheticInstallation (create, or open by paths in a matrix child) through the real creator path with InitialCore's injected loaded image and the synthetic V2 profile set; X4T-0's signed accepted store written in place; publish_revocation, a thin caller of the shared fenced state.v1 publisher, returning the revocation version before and after, and refusing without writing in a process that made a driver entry; the inherited format-1 and format-2 carrier fixture; X3b-2's reserved-slot technique; and the FIXTURE and PROFILE_STANDING labels every run records. Every producer is a thin caller of a site on the pinned shared fixture-gate list. Law X9 r4 item 6's three driver entries (unit X9-2) are the one exception to returning no authority type: operation, the production chain to a root's ProjectOperation under an APPEND-WRITE lease (X1's ordinary writer with the gate over HomeSource::Fixture, X2's root admission and tracking with the first registration when the root is unregistered, and begin_operation); recovery_admission, a read receipt for the installation's H, recovery's own allocated ledger and recovery_admission::admit_on over HomeSource::Fixture; and settlement_sweep, X1's writer and then settlement_sweep::admit_on. Each takes only the synthetic installation, a root path or an inert RecoveryRequest and returns only that composition's result or refusal, and a process makes at most one call to any of the three (X1 items 1 and 7): the module's own flag makes a second refuse on the invariant row without effect. Synthetic; no compiler qualification.", "before": "Security's synthetic crash-matrix support surface (law X9 r1 item 6, unit X9-1), doc(hidden). Compiled only under the test-only crash-matrix feature, which no manifest enables from a dependency table and which the crate refuses in any build without debug assertions (law X9 r1 item 2), so it is absent from every release build. It produces inputs on disk and returns paths, values and outcomes, never an authority type: SyntheticInstallation (create, or open by paths in a matrix child) through the real creator path with InitialCore's injected loaded image and the synthetic V2 profile set; X4T-0's signed accepted store written in place; publish_revocation, a thin caller of the shared fenced state.v1 publisher, returning the revocation version before and after; the inherited format-1 and format-2 carrier fixture; X3b-2's reserved-slot technique; and the FIXTURE and PROFILE_STANDING labels every run records. Every producer is a thin caller of a site on the pinned shared fixture-gate list. Synthetic; no compiler qualification.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/374/description"}}, {"after": "Law X9 r1 item 7's post-state (unit X9-1), under the test-only crash-matrix feature: raw (every regular file under a scratch root by relative path, size and SHA-256, but a file the owner cannot read, which is never read and which logical's unreadable member records by path, size and mode instead, for rows F24 and F53's mode-000 mutations, unit X9-3), logical (every SQLite database dumped table by table from a private copy of its file and -wal, a BLOB holding UTF-8 text as that text (unit X9-2), the rows of a table without rowid ordered by their unnumbered shape and tied rows by how each numbers against the rest of the dump (unit X9-3), and a file with SQLite's header that SQLite cannot read recorded as undumpable (unit X9-3), the operational witness and floor files decoded, the object set, recorded by each name's shape, sorted, because some of its content-digest names derive from drawn values (unit X9-2), and trustState, the trust store reduced to one digest over its files normalized one by one) and normalizedSha256 (logical with every drawn value, ExecutionId, op- and req1_ token, staging and temporary-name nonce, drawn identifier, digest over drawn content and timestamp, replaced by a placeholder numbered in order of first appearance, members ordered by their unnumbered shape, and every native clock sample or wall-clock datum replaced by its member name alone, law X9 r2 item 7). Capturing opens nothing under the root for writing, and locks, checkpoints and creates nothing there.", "before": "Law X9 r1 item 7's post-state (unit X9-1), under the test-only crash-matrix feature: raw (every regular file under a scratch root by relative path, size and SHA-256), logical (every SQLite database dumped table by table from a private copy of its file and -wal, the operational witness and floor files decoded, the object set, and trustState, the trust store reduced to one digest over its files normalized one by one) and normalizedSha256 (logical with every drawn value, ExecutionId, op- and req1_ token, staging and temporary-name nonce, drawn identifier, digest over drawn content and timestamp, replaced by a placeholder numbered in order of first appearance, members ordered by their unnumbered shape, and every native clock sample or wall-clock datum replaced by its member name alone, law X9 r2 item 7). Capturing opens, locks, checkpoints and creates nothing under the root.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/375/description"}}, {"after": "Law X9 r1 item 7's run writer (unit X9-1, with law X9 r2 items 3 and 6), under the test-only crash-matrix feature: one canonical opensip.x9.run.v1 record per run, runs/<case>-<variant>.json, never replacing an existing file, carrying the product, the host with the synthetic-signed-v2 fixture and BASELINE-ATTESTED standing, the run set's clock (epoch and the x9-ordinal-3600 script), each child's role, ordinal, exit, lastHeld point, trace record count and digest (X9-0's trace lines without the process id) and outcome, the post-state, the ladder, the transcribed expected value and the caller's verdict, with the optional ladderEnd and notApplicable and, for a run that arms the observer tick, law X9 r1 item 11's timingGuard (r12 and r13, unit X9-3): the caller's measured monotonic milliseconds and the 5,000 ms limit, TIMING_GUARD_LIMIT_MS; and a census with its kill set in matrix.json's shape. It records and decides no verdict.", "before": "Law X9 r1 item 7's run writer (unit X9-1, with law X9 r2 items 3 and 6), under the test-only crash-matrix feature: one canonical opensip.x9.run.v1 record per run, runs/<case>-<variant>.json, never replacing an existing file, carrying the product, the host with the synthetic-signed-v2 fixture and BASELINE-ATTESTED standing, the run set's clock (epoch and the x9-ordinal-3600 script), each child's role, ordinal, exit, lastHeld point, trace record count and digest (X9-0's trace lines without the process id) and outcome, the post-state, the ladder, the transcribed expected value and the caller's verdict; and a census with its kill set in matrix.json's shape. It records and decides no verdict.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/376/description"}}, {"after": "The security side of the CommitSession storage facade (law X3d r6 items 1 to 4, 7, 8 and 9; unit X3d-1, with X4c's end path merged), custody.rs's macOS module beside X2e's operation_handoff and X4a's operation_guard. CommitSession::open consumes one ProjectOperation, draws the ExecutionId (exec1_) and the operationRef (op-) from 16 host-CSPRNG bytes each on the write receipt's attempt ledger (law X9 r1's x3d.session.execution-draw draw point), and binds N, (S, G, K), the carrier digest SHA-256(N) and the receipt's selected core closure, with a read-only execution_id. core_evaluator_closure is the receipt's core evaluator closure (law X3d r8 item 13, unit X3d-3; contract successor EC1), derived from the same authenticated core inventory as the selected core closure and never read from a Run, which a ReplayedRun's evaluator closure must equal; core_evaluator_closure_preimages, doc(hidden) and compiled only under the joint predicate (test, crash-matrix or scenario-fixtures) on X8 r3 item 4b's site list, returns that closure's descriptor, the TR-CORE-signed inventory body and each tree member's bytes for the synthetic run candidate, and no production path calls it. Two read-only hooks serve storage's commit (unit X3d-2): project_id, the ProjectId of N's ACTIVE row that a Run must name, and store_root, the selected store's directory I/stores/S for item 3 steps 4 to 6, opened no-follow from the retained I as one charged step on the attempt ledger, holding the marker the endpoint admitted and named by its absolute spelling, returned as the exported StoreRoot (the retained handle, that spelling and the invoking user; not authority), any failure latching the ledger. reserve_end_path is item 3 step 0: the attempt ledger's one settlement reserve (X3d-0), taken through the attempt, write receipt and operation (one production caller each, source-pinned), sized exactly at one REV and one CLN append at their bounded maximum bodies by X3b-2's own cost functions (writer open, witness read and append cost at the largest generation and the last ordinary sequence, with no literal), held only in a private end-path value. capacity is item 3 step 3 over seal_fits on the tail the start proved. begin_journal_txn takes the journal's level 3 (refusing a session without the reserve) and JournalWriteTxn keeps it detached across storage's evidence-ledger BEGIN IMMEDIATE, with a consuming abort. seal_under_append_lock runs item 4 step 3 with the security-owned two-phase adapter traits (CommitAdapter staging, StagedCommit COMMIT, EvidenceCommit) inside one attempt-ledger charge: level 4, X4's checkpoint, the SEAL built and bound to the caller's ReplayedRun through PreparedJournalSeal, its witnessed append, the repeated checkpoint, staging, the final checkpoint ending in FinalGate::admit, the evidence COMMIT only through the one AdmissionPermit, level 4's release; a certain stop rolls the evidence transaction back, then releases level 4 and any journal level 3, appends nothing and latches the gate; JournalSealBinding is read-only evidence of the SEAL. Every certain refusal before admission latches the operation's one gate (0 to 2). An uncertain journal commit or barrier, an undetermined attempt-admission COMMIT and an undetermined evidence COMMIT forfeit the reserve where classified. StoppedSession::finish owes a REV for a durable SEAL without its evidence commit, a latched gate or an observed revocation (reason from a closed set: trust-revoked, policy, observer-fail-stop, stale-guard, operation-stopped; no trustEpochObserved) and a CLN for F38's fixed residual pair, appends them in one settle of the reserve as fresh level-3-then-level-4 appends with no checkpoint, each refused on the invariant row before any effect if its draft exceeds its kind's body bound, stopping at the first failure (no CLN after a failed REV), then releases the lease and runs X3b's end step unless an outcome was uncertain or the attempt ledger is closed. SessionEnd's end_step_failure gives an entered end step's own failure on its row, and its rollover gives the capacity rollover's outcome when the end step ran it, as the public value RolloverDisclosure (Rolled with the opened generation, AlreadyRolled, Skipped, Refused on its row, Undetermined), a disclosure that grants nothing and never rewrites the session's outcome (law X7 r6 item 6, unit X7b). SessionRefusal projects every composition row through InstallationTermination. It places law X9 r1's x3d.session, x3d.publish (after-staging, before-final-checkpoint, commit-returned) and x3d.finish (settle.before, settle.after) points, and runs the SEAL's journal transaction (level 3 through level 4's release) and each end-path REV and CLN append inside law X9 r1 item 5's x3b.append.seal, x3b.append.rev and x3b.append.cln scopes. Library only: no command commits.", "before": "The security side of the CommitSession storage facade (law X3d r6 items 1 to 4, 7, 8 and 9; unit X3d-1, with X4c's end path merged), custody.rs's macOS module beside X2e's operation_handoff and X4a's operation_guard. CommitSession::open consumes one ProjectOperation, draws the ExecutionId (exec1_) and the operationRef (op-) from 16 host-CSPRNG bytes each on the write receipt's attempt ledger (law X9 r1's x3d.session.execution-draw draw point), and binds N, (S, G, K), the carrier digest SHA-256(N) and the receipt's selected core closure, with a read-only execution_id. reserve_end_path is item 3 step 0: the attempt ledger's one settlement reserve (X3d-0), taken through the attempt, write receipt and operation (one production caller each, source-pinned), sized exactly at one REV and one CLN append at their bounded maximum bodies by X3b-2's own cost functions (writer open, witness read and append cost at the largest generation and the last ordinary sequence, with no literal), held only in a private end-path value. capacity is item 3 step 3 over seal_fits on the tail the start proved. begin_journal_txn takes the journal's level 3 (refusing a session without the reserve) and JournalWriteTxn keeps it detached across storage's evidence-ledger BEGIN IMMEDIATE, with a consuming abort. seal_under_append_lock runs item 4 step 3 with the security-owned two-phase adapter traits (CommitAdapter staging, StagedCommit COMMIT, EvidenceCommit) inside one attempt-ledger charge: level 4, X4's checkpoint, the SEAL built and bound to the caller's ReplayedRun through PreparedJournalSeal, its witnessed append, the repeated checkpoint, staging, the final checkpoint ending in FinalGate::admit, the evidence COMMIT only through the one AdmissionPermit, level 4's release; a certain stop rolls the evidence transaction back, then releases level 4 and any journal level 3, appends nothing and latches the gate; JournalSealBinding is read-only evidence of the SEAL. Every certain refusal before admission latches the operation's one gate (0 to 2). An uncertain journal commit or barrier, an undetermined attempt-admission COMMIT and an undetermined evidence COMMIT forfeit the reserve where classified. StoppedSession::finish owes a REV for a durable SEAL without its evidence commit, a latched gate or an observed revocation (reason from a closed set: trust-revoked, policy, observer-fail-stop, stale-guard, operation-stopped; no trustEpochObserved) and a CLN for F38's fixed residual pair, appends them in one settle of the reserve as fresh level-3-then-level-4 appends with no checkpoint, each refused on the invariant row before any effect if its draft exceeds its kind's body bound, stopping at the first failure (no CLN after a failed REV), then releases the lease and runs X3b's end step unless an outcome was uncertain or the attempt ledger is closed. SessionRefusal projects every composition row through InstallationTermination. It places law X9 r1's x3d.session, x3d.publish (after-staging, before-final-checkpoint, commit-returned) and x3d.finish (settle.before, settle.after) points. Library only: no command commits.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/378/description"}}, {"after": "Check X3d-1 through X2e's real handoff on scratch homes (a creator-published P0 with X4T-0's signed accepted store, ordinary writers over 462's signed test trees, a scripted monitor clock, observer ticks on request, a test adapter standing in for storage's staging and COMMIT; no real home or installation), included under operation_handoff_tests.rs's cfg(test) module: open's ids, grammar and binding; a lawful commit appending one SEAL, no REV or CLN, and the end step copying the floor; after each certain refusal that closes the attempt ledger (a publication-reserve overrun, busy at the evidence ledger's BEGIN IMMEDIATE with the consuming abort, a staging I/O error, a failed final checkpoint on a stall), the REV (and the CLN after a durable SEAL) appended from the settlement with its closed reason and the end step taking no fence; a busy carrier failing the REV, with no CLN and the busy row disclosed; a refused step 0 holding no reserve and appending nothing; an unfunded journal transaction and a second reserve on the invariant row; nothing appended and no end step after an uncertain SEAL commit, an undetermined evidence COMMIT and an undetermined attempt admission; an exhausted generation returned with the ledger open and rolled over; the reserve equal to one bounded REV and one bounded CLN, the closed reasons, and a draft one byte over its bound refused; a real REV and CLN at the last two ordinary slots charging exactly the cost function; the settlement chain's single production caller per link; the x3d crash points' names; X3d-2's hooks (the session's project_id the ACTIVE row's ProjectId, its store root the endpoint's I/stores/S by identity and spelling, and a replaced store directory refused as required-files-changed with the attempt ledger latched); and X7b's disclosure through SessionEnd's accessors (law X7 r6 item 6): through finish on a real exhausted carrier, the rollover Rolled to generation 2, Skipped under a held readers.lease and Refused busy at a busy level 3, each with no end-step failure, and an end step refused on a moved namespace before the rollover disclosing its failure and no rollover; and, at X3b-4's test points, an undetermined rollover, one short of its reservation on the budget row, an I/O-refused rollover beside its copy's host I/O failure, and a failed copy after Rolled as an end-step failure.", "before": "Check X3d-1 through X2e's real handoff on scratch homes (a creator-published P0 with X4T-0's signed accepted store, ordinary writers over 462's signed test trees, a scripted monitor clock, observer ticks on request, a test adapter standing in for storage's staging and COMMIT; no real home or installation), included under operation_handoff_tests.rs's cfg(test) module: open's ids, grammar and binding; a lawful commit appending one SEAL, no REV or CLN, and the end step copying the floor; after each certain refusal that closes the attempt ledger (a publication-reserve overrun, busy at the evidence ledger's BEGIN IMMEDIATE with the consuming abort, a staging I/O error, a failed final checkpoint on a stall), the REV (and the CLN after a durable SEAL) appended from the settlement with its closed reason and the end step taking no fence; a busy carrier failing the REV, with no CLN and the busy row disclosed; a refused step 0 holding no reserve and appending nothing; an unfunded journal transaction and a second reserve on the invariant row; nothing appended and no end step after an uncertain SEAL commit, an undetermined evidence COMMIT and an undetermined attempt admission; an exhausted generation returned with the ledger open and rolled over; the reserve equal to one bounded REV and one bounded CLN, the closed reasons, and a draft one byte over its bound refused; a real REV and CLN at the last two ordinary slots charging exactly the cost function; the settlement chain's single production caller per link; and the x3d crash points' names.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/379/description"}}, {"after": "First registration of a project root (law X2 r8 item 6, unit X2c) on the law 468 durable write gate, under its held fence and on its one ledger, with the write-gate entries for project admission and item 6a tracking. Preconditions before any effect: a FirstUseCandidate with the marker positively absent, the admission's own recheck, the tracking observation rechecked, no entry beside P0's lineage under transitions (the active-transition-recovery gate), a present host or projects judged private, up to eight independent ProjectId and UUIDv4 namespace candidates checked against all history and a no-follow occupancy lookup, and capacity (rows, the RESERVED and ACTIVE documents and the worst later terminal spelling within 4 MiB, and the all-registered wrapper, within 4096 rows; PROJECT.SCOPE_LIMIT with S12 subjects). The registry replacement primitive, used for RESERVED and ACTIVE: reconfirm the current owner R by its retained descriptor and full sample and by name, with project-registry.v1 absent; build the complete canonical document and validate it with the same decoder; write a fresh exclusive private temporary name in I with F_FULLFSYNC, confirmed by identity and exact bytes; rename it over the registry; I's barrier and a name recheck by identity. R advances R0 to R1 to R2 only through these confirmed publications, and the gate's retained registry sample with it. The namespace I/host/projects/N is published whole from a private stage holding exactly two empty private lease files, by an exclusive rename with its parent's barrier; host and projects are created or admitted as 465 item 3 does; .opensip is created or admitted under S3 custody with item 1's premise; the 92-byte marker is created no-replace with its file and directory barriers. Every current owner is rechecked before ACTIVE, ACTIVE is built from R1's document, and R2 must classify the root Eligible. Each effect reserves its confirmation work first; any refusal spends the gate, with no retry and no deletion, and leftovers are the registry owner's explicit-recovery cases. Each refusal names its item 8 row; the public projection is the composition owner's. A cfg(test) seam stops at each step and scripts candidate draws. Later units build on it: namespace_lease reuses its owner checks, costs and rows, and its refusals add the Lease step, the busy row for a project lease held elsewhere and the write-gate refusal's namespace arm (law X2 r8 item 7, unit X2d); the registered project's recheck takes a namespace footprint, Exact (item 6's two empty leases only) or Started (after X3b's carrier start: N private and both leases present, private and empty, with no closed entry scan), beside the Handoff step and I/trust as an owner (law X2 r8 item 7a, unit X2e); and recheck_registered_owners rechecks only a registered project's original owners after the fence is released: the chain, the root's incarnation, .opensip by name and its custody, and the marker by descriptor and by name (law X4 r7 item 4, unit X4a). Library only: no lease (X2d) and no handoff (X2e).", "before": "First registration of a project root (law X2 r8 item 6, unit X2c) 