ty-degraded, and evidence.purged under a purged availability generation; a short ledger the budget row; ExistingAttempt's requested binding equal to the plan's and refused on operation against another attempt; and source pins that recover.rs and recovery_read.rs hold no SQL write, transaction, creation, barrier, publication, settlement or sleep and place after-ledger-snapshot once, after the snapshot.", "before": "Check X6b's storage half without an admission (storage cannot build security's RecoveryAdmission; X9 composes the two in fresh processes), included as commit_tests.rs's child module so that it runs recover_from over scratch I/stores/S stores that X3d-2's own storage steps committed into, with a scripted carrier verdict, checking every time that recovery leaves the ledger bytes unchanged: a confirmed commit committed-historically with pending settlement disclosed, then without it once settled committed, and the carrier asked exactly the association's carrier half; the settlement matrix (admitted with no receipt unknown-attempt-open and no row unknown-attempt-unobserved, both without asking the carrier; settled refused with both absent the only negative; settled committed without a receipt and settled refused beside one unknown custody); each carrier verdict its own standing, a budget refusal RecoveryFailure; F34's requested binding equal giving the standing and differing in operation, store generation, namespace or carrier, or with no attempt row, binding-unusable before the carrier is asked; no projects directory, a missing ledger file, a non-database and a drifted schema unknown custody; a deleted and a flipped committed object evidence.missing and evidence.corrupt in committed-availability-degraded, and evidence.purged under a purged availability generation; a short ledger the budget row; ExistingAttempt's requested binding equal to the plan's and refused on operation against another attempt; and source pins that recover.rs and recovery_read.rs hold no SQL write, transaction, creation, barrier, publication, settlement or sleep and place after-ledger-snapshot once, after the snapshot.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/738/description"}}, {"after": "Check X6c's storage step without a lease (storage's ordinary test build cannot build security's SweepLease, which security's crash-matrix support surface supplies through settlement_sweep only under the test-only feature, law X9 r4 item 6, unit X9-2; X9 composes the two in fresh processes), included as commit_tests.rs's child module so that sweep_from runs over scratch I/stores/S stores that X3d-2's own storage steps committed into, replaying commit_tests.rs's synthetic run candidate under a real session's binding values (unit X3d-3), read back by recovery's recover_from: a committed-but-unsettled attempt settled committed and a crashed one settled refused, with no receipt, association or object written, the reader moving from committed-historically with pending settlement and unknown-attempt-open to committed-historically without it and terminal-not-committed, and a second sweep listing and writing nothing; a one-sided ledger (receipt or association removed behind its reinstalled trigger, X9's F23 mutation) reported one-sided and left admitted while the crashed attempt beside it settles; a missing projects directory or ledger file no ledger, a non-database, a drifted schema and a non-private ledger file unreadable with nothing written; a foreign holder of the ledger's write lock stopping the namespace busy with every row left admitted until the next sweep settles each once; an admitted row under another binding never listed; a short ledger the budget row with nothing written; and source pins that the sweep's only write is the one settle UPDATE in ledger_store, guarded on admitted and never undetermined, called only from sweep_settle.rs, and that sweep.rs and sweep_settle.rs hold no other SQL write, staging, publication, creation, barrier, SEAL, witness, sleep or retry and place after-snapshot once between the snapshot and the first settle and settle.commit once after the settle statement.", "before": "Check X6c's storage step without a lease (storage cannot build security's SweepLease; X9 composes the two in fresh processes), included as commit_tests.rs's child module so that sweep_from runs over scratch I/stores/S stores that X3d-2's own storage steps committed into, read back by recovery's recover_from: a committed-but-unsettled attempt settled committed and a crashed one settled refused, with no receipt, association or object written, the reader moving from committed-historically with pending settlement and unknown-attempt-open to committed-historically without it and terminal-not-committed, and a second sweep listing and writing nothing; a one-sided ledger (receipt or association removed behind its reinstalled trigger, X9's F23 mutation) reported one-sided and left admitted while the crashed attempt beside it settles; a missing projects directory or ledger file no ledger, a non-database, a drifted schema and a non-private ledger file unreadable with nothing written; a foreign holder of the ledger's write lock stopping the namespace busy with every row left admitted until the next sweep settles each once; an admitted row under another binding never listed; a short ledger the budget row with nothing written; and source pins that the sweep's only write is the one settle UPDATE in ledger_store, guarded on admitted and never undetermined, called only from sweep_settle.rs, and that sweep.rs and sweep_settle.rs hold no other SQL write, staging, publication, creation, barrier, SEAL, witness, sleep or retry and place after-snapshot once between the snapshot and the first settle and settle.commit once after the settle statement.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/747/description"}}, {"after": "Exercise the actual carrier at each object/journal/ledger/acknowledgement fault point, lock order, revoked sessions and fresh-process recovery. It is storage's crash-matrix target (law X9 r1 items 2, 3 and 7 to 9; units X9-2, X9-3, X9-4 and X9-6), declared with required-features crash-matrix and compiled only under cfg(all(feature = crash-matrix, target_os = macos)), so a plain cargo test skips it. Every process of a run is a matrix child of this test binary under X9 r2 item 3's scripted wall clock, numbered in spawn order: fixture (ordinal 0: the synthetic installation, X4T-0's accepted store and a project root); commit and competitor-writer (X9 r4 item 6's operation, CommitSession::open, X3d-3's synthetic run candidate or r12's distinct variant replayed by replay_run, prepare_commit, publish and finish, or F42's forgotten stopped session, or X9-4's refused end with nothing staged); recover and reader (recovery_admission with a plain or binding-carrying request, then recover); sweep (settlement_sweep, then X6c's sweep_namespace for each namespace); revoke (publish_revocation of the session's core closure in a process of its own); and entries (one driver entry per process). The parent transcribes nothing: x9_2_matrix, x9_3_matrix, x9_4_matrix and x9_6_matrix each run, only when OPENSIP_X9_RUN_SET is set, their unit's rows of the reviewed required-runs.v1.json (X9-6: every storage row) after that unit's census, each driver part run twice and equal and the parts unioned by point name at the largest count (X9-2: the commit driver, with r9's reference run for transcription; X9-3: commit, recover and sweep; X9-4: the lawful commit and the refused end; X9-6: X9-3's three and the refused end). They arm, await, resume and kill by blocking record reads, in X9-2's single-commit form or X9-3's step form of named overlapping children (with X9-4's foreign SQLite holder, @m injected ids, requested bindings and before-and-after comparisons), apply parent mutations, capture the post state between processes, run item 8's ladder R1 to R4 (R1's stateUnchanged compared) and F36's R5, make a run whose timing guard on the parent's monotonic GuardClock exceeds 5,000 ms a HARNESS-ERROR (law X9 r12 to r15), digest each child's trace normalized per thread with r14's object-publication group order, fail a run whose Committed child breaks law X9 r16 item 8's guard order (journal level 3, ledger level 3, level 4; evidence COMMIT, level 4, level 3, writer lease, end fence, an owed REV or CLN released before the lease), and write one run record per run and matrix.json with the census, kill set, release-absence record and limits L1 to L11. Lane tests check one driver entry per process, the commit driver's census equal across two runs and in the guard order, and the release-order condition over synthetic traces. Nothing here sleeps, polls or waits on a timer; X9-0's watchdog is the one timed wait.", "before": "Exercise the actual carrier at each object/journal/ledger/acknowledgement fault point, lock order, revoked sessions and fresh-process recovery; API compile-fail fixtures require the selected harness.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/748/description"}}, {"after": "The reviewed required runs of the crash matrix (law X9 r1 items 7 and 9; r4 item 7's per-unit subset), opensip.x9.required-runs.v1 canonical JSON with the run set's clockEpoch. Unit X9-2 transcribes its rows (F00, F02 to F05, F07 to F10, F20 to F22, F31, F46; 231 runs) from law X9 r9 before any run: the kill points and their order from the unarmed census of its commit driver, each X4T dependency occurrence's R2 from r9's unarmed reference run, and every expected value from the law's row, never read back from a run. Each row names its case, variant, owning units, labels (synthetic and scripted-clock always; process-death, injected or mutation as the run is), script and expected per-step values, and (law X9 r15 and r16) optionally the X9 unit that owns it. A script is X9-2's single-commit form (arm, await-and-kill, hold-copy-resume or mutate, with X9-3's r2, ladder, request and commit directives) or X9-3's step form of named overlapping children (spawn, await with hold, pass or kill, resume, finish, mutate and ladder of a child), with X9-4's sqlite-hold and sqlite-release steps and binding, unchanged and observe options. Storage's crash-matrix target reads it; tools/check_crash_matrix.py check (with host's required runs, law X9 r10) and check-unit check run sets against it, and coverage checks its kills against the union census. Later X9 units append their rows after the earlier ones, which stay unchanged: X9-3's 57 (F11 to F15, F23 to F25, F27 to F29, F33, F36, F42 to F45, F49, F52, F53; law X9 r14), X9-4's 47 (F06, F18, F19, F26, F30, F34, F38, F39's storage half, F40, F41, and F14's moved x3d.finish.settle.before kill, whose row names unit X9-4; r15) and X9-6's 46 storage rows of r16's 50 kill-set coverage rows, each naming unit X9-6, for 381 runs. Synthetic; no compiler qualification.", "before": "The reviewed required runs of the crash matrix (law X9 r1 items 7 and 9; r4 item 7's per-unit subset), opensip.x9.required-runs.v1 canonical JSON with the run set's clockEpoch. Unit X9-2 transcribes its rows (F00, F02 to F05, F07 to F10, F20 to F22, F31, F46; 231 runs) from law X9 r9 before any run: the kill points and their order from the unarmed census of its commit driver, each X4T dependency occurrence's R2 from r9's unarmed reference run, and every expected value from the law's row, never read back from a run. Each row names its case, variant, owning units, labels (synthetic and scripted-clock always; process-death, injected or mutation as the run is), script (arm, await-and-kill, or mutate) and expected per-step values. Storage's crash-matrix target reads it; tools/check_crash_matrix.py check and check-unit check run sets against it. Later X9 units append their rows. Synthetic; no compiler qualification.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/749/description"}}, {"after": "Check an X9 crash-matrix run set (law X9 r1 item 7) and scan a release binary (item 2, guard 4); read-only, standard library only. check (law X9 r10 and r16, unit X9-6) is the two-target M2 exit gate: it takes storage's and host's reviewed required-runs.v1.json, each with its pair of lead run sets, exactly once each, and refuses unless, for each target, both lead run sets list exactly that file's rows (a row's optional unit member, X9-2 to X9-6, admitted; law X9 r15 and r16), each run and matrix.json is canonical JSON of the opensip.x9 v1 shapes, every verdict is PASS with labels, units, script and expected values equal to the required row, the reviewed clean commit and dev crash-matrix profile, synthetic-signed-v2 and BASELINE-ATTESTED host labels, process-death exactly when the script kills with a signal-9 child held there, a census of registered scopes with the right durability whose derived kill set equals the recorded one, a passed release absence, limits L1 to L11 (law X9 r8, unit X9-2), and agreement of the two repetitions on normalizedSha256, trace digests and census; every run labelled scripted-clock with the run set's clock (the required runs' clockEpoch, script x9-ordinal-3600) and its children's ordinals in spawn order (law X9 r2 item 3, unit X9-1); timingGuard {monotonicMs, limitMs 5000} within its limit on exactly the runs whose script arms x4.observer.tick, not compared between repetitions (law X9 r12 and r13, unit X9-3); one clockEpoch in both files; and every point of the union census's kill set (by point name, each at its largest occurrence count, its durability equal in both) killed by a process-death run of either target, in each repetition. Only the two targets together report matrixPass. check-unit (law X9 r4 item 7, unit X9-2) applies the per-run check and repetition agreement to one unit's subset (X9-2 to X9-5: the rows whose case is in the unit's list; r15: a row with a unit member is in that unit's subset only, and an X9-6 row in none) with the stated base commit, a worktree that may be dirty and no full coverage, only every killed point inside the derived kill set. coverage (unit X9-6) runs nothing and reads no run set: from each target's census and required runs it reports every union kill-set point no row kills and every killed point outside it. release-absence reports whether OPENSIP_X9_ or any scope name from the barrier's registry block occurs in a binary. It runs no matrix and qualifies nothing.", "before": "Check an X9 crash-matrix run set (law X9 r1 item 7) and scan a release binary (item 2, guard 4); read-only, standard library only. check refuses unless both lead run sets list exactly the reviewed required-runs.v1.json rows, each run and matrix.json is canonical JSON of the opensip.x9 v1 shapes, every verdict is PASS with labels, units, script and expected values equal to the required row, the reviewed clean commit and dev crash-matrix profile, synthetic-signed-v2 and BASELINE-ATTESTED host labels, process-death exactly when the script kills with a signal-9 child held there, a census of registered scopes with the right durability whose derived kill set equals the recorded one and is fully killed, a passed release absence, limits L1 to L10, and agreement of the two repetitions on normalizedSha256, trace digests and census. release-absence reports whether OPENSIP_X9_ or any scope name from the barrier's registry block occurs in a binary. It runs no matrix and qualifies nothing.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/863/description"}}, {"after": "Exercise tools/check_crash_matrix.py on a disposable synthetic run pair against the repository's real scope registry: the registry read, kill-set derivation and point grammar; a complete agreeing pair passing; missing and extra runs; each run-field corruption (verdicts, labels, clock epoch and script, child ordinals, expected, script, commit, dirty worktree, profile, fixture labels, unverified kills, keys, postState); a claimed process death; noncanonical bytes, floats and duplicate keys; matrix corruptions (run bytes, kill set, durability, unregistered census point, release absence, limits, product); an uncovered new durability point; disagreeing repetitions; required runs without clockEpoch or the scripted-clock label (unit X9-1); check-unit (unit X9-2) passing an uncommitted unit that check refuses, taking only its cases without full coverage, refusing a kill outside the kill set, keeping the per-run check and repetition agreement, and refusing an unknown unit; a row's unit member, X9-4's moved F14 row in that unit's subset only and still required by check, an invalid unit refused (unit X9-4), and an X9-6 row in no unit's subset, required by check and refused as a check-unit unit (unit X9-6); the timing guard required and bounded where the observer tick is armed and refused elsewhere (unit X9-3); the release-absence scan; and X9-6's two-target gate over synthetic host sets (the union kill set covered from either target, a union point no target kills, a larger occurrence count in the other target, one clock epoch and one name per target, each target's per-run check and repetition agreement, and the command line needing both targets) and coverage reporting the uncovered union points before any run.", "before": "Exercise tools/check_crash_matrix.py on a disposable synthetic run pair against the repository's real scope registry: the registry read, kill-set derivation and point grammar; a complete agreeing pair passing; missing and extra runs; each run-field corruption (verdicts, labels, expected, script, commit, dirty worktree, profile, fixture labels, unverified kills, keys, postState); a claimed process death; noncanonical bytes, floats and duplicate keys; matrix corruptions (run bytes, kill set, durability, unregistered census point, release absence, limits, product); an uncovered new durability point; disagreeing repetitions; and the release-absence scan.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/916/description"}}], "passageSupersessions": [{"after": "The opensip doctor ingress (law X10 r3), beside the metadata ingress under the same process-custody request authority; doctor writes nothing durable. It makes exactly one producer call, observe_installation_for_doctor, the process's one attempt, read receipt and observation session, with the native account and the authenticated release and profile producers as its only inputs, and passes doctor's other checks as empty. A produced report or a report that cannot be produced is a kind doctor envelope carrying the assembled DoctorResult unchanged; an unreachable installation is a kind failure envelope with its law 468 item 6 row as termination and its one detail in errors (HOST.IO_FAILURE for the host I/O row). Exit codes derive from the class only. Remedies are the selected golden's where one exists and otherwise one fixed text per row. The STATE.SCHEMA_UNSUPPORTED row (law X3a item 5), which has no registered remedy, takes its own fixed text. The same kind failure envelope projects law X12 r3 item 7's policy-selection rows, whose CONFIG.INVALID and POLICY.IMPERATIVE_KEY_REFUSED details take the configuration module's remedies, and law X5 r3 item 5's replay-join rows, whose evidence.missing, evidence.regeneration-mismatch and EVALUATION.INPUT_REFUSED details take fact_admission's route remedies (item 5d, unit X5a); a detail with no remedy is a projection error.", "before": "The opensip doctor ingress (law X10 r3), beside the metadata ingress under the same process-custody request authority; doctor writes nothing durable. It makes exactly one producer call, observe_installation_for_doctor, the process's one attempt, read receipt and observation session, with the native account and the authenticated release and profile producers as its only inputs, and passes doctor's other checks as empty. A produced report or a report that cannot be produced is a kind doctor envelope carrying the assembled DoctorResult unchanged; an unreachable installation is a kind failure envelope with its law 468 item 6 row as termination and its one detail in errors (HOST.IO_FAILURE for the host I/O row). Exit codes derive from the class only. Remedies are the selected golden's where one exists and otherwise one fixed text per row. The STATE.SCHEMA_UNSUPPORTED row (law X3a item 5), which has no registered remedy, takes its own fixed text. The same kind failure envelope projects law X12 r3 item 7's policy-selection rows, whose CONFIG.INVALID and POLICY.IMPERATIVE_KEY_REFUSED details take the configuration module's remedies; a detail with no remedy is a projection error.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/111/description"}, "supersedes": {"parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "record": {"bytes": 124472, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/description-batch-d1/successor.json", "sha256": "2a2f94a61515c175efb3e88eb619626b0c0709682a8724a7fa119576f95a27c9"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/107/description"}}}, {"after": "Verify malformed provider joins, identity-valid but replay-invalid Runs, revoked authority and stale fences cannot produce acknowledged authoritative commits. It also holds the opaque API refusal suite's compile-fail driver (law X8 r3, unit X8a): a census of the cases under crates/host/tests/refusal/cases, each row naming its file, owning crate, type, owner unit, group and category (groups I, K and L from unit X8a, group D's AdmissionPermit, FinalGate and OperationGuard rows from unit X4a, the session types' rows of groups D, E, F and G from unit X3d-1, storage's facade rows of groups A to E, G and H for prepare_commit, PreparedCommit and PublishedCommit from unit X3d-2, group C's unnameable RunCandidateInputs and group E's ReplayedRun reuse from unit X5a, the authoritative projection's rows of groups A and B and AuthoritativeRun's capability rows of groups D, E and G from unit X7a, and group J's unresolved scenario modules, category TestSeam, from unit X8b), checked before anything compiles; a nested cargo check of opensip-host's release surface; each case compiled twice by the pinned rustc 1.95.0, the control compiling and the misuse under --cfg x8_misuse failing with exactly its one annotated error; the must-reject self-tests under crates/host/tests/refusal/selftest each rejected for the reason it names; the annotation grammar; the scenario-fixtures source pin over the workspace manifests, with its refusals; and law X8 r3 item 3b's source pin confining security's two-phase adapter traits, begin_journal_txn and seal_under_append_lock to security's defining module (with its root re-export) and storage's commit.rs, with its self-check (unit X3d-2). The behavioural cases that verify the first sentence are unit X8c's. They are in this file (law X8 r4 item 5, macOS only): B0 to B8, each a synthetic_ test on its own ScenarioHome driving the public facade (scenario::operation, CommitSession::open, replay_run, prepare_commit, publish and finish; B8 replay_run alone), each refusal from B1 to B6 asserting its outcome variant, exact row and public projection, that no PublishedCommit exists, and the carrier and evidence-ledger census after finish: B0, the lawful control, commits; B1 another Run's replay and B2 another evaluator closure are refused on the invariant row; B3 a replaced project marker or writer lease file and B5 a lineage node naming another generation are refused on the custody row at publish's first checkpoint; B4 a planted attempt row for the session's ExecutionId is ExistingAttempt with the requested binding and stays unchanged; B6 a helper process's live revocation of the core closure is TRUST.COMPONENT_REVOKED_DURING_OPERATION, while B7's unrelated revocation still commits; and B8 an altered claimed output never replays and leaves no namespace, carrier, lease, ledger or marker. Beside them are item 5a's revocation helper entry, storage's synthetic run candidate included by path (law X3d r8 item 13), and the run candidate's registry pin (host's and storage's schema sources list the same 48 files, in order).", "before": "Verify malformed provider joins, identity-valid but replay-invalid Runs, revoked authority and stale fences cannot produce acknowledged authoritative commits. It also holds the opaque API refusal suite's compile-fail driver (law X8 r3, unit X8a): a census of the cases under crates/host/tests/refusal/cases, each row naming its file, owning crate, type, owner unit, group and category (groups I, K and L from unit X8a, group D's AdmissionPermit, FinalGate and OperationGuard rows from unit X4a, and the session types' rows of groups D, E, F and G from unit X3d-1), checked before anything compiles; a nested cargo check of opensip-host's release surface; each case compiled twice by the pinned rustc 1.95.0, the control compiling and the misuse under --cfg x8_misuse failing with exactly its one annotated error; the must-reject self-tests under crates/host/tests/refusal/selftest each rejected for the reason it names; the annotation grammar; and the scenario-fixtures source pin over the workspace manifests, with its refusals. The behavioural cases that verify the first sentence are unit X8c's.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/143/description"}, "supersedes": {"parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "record": {"bytes": 124472, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/description-batch-d1/successor.json", "sha256": "2a2f94a61515c175efb3e88eb619626b0c0709682a8724a7fa119576f95a27c9"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/134/description"}}}, {"after": "Test support for installation readers on scratch account homes: publish a real P0 with the creator composition under a scratch H, then hold read fences on it through the observation session with the read receipt over signed on-disk test trees, and write further files below I as private creations. It also extends the published store's node chain and reselects the pair at a given generation, or rewrites the root node to name an unread predecessor (law X3a item 3's chains); replaces the P0 trust store with the signed accepted store for the same S and K, in place or as another writer would by atomic replacement with state.v1 renamed last, giving every new trust file the zero-rights owner allow (unit X4a); and restarts a creation or session refused only for churn in a shared ancestor above the scratch parent, at most eight attempts (F4). churned runs a whole test again on fresh fixtures only when it panicked in an attempt whose ancestor walks refused one or more captures as changed, every one above the private scratch parent by installation_root's record, at most eight attempts with every assertion strict (F5). It is the shared installation fixture of the HomeSource::Fixture gate, compiled under the joint predicate any(test, crash-matrix, scenario-fixtures) (law X8 r3 item 4b, unit X9-1), with its read-session helpers, trust rewrites and churned still cfg(test) inside it. ReadFixture::new_in puts the scratch root under the caller's scratch parent (law X8 r3 item 4c, unit X8b). InstallationAt names a published synthetic installation by its paths, for a matrix child or helper process that did not create it, and owns and removes nothing (law X9 r1 item 6, unit X9-1); ReadFixture::at gives a fixture's own. Its producers: writer, an ordinary writer on I by X1's composition (the write receipt over a signed test tree on its own test attempt, then the write gate over the scratch H by the production walk), and admitted_writer, the same keeping the refusal's stage and 468 item 6 row (unit X8b), which the crash-matrix settlement-sweep entry also admits (unit X9-2); current_trust_values, state.v1's K and revocation version read by name; install_accepted_trust, X4T-0's signed accepted store for S and K written in place with no fence, each fresh directory and file given the zero-rights owner allow; and the shared fenced state.v1 publisher publish_accepted_trust_fenced, under which a write gate takes I's fence by the production walk, the store is published by the trust owner's protocol, the gate's retained owner advances to the confirmed state.v1 and the fence is released, with publish_trust_fenced keeping the refusal by stage as FencedRefusal and its 468 item 6 row (unit X8b); both support surfaces call the last three. InstallationAt::operation is the one production-chain composition to a ProjectOperation that both support surfaces' operation entries call (law X8 r3 item 4c, unit X8b): X1's ordinary writer, X2b's root admission and tracking, X2c's first registration for a first-use candidate, then begin_operation (X2d's namespace and lease, X3a's endpoint, X4's lease-free first read and monitor, X3b's floor step and carrier start, X2e's handoff), with its RequestId and ExecutionId drawn from the host CSPRNG, each refusal its own row through X3d r6 item 9's maps, and the writer, with the fence, released on every refusal. It never opens the real account's I.", "before": "Test support for installation readers on scratch account homes: publish a real P0 with the creator composition under a scratch H, then hold read fences on it through the observation session with the read receipt over signed on-disk test trees, and write further files below I as private creations. It also extends the published store's node chain and reselects the pair at a given generation, or rewrites the root node to name an unread predecessor (law X3a item 3's chains); replaces the P0 trust store with the signed accepted store for the same S and K, in place or as another writer would by atomic replacement with state.v1 renamed last, giving every new trust file the zero-rights owner allow (unit X4a); and restarts a creation or session refused only for churn in a shared ancestor above the scratch parent, at most eight attempts (F4). It never opens the real account's I.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/396/description"}, "supersedes": {"parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "record": {"bytes": 124472, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/description-batch-d1/successor.json", "sha256": "2a2f94a61515c175efb3e88eb619626b0c0709682a8724a7fa119576f95a27c9"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/348/description"}}}, {"after": "The read side's observation path (law 458c r5 items 5, 6, 7 and 11). One session per process on one failure-latching ledger with the owner's limits; one observation per session. It reaches I through the durable write gate's own step 0 and step 1 code, shared rather than copied, with no barrier: a charged, retained, no-follow walk from the root to I under the read receipt's sealed lending, a positive absence only when the first missing suffix component is observed absent under its retained parent, and lifecycle.fence opened through the retained I. The lock wait repeats one nonblocking attempt on that same descriptor, paced at 25 ms, for at most 5 s and 201 attempts, all reserved before the first; busy retries, any other failure stops on the host I/O row, and a lock still busy at the bound is the busy row. Under the held fence it runs the receipt's rechecks and the full recheck set, reads the required members charged before they run (the fixed members up front, each lineage node before its own read), records missing, undecodable or misbound members as structural findings, and runs the same full recheck over every captured file, also after a failed read. The receipt's rechecks run again after that final recheck. Within the same one read (law X3a items 1 to 5, unit X3a-1): the selection pair must name the read receipt's selected core, or it is the core finding; the trust current record trust/stores/S/state.v1 is read once under CURRENT_STATE_CAP and decoded, and a record over its cap, undecodable or naming a store other than the pair's is the current-store finding; a lineage chain longer than its generation allows is a finding, while the session's own node limit is the budget row; and a complete I keeps the endpoint values (the pair, the marker, the node chain and the current record) for the selected store endpoint. retain (unit 458c-b2) requires I complete and keeps the observation, the receipt and the session's ledger in the ReadSession. Every refusal takes exactly the write gate's row. Never a write capability. Its consumers are the read session behind InstallationReadFence::acquire and doctor's installation check. Read-only recovery's admission takes only its member reads (law X6 r3 item 3 step 3, unit X6b): read_recovery_members reads them through the retained I with no fence, the fence member neither opened nor judged and every other member read as here, each charged before it runs, and returns every captured file with its full sample and the endpoint values, a structural finding on the incomplete row and an ordinary failure on its own row. Its one wired CLI path is opensip doctor (law X10), through doctor's installation check.", "before": "The read side's observation path (law 458c r5 items 5, 6, 7 and 11). One session per process on one failure-latching ledger with the owner's limits; one observation per session. It reaches I through the durable write gate's own step 0 and step 1 code, shared rather than copied, with no barrier: a charged, retained, no-follow walk from the root to I under the read receipt's sealed lending, a positive absence only when the first missing suffix component is observed absent under its retained parent, and lifecycle.fence opened through the retained I. The lock wait repeats one nonblocking attempt on that same descriptor, paced at 25 ms, for at most 5 s and 201 attempts, all reserved before the first; busy retries, any other failure stops on the host I/O row, and a lock still busy at the bound is the busy row. Under the held fence it runs the receipt's rechecks and the full recheck set, reads the required members charged before they run (the fixed members up front, each lineage node before its own read), records missing, undecodable or misbound members as structural findings, and runs the same full recheck over every captured file, also after a failed read. The receipt's rechecks run again after that final recheck. Within the same one read (law X3a items 1 to 5, unit X3a-1): the selection pair must name the read receipt's selected core, or it is the core finding; the trust current record trust/stores/S/state.v1 is read once under CURRENT_STATE_CAP and decoded, and a record over its cap, undecodable or naming a store other than the pair's is the current-store finding; a lineage chain longer than its generation allows is a finding, while the session's own node limit is the budget row; and a complete I keeps the endpoint values (the pair, the marker, the node chain and the current record) for the selected store endpoint. retain (unit 458c-b2) requires I complete and keeps the observation, the receipt and the session's ledger in the ReadSession. Every refusal takes exactly the write gate's row. Never a write capability. Its consumers are the read session behind InstallationReadFence::acquire and doctor's installation check. Its one wired CLI path is opensip doctor (law X10), through doctor's installation check.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/401/description"}, "supersedes": {"parent": {"bytes": 485705, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v122.json", "sha256": "69cf90db0ade09fc0f9536b3ef293d7de6aff68c7d3ae9a2aabfab4f6c244c7a"}, "record": {"bytes": 14785, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/description-batch-d2/successor.json", "sha256": "21e1ce5141834b8df62f73ffd84b59ccbf9262c1ed58828112929315ed6d048a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/372/description"}}}, {"after": "Namespace admission and leases (law X2 r8 item 7, unit X2d) under a held installation fence, on the fence holder's one ledger: the ordinary writer's law 468 gate (APPEND-WRITE or EXCLUSIVE) or the 458c read session (SHARED-READ only). For an Eligible root with its item 6a tracking observation (N from R0's ACTIVE row) or a project the same writer has just registered (N from R2's ACTIVE row, which must still classify Eligible), never from a caller: the root admission's recheck (chain, incarnation, marker, registry owner, v1 absence) with the tracking recheck, or the registered project's recheck; then I/host, I/host/projects and N confirmed private, exactly named and on their parents' device, and writer.lease and readers.lease opened no-follow and judged private, all retained, with no project lock taken. That NamespaceTarget is the seam where X3b's floor step runs (X3b r8 items 1 and 3, composed by X3b-3): fence held, R current, no project lock. The lease rechecks the subject, the directories and both retained carriers again, then, as one effect reserved before the first lock, takes S7's locks without blocking (SHARED-READ readers.lease LOCK_SH|LOCK_NB; APPEND-WRITE writer.lease LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB; EXCLUSIVE writer.lease then readers.lease, both LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB) on the retained carriers and rechecks each locked carrier's name binding and the directories, then the holder's full recheck. A busy carrier is the busy row; a busy or failed lock or any later refusal releases every lock already taken, in reverse order, while the fence is still held, and spends the gate or latches the session. Absent namespace objects are the incomplete row, custody is 468's custody row, a change is required-files-changed, and FirstUseCandidate is a broken composition. The result, FencedNamespace (the held lease, the ACTIVE row snapshot and the root admission), borrows the fence holder, has no upgrade and never releases the fence: it can be rechecked or dropped, which releases its lease, until item 7a's handoff (X2e). For X2e it lends the lease-free point (NamespaceTarget::lease_free: the holder, the retained N and N's id, where X4a's fenced trust admission runs before X3b's floor step) and moves its owners out (FencedNamespace::into_parts to NamespaceParts, the lease still held, nothing copied). After the fence's release (law X4 r7 items 4 and 8, unit X4a), recheck_namespace_at rechecks host, projects and N by name binding and privacy under the retained I, and HeldLease::recheck_held rechecks each held carrier's name binding and that its lock is still held: a fresh nonblocking EXCLUSIVE attempt on a new descriptor must be refused, and a granted probe is dropped at once and refused as S7's busy row, never a changed file. A cfg(test) seam releases the locks while keeping each carrier's judged sample (the lost-lock row). No fence-free lease (item 7's recovery exception is X6's). For X6 it lends its own carrier, lock and recheck primitives (X2d's open_carrier, required_directory, lock, recheck_binding and effect_lease, X4a's recheck_namespace_at, and their costs) and the two HeldLease values built outside take_lease: shared_reader, readers.lease alone locked shared, which read-only recovery's admission takes without the fence (unit X6b), and exclusive, writer.lease then readers.lease locked exclusive with no subject, which the settlement sweep takes under the held fence (law X6 r3 item 7, unit X6c), its drop releasing readers.lease before writer.lease; try_lock is lock for that sweep, a busy carrier returned as no lock so that nothing latches. Library only.", "before": "Namespace admission and leases (law X2 r8 item 7, unit X2d) under a held installation fence, on the fence holder's one ledger: the ordinary writer's law 468 gate (APPEND-WRITE or EXCLUSIVE) or the 458c read session (SHARED-READ only). For an Eligible root with its item 6a tracking observation (N from R0's ACTIVE row) or a project the same writer has just registered (N from R2's ACTIVE row, which must still classify Eligible), never from a caller: the root admission's recheck (chain, incarnation, marker, registry owner, v1 absence) with the tracking recheck, or the registered project's recheck; then I/host, I/host/projects and N confirmed private, exactly named and on their parents' device, and writer.lease and readers.lease opened no-follow and judged private, all retained, with no project lock taken. That NamespaceTarget is the seam where X3b's floor step runs (X3b r8 items 1 and 3, composed by X3b-3): fence held, R current, no project lock. The lease rechecks the subject, the directories and both retained carriers again, then, as one effect reserved before the first lock, takes S7's locks without blocking (SHARED-READ readers.lease LOCK_SH|LOCK_NB; APPEND-WRITE writer.lease LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB; EXCLUSIVE writer.lease then readers.lease, both LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB) on the retained carriers and rechecks each locked carrier's name binding and the directories, then the holder's full recheck. A busy carrier is the busy row; a busy or failed lock or any later refusal releases every lock already taken, in reverse order, while the fence is still held, and spends the gate or latches the session. Absent namespace objects are the incomplete row, custody is 468's custody row, a change is required-files-changed, and FirstUseCandidate is a broken composition. The result, FencedNamespace (the held lease, the ACTIVE row snapshot and the root admission), borrows the fence holder, has no upgrade and never releases the fence: it can be rechecked or dropped, which releases its lease, until item 7a's handoff (X2e). For X2e it lends the lease-free point (NamespaceTarget::lease_free: the holder, the retained N and N's id, where X4a's fenced trust admission runs before X3b's floor step) and moves its owners out (FencedNamespace::into_parts to NamespaceParts, the lease still held, nothing copied). After the fence's release (law X4 r7 items 4 and 8, unit X4a), recheck_namespace_at rechecks host, projects and N by name binding and privacy under the retained I, and HeldLease::recheck_held rechecks each held carrier's name binding and that its lock is still held: a fresh nonblocking EXCLUSIVE attempt on a new descriptor must be refused, and a granted probe is dropped at once and refused as S7's busy row, never a changed file. A cfg(test) seam releases the locks while keeping each carrier's judged sample (the lost-lock row). No fence-free lease (item 7's recovery exception is X6's). Library only.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/404/description"}, "supersedes": {"parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "record": {"bytes": 124472, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/description-batch-d1/successor.json", "sha256": "2a2f94a61515c175efb3e88eb619626b0c0709682a8724a7fa119576f95a27c9"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/356/description"}}}, {"after": "The checked operation handoff (law X2 r8 item 7a, unit X2e) composed with the grant journal's floor step, carrier start and end step (law X3b r10 items 1, 3, 3a and 4, unit X3b-3) and with X4a's live guards (law X4 r7 items 2 and 6), on one ordinary writer's law 468 gate under one held installation fence. In order: the selected store endpoint joined from the gate's one read (law X3a r5); the subject's namespace admitted (X2d, no lock); at the lease-free point, with R current and no project lock held, first X4a's gate and monitor created and X4T's fenced admission run as the monitor's first read on the gate's ledger, with X4B-b's bootstrap acceptance (from a BootstrapSource the write receipt lends) and its one confirming admission inside that read on F absent, the gate following each confirmed publication, and the trust store's handles retained; then I/trust confirmed private and N's absolute spelling admitted only if a no-follow status finds the retained N, then X3b's floor step on the write receipt's attempt ledger (X3b's operation ledger) followed by the gate's and the receipt's rechecks; the APPEND-WRITE or EXCLUSIVE lease (X2d); the join, rechecking the subject, the namespace, each locked carrier's binding and the gate, and deriving owner \u00a78's five-field binding {schemaVersion 1, N, S, G, K} from the ACTIVE row and the admitted endpoint; the transfer of the owners out of the fenced gate, never copied; X3b's carrier start on the attempt ledger (INIT creation when the floor step found INIT, the start against the floor step's observation, the append lock), then every owner rechecked again with a registered subject's namespace held to the started footprint; OperationGuard built under the fence and moved into the operation, starting its observer; and only then the fence's release, ending the gate's ledger. Any refusal spends the gate and releases the append lock, the lease and then the fence. ProjectOperation is not Clone, exported only because the public CommitSession::open takes it (law X3d r6 item 2, unit X3d-1), and cannot be built or read outside security. It holds X4a's OperationGuard, the started carrier and start tail, the held lease, the binding and the ACTIVE row snapshot, the subject's owners, the carrier place, the endpoint values and required files' full samples with the retained chain to I (no fence), and the write receipt, whose attempt ledger charges the operation's journal steps, each lent X4's Checkpoint by journal; charge, reserve_end_path_settlement and settle_end_path serve X3d-1's commit session (a step with no checkpoint; the one settlement reserve, spent with no checkpoint even on a closed ledger). It lends the receipt's core evaluator closure beside selected_core as a value (law X3d r8 item 13, unit X3d-3), with the joint-predicate read of its preimages behind CommitSession's test-support accessor. open_store_root, which CommitSession::store_root calls (law X3d r6 item 3 steps 4 to 6, unit X3d-2), opens I/stores/S as one charged step on the attempt ledger: stores and S each present, private, exactly named and on I's device, the store-instance.v1 marker under it the endpoint's admitted marker by device and inode, and its absolute spelling from H as walked naming the same directory, a change or a gone directory being required-files-changed; it is read-only and takes no fence. Its steps, store_root_in, run on any caller's ledger and are shared with read-only recovery's admission (unit X6b), which also shares namespace_spelling, and with the settlement sweep (unit X6c). Its end path drops the guard (its observer stops) and the append lock and releases the lease; the end step is then not entered when the owner reports an uncertain journal outcome or when the attempt ledger is closed (law X3b r10 item 4): ProjectOperation::end returns NotEntered, with no fence walk, read or copy and nothing disclosed; otherwise end_entered, on the attempt ledger, retakes the fence by the shared charged walk, no-follow fence open and S7's bounded wait, requires the walked I to be the retained I and trust, host, projects and N still bound by name, runs, when the owner reports X3d's CarrierCapacityExhausted with every journal outcome certain, X3b-4's rollover (law X3b r10 item 13) under its own EXCLUSIVE lease, then the end step's probe, read and floor copy, and releases the fence; a failure is a value that never rewrites the operation's outcome, and a rollover outcome is returned for disclosure on its own row. It places x3d.finish's lease-release and end-step points, and the end step's fence wait and release run inside law X9 r1 item 5's x2.fence.end scope. CarrierPlace, built only here, is the one input of the carrier location's production constructor for an operation (CarrierLocation::admitted; read-only recovery's location is built from X6b's own place). Rows: the endpoint's 468c row, X2's item 8 rows, X3b's item 8 carrier rows, the budget row, X4T's trust rows through trust_termination, X4's fail-stop row and the observer's host I/O row. No read-session handoff. Library only.", "before": "The checked operation handoff (law X2 r8 item 7a, unit X2e) composed with the grant journal's floor step, carrier start and end step (law X3b r10 items 1, 3, 3a and 4, unit X3b-3) and with X4a's live guards (law X4 r7 items 2 and 6), on one ordinary writer's law 468 gate under one held installation fence. In order: the selected store endpoint joined from the gate's one read (law X3a r5); the subject's namespace admitted (X2d, no lock); at the lease-free point, with R current and no project lock held, first X4a's gate and monitor created and X4T's fenced admission run as the monitor's first read on the gate's ledger, with X4B-b's bootstrap acceptance (from a BootstrapSource the write receipt lends) and its one confirming admission inside that read on F absent, the gate following each confirmed publication, and the trust store's handles retained; then I/trust confirmed private and N's absolute spelling admitted only if a no-follow status finds the retained N, then X3b's floor step on the write receipt's attempt ledger (X3b's operation ledger) followed by the gate's and the receipt's rechecks; the APPEND-WRITE or EXCLUSIVE lease (X2d); the join, rechecking the subject, the namespace, each locked carrier's binding and the gate, and deriving owner \u00a78's five-field binding {schemaVersion 1, N, S, G, K} from the ACTIVE row and the admitted endpoint; the transfer of the owners out of the fenced gate, never copied; X3b's carrier start on the attempt ledger (INIT creation when the floor step found INIT, the start against the floor step's observation, the append lock), then every owner rechecked again with a registered subject's namespace held to the started footprint; OperationGuard built under the fence and moved into the operation, starting its observer; and only then the fence's release, ending the gate's ledger. Any refusal spends the gate and releases the append lock, the lease and then the fence. ProjectOperation is not Clone, exported only because the public CommitSession::open takes it (law X3d r6 item 2, unit X3d-1), and cannot be built or read outside security. It holds X4a's OperationGuard, the started carrier and start tail, the held lease, the binding and the ACTIVE row snapshot, the subject's owners, the carrier place, the endpoint values and required files' full samples with the retained chain to I (no fence), and the write receipt, whose attempt ledger charges the operation's journal steps, each lent X4's Checkpoint by journal; charge, reserve_end_path_settlement and settle_end_path serve X3d-1's commit session (a step with no checkpoint; the one settlement reserve, spent with no checkpoint even on a closed ledger). Its end path drops the guard (its observer stops) and the append lock and releases the lease; the end step is then not entered when the owner reports an uncertain journal outcome or when the attempt ledger is closed (law X3b r10 item 4): ProjectOperation::end returns NotEntered, with no fence walk, read or copy and nothing disclosed; otherwise end_entered, on the attempt ledger, retakes the fence by the shared charged walk, no-follow fence open and S7's bounded wait, requires the walked I to be the retained I and trust, host, projects and N still bound by name, runs, when the owner reports X3d's CarrierCapacityExhausted with every journal outcome certain, X3b-4's rollover (law X3b r10 item 13) under its own EXCLUSIVE lease, then the end step's probe, read and floor copy, and releases the fence; a failure is a value that never rewrites the operation's outcome, and a rollover outcome is returned for disclosure on its own row. It places x3d.finish's lease-release and end-step points. CarrierPlace, built only here, is the one input of the carrier location's production constructor. Rows: the endpoint's 468c row, X2's item 8 rows, X3b's item 8 carrier rows, the budget row, X4T's trust rows through trust_termination, X4's fail-stop row and the observer's host I/O row. No read-session handoff. Library only.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/408/description"}, "supersedes": {"parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "record": {"bytes": 124472, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/description-batch-d1/successor.json", "sha256": "2a2f94a61515c175efb3e88eb619626b0c0709682a8724a7fa119576f95a27c9"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/360/description"}}}, {"after": "Check X2e with X3b-3 on scratch homes with a creator-published P0 whose trust store is replaced by X4T-0's signed accepted store for the same S and K, ordinary writers over 462's signed test trees and a registered scratch project (no real home, installation or Git), each handoff run through a begin_test adapter on a scripted guard seam (a scripted monitor clock, observer ticks on request, a fixed invocation), other holders simulated by nonblocking flocks on separate descriptors and S7's wait run on a scripted clock: a fresh registration's APPEND-WRITE handoff (fence released, writer.lease held and readers.lease free, the INIT floor, the created carrier and its COMMITTED 0 witness, owner \u00a78's binding equal to the ACTIVE row's N and the endpoint's S, G and K, then an end step leaving the floor unchanged and nothing locked); an Eligible root's EXCLUSIVE handoff, a certain append leaving the floor until the end step copies it forward, and the next writer's start at that tail; the floor step written before the lease when EXCLUSIVE then refuses busy, with no carrier created; a busy writer.lease skipping the floor step and refusing busy with nothing written; a floorLost quarantine refused before any lease with nothing written; a budget cut at the handoff's last charge, after the carrier start, releasing the lease and the fence, with the next writer starting on what it left; an uncertain outcome releasing the lease with no fence taken and no copy, and the next writer's floor step copying forward; a busy fence at the end waited for within S7's bound, then disclosed as the busy row with the lease already released; a closed attempt ledger not entering the end step (no walk while the fence is held elsewhere, no copy, nothing disclosed), and the next writer's floor step copying forward; a moved namespace refusing the end copy as required-files-changed with the fence released; a capacity exhaustion reported at an exhausted tail rolling the generation over under the retaken fence (TERMINAL closing generation 1, witness COMMITTED at generation 2) and copying the floor to generation 2's empty tail, with the next writer starting there; the rollover's outcome returned whatever the attempt-ledger charge returned, with the copy's result beside it: a failed reservation (Refused on the budget, no copy), a writer.lease taken by another writer before the rollover (Skipped, then the copy Skipped), a floor I/O refusal (Refused on the host I/O row, then the copy's host I/O failure), an undetermined OPEN at X3b-4's test point (Undetermined on the durability row, no copy), and a copy failure after Rolled (Rolled kept, generation 2 open, the copy's host I/O failure); and a source pin that carrier_floor builds a carrier location only in its cfg(test) constructor and that the production constructor for an operation (admitted) takes this module's carrier place. It also includes operation_live_tests.rs (X4a through this handoff) and commit_session_tests.rs (X3d-1) as child modules that reuse its scratch-home helpers.", "before": "Check X2e with X3b-3 on scratch homes with a creator-published P0 whose trust store is replaced by X4T-0's signed accepted store for the same S and K, ordinary writers over 462's signed test trees and a registered scratch project (no real home, installation or Git), each handoff run through a begin_test adapter on a scripted guard seam (a scripted monitor clock, observer ticks on request, a fixed invocation), other holders simulated by nonblocking flocks on separate descriptors and S7's wait run on a scripted clock: a fresh registration's APPEND-WRITE handoff (fence released, writer.lease held and readers.lease free, the INIT floor, the created carrier and its COMMITTED 0 witness, owner \u00a78's binding equal to the ACTIVE row's N and the endpoint's S, G and K, then an end step leaving the floor unchanged and nothing locked); an Eligible root's EXCLUSIVE handoff, a certain append leaving the floor until the end step copies it forward, and the next writer's start at that tail; the floor step written before the lease when EXCLUSIVE then refuses busy, with no carrier created; a busy writer.lease skipping the floor step and refusing busy with nothing written; a floorLost quarantine refused before any lease with nothing written; a budget cut at the handoff's last charge, after the carrier start, releasing the lease and the fence, with the next writer starting on what it left; an uncertain outcome releasing the lease with no fence taken and no copy, and the next writer's floor step copying forward; a busy fence at the end waited for within S7's bound, then disclosed as the busy row with the lease already released; a closed attempt ledger not entering the end step (no walk while the fence is held elsewhere, no copy, nothing disclosed), and the next writer's floor step copying forward; a moved namespace refusing the end copy as required-files-changed with the fence released; a capacity exhaustion reported at an exhausted tail rolling the generation over under the retaken fence (TERMINAL closing generation 1, witness COMMITTED at generation 2) and copying the floor to generation 2's empty tail, with the next writer starting there; the rollover's outcome returned whatever the attempt-ledger charge returned, with the copy's result beside it: a failed reservation (Refused on the budget, no copy), a writer.lease taken by another writer before the rollover (Skipped, then the copy Skipped), a floor I/O refusal (Refused on the host I/O row, then the copy's host I/O failure), an undetermined OPEN at X3b-4's test point (Undetermined on the durability row, no copy), and a copy failure after Rolled (Rolled kept, generation 2 open, the copy's host I/O failure); and a source pin that the carrier location's only production constructor takes this module's carrier place. It also includes operation_live_tests.rs (X4a through this handoff) and commit_session_tests.rs (X3d-1) as child modules that reuse its scratch-home helpers.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/409/description"}, "supersedes": {"parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "record": {"bytes": 124472, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/description-batch-d1/successor.json", "sha256": "2a2f94a61515c175efb3e88eb619626b0c0709682a8724a7fa119576f95a27c9"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/361/description"}}}, {"after": "Produce the read side's premise receipt (law 458c r5 items 1 to 4): the process's one attempt, the actor, InitialCore and InitialPlatform, exactly the creator's producers, with no intent, storage choice, disclosure, effect or fence. Law X1 r1 items 1 and 2 type it by purpose: the one producer composition makes PlatformReceipt<Read> (ReadPremiseReceipt, produce_read_platform) or PlatformReceipt<Write> (WritePlatformReceipt, produce_write_platform); Read and Write are sealed, neither receipt converts into the other, and a receipt whose actor, core or platform belongs to another attempt is the invariant row. The read receipt is private and not Clone, holds its own attempt, and lends only the sealed ReadPremiseQualification that InitialPlatform alone implements: the check that a filesystem is H's and the omission premise with 465 item 4's scope, no barrier policy. The write receipt is likewise private, not Clone and holds its own attempt; its lending is the durable write gate's sealed DurableBarrierQualification, beside which it lends the running core's embedded bootstrap source to the fenced first read alone (law X4B r5 item 2, unit X4B-b), charge, one charged step on its attempt ledger with that lending (unit X2e), charge_guarded, the same step with the actor, core and platform rechecks lent beside it for the checkpoint's guard (law X4 r7 item 4, unit X4a), and the attempt ledger's one end-path settlement reserve and its single spend, on the commit session's way only (law X3d r6 item 8, unit X3d-1). Both purposes expose recheck, the actor, core and platform rechecks on the receipt's own attempt, and selected_core, the selected core's closure and state writer K as values only (law X3a item 1). Both also expose core_evaluator_closure, the selected core's evaluator closure (contract successor EC1) derived from the same authenticated inventory, as a value only (law X3d r8 item 13, unit X3d-3), and, only under the joint predicate (test, crash-matrix or scenario-fixtures), the read of its preimages behind CommitSession's test-support accessor. Every refusal ends in its law 468 item 6 row through 468c's maps. InstallationReadFence::acquire and doctor's installation check pass it to the observation session. The ordinary writer's admission consumes the write receipt (law X1), and the operation handoff carries it (unit X2e). Its one wired CLI path is opensip doctor (law X10), through doctor's installation check; no CLI command reaches a write receipt.", "before": "Produce the read side's premise receipt (law 458c r5 items 1 to 4): the process's one attempt, the actor, InitialCore and InitialPlatform, exactly the creator's producers, with no intent, storage choice, disclosure, effect or fence. Law X1 r1 items 1 and 2 type it by purpose: the one producer composition makes PlatformReceipt<Read> (ReadPremiseReceipt, produce_read_platform) or PlatformReceipt<Write> (WritePlatformReceipt, produce_write_platform); Read and Write are sealed, neither receipt converts into the other, and a receipt whose actor, core or platform belongs to another attempt is the invariant row. The read receipt is private and not Clone, holds its own attempt, and lends only the sealed ReadPremiseQualification that InitialPlatform alone implements: the check that a filesystem is H's and the omission premise with 465 item 4's scope, no barrier policy. The write receipt is likewise private, not Clone and holds its own attempt; its lending is the durable write gate's sealed DurableBarrierQualification, beside which it lends the running core's embedded bootstrap source to the fenced first read alone (law X4B r5 item 2, unit X4B-b), charge, one charged step on its attempt ledger with that lending (unit X2e), charge_guarded, the same step with the actor, core and platform rechecks lent beside it for the checkpoint's guard (law X4 r7 item 4, unit X4a), and the attempt ledger's one end-path settlement reserve and its single spend, on the commit session's way only (law X3d r6 item 8, unit X3d-1). Both purposes expose recheck, the actor, core and platform rechecks on the receipt's own attempt, and selected_core, the selected core's closure and state writer K as values only (law X3a item 1). Every refusal ends in its law 468 item 6 row through 468c's maps. InstallationReadFence::acquire and doctor's installation check pass it to the observation session. The ordinary writer's admission consumes the write receipt (law X1), and the operation handoff carries it (unit X2e). Its one wired CLI path is opensip doctor (law X10), through doctor's installation check; no CLI command reaches a write receipt.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/417/description"}, "supersedes": {"parent": {"bytes": 485705, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v122.json", "sha256": "69cf90db0ade09fc0f9536b3ef293d7de6aff68c7d3ae9a2aabfab4f6c244c7a"}, "record": {"bytes": 14785, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/description-batch-d2/successor.json", "sha256": "21e1ce5141834b8df62f73ffd84b59ccbf9262c1ed58828112929315ed6d048a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/388/description"}}}, {"after": "Own grant journal append/witness/high-water admission and versioned SEAL carrier mapping; preserve append-only sequence/cap/restore rules and keep this carrier distinct from lifecycle transition journals; own grant-generation succession (law X3b r8, unit X3b-4): per-generation prefix admission in which a TERMINAL closes its generation and only cause grantGenerationClosure justifies the next, the SEAL ceiling predicate seal_fits, and, in the macOS carrier_floor child, the predecessor check, the successor rule and the rollover; re-export the carrier's operation-handoff (X3b-3 with X2e) and commit-session (X3d-1) surface and its private file protocol, X6a's read-only carrier recovery capture (recover_carrier and RecoveryQuery) for the recovery admission and the public, inert CarrierObservation and CarrierKind that storage's recover reads (law X6 r3, unit X6b), and, under cfg(test) or the test-only crash-matrix feature only, X9-1's carrier fixtures (law X9 r1 item 6).", "before": "Own grant journal append/witness/high-water admission and versioned SEAL carrier mapping; preserve append-only sequence/cap/restore rules and keep this carrier distinct from lifecycle transition journals; own grant-generation succession (law X3b r8, unit X3b-4): per-generation prefix admission in which a TERMINAL closes its generation and only cause grantGenerationClosure justifies the next, the SEAL ceiling predicate seal_fits, and, in the macOS carrier_floor child, the predecessor check, the successor rule and the rollover; re-export the carrier's operation-handoff (X3b-3 with X2e) and commit-session (X3d-1) surface and its private file protocol.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/435/description"}, "supersedes": {"parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "record": {"bytes": 124472, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/description-batch-d1/successor.json", "sha256": "2a2f94a61515c175efb3e88eb619626b0c0709682a8724a7fa119576f95a27c9"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/383/description"}}}, {"after": "The grant-journal append protocol (law X3b r6 items 5, 6, 8 and 9; unit X3b-2; X3b r8 item 5a's capacity window, unit X3b-4), as carrier_floor.rs's macOS child module append, under the operation lease the caller holds. JournalAppendLock (level 4) is one in-process mutex per carrier, made only from the CarrierStart it consumes, holding the committed tail its appends follow and two latches that are never reset: REV (after which no SEAL or RA is appended, S6) and undetermined. Level 3: begin opens the carrier read-write through the retained namespace (the same file before and after), sets the WAL and fullfsync writer pragmas with busy_timeout 0, takes BEGIN IMMEDIATE (busy is LEDGER.BUSY_TIMEOUT at once and holds nothing, F06), re-admits the exact definitions, the format row and the SHA-256(N) binding in that transaction, and confirms the tail is the lock's and the witness names it (COMMITTED at the tail, or a PENDING beyond it that a certain failure left; on a closed generation, item 4a's effective tail), refusing while level 4 is held or after an undetermined outcome. Level 4 is only tried after level 3, never waited on; the held value is the borrow X4's checkpoint receives, and dropping it releases level 4 and then rolls back any open level 3. One append, with both witness publications, their confirmations and the commit reserved before the first effect: build the record at tail + 1 (SEAL, REV, CLN and RA as closed schema-3 bodies through the existing parser, TERMINAL as the frozen recordSchema-1 body with cause grantGenerationClosure, built only through append_terminal, which only the rollover calls; the physical operationRef grammar, the domain-framed body hash, chain_law 1 from the genesis value), check item 5a's capacity table before any effect (nothing after a TERMINAL, a SEAL only while seal_fits holds, RA, REV and CLN refused as GenerationFull at tail 9007199254740990, a TERMINAL only in its window), witness PENDING by the file protocol, INSERT and COMMIT, witness COMMITTED. A SEAL keeps level 4 held after its commit until released (r5); other records release it on return. Failures before visibility are certain; a failure after the PENDING rename, a failed or lost COMMIT, and any failure after the commit are durability-undetermined: the lock latches and refuses every further effect, nothing follows in the operation, and the next writer's floor step and start reconcile (X3b r9 item 5). Refusals name item 8's rows, GenerationFull the busy row, and a broken composition (wrong carrier, lock order, after REV, capacity, the terminal slot, the SEAL ceiling, an unbuildable record) the invariant row. Nothing here takes the fence or a lease or writes trust state; grant-generation rollover itself is carrier_rollover.rs's. Its own crash and fault hooks (AppendStep, Fault) exist only under cfg(test); law X9's barrier points beside them in the caller's x3b.append.<kind> scope (begin, level-four, built, the witness-pending and witness-committed publications, insert, the fallible commit standing in for a COMMIT that fails before or after it lands, and the two releases; law X9 r1 item 5, unit X9-1) exist only under the test-only crash-matrix feature, and without it a point compiles to nothing and a scope or fallible point to the code it wraps. Library only.", "before": "The grant-journal append protocol (law X3b r6 items 5, 6, 8 and 9; unit X3b-2; X3b r8 item 5a's capacity window, unit X3b-4), as carrier_floor.rs's macOS child module append, under the operation lease the caller holds. JournalAppendLock (level 4) is one in-process mutex per carrier, made only from the CarrierStart it consumes, holding the committed tail its appends follow and two latches that are never reset: REV (after which no SEAL or RA is appended, S6) and undetermined. Level 3: begin opens the carrier read-write through the retained namespace (the same file before and after), sets the WAL and fullfsync writer pragmas with busy_timeout 0, takes BEGIN IMMEDIATE (busy is LEDGER.BUSY_TIMEOUT at once and holds nothing, F06), re-admits the exact definitions, the format row and the SHA-256(N) binding in that transaction, and confirms the tail is the lock's and the witness names it (COMMITTED at the tail, or a PENDING beyond it that a certain failure left; on a closed generation, item 4a's effective tail), refusing while level 4 is held or after an undetermined outcome. Level 4 is only tried after level 3, never waited on; the held value is the borrow X4's checkpoint receives, and dropping it releases level 4 and then rolls back any open level 3. One append, with both witness publications, their confirmations and the commit reserved before the first effect: build the record at tail + 1 (SEAL, REV, CLN and RA as closed schema-3 bodies through the existing parser, TERMINAL as the frozen recordSchema-1 body with cause grantGenerationClosure, built only through append_terminal, which only the rollover calls; the physical operationRef grammar, the domain-framed body hash, chain_law 1 from the genesis value), check item 5a's capacity table before any effect (nothing after a TERMINAL, a SEAL only while seal_fits holds, RA, REV and CLN refused as GenerationFull at tail 9007199254740990, a TERMINAL only in its window), witness PENDING by the file protocol, INSERT and COMMIT, witness COMMITTED. A SEAL keeps level 4 held after its commit until released (r5); other records release it on return. Failures before visibility are certain; a failure after the PENDING rename, a failed or lost COMMIT, and any failure after the commit are durability-undetermined: the lock latches and refuses every further effect, nothing follows in the operation, and the next writer's floor step and start reconcile (X3b r9 item 5). Refusals name item 8's rows, GenerationFull the busy row, and a broken composition (wrong carrier, lock order, after REV, capacity, the terminal slot, the SEAL ceiling, an unbuildable record) the invariant row. Nothing here takes the fence or a lease or writes trust state; grant-generation rollover itself is carrier_rollover.rs's. Crash and fault hooks exist only under cfg(test). Library only.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/438/description"}, "supersedes": {"parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "record": {"bytes": 124472, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/description-batch-d1/successor.json", "sha256": "2a2f94a61515c175efb3e88eb619626b0c0709682a8724a7fa119576f95a27c9"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/386/description"}}}, {"after": "The grant-journal carrier floor step and floor-first carrier creation (law X3b r4 items 2, 3, 3a and 5; unit X3b-1a). The private file protocol publishes a witness or floor by a fresh exclusive private temporary name, write and read-back with F_FULLFSYNC, rename, directory barrier, and a no-follow reopen confirming the same file and bytes. The floor step probes writer.lease without waiting and releases it at once (busy skips and writes nothing), observes the floor under trust/carrier-floors, the carrier and the witness read-only, classifies the carrier by format before any floor decision (inherited, footprint and project-binding refusals), decides by the floor table (INIT, resume, copy forward never down, floorLost, uncertainTailLoss, regression, and reconcile_witness as a read-only decision), and writes only the floor, with no project lock held. Creation re-observes INIT, creates the database exclusively and private, sets the WAL and fullfsync writer pragmas, applies the DDL and publishes the format row in one BEGIN IMMEDIATE transaction, takes the file and namespace barriers, and writes the witness COMMITTED 0. Generation succession (X3b r8 item 4a; unit X3b-4) is here too: the committed tail carries its TERMINAL flag, the tail query every writer open uses checks that a newer generation's predecessor is a TERMINAL of the generation before, succession applies the successor rule over one tail and one witness (OPEN on a closed generation, NoSuccessor at the last generation), and the floor follows its copy tail with the open-successor exception. Each refusal names its item 8 row, and the composition owner maps it. Its macOS child modules are start (carrier_start.rs: the carrier start and the end step), append (carrier_append.rs: the append protocol), rollover (carrier_rollover.rs: grant-generation rollover), operation (carrier_operation.rs: the journal half of the operation handoff, X3b-3 with X2e), recovery_capture (recovery_capture.rs: the read-only carrier recovery capture, law X6 r2 item 1, unit X6a, which reuses this file's tail query, successor rule and floor comparison and writes nothing) and recovery_location (recovery_location.rs: the recovery side of that capture and its public CarrierObservation, law X6 r3, unit X6b). The operation child's CarrierLocation::admitted, built from X2e's confirmed CarrierPlace (item 11), and the recovery_location child's CarrierLocation::recovered, built only from the RecoveryPlace a recovery admission confirmed (law X6 r3 item 12), are CarrierLocation's only production constructors. It also exports publish_private_file, through which the trust current pointer is replaced (law X4T r9 item 7), and project_key_digest for X3d-1's commit session. Library only.", "before": "The grant-journal carrier floor step and floor-first carrier creation (law X3b r4 items 2, 3, 3a and 5; unit X3b-1a). The private file protocol publishes a witness or floor by a fresh exclusive private temporary name, write and read-back with F_FULLFSYNC, rename, directory barrier, and a no-follow reopen confirming the same file and bytes. The floor step probes writer.lease without waiting and releases it at once (busy skips and writes nothing), observes the floor under trust/carrier-floors, the carrier and the witness read-only, classifies the carrier by format before any floor decision (inherited, footprint and project-binding refusals), decides by the floor table (INIT, resume, copy forward never down, floorLost, uncertainTailLoss, regression, and reconcile_witness as a read-only decision), and writes only the floor, with no project lock held. Creation re-observes INIT, creates the database exclusively and private, sets the WAL and fullfsync writer pragmas, applies the DDL and publishes the format row in one BEGIN IMMEDIATE transaction, takes the file and namespace barriers, and writes the witness COMMITTED 0. Generation succession (X3b r8 item 4a; unit X3b-4) is here too: the committed tail carries its TERMINAL flag, the tail query every writer open uses checks that a newer generation's predecessor is a TERMINAL of the generation before, succession applies the successor rule over one tail and one witness (OPEN on a closed generation, NoSuccessor at the last generation), and the floor follows its copy tail with the open-successor exception. Each refusal names its item 8 row, and the composition owner maps it. Its macOS child modules are start (carrier_start.rs: the carrier start and the end step), append (carrier_append.rs: the append protocol), rollover (carrier_rollover.rs: grant-generation rollover) and operation (carrier_operation.rs: the journal half of the operation handoff, X3b-3 with X2e), whose CarrierLocation::admitted, built from X2e's confirmed CarrierPlace, is CarrierLocation's only production constructor (item 11). It also exports publish_private_file, through which the trust current pointer is replaced (law X4T r9 item 7), and project_key_digest for X3d-1's commit session. Library only.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/441/description"}, "supersedes": {"parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "record": {"bytes": 124472, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/description-batch-d1/successor.json", "sha256": "2a2f94a61515c175efb3e88eb619626b0c0709682a8724a7fa119576f95a27c9"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/389/description"}}}, {"after": "Generate a signed accepted trust store for tests only (law X4T r4 item 13, X4T-0), declared solely under the shared fixture gates' one joint predicate any(test, feature = crash-matrix, feature = scenario-fixtures) (law X8 r3 item 4b, unit X9-1); its one accessor outside test code is root_payload's accepted_store_files, through which the shared installation fixture installs or publishes the store. It builds a real P0 publication with the product initial_publication producer, then one retained publication at revision 2. On macOS, wherever one acceptance reaches the whole requested six-role assignment (law X4B r5 item 9, unit X4B-c), that publication is X4B-a's producer's: Knobs::for_roles runs role_machine::bootstrap_sequence, the producer's own item 4 dispatch, over the store-wide root-expired, TR-INDEX catalog-expired and stale-list inputs with Revoked set per role (TR-PROFILE and TR-REPAIR uncarried; TR-BUNDLE, TR-CORE and TR-INDEX carried; TR-COMPONENT carried only with a component manifest); the module signs the payload with the public test-only quorum seeds (the root chain, the final root expired when required; the list, the caller's entries plus the third signer of each revoked role's document, issued more than 90 days before tEval when stale; a TR-INDEX catalog, expired when required, with the component manifest's release row when one is carried; 463h's component manifest under TR-COMPONENT unless that role is uncarried; a TR-BUNDLE bootstrap manifest; a synthetic TR-CORE inventory binding only the embedded bootstrap pair; and root 1's embedded binding), and every retained-phase record, the descriptor and state.v1 are build_over_p0's over that P0, placed where publish writes them (each file at its TrustWrite::path, state.v1 by name). A non-head chain, replaced signers, no reaching inputs (QuorumLost, an accepted TR-PROFILE or TR-REPAIR, an uncarried TR-BUNDLE, TR-CORE or TR-INDEX, a time state no store-wide input gives), a producer refusal or a role ending elsewhere (a caller's list entry revoking a role requested Trusted) leaves the module's own construction, as on every other target: a recorded root chain of n roots (default one; each later root rotates ROOT to the next public key set and is signed by both, and the head is root n), and a revocation list, catalog and payload manifest signed under the head with the public test-only quorum seeds; the payload metadata closure, a bootstrap operation, an S4 evaluation input as time evidence with a signed time source; ordinary root and catalog/revocation admission records and a list revocation history; an S4 clock-write event and, per role, an accepted EV-PRESENT-PAYLOAD role event plus one conditioning event for an expired, stale-revocation, quorum-lost or revoked target, with their role-change and publication-event rows; and the retained-phase capsule and descriptor. AcceptedStore::produced says which route built the store. Spec also chooses the store id S and the list's revocation version (X4a), and, for X4T-a3's negative cases, substitute root or list signers and a closure rootChain that ends on another envelope of the head, is short or is empty. Every record is canonically encoded and admitted by its closed shape before it is written (except the empty closure rootChain, written past its shape check, whose refusal is the shape's); files are content-addressed in the trust locators. No production path reaches it; X4B-a's trust_bootstrap is the product producer of these kinds.", "before": "Generate a signed accepted trust store for tests only (law X4T r4 item 13, X4T-0), declared solely under cfg(test). It builds a real P0 publication with the product initial_publication producer, then one retained publication at revision 2: a recorded root chain of n roots (default one; each later root rotates ROOT to the next public key set and is signed by both, and the head is root n), and a revocation list, catalog and payload manifest signed under the head with the public test-only quorum seeds; the payload metadata closure, a bootstrap operation, an S4 evaluation input as time evidence with a signed time source; ordinary root and catalog/revocation admission records and a list revocation history; an S4 clock-write event and, per role, an accepted EV-PRESENT-PAYLOAD role event plus one conditioning event for an expired, stale-revocation, quorum-lost or revoked target, with their role-change and publication-event rows; and the retained-phase capsule and descriptor. Spec also chooses the store id S and the list's revocation version (X4a), and, for X4T-a3's negative cases, substitute root or list signers and a closure rootChain that ends on another envelope of the head, is short or is empty. Every record is canonically encoded and admitted by its closed shape before it is written; files are content-addressed in the trust locators. No production path reaches it; X4B-a's trust_bootstrap is the product producer of these kinds.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/468/description"}, "supersedes": {"parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "record": {"bytes": 124472, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/description-batch-d1/successor.json", "sha256": "2a2f94a61515c175efb3e88eb619626b0c0709682a8724a7fa119576f95a27c9"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/411/description"}}}, {"after": "Check the signed accepted-store generator: every immutable file is canonical and addressed by its digest and each kind passes its closed shape; the root, catalog and revocation envelopes carry real ed25519 quorum signatures over their bodies by their owning role; current_record_bindings::bind, publication_events::bind_events (from P0's roles and event head) and bind_retained_head (through the capsule clock) accept the generated store for state schemas 1 and 2; a recorded rotation of two and three roots lists the whole chain with the head last, each link signed by both key sets and the list signed under root n, and the binders accept it; chosen role states publish with their accepted standing, INDEX-only catalog and revoked or quorum-lost evidence; capture_p2 accepts the written store through a supplied native fence on a scratch installation; a store with no accepted role is not offered; on macOS, ten requests one acceptance reaches (the default, no component manifest, an expired catalog, an expired root, a stale list, an expired catalog with a stale list, a revoked core, an expired root with component and core revoked, a revoked bundle on a two-root chain, and a successor list on another store at schema 2) are built by X4B's producer, each role's events exactly its item 4 sequence in order, accepted.by its EV-PRESENT-PAYLOAD, revokedBy its EV-REVOKE or null, uncarried roles blank and the binders accepting the store, with the default store's evalHighWater W and lastAccepted A (law X4B r5 items 9 and 10, unit X4B-c); ten requests no acceptance reaches (among them QuorumLost, all six roles trusted, a lone expired core, an uncarried index, the X4T-a3 negative stores and a list revoking the running core) keep the construction in their requested states; a source pin limits build_over_p0's callers to trust_bootstrap.rs and this generator; and a source pin shows the constructor is declared only under the shared fixture gates' one joint predicate any(test, feature = crash-matrix, feature = scenario-fixtures) (law X8 r3 item 4b, unit X9-1) and named by no other source file except X9-1's site pin crash_matrix_sites.rs, X4T-a's current_trust_admission_tests.rs and X4T-b's floor_publication_tests.rs, the two test files each checked to be included only under cfg(test).", "before": "Check the signed accepted-store generator: every immutable file is canonical and addressed by its digest and each kind passes its closed shape; the root, catalog and revocation envelopes carry real ed25519 quorum signatures over their bodies by their owning role; current_record_bindings::bind, publication_events::bind_events (from P0's roles and event head) and bind_retained_head (through the capsule clock) accept the generated store for state schemas 1 and 2; a recorded rotation of two and three roots lists the whole chain with the head last, each link signed by both key sets and the list signed under root n, and the binders accept it; chosen role states publish with their accepted standing, INDEX-only catalog and revoked or quorum-lost evidence; capture_p2 accepts the written store through a supplied native fence on a scratch installation; a store with no accepted role is not offered; and a source pin shows the constructor is declared only under cfg(test) and named by no other source file except X4T-a's current_trust_admission_tests.rs and X4T-b's floor_publication_tests.rs, each checked to be included only under cfg(test).", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/469/description"}, "supersedes": {"parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "record": {"bytes": 124472, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/description-batch-d1/successor.json", "sha256": "2a2f94a61515c175efb3e88eb619626b0c0709682a8724a7fa119576f95a27c9"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/412/description"}}}, {"after": "Produce the attempt-bound InitialCore receipt of owner \u00a71a step 3 (law 463 r3\u2013r9). Join the kernel-validated running image to the entrypoint row of the embedded, authenticated CoreInventoryV3 through fixed no-follow opens under core-tree custody, an in-memory anchor store charged to the attempt's one ledger, the release's own revocation, the required code-signing flags and K from stateWriter. No listing, latest selection, record from I, or caller revoked set. For first trust acceptance (law X4B r5 item 2) it retains, as raw bytes with their references and unverified, the catalog pair (only when an envelope's stored digest admitted it), the component-manifest pairs in members.manifests order and the revocation pair (bootstrap_documents), and lends the anchor's embedded root binding, the bootstrap manifest and core inventory pairs and the root chain's pairs in chain order (bootstrap_release); it grants nothing for them. It also lends evaluator_closure, the core evaluator closure of contract successor EC1 (law X3d r8 item 13, unit X3d-3), from the same authenticated inventory as its closure on the running and the injected arm alike, which the write receipt, its operation and the commit session pass on as a value that a replayed Run's evaluator closure must equal (item 3 step 1).", "before": "Produce the attempt-bound InitialCore receipt of owner \u00a71a step 3 (law 463 r3\u2013r9). Join the kernel-validated running image to the entrypoint row of the embedded, authenticated CoreInventoryV3 through fixed no-follow opens under core-tree custody, an in-memory anchor store charged to the attempt's one ledger, the release's own revocation, the required code-signing flags and K from stateWriter. No listing, latest selection, record from I, or caller revoked set. For first trust acceptance (law X4B r5 item 2) it retains, as raw bytes with their references and unverified, the catalog pair (only when an envelope's stored digest admitted it), the component-manifest pairs in members.manifests order and the revocation pair (bootstrap_documents), and lends the anchor's embedded root binding, the bootstrap manifest and core inventory pairs and the root chain's pairs in chain order (bootstrap_release); it grants nothing for them.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/504/description"}, "supersedes": {"parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "record": {"bytes": 124472, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/description-batch-d1/successor.json", "sha256": "2a2f94a61515c175efb3e88eb619626b0c0709682a8724a7fa119576f95a27c9"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/447/description"}}}, {"after": "Produce first trust acceptance from the running core's embedded bootstrap payload (law X4B r5 items 2 to 6, unit X4B-a; item 1, unit X4B-b), as a macOS child of the current-trust admission, under the held installation fence on the retained P0 state.v1 owner. The payload is exactly what InitialCore retains (law 463 r9): the root chain, the revocation pair, the bootstrap manifest (TR-BUNDLE), the core inventory (TR-CORE), the catalog pair (TR-INDEX) and the component-manifest pairs (TR-COMPONENT). Every signature is verified again: the chain from the embedded root binding within ChainBudget{16 links, 16 MiB}, the list under the final root, re-filtering every link, the index-0 root and its own quorum; each document's envelope at its role's threshold with the list's keyIds excluded. A bootstrap without a signed catalog, or any document that fails, refuses PAYLOAD-NOT-ADMISSIBLE before any write. S4 step 2 runs on the caller's one clock sample (F := max(W, A), L := A, the anchor, expiry from the presented documents). Each carried role's sequence goes through role_machine::decide: PresentOrdinary, then Clock when expired or stale, then Revoke when the payload's own list names its signer, namespace, release or catalog snapshot. Every record (objects, payload closure, operation, time evidence with the payload's signed time source, root and metadata admissions, revocation history, clock-write and role events with their role changes, the revision-2 descriptor and the retained capsule) is canonically encoded, admitted by its closed shape and bound by current_record_bindings, successor_record_bindings and publication_events before floor_publication's shared protocol publishes it, state.v1 last, advancing the retained owner from the confirming reread. That record builder is build_over_p0, over a P0 capsule and its exact bytes (build_acceptance passes the retained owner's); it writes and grants nothing, and its only other caller is X4T-0's test-only store generator, which places its files where publish writes them (law X4B r5 item 9, unit X4B-c). bootstrapping_first_read is the body of the operation monitor's single first read at the lease-free point: X4T-b's fenced_first_read on the retained owner, then, only on F absent (TRUST.NO_ADMITTED_TIME_CONTEXT) and only for a fenced read that is not report-only, acceptance of the payload from BootstrapSource (the write receipt's running core, copied and charged only on that branch) on the read's own clock sample under the same fence hold, and the one confirming admission on the confirmed state.v1, whose view is returned. An acceptance refusal ends the read, and a second F absent is the host invariant row (TrustRow::Invariant); the order lives in first_read_sequence. It grants nothing: the confirming admission is X4T's own.", "before": "Produce first trust acceptance from the running core's embedded bootstrap payload (law X4B r5 items 2 to 6, unit X4B-a; item 1, unit X4B-b), as a macOS child of the current-trust admission, under the held installation fence on the retained P0 state.v1 owner. The payload is exactly what InitialCore retains (law 463 r9): the root chain, the revocation pair, the bootstrap manifest (TR-BUNDLE), the core inventory (TR-CORE), the catalog pair (TR-INDEX) and the component-manifest pairs (TR-COMPONENT). Every signature is verified again: the chain from the embedded root binding within ChainBudget{16 links, 16 MiB}, the list under the final root, re-filtering every link, the index-0 root and its own quorum; each document's envelope at its role's threshold with the list's keyIds excluded. A bootstrap without a signed catalog, or any document that fails, refuses PAYLOAD-NOT-ADMISSIBLE before any write. S4 step 2 runs on the caller's one clock sample (F := max(W, A), L := A, the anchor, expiry from the presented documents). Each carried role's sequence goes through role_machine::decide: PresentOrdinary, then Clock when expired or stale, then Revoke when the payload's own list names its signer, namespace, release or catalog snapshot. Every record (objects, payload closure, operation, time evidence with the payload's signed time source, root and metadata admissions, revocation history, clock-write and role events with their role changes, the revision-2 descriptor and the retained capsule) is canonically encoded, admitted by its closed shape and bound by current_record_bindings, successor_record_bindings and publication_events before floor_publication's shared protocol publishes it, state.v1 last, advancing the retained owner from the confirming reread. bootstrapping_first_read is the body of the operation monitor's single first read at the lease-free point: X4T-b's fenced_first_read on the retained owner, then, only on F absent (TRUST.NO_ADMITTED_TIME_CONTEXT) and only for a fenced read that is not report-only, acceptance of the payload from BootstrapSource (the write receipt's running core, copied and charged only on that branch) on the read's own clock sample under the same fence hold, and the one confirming admission on the confirmed state.v1, whose view is returned. An acceptance refusal ends the read, and a second F absent is the host invariant row (TrustRow::Invariant); the order lives in first_read_sequence. It grants nothing: the confirming admission is X4T's own.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/554/description"}, "supersedes": {"parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "record": {"bytes": 124472, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/description-batch-d1/successor.json", "sha256": "2a2f94a61515c175efb3e88eb619626b0c0709682a8724a7fa119576f95a27c9"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/497/description"}}}, {"after": "Place, create and open the per-(S, N) evidence ledger and admit the attempt (law X3c r6 items 1 to 3, 9 and 10; unit X3c-1). Owner201's spellings: I/stores/S/projects/N/ledger.sqlite with its -wal and -shm, and objects under projects/N/objects/sha256. projects/, projects/N/, objects/ and objects/sha256/ are created or admitted through security's store custody. A new ledger is created exclusively, WAL is selected, then one BEGIN IMMEDIATE runs the selected DDL fragments in their pinned order and COMMITs under synchronous=FULL and fullfsync, the namespace directory is barriered, and the file is reopened through open_existing with a whole-schema equality against the same DDL. An empty file with no -wal is the only resumable creation state; every other stored state is LEDGER.CORRUPT, and nothing is migrated or adopted. The attempt_custody row (admitted, this namespace) is committed in its own non-waiting transaction before any object; a reused ExecutionId is the invariant row and a failed COMMIT is durability-undetermined. Every step is charged. Refusals name their item 10 row; the host projection is X3d's. admit_attempt returns a non-Clone AdmittedAttempt, which object publication requires. Object publication, the prepared ledger transaction, staging and the commit with its durability classification are the child module commit (project_commit.rs, unit X3c-2), whose ObjectDeclaration and StagingMismatch refusals take the invariant row and CommitUndetermined the durability row here. Nothing here settles an attempt or reads an outcome back. The production location is ProjectStoreLocation::selected (law X3d r6 item 3 steps 4 to 6, unit X3d-2), which the commit facade builds from the selected store directory I/stores/S that the commit session opened and confirmed, its absolute spelling, the session's (S, N) and the invoking user (X8b's test-only scenario-fixtures seam also builds one); tests construct one through a cfg(test) constructor. The file re-exports the commit child's entries crate-privately for the storage facade (unit X3d-2) and declares two further children: recovery_read (recovery_read.rs), read-only recovery's one ledger snapshot and object reads with no creation, barrier or write (law X6 r3, unit X6b), and sweep_settle (sweep_settle.rs), the authorized settlement sweep's one ledger snapshot and its settle write under the sweep's EXCLUSIVE lease (law X6 r3 item 7, unit X6c).", "before": "Place, create and open the per-(S, N) evidence ledger and admit the attempt (law X3c r6 items 1 to 3, 9 and 10; unit X3c-1). Owner201's spellings: I/stores/S/projects/N/ledger.sqlite with its -wal and -shm, and objects under projects/N/objects/sha256. projects/, projects/N/, objects/ and objects/sha256/ are created or admitted through security's store custody. A new ledger is created exclusively, WAL is selected, then one BEGIN IMMEDIATE runs the selected DDL fragments in their pinned order and COMMITs under synchronous=FULL and fullfsync, the namespace directory is barriered, and the file is reopened through open_existing with a whole-schema equality against the same DDL. An empty file with no -wal is the only resumable creation state; every other stored state is LEDGER.CORRUPT, and nothing is migrated or adopted. The attempt_custody row (admitted, this namespace) is committed in its own non-waiting transaction before any object; a reused ExecutionId is the invariant row and a failed COMMIT is durability-undetermined. Every step is charged. Refusals name their item 10 row; the host projection is X3d's. admit_attempt returns a non-Clone AdmittedAttempt, which object publication requires. Object publication, the prepared ledger transaction, staging and the commit with its durability classification are the child module commit (project_commit.rs, unit X3c-2), whose ObjectDeclaration and StagingMismatch refusals take the invariant row and CommitUndetermined the durability row here. Nothing here settles an attempt or reads an outcome back. Only tests construct a location.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v134.json", "sha256": "626cd71996d79de5c0efd914438dd65b3123e701e774ccdad1f8f9681466c58a"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/726/description"}, "supersedes": {"parent": {"bytes": 469771, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v119.json", "sha256": "34443e615d5c30f64e7f93c56d3b6e60b6afa06712d3489c127ecc858e4e21c6"}, "record": {"bytes": 124472, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/description-batch-d1/successor.json", "sha256": "2a2f94a61515c175efb3e88eb619626b0c0709682a8724a7fa119576f95a27c9"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/665/description"}}}, {"after": "Read and join immutable manifest and receipt-inventory material from the recovery ledger snapshot, and stage a Run's manifest and commit inventory into commit_run_material inside an open write transaction (WriteTransaction::stage_run_material, law X3c r7 item 6, unit X3c-2) under the same schema check and body admission recovery uses, after the receipt staged in that transaction, returning the inventory's typed object identities and its blob digests, both of which the commit publishes (unit X3d-2), and committing nothing; signatures, live retention, pins and full replay remain separate owners.", "before": "Read and join immutable manifest and receipt-inventory material from the recovery ledger snapshot, and stage a Run's manifest and commit inventory into commit_run_material inside an open write transaction (WriteTransaction::stage_run_material, law X3c r7 item 6, unit X3c-2) under the same schema check and body admission recovery uses, after the receipt staged in that transaction, returning the inventory's blob digests and committing nothing; signatures, live retention, pins and full replay remain separate owners.", "parent": {"bytes": 552176, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.