h": "docs/implementation/m3/supervisor-d/sd-7/evidence/check_sd7.py", "sha256": "432e10966fb0de9503374bf7cb5fb98936b2b7f4abc4b4c2d6738de371c8f477"}, {"bytes": 13872, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/supervisor-d/sd-7/evidence/fold-report.json", "sha256": "b8e794cb2502aaac440da69e9c459f5fae52ca66124c2f3fd6b0ada27169fe43"}, {"bytes": 11738, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/supervisor-d/sd-7/evidence/verify_scratch.py", "sha256": "12354454b18000c6f83206a99c46010f49de3a97ced8e5d1a56d812a40e60266"}], "passageOverrides": [{"after": "| **unsatisfiable capability REQUEST**: id and mode both registered, but the `(capability, mode)` cell is `NOT-SELECTED` \u2014 outside D-371, no promise to serve (`native.requested-capability-mode-not-selected`) | | `request-rejected` (2) | `REQUEST.UNSATISFIABLE` | request detail (capability, mode) |\n| **request that is a request-class excluded form** (contract successor SD-7 of law M3-D r5, item 25, lead decision LD-R4-2): a well-formed request refused at typed request admission, before the durable entry, the creation prelude and any `ExecutionId` draw, as `ExcludedForm {class, subject}`, because it represents one of DR-117's request-class excluded forms: `EE-2`, a PlanIntent or admission request that requires an external discovery or public-lifecycle endpoint; `EE-4` (request part), a PlanIntent requesting untrusted native or WASM admission; `EE-6a`, a PlanIntent whose analysis branch requests network-granted analysis. Each is outside D-371's selected product, as the `NOT-SELECTED` cell of the row above is: nothing is malformed and the host made no error, so the route is the same whatever the request's origin, and it is never a malformed request or a host fault | | `request-rejected` (2) | `REQUEST.UNSATISFIABLE` | `PROVIDER.NOT_SELECTED`, carried as `domainDetail` with subject `excluded-form:<class>`, naming the first class in the order `EE-2`, `EE-4`, `EE-6a` when one request carries several; remedy \"this capability is not selected for that language mode, or the request asks for an external discovery or public-lifecycle endpoint, untrusted native or WASM admission, or network-granted analysis; no promise is made for it; restate the request without it\", the code-keyed remedy it shares with the row above; the failure envelope's `errors` is exactly that detail; every `ExcludedForm` goes to the operational record; no runId and no executionId |", "before": "| **unsatisfiable capability REQUEST**: id and mode both registered, but the `(capability, mode)` cell is `NOT-SELECTED` \u2014 outside D-371, no promise to serve (`native.requested-capability-mode-not-selected`) | | `request-rejected` (2) | `REQUEST.UNSATISFIABLE` | request detail (capability, mode) |", "parent": {"bytes": 329013, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/native-evidence.md", "sha256": "83b99783893bec4bcca76bc043310e1d33305fc41ef85e012fbcb19e5b222ca0"}, "selector": {"line": 3539}}, {"after": "    \"PROVIDER.NOT_SELECTED\": \"this capability is not selected for that language mode, or the request asks for an external discovery or public-lifecycle endpoint, untrusted native or WASM admission, or network-granted analysis; no promise is made for it; restate the request without it\",", "before": "    \"PROVIDER.NOT_SELECTED\": \"this capability is not selected for that language mode; no promise is made for it\",", "parent": {"bytes": 320462, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/config-discovery-b/b-s9/reference/native_evidence_model.py", "sha256": "a7e40715ce9b204f3ba562ffda69ccd542f3ca6096dd3a78d257b3e62f8c874b"}, "selector": {"line": 1159}}, {"after": "    \"PROVIDER.NOT_SELECTED\": \"this capability is not selected for that language mode, or the request asks for an external discovery or public-lifecycle endpoint, untrusted native or WASM admission, or network-granted analysis; no promise is made for it; restate the request without it\",", "before": "    \"PROVIDER.NOT_SELECTED\": \"this capability is not selected for that language mode; no promise is made for it\",", "parent": {"bytes": 319894, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/config-discovery-b/b-s9/reference/native_evidence_model.v2.py", "sha256": "4f800faaf75c36ffd38fa9ae0f3618bee1bedbe586026e280326b0d7d73ebca8"}, "selector": {"line": 1159}}], "passageSupersessions": [{"after": "| **invalid authenticated RELEASE DECLARATION**: a row naming a capability the matrix does not register, a mode outside the registered set, a mode whose cell is `NOT-SELECTED`, a duplicate `capabilityId`, or a `preview-*` spelling (`native.release-capability-unregistered`, `\u2026-mode-unregistered`, `\u2026-mode-not-selected`, `\u2026-duplicate`) | | `request-rejected` (2) | `REQUEST.PRECONDITION_FAILED` | request detail (before PlanId; no worker is spawned) |\n| **component manifest that is an excluded form** (contract successor SD-5 of law M3-D r3, item 24; conformed by contract successor SD-7 to law M3-D r5, item 24, lead decision LD-R4-1): an authenticated component manifest (security S1: its signature envelope and signed catalog association) that current trust admits and that the analysis step can select, refused at component admission before any analysis attempt's `ExecutionId` is drawn or reserved, on the durable and the ephemeral path, as `ExcludedForm {class, subject}`, because it represents one of DR-117's manifest-class excluded forms: `EE-1`, a publisher neither first-party nor explicitly trusted; `EE-3b`, a claim of policy, persistence, rendering, termination or host-lifecycle authority (a capability outside the native capability matrix's provider capabilities); `EE-4`, untrusted native or WASM code; `EE-5a`, a project hook, a contribution-granted probe, or a root-command claim on the host-owned root namespace, which is exactly: (a) a closure-only manifest (role `toolchain`, `stdlib`, `rust-dev-llvm` or `grammar`) that declares `commands` at all; (b) an `analyzer` manifest whose command tree does not have exactly one entry without `parent`, or whose parentless entry's `name` differs from the manifest's `name`; or (c) a reserved root-command name among its root-namespace keys (its `name`, each of its `aliases` and, for `analyzer`, each alias of its parentless entry). An `analyzer` manifest's own name-bound mounted root, with any declared depth below it, is not a root-command claim, and neither is a collision with another component's live name; a command tree is never an `EE-3b` form. A manifest that the security owner's manifest admission refuses first never reaches component admission and keeps that route. No Plan, Coverage, Run or closure selection follows, so the refusal is never a `provider-unavailable` deficiency and never the not-installed golden of workflows \u00a79. A required closure that is not installed, or that current trust does not admit (an ephemeral request with no trust view included), is never an excluded form and keeps that golden: `indeterminate` (3), `COVERAGE.PROVIDER_UNAVAILABLE`, `COMPONENT.REQUIRED_CLOSURE_NOT_INSTALLED`. A condition the authenticated release declaration row above routes keeps that row, and a manifest refused here never reaches HelloAck, closure selection or spawn | | `request-rejected` (2) | `EXTENSION.ADMISSION_REJECTED` | `PAYLOAD-NOT-ADMISSIBLE`, security S12's admission row for a signed document that is not admissible, carried as `domainDetail` with subject `excluded-form:<class>:<manifestDigest>` (the refused manifest's identity `closure.manifestDigest`, 64 hex): the refused manifest least by `manifestDigest`, with its first class in the order above; remedy \"This installed component is a form OpenSIP never admits. Only first-party or explicitly trusted components are admitted, and none may claim policy, persistence, rendering, termination or host-lifecycle authority, run untrusted native or WASM code, or claim a project hook, a reserved or additional root command, or a probe. Remove the component, or install its first-party release.\"; the failure envelope's `errors` is exactly that detail; every `ExcludedForm` goes to the operational record; no runId and no executionId |", "before": "| **invalid authenticated RELEASE DECLARATION**: a row naming a capability the matrix does not register, a mode outside the registered set, a mode whose cell is `NOT-SELECTED`, a duplicate `capabilityId`, or a `preview-*` spelling (`native.release-capability-unregistered`, `\u2026-mode-unregistered`, `\u2026-mode-not-selected`, `\u2026-duplicate`) | | `request-rejected` (2) | `REQUEST.PRECONDITION_FAILED` | request detail (before PlanId; no worker is spawned) |\n| **component manifest that is an excluded form** (contract successor SD-5 of law M3-D r3, item 24): an authenticated component manifest (security S1: its signature envelope and signed catalog association) that current trust admits and that the analysis step can select, refused at component admission before any analysis attempt's `ExecutionId` is drawn or reserved, on the durable and the ephemeral path, as `ExcludedForm {class, subject}`, because it represents one of DR-117's manifest-class excluded forms: `EE-1`, a publisher neither first-party nor explicitly trusted; `EE-3b`, a claim of policy, persistence, rendering, termination or host-lifecycle authority (a `commands` entry for role `analyzer`, or a capability outside the native capability matrix's provider capabilities); `EE-4`, untrusted native or WASM code; `EE-5a`, a project hook, root command or contribution-granted probe. No Plan, Coverage, Run or closure selection follows, so the refusal is never a `provider-unavailable` deficiency and never the not-installed golden of workflows \u00a79. A required closure that is not installed, or that current trust does not admit (an ephemeral request with no trust view included), is never an excluded form and keeps that golden: `indeterminate` (3), `COVERAGE.PROVIDER_UNAVAILABLE`, `COMPONENT.REQUIRED_CLOSURE_NOT_INSTALLED`. A condition the authenticated release declaration row above routes keeps that row, and a manifest refused here never reaches HelloAck, closure selection or spawn | | `request-rejected` (2) | `EXTENSION.ADMISSION_REJECTED` | `PAYLOAD-NOT-ADMISSIBLE`, security S12's admission row for a signed document that is not admissible, carried as `domainDetail` with subject `excluded-form:<class>:<manifestDigest>` (the refused manifest's identity `closure.manifestDigest`, 64 hex): the refused manifest least by `manifestDigest`, with its first class in the order above; remedy \"This installed component is a form OpenSIP never admits. Only first-party or explicitly trusted components are admitted, and none may claim policy, persistence, rendering, termination or host-lifecycle authority, run untrusted native or WASM code, or declare a project hook, root command or probe. Remove the component, or install its first-party release.\"; the failure envelope's `errors` is exactly that detail; every `ExcludedForm` goes to the operational record; no runId and no executionId |", "parent": {"bytes": 329013, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/native-evidence.md", "sha256": "83b99783893bec4bcca76bc043310e1d33305fc41ef85e012fbcb19e5b222ca0"}, "selector": {"line": 3540}, "supersedes": {"parent": {"bytes": 329013, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/native-evidence.md", "sha256": "83b99783893bec4bcca76bc043310e1d33305fc41ef85e012fbcb19e5b222ca0"}, "record": {"bytes": 5805, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/supervisor-d/sd-5/successor.json", "sha256": "5e11581804098116a4afa5052ff27ed426beaebcc6e0a38607d1628ea8a595e7"}, "selector": {"line": 3540}}}], "selected": "docs/implementation/m3/supervisor-d/sd-7/successor.json", "sha256": "350a249afaa001fc97292c2837395d930cc7bdc0d6b62b594b04df55d9767c04"}, {"inputs": [{"bytes": 11045, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/project-registry-owner-selection-v3/README.md", "sha256": "4de5f14f4d236151317488c9bf4766244332ef10489143c7c320ca99465e101d"}], "passageOverrides": [{"after": "Then, under the same fence: (1) durably publish RESERVED; (2) durably publish the new namespace directory with stable writer/readers lease inodes through the explicit initial-namespace publication profile below; (3) create-new the exact project marker, make the file and parent durable; (4) durably replace RESERVED by ACTIVE after rechecking every original root/marker/namespace observation. No project ledger, grant, Run or authoritative analysis is created before ACTIVE. Marker publication must not overwrite a tracked, foreign, different, malformed or unreadable object. Any one-sided or uncertain state ends the ordinary operation and requires explicit recovery. (v3, J-RW RW-S2) Completing a marker in place is not an overwrite when its bytes are a strict prefix (the empty prefix included) of the RESERVED row's own marker frame. It is the reservation's own interrupted create-new step. It writes only the missing suffix at the existing length, rewrites no existing byte, and then makes the file and parent durable. It is lawful only inside an authorized reservation completion, with the complete namespace present (below).", "before": "Then, under the same fence: (1) durably publish RESERVED; (2) durably publish the new namespace directory with stable writer/readers lease inodes through the explicit initial-namespace publication profile below; (3) create-new the exact project marker, make the file and parent durable; (4) durably replace RESERVED by ACTIVE after rechecking every original root/marker/namespace observation. No project ledger, grant, Run or authoritative analysis is created before ACTIVE. Marker publication must not overwrite a tracked, foreign, different, malformed or unreadable object. Any one-sided or uncertain state ends the ordinary operation and requires explicit recovery.", "parent": {"bytes": 32488, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/project-registry-owner-selection-v2/owner.md", "sha256": "2d4b65c9b0bc088b2667c35e75bf82d1702d13703be6effb1df975e7c67c179f"}, "selector": {"line": 60}}, {"after": "Recovery is an independently authorized local operation, not the constructor's fallback. It first performs the same whole-registry/root/marker/custody admission and observes the actual recorded state under the fence. RESERVED cannot grant a project lease or data access. RESERVED and ABANDONED are unregistered for project-lease admission: attempting flock on their file names is refused as lease-on-unregistered-namespace before lock acquisition, never treated as BUSY or permission to continue. Publishing empty lock carriers is not acquiring a lease. (v3, J-RW RW-S2) For an allocationKind=random row, J-RW is that independently authorized operation. Completion runs as its own classified step inside a later, separately admitted durable write, under the installation fence. It never runs in the process that crashed, never as the constructor's fallback, and never on a read.", "before": "Recovery is an independently authorized local operation, not the constructor's fallback. It first performs the same whole-registry/root/marker/custody admission and observes the actual recorded state under the fence. RESERVED cannot grant a project lease or data access. RESERVED and ABANDONED are unregistered for project-lease admission: attempting flock on their file names is refused as lease-on-unregistered-namespace before lock acquisition, never treated as BUSY or permission to continue. Publishing empty lock carriers is not acquiring a lease.", "parent": {"bytes": 32488, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/project-registry-owner-selection-v2/owner.md", "sha256": "2d4b65c9b0bc088b2667c35e75bf82d1702d13703be6effb1df975e7c67c179f"}, "selector": {"line": 74}}, {"after": "Completion requires positively proved agreement of the recorded root incarnation/locator, exact marker, and the reservation's admitted complete initial namespace/stable lease footprint, with all required durability barriers reconfirmed. Missing namespace publication may be completed only under the initial-namespace profile above from positively observed absence; an occupied incomplete or foreign namespace is unavailable and cannot be adopted from its spelling. An absent marker may be created only after the same untracked/absence/root checks as the original allocation. Only then may RESERVED\u2192ACTIVE be published. These observations are not proof that every combination is reachable by an ordinary first-use crash. The complete admitted RegistryDocument and selected RESERVED row, including its immutable allocationKind, are mandatory recovery inputs. Ordinary reservation recovery can activate only allocationKind=random. Every allocationKind=adopt completion requires a fresh admitted-adoption context for the exact reserved ProjectId and target root, EVEN when namespace and marker are both already complete. History lookup is not a replacement for this stored kind: first adoption on another machine can have no historical ProjectId row. An unknown/missing kind or mismatching mode refuses. Ordinary first-use publishes the complete namespace before creating its marker; RESERVED plus an exact marker and absent namespace is NOT its lawful crash prefix. An ordinary reservation-recovery continuation refuses that combination. It must not infer adoption authority from the marker, RESERVED row, caller flag or directory name. The random reservation is NOT convertible to adopt; that inconsistent prefix grants no completion. A separate authorized adoption must use its own fresh adopt reservation and satisfy all original collision/retirement rules; recovery cannot silently abandon or rewrite the random row to make it fit. Unreadability/incomplete namespace still refuses in either mode. (v3, J-RW RW-S2) Ordinary reservation completion of an allocationKind=random row may also find a marker that is a strict prefix (the empty prefix included) of the row's own marker frame. It completes that marker in place, as the marker publication paragraph above states, and only when the reservation's complete initial namespace footprint is admitted present. The ordering rule above covers every present-marker form, not only the exact one: RESERVED plus a present marker of any form (exact, zero-length, a strict prefix, ACL-omitted or private) and an absent namespace is not a lawful crash prefix, and ordinary reservation completion refuses it before any effect. This changes no row of the observation table above, which classifies without writing.", "before": "Completion requires positively proved agreement of the recorded root incarnation/locator, exact marker, and the reservation's admitted complete initial namespace/stable lease footprint, with all required durability barriers reconfirmed. Missing namespace publication may be completed only under the initial-namespace profile above from positively observed absence; an occupied incomplete or foreign namespace is unavailable and cannot be adopted from its spelling. An absent marker may be created only after the same untracked/absence/root checks as the original allocation. Only then may RESERVED\u2192ACTIVE be published. These observations are not proof that every combination is reachable by an ordinary first-use crash. The complete admitted RegistryDocument and selected RESERVED row, including its immutable allocationKind, are mandatory recovery inputs. Ordinary reservation recovery can activate only allocationKind=random. Every allocationKind=adopt completion requires a fresh admitted-adoption context for the exact reserved ProjectId and target root, EVEN when namespace and marker are both already complete. History lookup is not a replacement for this stored kind: first adoption on another machine can have no historical ProjectId row. An unknown/missing kind or mismatching mode refuses. Ordinary first-use publishes the complete namespace before creating its marker; RESERVED plus an exact marker and absent namespace is NOT its lawful crash prefix. An ordinary reservation-recovery continuation refuses that combination. It must not infer adoption authority from the marker, RESERVED row, caller flag or directory name. The random reservation is NOT convertible to adopt; that inconsistent prefix grants no completion. A separate authorized adoption must use its own fresh adopt reservation and satisfy all original collision/retirement rules; recovery cannot silently abandon or rewrite the random row to make it fit. Unreadability/incomplete namespace still refuses in either mode.", "parent": {"bytes": 32488, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/project-registry-owner-selection-v2/owner.md", "sha256": "2d4b65c9b0bc088b2667c35e75bf82d1702d13703be6effb1df975e7c67c179f"}, "selector": {"line": 76}}], "passageSupersessions": [{"after": "This owner introduces private registry encoding and internal observation kinds only. It adds no public command, wire field, semantic identity input, signature domain, role state or public error code. Existing operation owners must supply authorization for installation, first-write, explicit recovery, move, fork, adoption and retirement. Their absence is not permission to invent a CLI surface or initialize on an ordinary read. The separately reviewed initial-root-binding owner supplies only initial installation publication and ordinary selected-store binding; its positive creator predicate, fixed-root storage choice, read/write split, bounded original-owner sequence and complete P0 publication are mandatory. Other recovery/move/fork/adoption/retirement owners remain separate. See docs/implementation/m2/initial-root-binding-owner-selection-v1/owner.md. (v3, J-RW RW-S2) For ordinary, allocationKind=random reservation completion, that operation owner is law J-RW, M3's resume/repair writer (`docs/implementation/m3/resume-repair-jrw/PROPOSAL-r4.md`, item 2). Its authorization is the write gate's ordinary write admission for a durable request on the same root, whose recorded locator and incarnation agree with the row: the authorization class, on the same root, that created the reservation. It adds no CLI surface. Adoption, move, fork, retirement and abandonment keep their own owners, unchanged.", "before": "This owner introduces private registry encoding and internal observation kinds only. It adds no public command, wire field, semantic identity input, signature domain, role state or public error code. Existing operation owners must supply authorization for installation, first-write, explicit recovery, move, fork, adoption and retirement. Their absence is not permission to invent a CLI surface or initialize on an ordinary read. The separately reviewed initial-root-binding owner supplies only initial installation publication and ordinary selected-store binding; its positive creator predicate, fixed-root storage choice, read/write split, bounded original-owner sequence and complete P0 publication are mandatory. Other recovery/move/fork/adoption/retirement owners remain separate. See docs/implementation/m2/initial-root-binding-owner-selection-v1/owner.md.", "parent": {"bytes": 32488, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/project-registry-owner-selection-v2/owner.md", "sha256": "2d4b65c9b0bc088b2667c35e75bf82d1702d13703be6effb1df975e7c67c179f"}, "selector": {"line": 9}, "supersedes": {"parent": {"bytes": 32488, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/project-registry-owner-selection-v2/owner.md", "sha256": "2d4b65c9b0bc088b2667c35e75bf82d1702d13703be6effb1df975e7c67c179f"}, "record": {"bytes": 43921, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/initial-root-binding-owner-selection-v1/successor.json", "sha256": "3a713c5c39a3feca2db3a05924103d49c10f11bef3c32f93d8c286fecd813e54"}, "selector": {"line": 9}}}], "selected": "docs/implementation/m2/project-registry-owner-selection-v3/successor.json", "sha256": "974ef73f539fb20386b57b2063c49c40573da27e72c443443b666da6ab3d6691"}, {"inputs": [{"bytes": 20816, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/crc-2/PASSAGES.md", "sha256": "c60c9993adf1c493cdeab476227b87b160ec1014c22262e35769780990678ac9"}, {"bytes": 17163, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/crc-2/README.md", "sha256": "67a9613b49e7b84e593795c4a21ba056da2266645215e808eca23ef3d61e8a3f"}, {"bytes": 20019, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/crc-2/evidence/build_crc_2.py", "sha256": "490aada72fd20828d204631c8c94421bbe36e32d0649cc0178f00c2988ca7714"}, {"bytes": 11393, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/crc-2/evidence/check_crc_2.py", "sha256": "375b8a7966127cacac260a1edbe25555652f1b318609f259ecd3315ab26aa9f4"}, {"bytes": 10647, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/crc-2/evidence/verify_scratch.py", "sha256": "41fb79bd32704f6529831ff812b0c43d4c8b1592a8af1977712eae773ac72da8"}], "passageOverrides": [{"after": "No new closure kind is invented for producer acts: enumeration, view production, fact production and stage production are all provider acts under the existing kind. `grammar` is a genuinely new INSTALLED-COMPONENT kind, in the same family as `toolchain`, `stdlib` and `rust-dev-llvm`: the syntax-only mode's body interpreter is a bundled grammar set, and binding it to an existing kind would either claim it is a toolchain (it executes no compiler) or leave the component that interprets a body span outside the closure law, which is the one thing every other universe pins down. The evaluator kind names no separately delivered component, because the evaluator is the pure core (contract successor EC1). A core release's evaluator closure is closure2: + H(\"closure\", D), where D is the authenticated core inventory's selected-platform closure descriptor that security projects for the core closure, with kind set to \"evaluator\" and every other member unchanged. Its manifestDigest is the raw SHA-256 of the TR-CORE-signed inventory body and its tree is that platform's regular files. The core closure and the core evaluator closure therefore differ only by kind. Contract successor CRC-1 adds the other core role closures: the core detector, provider and adapter closures are the same projection with kind set to \"detector\", \"provider\" and \"adapter\", so the four core role closures of one release differ only by kind, and none is a component-manifest closure. A closure is a core role closure of a Plan when its descriptor equals, except for kind, that of the core evaluator closure the Plan selects. Each is confined to named fields. The core detector closure is the detectorClosure, and so the finding.ruleClosure, of the rules of the core's bundled packs. The core adapter closure is only the import.adapterClosure of a dependency or prepared import. The core provider closure has exactly three uses under one identity: the import.producerClosure of a dependency or prepared import; the producer of syntax-universe work (subject-scope.enumeratorClosure, view.producerClosure and so fact.producerClosure, stage-spec.producerClosure, the enumeration binding's enumerator.closureId and CandidateProducerResultV1.producerClosure) only where the universe is a native.semantic-universe.syntax.v2 identity; and (contract successor CRC-2) the producer and enumerator of the host-derived records of the three inventory relations, file@enumerated, package@manifest-declared and vcs-change@vcs-reported, in every universe. Its third use has four fields: the enumerator.closureId of a requested inventory cell's binding; the host inventory stage's stage-spec.producerClosure; and that stage's view.producerClosure, so fact.producerClosure, and subject-scope.enumeratorClosure. Apart from that third use it never produces a TypeScript or Rust record, and no language provider closure produces or enumerates an inventory relation's record. A core role closure in any other field, cache-key.producerClosure included, refuses. byField is unchanged.", "before": "No new closure kind is invented for producer acts: enumeration, view production, fact production and stage production are all provider acts under the existing kind. `grammar` is a genuinely new INSTALLED-COMPONENT kind, in the same family as `toolchain`, `stdlib` and `rust-dev-llvm`: the syntax-only mode's body interpreter is a bundled grammar set, and binding it to an existing kind would either claim it is a toolchain (it executes no compiler) or leave the component that interprets a body span outside the closure law, which is the one thing every other universe pins down. The evaluator kind names no separately delivered component, because the evaluator is the pure core (contract successor EC1). A core release's evaluator closure is closure2: + H(\"closure\", D), where D is the authenticated core inventory's selected-platform closure descriptor that security projects for the core closure, with kind set to \"evaluator\" and every other member unchanged. Its manifestDigest is the raw SHA-256 of the TR-CORE-signed inventory body and its tree is that platform's regular files. The core closure and the core evaluator closure therefore differ only by kind. Contract successor CRC-1 adds the other core role closures: the core detector, provider and adapter closures are the same projection with kind set to \"detector\", \"provider\" and \"adapter\", so the four core role closures of one release differ only by kind, and none is a component-manifest closure. A closure is a core role closure of a Plan when its descriptor equals, except for kind, that of the core evaluator closure the Plan selects. Each is confined to named fields. The core detector closure is the detectorClosure, and so the finding.ruleClosure, of the rules of the core's bundled packs. The core adapter closure is only the import.adapterClosure of a dependency or prepared import. The core provider closure has exactly two uses under one identity: the import.producerClosure of a dependency or prepared import, and the producer of syntax-universe work (subject-scope.enumeratorClosure, view.producerClosure and so fact.producerClosure, stage-spec.producerClosure, the enumeration binding's enumerator.closureId and CandidateProducerResultV1.producerClosure) only where the universe is a native.semantic-universe.syntax.v2 identity; it never produces a TypeScript or Rust record. A core role closure in any other field, cache-key.producerClosure included, refuses. byField is unchanged.", "parent": {"bytes": 200510, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/syntax-e/syn-1f/design/foundation/identity-schemas.v3.json", "sha256": "73645b7633d95f5d7b8e5183d789b8aab028148be0dd158e0b503a4cc3e2bd19"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/x-opensip-digest-domains/closureKinds/note"}}, {"after": "Direct members must be in plan.semanticClosures. Other-input dependencies need not be flattened into it; extra admissible retained closures may be selected explicitly. There is no exact-minimal-set constraint. The core role closures are the exception (contract successors CRC-1 and CRC-2): the core provider closure is a direct member exactly when the Plan selects a native.semantic-universe.syntax.v2 universe or requests an inventory cell, and is never selected otherwise, not even explicitly; the core adapter closure is never a member.", "before": "Direct members must be in plan.semanticClosures. Other-input dependencies need not be flattened into it; extra admissible retained closures may be selected explicitly. There is no exact-minimal-set constraint. The core role closures are the exception (contract successor CRC-1): the core provider closure is a direct member exactly when the Plan selects a native.semantic-universe.syntax.v2 universe, and is never selected otherwise, not even explicitly; the core adapter closure is never a member.", "parent": {"bytes": 200510, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/syntax-e/syn-1f/design/foundation/identity-schemas.v3.json", "sha256": "73645b7633d95f5d7b8e5183d789b8aab028148be0dd158e0b503a4cc3e2bd19"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/x-opensip-digest-domains/closureMembership/selectionLaw"}}], "passageSupersessions": [{"after": "of the same closure and the exact reserved file commitment.\n\n**Core role closures (contract successors CRC-1 and CRC-2, 2026-10-04).** The core evaluator closure's rule (EC1, above) has three more projections. For one core release, the **core detector closure**, the **core provider closure** and the **core adapter closure** are each `closure2:` + H(\"closure\", D), where D is the authenticated core inventory's selected-platform closure descriptor that security projects for the core closure, with `kind` set to `\"detector\"`, `\"provider\"` or `\"adapter\"` respectively and every other member unchanged. The core closure and the four core role closures of one release (evaluator, detector, provider and adapter) therefore differ only by `kind`. For every core role closure, `manifestDigest` is raw SHA256 of the TR-CORE-signed core inventory body (`opensip.metadata.inventory.1`), excluding its signature envelope, and `tree` is the projection of that platform's regular files. The `closure.manifestDigest` sentence above and the `raw-artifact` row of the representation table name the kind-`evaluator` case; they hold equally for every core role closure. No core role closure is a component-manifest closure: none is admitted through a component manifest, a catalog release or security's role-to-kind table (security \u00a7S1, contract successor CR-1), and `core` is never an identity closure kind. A closure is a core role closure of a Plan exactly when its descriptor equals, except for `kind`, the descriptor of the core evaluator closure that the Plan selects and its evaluation seal names. `closureKinds.byField` is unchanged. Each core role closure is admitted only in the fields below; in any other field, `cache-key.producerClosure` included, it refuses.\n\n- **The core detector closure** is the `detectorClosure` of each `EvaluatorEmissionPlanV1` row whose rule's policy came from a pack of the core's bundled pack registry, and so the `finding.ruleClosure` of those rules' findings. **The detector join**, checked at Run closure: every emission row of a bundled pack names a retained kind-`detector` closure that is the Plan's core detector closure, and the row's `contributionId` is a member of that pack's registry-row `contributions`. An evaluator or provider closure, or another core's detector closure, cannot stand in (composition \u00a71).\n- **The core adapter closure** is only the `import.adapterClosure` of an import whose `kind` is `dependency` or `prepared`. It is never a `plan.semanticClosures` member.\n- **The core provider closure** has exactly three admitted uses under the one provider identity: (1) the `import.producerClosure` of an import whose `kind` is `dependency` or `prepared`; (2) the producer of syntax-universe work, only where the record's, stage's or binding's universe is a `native.semantic-universe.syntax.v2` identity: `subject-scope.enumeratorClosure`, `view.producerClosure` and so `fact.producerClosure`, `stage-spec.producerClosure`, an enumeration binding's `enumerator.closureId`, and `CandidateProducerResultV1.producerClosure`; (3) (contract successor CRC-2) the producer and enumerator of host inventory records in every universe. These are the records of the three `inventory` relations, `file@enumerated`, `package@manifest-declared` and `vcs-change@vcs-reported`, that the host derives from retained records and that no language provider produces. Use (3) has exactly four fields: the `enumerator.closureId` of each enumeration binding of a requested `inventory` cell, available or not; the `stage-spec.producerClosure` of each host inventory stage; and that stage's `view.producerClosure`, and so `fact.producerClosure`, and its `subject-scope.enumeratorClosure`. Use (3) never admits a record of another relation, a record a language provider returned, the enumerator of a binding that owes a symbol inventory, or `CandidateProducerResultV1.producerClosure`. The core provider closure is a `plan.semanticClosures` member exactly when the Plan selects a syntax universe or requests an `inventory` cell, and is never selected otherwise, not even explicitly. Apart from use (3), it is never the producer of a TypeScript or Rust record. The grammar closure keeps kind `grammar` and is never a producer, a TypeScript or Rust provider closure is never the producer of a syntax record, and no language provider closure is the producer or enumerator of an `inventory` relation's record. As every stage producer does, the core provider closure registers each syntax stage's and each host inventory stage's output schema as a core platform tree member at `opensip-interface/stage-output/<operation>.schema.json` (below).\n\nSecurity derives every core role closure from the one authenticated inventory it already holds; none is read from a Run, a worker claim or a build-time string, and no new signed data, release format or trust change is needed. As for every closure, the inventory body and every tree member are retained preimages of a Run that names one, stored once per store by raw SHA256. A new core release is a new detector, provider and adapter closure, so the Plans and Runs that select them have new identities. Historical closures, Plans and Runs keep their bytes and are not re-read under this law.", "before": "of the same closure and the exact reserved file commitment.\n\n**Core role closures (contract successor CRC-1, 2026-10-04).** The core evaluator closure's rule (EC1, above) has three more projections. For one core release, the **core detector closure**, the **core provider closure** and the **core adapter closure** are each `closure2:` + H(\"closure\", D), where D is the authenticated core inventory's selected-platform closure descriptor that security projects for the core closure, with `kind` set to `\"detector\"`, `\"provider\"` or `\"adapter\"` respectively and every other member unchanged. The core closure and the four core role closures of one release (evaluator, detector, provider and adapter) therefore differ only by `kind`. For every core role closure, `manifestDigest` is raw SHA256 of the TR-CORE-signed core inventory body (`opensip.metadata.inventory.1`), excluding its signature envelope, and `tree` is the projection of that platform's regular files. The `closure.manifestDigest` sentence above and the `raw-artifact` row of the representation table name the kind-`evaluator` case; they hold equally for every core role closure. No core role closure is a component-manifest closure: none is admitted through a component manifest, a catalog release or security's role-to-kind table (security \u00a7S1, contract successor CR-1), and `core` is never an identity closure kind. A closure is a core role closure of a Plan exactly when its descriptor equals, except for `kind`, the descriptor of the core evaluator closure that the Plan selects and its evaluation seal names. `closureKinds.byField` is unchanged. Each core role closure is admitted only in the fields below; in any other field, `cache-key.producerClosure` included, it refuses.\n\n- **The core detector closure** is the `detectorClosure` of each `EvaluatorEmissionPlanV1` row whose rule's policy came from a pack of the core's bundled pack registry, and so the `finding.ruleClosure` of those rules' findings. **The detector join**, checked at Run closure: every emission row of a bundled pack names a retained kind-`detector` closure that is the Plan's core detector closure, and the row's `contributionId` is a member of that pack's registry-row `contributions`. An evaluator or provider closure, or another core's detector closure, cannot stand in (composition \u00a71).\n- **The core adapter closure** is only the `import.adapterClosure` of an import whose `kind` is `dependency` or `prepared`. It is never a `plan.semanticClosures` member.\n- **The core provider closure** has exactly two admitted uses under the one provider identity: (1) the `import.producerClosure` of an import whose `kind` is `dependency` or `prepared`; (2) the producer of syntax-universe work, only where the record's, stage's or binding's universe is a `native.semantic-universe.syntax.v2` identity: `subject-scope.enumeratorClosure`, `view.producerClosure` and so `fact.producerClosure`, `stage-spec.producerClosure`, an enumeration binding's `enumerator.closureId`, and `CandidateProducerResultV1.producerClosure`. It is a `plan.semanticClosures` member exactly when the Plan selects a syntax universe, and is never selected otherwise, not even explicitly. It is never the producer of a TypeScript or Rust record. The grammar closure keeps kind `grammar` and is never a producer, and a TypeScript or Rust provider closure is never the producer of a syntax record. As every stage producer does, the core provider closure registers each syntax stage's output schema as a core platform tree member at `opensip-interface/stage-output/<operation>.schema.json` (below).\n\nSecurity derives every core role closure from the one authenticated inventory it already holds; none is read from a Run, a worker claim or a build-time string, and no new signed data, release format or trust change is needed. As for every closure, the inventory body and every tree member are retained preimages of a Run that names one, stored once per store by raw SHA256. A new core release is a new detector, provider and adapter closure, so the Plans and Runs that select them have new identities. Historical closures, Plans and Runs keep their bytes and are not re-read under this law.", "parent": {"bytes": 135448, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/identity-and-evidence.md", "sha256": "c82404f3a0cf56fa6cc02e99cc3ebbd5356fedc3b36aeb38f9ef284077fbd31f"}, "selector": {"line": 285}, "supersedes": {"parent": {"bytes": 135448, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/identity-and-evidence.md", "sha256": "c82404f3a0cf56fa6cc02e99cc3ebbd5356fedc3b36aeb38f9ef284077fbd31f"}, "record": {"bytes": 21493, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/crc-1/successor.json", "sha256": "29df5f5e2b145daf3c9b8e231ccac1d08c5b36d2eb006fbdc3bba543d7084166"}, "selector": {"line": 285}}}, {"after": "evaluation inputs or remove any direct membership or retention obligation. **The core role closures are the exception (contract successors CRC-1 and CRC-2, above).** The core provider closure is a direct `semanticClosures` member exactly when the Plan selects a `native.semantic-universe.syntax.v2` universe or requests an `inventory` cell, and is never selected otherwise, not even explicitly; the core adapter closure is never a `semanticClosures` member. A Plan that breaks either rule refuses.", "before": "evaluation inputs or remove any direct membership or retention obligation. **The core role closures are the exception (contract successor CRC-1, above).** The core provider closure is a direct `semanticClosures` member exactly when the Plan selects a `native.semantic-universe.syntax.v2` universe, and is never selected otherwise, not even explicitly; the core adapter closure is never a `semanticClosures` member. A Plan that breaks either rule refuses.", "parent": {"bytes": 135448, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/identity-and-evidence.md", "sha256": "c82404f3a0cf56fa6cc02e99cc3ebbd5356fedc3b36aeb38f9ef284077fbd31f"}, "selector": {"line": 1377}, "supersedes": {"parent": {"bytes": 135448, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/identity-and-evidence.md", "sha256": "c82404f3a0cf56fa6cc02e99cc3ebbd5356fedc3b36aeb38f9ef284077fbd31f"}, "record": {"bytes": 21493, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/crc-1/successor.json", "sha256": "29df5f5e2b145daf3c9b8e231ccac1d08c5b36d2eb006fbdc3bba543d7084166"}, "selector": {"line": 1377}}}], "selected": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/crc-2/successor.json", "sha256": "3b38b06af9c0434e2a92a78a4498a5ea13761b15fdf4e0f54cfeb6f9cf9f3b8d"}, {"inputs": [{"bytes": 17766, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/enum-1/PASSAGES.md", "sha256": "fa8f93ba60d2268ec1fad7e4b8902b86a9db5578198ba04ec1681759c9975dda"}, {"bytes": 23823, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/enum-1/README.md", "sha256": "d6a7d4743b9edb27b3a74bc2479ee73d43bebf1fa6a22d0f597f7073d1773b2a"}, {"bytes": 19002, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/enum-1/evidence/build_enum_1.py", "sha256": "d4060dd318ff6e9f4c9eb6f0f6957eea0012232b001b1efa3d8cd819d707e64f"}, {"bytes": 13212, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/enum-1/evidence/check_enum_1.py", "sha256": "90091b3c46d0579e1582d2098b99ae68407c3e0d5a48251c72a23bd7a821e323"}], "passageOverrides": [{"after": "**Enumerator.** Available bindings: `{status:\"selected\", closureId}` only. Unavailable bindings: that selected shape, **or** `{status:\"unselected\", reason:\"optional-unselected\"}` when `required=false`, `universe=null`, a lawful native `DeficiencyV2`/`nativeCause` pair (typically `provider-unavailable` with `nativeCause` null), host extents still populated, and inventories empty `unavailable` matching that pair. `required=true` plus unselected refuses (`ENUMERATION_PLAN_REQUIRED_UNSELECTED_ENUMERATOR`), except in one case (contract successor ENUM-1, 2026-10-04): **a required cell whose closure is not admitted.** When no admitted closure can lawfully be the binding's selected enumerator, because a closure the cell's mode needs is not admitted for the request (for a TypeScript or Rust mode, its provider closure; for `syntax-only`, a grammar closure, without which no syntax universe exists for the core provider closure to enumerate), the binding of a `required=true` cell takes the same unselected shape, `{status:\"unselected\", reason:\"optional-unselected\"}`, and the `reason` token keeps its spelling. Its pair is exactly deficiency `provider-unavailable` with `nativeCause` null. Its `universe` is null, its host extents are still populated, and its inventories are empty `unavailable` with that pair. It covers a required provider closure that is not installed, or that current trust does not admit (workflows-and-surfaces \u00a79, \"required provider closure not installed\"), and an ephemeral request with no admitted trust view, which admits no component closure. The cell stays required. Its unavailable work holds the Run at `indeterminate` through `requiredCellDeficiencies` (`execution-inputs-contract.v1.md` \u00a74 and \u00a75), and required-cell totality is unchanged. A required cell whose closure is admitted never takes this shape: its binding names that closure, available or not. An `inventory` cell never meets the case, because its enumerator is the core provider closure (`identity-and-evidence.md` \u00a73, core role closures). Closure admission is not an input of this admission, so the host establishes the case when it builds the Plan, and admission checks the shape. A required unselected binding with any other pair still refuses. Non-null universe plus unselected refuses (available cannot express unselected; schema and join). A selected enumerator with a missing `closureId` is a structural missing pointer, not optional-unselected. Host extents are compared for every binding, including unselected. `PROVIDER.NOT_SELECTED` is **not** this case: it is only `native-evidence.schemas.v2.json#/x-opensip-public-route-registry/keys/native.requested-capability-mode-not-selected` (NOT-SELECTED matrix cell, `REQUEST.UNSATISFIABLE`). A NOT-SELECTED cell never mints a cell here. Provider protocol/crash remains operational (`PROVIDER.PROTOCOL_VIOLATION`); it is not rewritten as unavailable evidence.", "before": "**Enumerator.** Available bindings: `{status:\"selected\", closureId}` only. Unavailable bindings: that selected shape, **or** `{status:\"unselected\", reason:\"optional-unselected\"}` when `required=false`, `universe=null`, a lawful native `DeficiencyV2`/`nativeCause` pair (typically `provider-unavailable` with `nativeCause` null), host extents still populated, and inventories empty `unavailable` matching that pair. `required=true` plus unselected refuses (`ENUMERATION_PLAN_REQUIRED_UNSELECTED_ENUMERATOR`). Non-null universe plus unselected refuses (available cannot express unselected; schema and join). A selected enumerator with a missing `closureId` is a structural missing pointer, not optional-unselected. Host extents are compared for every binding, including unselected. `PROVIDER.NOT_SELECTED` is **not** this case: it is only `native-evidence.schemas.v2.json#/x-opensip-public-route-registry/keys/native.requested-capability-mode-not-selected` (NOT-SELECTED matrix cell, `REQUEST.UNSATISFIABLE`). A NOT-SELECTED cell never mints a cell here. Provider protocol/crash remains operational (`PROVIDER.PROTOCOL_VIOLATION`); it is not rewritten as unavailable evidence.", "parent": {"bytes": 24366, "path": "docs/coop/design-corrections/foundation/enumeration-contract.v1.md", "sha256": "b7858bc8a70a41280bb6d7d1b461cc267ae22598b0dc851d4f1061bcbfa1bc59"}, "selector": {"line": 53}}, {"after": "run a provider is not what either document asks for. Outside ENUM-1's one case (**Enumerator**, above), a `required=true` cell still may not be unselected", "before": "run a provider is not what either document asks for. A `required=true` cell still may not be unselected", "parent": {"bytes": 24366, "path": "docs/coop/design-corrections/foundation/enumeration-contract.v1.md", "sha256": "b7858bc8a70a41280bb6d7d1b461cc267ae22598b0dc851d4f1061bcbfa1bc59"}, "selector": {"line": 75}}, {"after": "| Invalid / refuse (no silent unknown) | tuple mismatch; required+unselected enumerator outside ENUM-1's one case (\u00a71, **Enumerator**); bindings >128; context/universe not Plan-selected; file totality fail; external path; schema/hash corruption |", "before": "| Invalid / refuse (no silent unknown) | tuple mismatch; required+unselected enumerator; bindings >128; context/universe not Plan-selected; file totality fail; external path; schema/hash corruption |", "parent": {"bytes": 24366, "path": "docs/coop/design-corrections/foundation/enumeration-contract.v1.md", "sha256": "b7858bc8a70a41280bb6d7d1b461cc267ae22598b0dc851d4f1061bcbfa1bc59"}, "selector": {"line": 145}}, {"after": "A selected provider closure. Missing capability/provider selection for a required cell refuses pre-Plan (no fictional closure), except in contract successor ENUM-1's one case: when no admitted closure can lawfully be the binding's selected enumerator, because a closure the cell's mode needs is not admitted for the request, the binding is UnavailableProgramBindingV1 with UnselectedEnumeratorRef and deficiency provider-unavailable with nativeCause null, never a fictional closure. Executable unavailability with this selected closure is UnavailableProgramBindingV1, not an unselected enumerator.", "before": "A selected provider closure. Missing capability/provider selection for a required cell refuses pre-Plan (no fictional closure). Executable unavailability with this selected closure is UnavailableProgramBindingV1, not an unselected enumerator.", "parent": {"bytes": 20005, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/syntax-e/syn-1f/design/foundation/enumeration-plan.schema.v1.json", "sha256": "cc29483f1d45b1c620e295fe2d9d20f28159ae987bf3df410f082093ac5fad5e"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/$defs/SelectedEnumeratorRef/description"}}, {"after": "Enumerator not selected. Lawful only on UnavailableProgramBindingV1, in two cases: the cell required=false; or contract successor ENUM-1's one case, where the cell required=true and no admitted closure can lawfully be the binding's selected enumerator, because a closure the cell's mode needs is not admitted for the request, with deficiency provider-unavailable and nativeCause null. The reason token is optional-unselected in both cases. Not PROVIDER.NOT_SELECTED (matrix cell never mints a cell here). closureId is absent: a selected enumerator with a missing closure pointer is structural, not this shape.", "before": "Optional-cell enumerator not selected. Lawful only on UnavailableProgramBindingV1 when the cell required=false. Not PROVIDER.NOT_SELECTED (matrix cell never mints a cell here). closureId is absent: a selected enumerator with a missing closure pointer is structural, not this shape.", "parent": {"bytes": 20005, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/syntax-e/syn-1f/design/foundation/enumeration-plan.schema.v1.json", "sha256": "cc29483f1d45b1c620e295fe2d9d20f28159ae987bf3df410f082093ac5fad5e"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/$defs/UnselectedEnumeratorRef/description"}}, {"after": "Available bindings may only use SelectedEnumeratorRef. Unavailable bindings may use either: selected+unavailable execution/input, or unselected optional-unselected when required=false or in contract successor ENUM-1's one case (required=true, no admitted closure can lawfully be the binding's selected enumerator, because a closure the cell's mode needs is not admitted for the request, provider-unavailable with nativeCause null).", "before": "Available bindings may only use SelectedEnumeratorRef. Unavailable bindings may use either: selected+unavailable execution/input, or unselected optional-unselected when required=false.", "parent": {"bytes": 20005, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/syntax-e/syn-1f/design/foundation/enumeration-plan.schema.v1.json", "sha256": "cc29483f1d45b1c620e295fe2d9d20f28159ae987bf3df410f082093ac5fad5e"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/$defs/EnumeratorRef/description"}}, {"after": "`EXECUTION_INPUTS_CANDIDATE_REQUIRED` first, except in the enumeration contract's ENUM-1 case (`enumeration-contract.v1.md` \u00a71, **Enumerator**). There the cell's closure is not admitted, its enumerator is unselected and no producer can return an envelope, so its row is `unavailable` with the binding carrier (cross-source row 1, above) and emits its `requiredCellDeficiencies` row. The row state is unchanged \u2014 an absent or unavailable", "before": "`EXECUTION_INPUTS_CANDIDATE_REQUIRED` first. The row state is unchanged \u2014 an absent or unavailable", "parent": {"bytes": 35845, "path": "docs/coop/design-corrections/foundation/execution-inputs-contract.v1.md", "sha256": "22ee2507016aecc1102c7836e754aa5865b01121f2ca0a30d8d63f934f99ec29"}, "selector": {"line": 103}}, {"after": "pair there: it refuses an unselected enumerator on an available binding, and on a required cell except in its ENUM-1 case, which carries exactly `provider-unavailable` with `nativeCause` null; and it", "before": "pair there: it refuses an unselected enumerator on an available binding or a required cell, and", "parent": {"bytes": 35845, "path": "docs/coop/design-corrections/foundation/execution-inputs-contract.v1.md", "sha256": "22ee2507016aecc1102c7836e754aa5865b01121f2ca0a30d8d63f934f99ec29"}, "selector": {"line": 110}}, {"after": "In this order they have distinct reachable meanings: **the enumerator was not selected** (an optional, or in the enumeration contract's ENUM-1 case a required,", "before": "In this order they have distinct reachable meanings: **the enumerator was not selected** (an optional", "parent": {"bytes": 35845, "path": "docs/coop/design-corrections/foundation/execution-inputs-contract.v1.md", "sha256": "22ee2507016aecc1102c7836e754aa5865b01121f2ca0a30d8d63f934f99ec29"}, "selector": {"line": 131}}, {"after": "A candidate item's pair is the retained CandidateProducerResultV1's own (deficiency, nativeCause); failing that the binding's own DECLARED pair; failing that explicitly (null, null). The branch runs only under a selected enumerator at a non-null universe, i.e. an AvailableProgramBindingV1, which declares no deficiency/nativeCause property at all - so any binding default there would be manufactured on every path. An optional candidate cell with no retained envelope admits, so this is reachable; a required one refuses EXECUTION_INPUTS_CANDIDATE_REQUIRED first, except in the enumeration contract's ENUM-1 case, where the cell's closure is not admitted and its unselected binding's row is unavailable with the binding carrier. Row state is unchanged. An existing envelope keeps its originating candidate-producer-result ref; an absent envelope contributes no invented ref.", "before": "A candidate item's pair is the retained CandidateProducerResultV1's own (deficiency, nativeCause); failing that the binding's own DECLARED pair; failing that explicitly (null, null). The branch runs only under a selected enumerator at a non-null universe, i.e. an AvailableProgramBindingV1, which declares no deficiency/nativeCause property at all - so any binding default there would be manufactured on every path. An optional candidate cell with no retained envelope admits, so this is reachable; a required one refuses EXECUTION_INPUTS_CANDIDATE_REQUIRED first. Row state is unchanged. An existing envelope keeps its originating candidate-producer-result ref; an absent envelope contributes no invented ref.", "parent": {"bytes": 39940, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/syntax-e/syn-1f/design/foundation/execution-inputs.schema.v1.json", "sha256": "0c196cbaed43d823d07d8a84213fb3640359b21d652e988a503a32ed60bec6ac"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/x-opensip-derived-carrier-law/candidateCarrier"}}, {"after": "| Required candidate envelope `partial` or `unavailable` | that envelope's **own** `deficiency`, else the binding's own **declared** pair, else `null`/`null` \u2014 the candidate leg manufactures no carrier either (execution-inputs \u00a74). A required cell with **no** retained envelope refuses `EXECUTION_INPUTS_CANDIDATE_REQUIRED` before reaching this bridge, except in the enumeration contract's ENUM-1 case, whose unselected binding takes the row above. | `[{domain:candidate-producer-result, digest: envelope digest}]` |", "before": "| Required candidate envelope `partial` or `unavailable` | that envelope's **own** `deficiency`, else the binding's own **declared** pair, else `null`/`null` \u2014 the candidate leg manufactures no carrier either (execution-inputs \u00a74). A required cell with **no** retained envelope refuses `EXECUTION_INPUTS_CANDIDATE_REQUIRED` before reaching this bridge. | `[{domain:candidate-producer-result, digest: envelope digest}]` |", "parent": {"bytes": 51833, "path": "docs/coop/design-corrections/foundation/evaluator-composition-contract.v3.md", "sha256": "30d4d9d2e7cb78c2130ca55bbb708195e40abaa548fa53c3e68e6742b87b1f2c"}, "selector": {"line": 235}}], "passageSupersessions": [], "selected": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/enum-1/successor.json", "sha256": "3e8a230458cf6f0da5dd9f9dfc00bf3a0f0d093101011e9beab33bef031f2975"}, {"inputs": [{"bytes": 9095, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/supervisor-d/sd-8/PASSAGES.md", "sha256": "4e0497a181451ac228108966ed56d2ecfacfe3c24790118b2ec2bf7b5ddf8d3c"}, {"bytes": 12067, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/supervisor-d/sd-8/README.md", "sha256": "c906913bc72c22dcc67f475db37cc33f7f6e681bf0e2c5c91793bb88efe739e5"}, {"bytes": 11572, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/supervisor-d/sd-8/evidence/build_sd8.py", "sha256": "976f879bb6a158e3b555c01e77879e73b7587697fab9c41d56540c5dc572f2b9"}, {"bytes": 8042, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/supervisor-d/sd-8/evidence/check_sd8.py", "sha256": "a6c694773b0ee3c3e81b06e88e98b432f1ece6e9bbfdac1b86ddd63a2eeac2e9"}], "passageOverrides": [], "passageSupersessions": [{"after": "| **invalid authenticated RELEASE DECLARATION**: a row naming a capability the matrix does not register, a mode outside the registered set, a mode whose cell is `NOT-SELECTED`, a duplicate `capabilityId`, or a `preview-*` spelling (`native.release-capability-unregistered`, `\u2026-mode-unregistered`, `\u2026-mode-not-selected`, `\u2026-duplicate`) | | `request-rejected` (2) | `REQUEST.PRECONDITION_FAILED` | request detail (before PlanId; no worker is spawned) |\n| **component manifest that is an excluded form** (contract successor SD-5 of law M3-D r3, item 24; conformed by contract successor SD-7 to law M3-D r5, item 24, lead decision LD-R4-1): an authenticated component manifest (security S1: its signature envelope and signed catalog association) that current trust admits and that the analysis step can select, refused at component admission before any analysis attempt's `ExecutionId` is drawn or reserved, on the durable and the ephemeral path, as `ExcludedForm {class, subject}`, because it represents one of DR-117's manifest-class excluded forms: `EE-1`, a publisher neither first-party nor explicitly trusted; `EE-3b`, a claim of policy, persistence, rendering, termination or host-lifecycle authority (a capability outside the native capability matrix's provider capabilities); `EE-4`, untrusted native or WASM code; `EE-5a`, a project hook, a contribution-granted probe, or a root-command claim on the host-owned root namespace, which is exactly: (a) a closure-only manifest (role `toolchain`, `stdlib`, `rust-dev-llvm` or `grammar`) that declares `commands` at all; (b) an `analyzer` manifest whose command tree does not have exactly one entry without `parent`, or whose parentless entry's `name` differs from the manifest's `name`; or (c) a reserved root-command name among its root-namespace keys (its `name`, each of its `aliases` and, for `analyzer`, each alias of its parentless entry). An `analyzer` manifest's own name-bound mounted root, with any declared depth below it, is not a root-command claim, and neither is a collision with another component's live name; a command tree is never an `EE-3b` form. A manifest that the security owner's manifest admission refuses first never reaches component admission and keeps that route. No Plan, Coverage, Run or closure selection follows, so the refusal is never a `provider-unavailable` deficiency and never the not-installed golden of workflows \u00a79. A required closure that is not installed, or that current trust does not admit (an ephemeral request with no trust view included), is never an excluded form and keeps that golden: `indeterminate` (3), `COVERAGE.PROVIDER_UNAVAILABLE`, with `COMPONENT.REQUIRED_CLOSURE_NOT_INSTALLED` on a durable request. On an ephemeral request, the no-trust form included, it is `indeterminate` (3), `COVERAGE.PROVIDER_UNAVAILABLE`, `authority: ephemeral`, with no runId and no detail, because the run-termination contract's \u00a77.4 admits no detail on an ephemeral attempt (contract successor SD-8). A condition the authenticated release declaration row above routes keeps that row, and a manifest refused here never reaches HelloAck, closure selection or spawn | | `request-rejected` (2) | `EXTENSION.ADMISSION_REJECTED` | `PAYLOAD-NOT-ADMISSIBLE`, security S12's admission row for a signed document that is not admissible, carried as `domainDetail` with subject `excluded-form:<class>:<manifestDigest>` (the refused manifest's identity `closure.manifestDigest`, 64 hex): the refused manifest least by `manifestDigest`, with its first class in the order above; remedy \"This installed component is a form OpenSIP never admits. Only first-party or explicitly trusted components are admitted, and none may claim policy, persistence, rendering, termination or host-lifecycle authority, run untrusted native or WASM code, or claim a project hook, a reserved or additional root command, or a probe. Remove the component, or install its first-party release.\"; the failure envelope's `errors` is exactly that detail; every `ExcludedForm` goes to the operational record; no runId and no executionId |", "before": "| **invalid authenticated RELEASE DECLARATION**: a row naming a capability the matrix does not register, a mode outside the registered set, a mode whose cell is `NOT-SELECTED`, a duplicate `capabilityId`, or a `preview-*` spelling (`native.release-capability-unregistered`, `\u2026-mode-unregistered`, `\u2026-mode-not-selected`, `\u2026-duplicate`) | | `request-rejected` (2) | `REQUEST.PRECONDITION_FAILED` | request detail (before PlanId; no worker is spawned) |\n| **component manifest that is an excluded form** (contract successor SD-5 of law M3-D r3, item 24; conformed by contract successor SD-7 to law M3-D r5, item 24, lead decision LD-R4-1): an authenticated component manifest (security S1: its signature envelope and signed catalog association) that current trust admits and that the analysis step can select, refused at component admission before any analysis attempt's `ExecutionId` is drawn or reserved, on the durable and the ephemeral path, as `ExcludedForm {class, subject}`, because it represents one of DR-117's manifest-class excluded forms: `EE-1`, a publisher neither first-party nor explicitly trusted; `EE-3b`, a claim of policy, persistence, rendering, termination or host-lifecycle authority (a capability outside the native capability matrix's provider capabilities); `EE-4`, untrusted native or WASM code; `EE-5a`, a project hook, a contribution-granted probe, or a root-command claim on the host-owned root namespace, which is exactly: (a) a closure-only manifest (role `toolchain`, `stdlib`, `rust-dev-llvm` or `grammar`) that declares `commands` at all; (b) an `analyzer` manifest whose command tree does not have exactly one entry without `parent`, or whose parentless entry's `name` differs from the manifest's `name`; or (c) a reserved root-command name among its root-namespace keys (its `name`, each of its `aliases` and, for `analyzer`, each alias of its parentless entry). An `analyzer` manifest's own name-bound mounted root, with any declared depth below it, is not a root-command claim, and neither is a collision with another component's live name; a command tree is never an `EE-3b` form. A manifest that the security owner's manifest admission refuses first never reaches component admission and keeps that route. No Plan, Coverage, Run or closure selection follows, so the refusal is never a `provider-unavailable` deficiency and never the not-installed golden of workflows \u00a79. A required closure that is not installed, or that current trust does not admit (an ephemeral request with no trust view included), is never an excluded form and keeps that golden: `indeterminate` (3), `COVERAGE.PROVIDER_UNAVAILABLE`, `COMPONENT.REQUIRED_CLOSURE_NOT_INSTALLED`. A condition the authenticated release declaration row above routes keeps that row, and a manifest refused here never reaches HelloAck, closure selection or spawn | | `request-rejected` (2) | `EXTENSION.ADMISSION_REJECTED` | `PAYLOAD-NOT-ADMISSIBLE`, security S12's admission row for a signed document that is not admissible, carried as `domainDetail` with subject `excluded-form:<class>:<manifestDigest>` (the refused manifest's identity `closure.manifestDigest`, 64 hex): the refused manifest least by `manifestDigest`, with its first class in the order above; remedy \"This installed component is a form OpenSIP never admits. Only first-party or explicitly trusted components are admitted, and none may claim policy, persistence, rendering, termination or host-lifecycle authority, run untrusted native or WASM code, or claim a project hook, a reserved or additional root command, or a probe. Remove the component, or install its first-party release.\"; the failure envelope's `errors` is exactly that detail; every `ExcludedForm` goes to the operational record; no runId and no executionId |", "parent": {"bytes": 329013, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/native-evidence.md", "sha256": "83b99783893bec4bcca76bc043310e1d33305fc41ef85e012fbcb19e5b222ca0"}, "selector": {"line": 3540}, "supersedes": {"parent": {"bytes": 329013, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/native-evidence.md", "sha256": "83b99783893bec4bcca76bc043310e1d33305fc41ef85e012fbcb19e5b222ca0"}, "record": {"bytes": 14706, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/supervisor-d/sd-7/successor.json", "sha256": "350a249afaa001fc97292c2837395d930cc7bdc0d6b62b594b04df55d9767c04"}, "selector": {"line": 3540}}}], "selected": "docs/implementation/m3/supervisor-d/sd-8/successor.json", "sha256": "469b685071dccee4c87932d8ca0ff6a88fa27404b1a97c36fa8fd0c38683aa6e"}, {"inputs": [{"bytes": 24083, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/syntax-e/hsr/hsr-1/PASSAGES.md", "sha256": "9b10cf28b990bb81b9e70f546f9c9f6a688a6cc98b01c8dfa68da5060f6d8a67"}, {"bytes": 17625, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/syntax-e/hsr/hsr-1/README.md", "sha256": "ded9d603edfba64bc9218910cef8b22c278c0f4e7b107b93f90306095120a58b"}, {"bytes": 27707, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/syntax-e/hsr/hsr-1/evidence/build_hsr_1.py", "sha256": "ab342636e146ed10d8a936386a9679a696f2ccaec3c2ad12583fb1d762418bcb"}, {"bytes": 12449, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/syntax-e/hsr/hsr-1/evidence/check_hsr_1.py", "sha256": "216086876349c574a12b4a64beaa0f8247544ee38ac66c57df79558b674015cd"}, {"bytes": 13341, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/syntax-e/hsr/hsr-1/evidence/verify_scratch.py", "sha256": "47c833eb6b386a26bdb6bf8b3469e05cbe410facd545101751decb6558ec42e7"}], "passageOverrides": [{"after": "  document the row names** \u2014 never a canonicalization of a selected `$def`. Those bytes are the document's selected current bytes or, for a retained record only, the bytes of one of its historical schema readers (contract successor HSR-1, at the end of this section);", "before": "  document the row names** \u2014 never a canonicalization of a selected `$def`;", "parent": {"bytes": 135448, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/identity-and-evidence.md", "sha256": "c82404f3a0cf56fa6cc02e99cc3ebbd5356fedc3b36aeb38f9ef284077fbd31f"}, "selector": {"line": 662}}, {"after": "`view.schemaDigests` is the producer's own **declaration** of the schema documents its view was produced under. Each member is the raw SHA-256 of the exact full bytes of a document on the closed registry above. Those are the document's selected current bytes or, in a retained view only, the bytes of one of its historical schema readers (contract successor HSR-1, at the end of this section). A member naming anything else refuses (`SCHEMA_DOCUMENT_UNREGISTERED`). The set is **selected, not derived**: it is neither required to name every document a member fact or Coverage was admitted under, nor forbidden from naming a registered document this view admitted nothing under. It carries no admission authority of its own \u2014 every fact and Coverage pins its own document through its own `payloadSchemaDigest`, whose exact bytes are re-hashed and retained on every reference, so a document omitted here is still pinned there and a document added here admits nothing. The same rule holds wherever the `registered-schema-document` artifact class appears, including the `schema` proof-input-ref domain.", "before": "`view.schemaDigests` is the producer's own **declaration** of the schema documents its view was produced under. Each member is the raw SHA-256 of the exact full bytes of a document on the closed registry above; a member naming anything else refuses (`SCHEMA_DOCUMENT_UNREGISTERED`). The set is **selected, not derived**: it is neither required to name every document a member fact or Coverage was admitted under, nor forbidden from naming a registered document this view admitted nothing under. It carries no admission authority of its own \u2014 every fact and Coverage pins its own document through its own `payloadSchemaDigest`, whose exact bytes are re-hashed and retained on every reference, so a document omitted here is still pinned there and a document added here admits nothing. The same rule holds wherever the `registered-schema-document` artifact class appears, including the `schema` proof-input-ref domain.", "parent": {"bytes": 135448, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/identity-and-evidence.md", "sha256": "c82404f3a0cf56fa6cc02e99cc3ebbd5356fedc3b36aeb38f9ef284077fbd31f"}, "selector": {"line": 678}}, {"after": "policy over that extent.\n\n### Historical schema readers (contract successor HSR-1, 2026-10-04)\n\nA retained record names the schema document it was admitted under by the raw SHA-256 of that document's exact bytes. It does so through a `payloadSchemaDigest`, an analysis-spec or stage-spec parameter's `schemaDigest`, a `view.schemaDigests` member or a `schema` proof-input-ref, and each such document is named by a row of the payload registry above. A stage-spec's `outputSchemaDigest` names a member of its producer closure's tree instead, and a new output schema is a new closure (below). A reviewed exception to the major rule of section 3 may change a registry document's selected bytes under its existing major. A record admitted before that change still names the earlier bytes. A **historical schema reader** reads it: the earlier selected bytes of the same document, kept and named by their exact digest.\n\n**The closed table.** These are all the historical schema readers. Each row is one earlier selected version of one registry document. Its reference closure is its own bytes, and its accepted architecture copy holds exactly those bytes.\n\n| Row | Document | `$id` | sha256 | Bytes | Accepted architecture copy | Retired by |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| H1 | `native/native-evidence.schemas.v2.json` | `urn:opensip:product-v1:native:evidence-schemas:v2` | `e5834d37aebd96d77d352975878da349033f8633ecbd83322ad0fbea461f7773` | 280357 | `docs/implementation/m1/source-selection-v2/schemas/sources/native.v2.schema.json` | SYN-1 |\n| H2 | `foundation/enumeration-plan.schema.v1.json` | `opensip.product.enumeration-plan.1` | `10627cb6a22a9ff1674c16c5fa4863a58dc86e5df8ac7ae55c45747b0e60197c` | 19975 | `docs/implementation/m2/admission-runtime-selection-v1/schemas/sources/enumeration-plan-v1.schema.json` | SYN-1F |\n\n**Minting.** A boundary that creates a record names the selected current bytes of each document, and only those. The host chooses the digest: a caller may restate it but never choose it. This holds at `admit_coverage_result_v3` (native-evidence section 4, step 6), at import admission, at pre-Plan analysis-spec admission, at view production and at every other boundary that creates a record. A historical digest there refuses with the boundary's existing refusal for an unregistered document.\n\n**Retained records.** Retained closure, replay, Run closure and every later check of a retained record admit the record's carried digest when it is either the selected current digest of the document its payload registry row names, or the digest of a table row for that document.\n- The record is validated against exactly the bytes its digest names, through the payload registry row's selector. Every digest annotation and reference of that document is read from the same bytes. A reader never mixes the bytes of two versions.\n- The store still retains those schema bytes under that digest. A compiled-in reader never stands in for missing retained bytes: their absence stays retention loss.\n- A check that re-applies a producer's admission law to a retained record, such as the Coverage producer law that Run closure re-runs over retained Coverage, is a retained check. There, \"the registered document\" is the document version the record's own digest names.\n- A digest resolves to its document before any registry row, key or cardinality rule applies. A current and a historical digest of one document therefore cite one row, and two parameters of one spec citing them refuse under the one-per-row rule above.\n- No other digest is admitted. A schema `$id`, URI, major or version alone never selects a reader, and no caller, store, environment or configuration adds one.\n\n**One reader per digest.** Every digest in the selected registry and in this table names exactly one document. A row whose digest is still its document's selected current digest in an implementation, because that implementation has not yet selected the retiring successor's bytes, is read there by the current reader. It is never a second reader of the same bytes.\n\n**Identity.** Nothing is re-minted. A historical record keeps its stored identifiers: its `coverage2`, `import2` and `fact2` identities, its Plan's `analysisSpecDigest` and PlanId, and every identity above them. Their descriptors carry the historical digest, so recomputing them from retained bytes gives the stored values. The same payload admitted now under the current bytes is a different record with a different identity, and the two are never equated. `finding-key2` names no schema digest, so findings still correspond across the change.\n\n**Mixed stores and Runs.** Each record is read by the digest it carries. One store may hold records of both readers, and so may one Run: a Plan created after the change may select an import retained before it. Replay, Run closure and comparison need no mode, flag or per-store setting.\n\n**Verification.** A record admitted by a historical reader is admitted, and no defect, field, code or class is added for it. A record whose digest is neither current nor in this table refuses with its existing refusal. Every verification surface reports replay's result.\n\n**Extension.** The table changes only by a reviewed contract successor that supersedes this passage in law VD2's form. A successor that changes the selected bytes of a document named by the payload registry adds exactly one row for that document: the digest it retires, its bytes, its accepted architecture copy and the successor's name. No row is ever removed or edited, and nothing else adds one. This table keeps records that name a document by digest readable. A record that names no schema digest is read against the current bytes, so an exception that is not purely additive still needs a new major and a reviewed migration (section 3).", "before": "policy over that extent.", "parent": {"bytes": 135448, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/identity-and-evidence.md", "sha256": "c82404f3a0cf56fa6cc02e99cc3ebbd5356fedc3b36aeb38f9ef284077fbd31f"}, "selector": {"line": 808}}, {"after": "raw SHA-256 of the EXACT FULL schema DOCUMENT file bytes named by the row: the document's selected current bytes or, for a retained record only, the bytes of one of its historical schema readers in the closed table of identity-and-evidence section 3 (contract successor HSR-1). A boundary that creates a record names the current bytes only. A cited digest resolves to its document, through the current bytes and that table, before any row, key or cardinality rule applies.", "before": "raw SHA-256 of the EXACT FULL schema DOCUMENT file bytes named by the row", "parent": {"bytes": 200510, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/syntax-e/syn-1f/design/foundation/identity-schemas.v3.json", "sha256": "73645b7633d95f5d7b8e5183d789b8aab028148be0dd158e0b503a4cc3e2bd19"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/x-opensip-payload-registry/law/payloadSchemaDigest"}}, {"after": "Changed output domains dispatch explicitly to version3 records and prefixes. Frozen version2 bytes remain historical; no coercion or permissive mixed-version parse. Unchanged ancestors referenced by digest retain their existing identifier majors; their values change if committed input/schema bytes change. The single additive operation member authorized by identity-and-evidence \u00a73's reviewed M3-I1 exception keeps proof3 and the program-predicate schemaVersion; it is not a permissive mixed-version parse. The single additive NativeCause member source-parse-error, authorized by identity-and-evidence section 3's second reviewed exception (contract successors SYN-1, SYN-1F and HSR-1), keeps every identifier major as well. Retained records that name the earlier native evidence or enumeration plan schema bytes by digest are read by HSR-1's historical schema readers and keep their identifiers.", "before": "Changed output domains dispatch explicitly to version3 records and prefixes. Frozen version2 bytes remain historical; no coercion or permissive mixed-version parse. Unchanged ancestors referenced by digest retain their existing identifier majors; their values change if committed input/schema bytes change. The single additive operation member authorized by identity-and-evidence \u00a73's reviewed M3-I1 exception keeps proof3 and the program-predicate schemaVersion; it is not a permissive mixed-version parse.", "parent": {"bytes": 200510, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/syntax-e/syn-1f/design/foundation/identity-schemas.v3.json", "sha256": "73645b7633d95f5d7b8e5183d789b8aab028148be0dd158e0b503a4cc3e2bd19"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/x-opensip-evaluator-profile/majorLaw"}}], "passageSupersessions": [{"after": "major and reviewed migration, not a permissive parser. One reviewed exception: the M3-I1 successor appends the single member `cycle-representative` to the closed `operation` vocabularies of proof-bundle `predicateProofs[]` and of `program-predicate`, under their existing majors and without migration. It changes no other field, member, bound, order, recipe or prefix. No record admitted before that successor can carry the member, because the member becomes admissible in a policy only through the same successor. So every existing record keeps its bytes, identity, validity and replay result, and `finding-key2`, whose descriptor names no operation, is unaffected. A reader that selects the successor's schema bytes admits records with or without the member under the same majors. A reader that does not refuses the member as a schema mismatch and never coerces, ignores or drops it. A second reviewed exception (contract successor HSR-1): contract successors SYN-1 and SYN-1F of law M3-E1 append the single member `source-parse-error` to the closed `NativeCause` vocabulary of the native evidence schemas and of its five foundation copies, under their existing majors and without migration. It adds no other member and changes no field, bound, recipe or prefix of any record. The member takes its code-point place in each list, and no record names a list position. No record admitted before those successors carries the member. Unlike the first exception, this one changes the exact bytes of two documents that retained records name by digest: `native/native-evidence.schemas.v2.json` and `foundation/enumeration-plan.schema.v1.json`. Their earlier selected bytes stay readable as historical schema readers (HSR-1, at the end of the payload registry section below). So here too every existing record keeps its bytes, identity, validity and replay result. Every other schema or domain change keeps this rule. A later reviewed exception that changes the bytes of a document a retained record names by digest adds exactly one historical schema reader for that document, by its own successor.", "before": "major and reviewed migration, not a permissive parser. One reviewed exception: the M3-I1 successor appends the single member `cycle-representative` to the closed `operation` vocabularies of proof-bundle `predicateProofs[]` and of `program-predicate`, under their existing majors and without migration. It changes no other field, member, bound, order, recipe or prefix. No record admitted before that successor can carry the member, because the member becomes admissible in a policy only through the same successor. So every existing record keeps its bytes, identity, validity and replay result, and `finding-key2`, whose descriptor names no operation, is unaffected. A reader that selects the successor's schema bytes admits records with or without the member under the same majors. A reader that does not refuses the member as a schema mismatch and never coerces, ignores or drops it. Every other schema or domain change keeps this rule.", "parent": {"bytes": 135448, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/identity-and-evidence.md", "sha256": "c82404f3a0cf56fa6cc02e99cc3ebbd5356fedc3b36aeb38f9ef284077fbd31f"}, "selector": {"line": 214}, "supersedes": {"parent": {"bytes": 135448, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/identity-and-evidence.md", "sha256": "c82404f3a0cf56fa6cc02e99cc3ebbd5356fedc3b36aeb38f9ef284077fbd31f"}, "record": {"bytes": 35443, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/preview-pack-i1/i1-l/successor.json", "sha256": "9c490137998222627a011eec72af8c0ed3bb568e3085725fdf11b8610d42c6a1"}, "selector": {"line": 214}}}], "selected": "docs/implementation/m3/syntax-e/hsr/hsr-1/successor.json", "sha256": "224b0037105fd7aa896e2d83425136688e38c09c6bdfb7c51225da50aeb9c61e"}, {"inputs": [{"bytes": 7523, "path": "docs/implementation/fallow-expansion/capacity-r2/CONTRACT.md", "sha256": "f12adb1955fc9d4be179b7f6cba6fa01ceca02b5387b621a161f104832b105ff"}, {"bytes": 23828, "path": "docs/implementation/fallow-expansion/capacity-r2/PASSAGES.md", "sha256": "7ada8bf02664918dfc6392532c8dd0c5b87e3a52c8a1059624365e2c8cb79663"}, {"bytes": 13036, "path": "docs/implementation/fallow-expansion/capacity-r2/build.py", "sha256": "73f5c275fc0f18a6ebfbb6feeba8fe1bd37ff5daa2a9993006b28f960a570ff3"}, {"bytes": 1982, "path": "docs/implementation/fallow-expansion/capacity-r2/cases.json", "sha256": "d9c37523094eedab3f4063d934f338c22185557db74db517d61f9e28d0349005"}, {"bytes": 54922, "path": "docs/implementation/fallow-expansion/capacity-r2/schemas/common.schema.json", "sha256": "2da92b6786c4e06fafc8e60a4224d295d4a06bb379dd12c38b503d2a847135ce"}, {"bytes": 64503, "path": "docs/implementation/fallow-expansion/capacity-r2/schemas/evaluator3-common.schema.json", "sha256": "1aedb3da1571e99b97b59ba81d5df13f73cd2f16403f77435fbe469d93df8398"}, {"bytes": 202676, "path": "docs/implementation/fallow-expansion/capacity-r2/schemas/identity-schemas.v3.json", "sha256": "b3ffd2fe4f535695f291b45eaeeb27f5585e4d20dca8f09f0e8b33d6ee27c3f2"}, {"bytes": 3998, "path": "docs/implementation/fallow-expansion/evidence/capacity-shapes.json", "sha256": "20f62860a64744173419c0ee83485027e5d2909be63bf02affa8a780f3a83478"}, {"bytes": 2712, "path": "docs/implementation/fallow-expansion/evidence/capacity_shape_spike.py", "sha256": "bbf6d9661b85e1a926cfe095999b7d47e5d110a4a837777d52b4508c5087b813"}], "passageOverrides": [{"after": "- **Composed per step, because 11264 is the analysis-spec bound.** Each analysis", "before": "- **Composed per step, because 1024 is the analysis-spec bound.** Each analysis", "parent": {"bytes": 329013, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/native-evidence.md", "sha256": "83b99783893bec4bcca76bc043310e1d33305fc41ef85e012fbcb19e5b222ca0"}, "selector": {"line": 1115}}, {"after": "  (`{stepId, noticeCount, notices[\u226411264]}`), and the invocation carries", "before": "  (`{stepId, noticeCount, notices[\u22641024]}`), and the invocation carries", "parent": {"bytes": 329013, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/native-evidence.md", "sha256": "83b99783893bec4bcca76bc043310e1d33305fc41ef85e012fbcb19e5b222ca0"}, "selector": {"line": 1117}}, {"after": "  truncate \u2014 11264 is exactly the `analysis-spec.requestedCapabilities` bound and", "before": "  truncate \u2014 1024 is exactly the `analysis-spec.requestedCapabilities` bound and", "parent": {"bytes": 329013, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/native-evidence.md", "sha256": "83b99783893bec4bcca76bc043310e1d33305fc41ef85e012fbcb19e5b222ca0"}, "selector": {"line": 1119}}, {"after": "`{stepId, noticeCount, notices[\u226411264]}` \u2014 because 11264 is the", "before": "`{stepId, noticeCount, notices[\u22641024]}` \u2014 because 1024 is the", "parent": {"bytes": 133335, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/workflows-and-surfaces.md", "sha256": "1ee203e3ce626d88a53d0247881cd3ffb0d52b421821ac798b9f0b57346ff6ed"}, "selector": {"line": 1317}}, {"after": "| bounded selection array exceeds its published bound, without truncation: the scope-descriptor arrays (workspaceRoots1024, pathPrefixes/excludedPathPrefixes65536) or the analysis-spec `requestedCapabilities` (11264 under FX-CAP-1; all other bounded-selection fields keep their own limits) | | `request-rejected` (2) | `REQUEST.UNSATISFIABLE` | `PROJECT.SCOPE_LIMIT`, subject `field:count>limit`; pre-Plan for the refused analysis step, which mints no `plan2`/`run3` |", "before": "| bounded selection array exceeds its published bound, without truncation: the scope-descriptor arrays (workspaceRoots1024, pathPrefixes/excludedPathPrefixes65536) or the analysis-spec `requestedCapabilities` (1024, which the matrix-fixed default exceeds from94 TypeScript units) | | `request-rejected` (2) | `REQUEST.UNSATISFIABLE` | `PROJECT.SCOPE_LIMIT`, subject `field:count>limit`; pre-Plan for the refused analysis step, which mints no `plan2`/`run3` |", "parent": {"bytes": 329013, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/native-evidence.md", "sha256": "83b99783893bec4bcca76bc043310e1d33305fc41ef85e012fbcb19e5b222ca0"}, "selector": {"line": 3534}}, {"after": "A **bounded selection array** that exceeds its published schema bound refuses REQUEST.UNSATISFIABLE (request-rejected, exit2), public detail PROJECT.SCOPE_LIMIT and subject `field:count>limit`; it is never truncated. **Seven** fields are bounded this way, across **three record families**, and the subject always names which one overflowed. The **scope descriptor** family contributes three: workspaceRoots has limit1024 (distinct roots after co-located unit deduplication), and pathPrefixes/excludedPathPrefixes each65536; thus1025..4096 valid discovered unit roots produce this typed scope refusal, while more than4096 first-party unit directories produce PROJECT.WORKSPACE_UNIT_LIMIT earlier. The **analysis-spec** array `requestedCapabilities` has limit11264 (contract successor FX-CAP-1). The current selected matrix requests11 capabilities per TypeScript unit and10 per Rust unit, so1024 all-TypeScript selected language program units require11264 rows and fit the cardinality bound. Colocated TypeScript and Rust units count separately for this sizing target while workspaceRoots still deduplicates their root. This raises no root, file, path, record-byte, frame, stage, dependency or work bound and does not guarantee that every1024-unit input fits those independent bounds. There is no new1024-unit admission ceiling. A matrix expansion reassesses this target by a reviewed successor; it does not silently raise this constant. The public code is shared across host/native scope admission; callers must narrow explicit selection \u2014 for `requestedCapabilities` that means fewer workspace roots for the invocation, or an explicit `analysis.capabilities` selection carrying its own provenance. The **Plan** family contributes the remaining three, and they are reachable by ordinary valid selections that every earlier bound admits: `nativeContextDigests` has limit1024 (FX-CAP-1) and carries one member per DISTINCT admitted native context, so an inventory-only or otherwise narrow capability selection keeps `requestedCapabilities` far below its own11264 while1025 distinct admitted contexts cannot be expressed in the Plan, including when colocated language units share selected workspace roots; identical full context descriptors still deduplicate; `importIds` has limit256 while the selecting field `semantic-configuration.evidence.importIds` admits1024, so257 imported evidence records are an admitted configuration and an unrepresentable Plan; and `semanticClosures` has limit128 while `policyPackIds` admits128 packs that need not share closures. Those three are accounted **before plan2 is minted**, on the PROSPECTIVE Plan assembled from an already-admitted request, so a refusal mints no Plan and no Run for the refused step. They are **not** a retained-record check: an externally retained Plan over its bound is a corrupt or malformed retained record and keeps its schema-first refusal and this section's origin-dependent routing, because re-deriving a caller's remedy from already-committed bytes would misreport a corrupt store as an oversized request. Only an ACTUAL array over its bound refuses on cardinality; a missing field, null, boolean, number, string or object is a shape the schema owns and reaches it untouched. When more than one array of an assembled prospective Plan overflows at once the subject is the first in the published `$defs/plan` declaration order \u2014 `semanticClosures`, `nativeContextDigests`, `importIds` \u2014 so one request always yields one subject and narrowing makes deterministic progress. That order governs the prospective-Plan boundary, among the fields present there. It is not an ordering claim about the whole request: `nativeContextDigests` is also accounted at the earlier producer boundary that computes, admission-validates and deduplicates the native contexts, and an overflow refuses there before any prospective Plan is assembled. Native ScopeRefusal retains detail, D9 and observed bound fields for the host's closed termination projection, and is already field-generic, so all seven fields use one law and one code: the remedy class is identical \u2014 narrow the selection explicitly, nothing was truncated \u2014 which is why widening it across the further record families adds no public code, while each field keeps its OWN remedy wording because what a caller narrows differs per field. The effective schema sources for FX-CAP-1 are the exact complete copies schemas/identity-schemas.v3.json, schemas/common.schema.json and schemas/evaluator3-common.schema.json under docs/implementation/fallow-expansion/capacity-r2/. They replace only the stated four numeric maxima and carry the selected major-law/notice annotations; every other effective schema value is retained. Numeric maxima are selected through these copies, not text-value JSON Pointer overrides. Implementation generation materializes these selected copies under existing source URIs in one reviewed schema unit; no caller can substitute an earlier source as a current minting schema.", "before": "A **bounded selection array** that exceeds its published schema bound refuses REQUEST.UNSATISFIABLE (request-rejected, exit2), public detail PROJECT.SCOPE_LIMIT and subject `field:count>limit`; it is never truncated. **Seven** fields are bounded this way, across **three record families**, and the subject always names which one overflowed. The **scope descriptor** family contributes three: workspaceRoots has limit1024 (distinct roots after co-located unit deduplication), and pathPrefixes/excludedPathPrefixes each65536; thus1025..4096 valid discovered unit roots produce this typed scope refusal, while more than4096 first-party unit directories produce PROJECT.WORKSPACE_UNIT_LIMIT earlier. The **analysis-spec** array `requestedCapabilities` has limit1024, and it overflows for a reason worth stating: the matrix-fixed default requests one row per (discovered unit, capability whose cell is not NOT-SELECTED), which is11 capabilities per TypeScript unit and10 per Rust unit, so93 TypeScript units fit, at1023 rows - one row under the bound, not exactly at it - and94 do not, at1034. Units94..1024 are therefore ordinary repositories the scope law admits and the default analysis cannot express, and they refuse HERE rather than through a generic schema exception that carries no typed scope projection \u2014 no public detail, no class or exit, no `field:count>limit` subject and no per-field remedy \u2014 even though its own structured fields do identify the failing path and the bound. The public code is shared across host/native scope admission; callers must narrow explicit selection \u2014 for `requestedCapabilities` that means fewer workspace roots for the invocation, or an explicit `analysis.capabilities` selection carrying its own provenance. The **Plan** family contributes the remaining three, and they are reachable by ordinary valid selections that every earlier bound admits: `nativeContextDigests` has limit128 and carries one member per DISTINCT admitted native context, so an inventory-only or otherwise narrow capability selection keeps `requestedCapabilities` far below its own1024 while129 units whose compiler closure, standard library and effective options differ pass the workspace bound and cannot be expressed in the Plan; `importIds` has limit256 while the selecting field `semantic-configuration.evidence.importIds` admits1024, so257 imported evidence records are an admitted configuration and an unrepresentable Plan; and `semanticClosures` has limit128 while `policyPackIds` admits128 packs that need not share closures. Those three are accounted **before plan2 is minted**, on the PROSPECTIVE Plan assembled from an already-admitted request, so a refusal mints no Plan and no Run for the refused step. They are **not** a retained-record check: an externally retained Plan over its bound is a corrupt or malformed retained record and keeps its schema-first refusal and this section's origin-dependent routing, because re-deriving a caller's remedy from already-committed bytes would misreport a corrupt store as an oversized request. Only an ACTUAL array over its bound refuses on cardinality; a missing field, null, boolean, number, string or object is a shape the schema owns and reaches it untouched. When more than one array of an assembled prospective Plan overflows at once the subject is the first in the published `$defs/plan` declaration order \u2014 `semanticClosures`, `nativeContextDigests`, `importIds` \u2014 so one request always yields one subject and narrowing makes deterministic progress. That order governs the prospective-Plan boundary, among the fields present there. It is not an ordering claim about the whole request: `nativeContextDigests` is also accounted at the earlier producer boundary that computes, admission-validates and deduplicates the native contexts, and an overflow refuses there before any prospective Plan is assembled. Native ScopeRefusal retains detail, D9 and observed bound fields for the host's closed termination projection, and is already field-generic, so all seven fields use one law and one code: the remedy class is identical \u2014 narrow the selection explicitly, nothing was truncated \u2014 which is why widening it across the further record families adds no public code, while each field keeps its OWN remedy wording because what a caller narrows differs per field.", "parent": {"bytes": 329013, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/native-evidence.md", "sha256": "83b99783893bec4bcca76bc043310e1d33305fc41ef85e012fbcb19e5b222ca0"}, "selector": {"line": 4276}}, {"after": "What this refusal is **not**. It is not a truncation: no capability is dropped, no root is dropped, and the matrix-fixed default and every advertised capability promise are unchanged. It is not a host defect: the host computed the default correctly and the *request* simply cannot be expressed within a published bound, so it is `request-rejected`/`REQUEST.UNSATISFIABLE` and never `host-invariant`. It is not a licence to shard the invocation, execute extra steps or raise a bound \u2014 those require their own selection. FX-CAP-1 selects only the two coordinated identity array maxima and both per-step notice maxima; sharding and extra invocation steps remain unselected. And it is **pre-Plan for the analysis step it refuses**: that step mints no `plan2` and no `run3`. That is a statement about the step, not about attribution - the request and the invocation keep their ordinary operational attribution under the workflow law, and any earlier step of the same invocation that already committed an outcome keeps it. A pure helper `ValidationError` is not a public termination, which is exactly why the typed refusal exists.", "before": "What this refusal is **not**. It is not a truncation: no capability is dropped, no root is dropped, and the matrix-fixed default and every advertised capability promise are unchanged. It is not a host defect: the host computed the default correctly and the *request* simply cannot be expressed within a published bound, so it is `request-rejected`/`REQUEST.UNSATISFIABLE` and never `host-invariant`. It is not a licence to shard the invocation, execute extra steps or raise a bound \u2014 any of those would be a new product capability and none is selected here. And it is **pre-Plan for the analysis step it refuses**: that step mints no `plan2` and no `run3`. That is a statement about the step, not about attribution - the request and the invocation keep their ordinary operational attribution under the workflow law, and any earlier step of the same invocation that already committed an outcome keeps it. A pure helper `ValidationError` is not a public termination, which is exactly why the typed refusal exists.", "parent": {"bytes": 329013, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/native-evidence.md", "sha256": "83b99783893bec4bcca76bc043310e1d33305fc41ef85e012fbcb19e5b222ca0"}, "selector": {"line": 4280}}, {"after": "The availability account of one analysis step, associated by stepId. Under FX-CAP-1 notices is bounded at11264 because that is the analysis-spec requestedCapabilities bound and one selection produces at most one absence per requested row. No notice is truncated; noticeCount equals the array length. Ownership tuple uniqueness is within a step; the same tuple may recur across steps.", "before": "The availability account of ONE analysis step's own selection, associated with that step by stepId. `notices` is bounded at 1024 because that is the analysis-spec requestedCapabilities bound and one selection produces at most one absence per requested row, so a STEP's collection can never truncate and its noticeCount always equals its array length. The same ownership tuple may recur in different steps - two steps may select the same unit - so uniqueness is within a step, never across the invocation.", "parent": {"bytes": 55042, "path": "docs/coop/design-corrections/workflows/schemas/common.schema.json", "sha256": "b7b25d5e7c2bf2c8d3496a2f222c31bcc87360f3e2541fbdb551136358f7d39c"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/$defs/CapabilityAvailabilityStepV1/description"}}, {"after": "The availability account of one analysis step, associated by stepId. Under FX-CAP-1 notices is bounded at11264 because that is the analysis-spec requestedCapabilities bound and one selection produces at most one absence per requested row. No notice is truncated; noticeCount equals the array length. Ownership tuple uniqueness is within a step; the same tuple may recur across steps.", "before": "The availability account of ONE analysis step's own selection, associated with that step by stepId. `notices` is bounded at 1024 because that is the analysis-spec requestedCapabilities bound and one selection produces at most one absence per requested row, so a STEP's collection can never truncate and its noticeCount always equals its array length. The same ownership tuple may recur in different steps - two steps may select the same unit - so uniqueness is within a step, never across the invocation.", "parent": {"bytes": 64623, "path": "docs/coop/design-corrections/workflows/schemas/evaluator3/common.schema.json", "sha256": "ce45b9ff712fa0ea4302fbdaf04cb84dfd8a3cf734f4f9677c4547672af30a8b"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/$defs/CapabilityAvailabilityStepV1/description"}}], "passageSupersessions": [{"after": "major and reviewed migration, not a permissive parser. One reviewed exception: the M3-I1 successor appends the single member `cycle-representative` to the closed `operation` vocabularies of proof-bundle `predicateProofs[]` and of `program-predicate`, under their existing majors and without migration. It changes no other field, member, bound, order, recipe or prefix. No record admitted before that successor can carry the member, because the member becomes admissible in a policy only through the same successor. So every existing record keeps its bytes, identity, validity and replay result, and `finding-key2`, whose descriptor names no operation, is unaffected. A reader that selects the successor's schema bytes admits records with or without the member under the same majors. A reader that does not refuses the member as a schema mismatch and never coerces, ignores or drops it. A second reviewed exception (contract successor HSR-1): contract successors SYN-1 and SYN-1F of law M3-E1 append the single member `source-parse-error` to the closed `NativeCause` vocabulary of the native evidence schemas and of its five foundation copies, under their existing majors and without migration. It adds no other member and changes no field, bound, recipe or prefix of any record. The member takes its code-point place in each list, and no record names a list position. No record admitted before those successors carries the member. Unlike the first exception, this one changes the exact bytes of two documents that retained records name by digest: `native/native-evidence.schemas.v2.json` and `foundation/enumeration-plan.schema.v1.json`. Their earlier selected bytes stay readable as historical schema readers (HSR-1, at the end of the payload registry section below). So here too every existing record keeps its bytes, identity, validity and replay result. Every other schema or domain change keeps this rule. A later reviewed exception that changes the bytes of a document a retained record names by digest adds exactly one historical schema reader for that document, by its own successor. A third narrowly scoped reviewed exception (FX-CAP-1): analysis-spec.requestedCapabilities.maxItems increases from 1024 to 11264; plan.nativeContextDigests.maxItems increases from 128 to 1024; CapabilityAvailabilityStepV1.notices.maxItems increases from 1024 to 11264 in both workflow common schema versions. Existing majors, fields, domains, preimages, meanings and authority are retained. Every earlier admitted record preserves its exact bytes, identity and replay result; older readers may refuse the larger sets and never truncate or reinterpret them. No directly registered payload-schema document changes, so no historical payload reader row is added; retained schema dependency closures remain exact. All other schema/domain changes and HSR reader-extension rules retain their prior law.", "before": "major and reviewed migration, not a permissive parser. One reviewed exception: the M3-I1 successor appends the single member `cycle-representative` to the closed `operation` vocabularies of proof-bundle `predicateProofs[]` and of `program-predicate`, under their existing majors and without migration. It changes no other field, member, bound, order, recipe or prefix. No record admitted before that successor can carry the member, because the member becomes admissible in a policy only through the same successor. So every existing record keeps its bytes, identity, validity and replay result, and `finding-key2`, whose descriptor names no operation, is unaffected. A reader that selects the successor's schema bytes admits records with or without the member under the same majors. A reader that does not refuses the member as a schema mismatch and never coerces, ignores or drops it. A second reviewed exception (contract successor HSR-1): contract successors SYN-1 and SYN-1F of law M3-E1 append the single member `source-parse-error` to the closed `NativeCause` vocabulary of the native evidence schemas and of its five foundation copies, under their existing majors and without migration. It adds no other member and changes no field, bound, recipe or prefix of any record. The member takes its code-point place in each list, and no record names a list position. No record admitted before those successors carries the member. Unlike the first exception, this one changes the exact bytes of two documents that retained records name by digest: `native/native-evidence.schemas.v2.json` and `foundation/enumeration-plan.schema.v1.json`. Their earlier selected bytes stay readable as historical schema readers (HSR-1, at the end of the payload registry section below). So here too every existing record keeps its bytes, identity, validity and replay result. Every other schema or domain change keeps this rule. A later reviewed exception that changes the bytes of a document a retained record names by digest adds exactly one historical schema reader for that document, by its own successor.", "parent": {"bytes": 135448, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/identity-and-evidence.md", "sha256": "c82404f3a0cf56fa6cc02e99cc3ebbd5356fedc3b36aeb38f9ef284077fbd31f"}, "selector": {"line": 214}, "supersedes": {"parent": {"bytes": 135448, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/identity-and-evidence.md", "sha256": "c82404f3a0cf56fa6cc02e99cc3ebbd5356fedc3b36aeb38f9ef284077fbd31f"}, "record": {"bytes": 18546, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/syntax-e/hsr/hsr-1/successor.json", "sha256": "224b0037105fd7aa896e2d83425136688e38c09c6bdfb7c51225da50aeb9c61e"}, "selector": {"line": 214}}}, {"after": "Changed output domains dispatch explicitly to version3 records and prefixes. Frozen version2 bytes remain historical; no coercion or permissive mixed-version parse. Unchanged ancestors referenced by digest retain their existing identifier majors; their values change if committed input/schema bytes change. The single additive operation member authorized by identity-and-evidence \u00a73's reviewed M3-I1 exception keeps proof3 and the program-predicate schemaVersion; it is not a permissive mixed-version parse. The single additive NativeCause member source-parse-error, authorized by identity-and-evidence section 3's second reviewed exception (contract successors SYN-1, SYN-1F and HSR-1), keeps every identifier major as well. Retained records that name the earlier native evidence or enumeration plan schema bytes by digest are read by HSR-1's historical schema readers and keep their identifiers. A third narrowly scoped reviewed exception (FX-CAP-1): analysis-spec.requestedCapabilities.maxItems increases from 1024 to 11264; plan.nativeContextDigests.maxItems increases from 128 to 1024; CapabilityAvailabilityStepV1.notices.maxItems increases from 1024 to 11264 in both workflow common schema versions. Existing majors, fields, domains, preimages, meanings and authority are retained. Every earlier admitted record preserves its exact bytes, identity and replay result; older readers may refuse the larger sets and never truncate or reinterpret them. No directly registered payload-schema document changes, so no historical payload reader row is added; retained schema dependency closures remain exact. All other schema/domain changes and HSR reader-extension rules retain their prior law.", "before": "Changed output domains dispatch explicitly to version3 records and prefixes. Frozen version2 bytes remain historical; no coercion or permissive mixed-version parse. Unchanged ancestors referenced by digest retain their existing identifier majors; their values change if committed input/schema bytes change. The single additive operation member authorized by identity-and-evidence \u00a73's reviewed M3-I1 exception keeps proof3 and the program-predicate schemaVersion; it is not a permissive mixed-version parse. The single additive NativeCause member source-parse-error, authorized by identity-and-evidence section 3's second reviewed exception (contract successors SYN-1, SYN-1F and HSR-1), keeps every identifier major as well. Retained records that name the earlier native evidence or enumeration plan schema bytes by digest are read by HSR-1's historical schema readers and keep their identifiers.", "parent": {"bytes": 200510, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/syntax-e/syn-1f/design/foundation/identity-schemas.v3.json", "sha256": "73645b7633d95f5d7b8e5183d789b8aab028148be0dd158e0b503a4cc3e2bd19"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/x-opensip-evaluator-profile/majorLaw"}, "supersedes": {"parent": {"bytes": 200510, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/syntax-e/syn-1f/design/foundation/identity-schemas.v3.json", "sha256": "73645b7633d95f5d7b8e5183d789b8aab028148be0dd158e0b503a4cc3e2bd19"}, "record": {"bytes": 18546, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/syntax-e/hsr/hsr-1/successor.json", "sha256": "224b0037105fd7aa896e2d83425136688e38c09c6bdfb7c51225da50aeb9c61e"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/x-opensip-evaluator-profile/majorLaw"}}}], "selected": "docs/implementation/fallow-expansion/capacity-r2/successor.json", "sha256": "9f7de1a2a27414d24834ee41ddbd8fa7017580e70c75a526fa4fa55540c51d16"}, {"inputs": [{"bytes": 9900, "path": "docs/implementation/fallow-expansion/components-r2/CONTRACT.md", "sha256": "f316c5c7adfab657593a1d7eb666aa3f1a76bd6c875198c05bc685184e235610"}, {"bytes": 10081, "path": "docs/implementation/fallow-expansion/components-r2/PASSAGES.md", "sha256": "1b8f6255fbf57982db3714f89d6097ad445fbeb0eda1567cfe46927ed2510d5b"}, {"bytes": 3494, "path": "docs/implementation/fallow-expansion/components-r2/build.py", "sha256": "655f3a8a23a0dfa1dfde6367266caada8ff2b9be281fc28507b1a28eddf25470"}, {"bytes": 3097, "path": "docs/implementation/fallow-expansion/components-r2/check.py", "sha256": "f84a7d5299c16c3fde45cbc39a9f23e5c6f4a43701ae75f7f5437ee5fdbd66ee"}, {"bytes": 5486, "path": "docs/implementation/fallow-expansion/components-r2/component-source-model.schema.json", "sha256": "254d4cf58fa639a909901cb3faab52dbdae70a69e7df470dcd88833d656b0f97"}, {"bytes": 17884, "path": "docs/implementation/fallow-expansion/components-r2/fixtures.json", "sha256": "1b4c729f04c6ee169d2fbc0cc394520abaa1b7d71bfff27255822fa9ac04e66d"}, {"bytes": 7551, "path": "docs/implementation/fallow-expansion/components-r2/reference.py", "sha256": "914f1f325d6e55e19613a02ce7e915ca17b569741d00121bb747a61a1fc0813d"}], "passageOverrides": [{"after": "they are composed in \u00a74.5 from manifest, entry points, edges and declared consumers.\n\n### Shared original-source extraction model (FX-COMP-1)\n\nThe following exact source model is selected for staged Vue/Svelte/Astro/CSS adapter design. It is an extraction intermediate only; existing native capability cells, grammars, public fault routes and facts remain unchanged until their own reviewed adapter successors select a bridge. Its schema, pure reference and fixtures are the candidate members of FX-COMP-1.\n\n## The closed record and its authority\n\n`ComponentSourceModelV1` is the closed record in `component-source-model.schema.json`. It is an independently checkable extraction intermediate, not `fact2`, Coverage, a Run, an imported observation, a new identifier domain or a prepared-output grant. Its `source` names one admitted snapshot regular file by canonical path, raw SHA-256 and exact byte length. `snapshotId` is that admitted source snapshot. `producerClosureId` is the exact retained authenticated closure containing the extractor/parser and its complete dependency/specification bytes; an ambient parser name/version is insufficient.\n\nThe record can be constructed and validated by the syntax/adapter layer after source admission. It acquires no authority by being schema-valid. Its canonical SHA-256 may be carried by a later selected stage-input commitment only when that stage's exact successor defines the carrier, retention and admission bridge. Until then, it is design/reference evidence only and cannot enter a current closed Plan/stage/fact record as an undeclared property. The source, any derived blobs, parser closure and mapping specification must be available to reconstruct extraction when a future bridge selects it; a missing blob or closure never permits a complete claim.\n\nThe closed formats are `vue`, `svelte`, `astro`, `css`, `typescript`, `javascript`, `rust`. Whole ordinary source files use one `whole` region, with their existing dialect/grammar rules unchanged. The three component formats can carry `container`, `script`, `template`, `style` and `unmapped` regions. CSS uses a `whole` region. Template languages are distinct: `vue-template`, `svelte-template`, `astro-template`; no adapter treats one as another or treats a template as resolved TS/JS solely because expression text resembles it.\n\n## Original byte partition\n\nRegions are in original byte order. `regionId` equals the zero-based ordinal. For a nonempty file, regions partition `[0, byteLength)` exactly: the first starts at0, each next starts at the preceding end, all are nonempty, and the last ends at byteLength. Overlap, duplicate ownership, a gap, out-of-range position or an incorrect original content digest refuses the intermediate. An empty file has exactly zero regions. An extractor represents bytes it cannot classify as `unmapped`, with language `unknown`, state `unsupported` and blocker `unmapped-region`; it never omits them.\n\nOffsets are original bytes, not Unicode code points or UTF-16 code units. A region or mapping endpoint used as a text span must be at a valid UTF-8 boundary. Exact text-span translation rejects a malformed UTF-8 original; its byte inventory remains available for diagnostic accounting and grants no text span. A malformed UTF-8 file is represented by a whole unmapped region with `malformed` state and `malformed-source`, rather than repairing encoding silently. Conversions from a compiler's UTF-16 offsets must be checked against the original or explicitly retained derived buffer before emission. BOMs, newline forms and non-ASCII text are preserved. Neither normalization nor decoder replacement changes source identity.\n\nClassification of delimiters/comments/markup as `container` requires the selected component grammar's rule; it is not a way to hide unknown consumer-bearing bytes. Script language follows an explicit supported `lang` attribute or the selected format's documented default. Unsupported preprocessors stay unavailable; host analysis executes no preprocessor/config to make them complete. Grammar-specific region production is specified and independently tested in each adapter successor; this foundation supplies validation, not an invented parser for every format.\n\n## Derived bytes and maps\n\nA region either uses original bytes directly (`derived: null`) or names a retained derived blob by raw SHA-256/byte length and a complete sequence of mapping segments. Derived segments partition `[0, derived.byteLength)` in order, with nonempty ranges, unless the blob is empty, when the segment list is empty. Each segment is exactly one of:\n\n- `exact`: original source start/end are nonnull and inside this region; lengths match, both endpoint pairs are valid UTF-8 boundaries, and the two byte slices are identical.\n- `anchor`: original source start/end are nonnull and inside this region; endpoints are valid UTF-8 boundaries. Bytes and lengths may differ. This is an explanatory original-source anchor only, not token identity, semantic equivalence or repair authority.\n- `generated`: both source endpoints are null. It has no original-source citation and cannot become an original fact/repair span.\n\nSource ranges in different derived segments may recur for generated copies, but never make different original regions own the same original bytes. Derived identities do not replace original snapshot identity. A proposed derived span translates to an exact original span only if all intersecting segments are `exact`, both span endpoints translate, and the corresponding original ranges are contiguous and in the same original order. Any anchor/generated segment or discontiguous source result prevents that exact translation. No bounding-box approximation, first-segment pick or guessed filename is allowed.\n\nA source map failing structural/content validation refuses that intermediate. An adapter may emit a separately reconstructed direct-source region with `invalid-source-map` and incomplete state; it may not salvage authoritative mapped facts from a rejected map. A derived schema-valid buffer alone is not authority to call it equivalent source.\n\n## Completeness and consumer relationships\n\n`complete`, `partial`, `unsupported`, `malformed` describe extraction only. A complete region has no blockers. The model's state is `complete` exactly when every region is complete (the empty model is extraction-complete), otherwise `partial`. This never proves resolution-complete, closed exports, complete template consumers, class resolution, reachability non-use or repair eligibility. Only existing or later selected per-relation Coverage can prove those propositions over its full admitted population.\n\nThe blocker vocabulary is closed in the schema. Dynamic component selection/class construction, unresolved preprocessors, an unavailable grammar, unsupported syntax, unclassified bytes and config requiring evaluation remain explicit; no empty relation set proves that missing consumers do not exist. An unsupported script/template/style portion that could consume a subject blocks that subject's universal non-use and destructive repair unless a separately selected rule establishes a complete relevant population without excluding the portion.\n\nThe native provider retains separate lexical scopes for each script/template construct until its adapter law defines the cross-region binding relation. Framework-generated helper names never become public original symbols by accident. CSS selector references, CSS-in-JS and dynamic class expressions start as separately identified candidates; extraction completeness does not qualify them as resolved references or safe unused-style deletion.\n\n## Admission checks and bounds\n\nValidate the complete JSON shape first against this exact document; enforce unchanged canonical JSON byte/depth rules and current scalar/path/snapshot/closure rules. Then join the source to the admitted snapshot and re-hash bytes, join the producer closure, validate the original partition and format/kind/language compatibility, join/re-hash derived blobs, validate maps, and derive the model state. Bounds: at most65536 regions per file, at most65536 segments per derived region, and canonical intermediate bytes at most4 MiB. Derived/source bytes remain under the selecting stage's existing explicit byte/work bounds; this model grants none of its own file reads. Counts are checked before allocating from an untrusted declaration. A schema maximum does not override the record-byte maximum.\n\nValidation defects are internal intermediate defects. A future producer bridge must supply exact existing/native fault projection before this record can cross an authoritative boundary; this foundation creates no public code or fallback. Reference rejection means not admissible, never a silently partial authoritative record.\n\n## Staged implementation and acceptance\n\nFirst implement the pure record validator and original/derived span translation with retained reference fixtures. Then each adapter successor selects pinned grammar/compiler closures, complete extraction algorithms and dialect/version scope, source input ownership, capability/fact/Coverage schema bridges, refusal routes, default capability effects and policy eligibility. It recomputes the full-default capacity envelope when it widens the matrix. Recognition registry changes remain separate host-owned changes.\n\nThe independently adjudicated cases must cover original-source hashing/partition, empty files, Unicode/BOM/CRLF boundaries, exact/anchor/generated maps, duplicate source copies, cross-segment/discontiguous mapping, malformed/unsupported templates, dynamic components/classes, missing grammar/preprocessor and unresolved cross-region bindings. Semantic and repair positives require adapter-specific consumer-completeness cases, including script used only from a template. This subject grants no adapter or rule qualification.", "before": "they are composed in \u00a74.5 from manifest, entry points, edges and declared consumers.", "parent": {"bytes": 329013, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/native-evidence.md", "sha256": "83b99783893bec4bcca76bc043310e1d33305fc41ef85e012fbcb19e5b222ca0"}, "selector": {"line": 2777}}], "passageSupersessions": [], "selected": "docs/implementation/fallow-expansion/components-r2/successor.json", "sha256": "cd1e397d71fe1af006d679f6d2757cc8d55321dc37e061f4d45ae9ede1dffbad"}, {"inputs": [{"bytes": 7148, "path": "docs/implementation/fallow-expansion/DECISIONS.md", "sha256": "358f7891d9194e39d8d6c4edd704dad621dead4f6356bb67a337f33f276f7da7"}, {"bytes": 6455, "path": "docs/implementation/fallow-expansion/GAP-REGISTER-r2.md", "sha256": "56f9502fc21d9de1f66815131b5de6d4ba4e07d58812be242b24cd9f2093c942"}, {"bytes": 5621, "path": "docs/implementation/fallow-expansion/IMPLEMENTATION-r2.md", "sha256": "34f02e49b4f8386decf0c7cb97e4ccf5f6d3fa1b86aae83b2a363a618376c75b"}, {"bytes": 3633, "path": "docs/implementation/fallow-expansion/PACKAGES.md", "sha256":