) | `REQUEST.UNSATISFIABLE` | `PROJECT.SCOPE_LIMIT`, the S-B field and exact observed/limit subject; before Plan minting, no Plan or Run minted for the refused step |\n| **host-derived subject-scope subjects or canonical descriptor over its S-B ceiling**, after execution at H atomic admission; no truncation or splitting | | `request-rejected` (2) | `REQUEST.UNSATISFIABLE` | `PROJECT.SCOPE_LIMIT`, S-B subject-scope count or canonical-byte field; actual H/J effect, settlement and composition boundaries remain; no admitted Run minted from refused assembly and an already minted Plan is not undone |", "before": "| bounded selection array exceeds its published bound, without truncation: the scope-descriptor arrays (workspaceRoots1024, pathPrefixes/excludedPathPrefixes65536) or the analysis-spec `requestedCapabilities` (11264 under FX-CAP-1; all other bounded-selection fields keep their own limits) | | `request-rejected` (2) | `REQUEST.UNSATISFIABLE` | `PROJECT.SCOPE_LIMIT`, subject `field:count>limit`; pre-Plan for the refused analysis step, which mints no `plan2`/`run3` |", "parent": {"bytes": 329013, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/native-evidence.md", "sha256": "83b99783893bec4bcca76bc043310e1d33305fc41ef85e012fbcb19e5b222ca0"}, "selector": {"line": 3534}, "supersedes": {"parent": {"bytes": 329013, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/native-evidence.md", "sha256": "83b99783893bec4bcca76bc043310e1d33305fc41ef85e012fbcb19e5b222ca0"}, "record": {"bytes": 30391, "path": "docs/implementation/fallow-expansion/capacity-r2/successor.json", "sha256": "9f7de1a2a27414d24834ee41ddbd8fa7017580e70c75a526fa4fa55540c51d16"}, "selector": {"line": 3534}}}, {"after": "A **bounded selection array** that exceeds its published schema bound refuses REQUEST.UNSATISFIABLE (request-rejected, exit2), public detail PROJECT.SCOPE_LIMIT and subject `field:count>limit`; it is never truncated. **Seven** fields are bounded this way, across **three record families**, and the subject always names which one overflowed. The **scope descriptor** family contributes three: workspaceRoots has limit1024 (distinct roots after co-located unit deduplication), and pathPrefixes/excludedPathPrefixes each65536; thus1025..4096 valid discovered unit roots produce this typed scope refusal, while more than4096 first-party unit directories produce PROJECT.WORKSPACE_UNIT_LIMIT earlier. The **analysis-spec** array `requestedCapabilities` has limit11264 (contract successor FX-CAP-1). The current selected matrix requests11 capabilities per TypeScript unit and10 per Rust unit, so1024 all-TypeScript selected language program units require11264 rows and fit the cardinality bound. Colocated TypeScript and Rust units count separately for this sizing target while workspaceRoots still deduplicates their root. This raises no root, file, path, record-byte, frame, stage, dependency or work bound and does not guarantee that every1024-unit input fits those independent bounds. There is no new1024-unit admission ceiling. A matrix expansion reassesses this target by a reviewed successor; it does not silently raise this constant. The public code is shared across host/native scope admission; callers must narrow explicit selection \u2014 for `requestedCapabilities` that means fewer workspace roots for the invocation, or an explicit `analysis.capabilities` selection carrying its own provenance. The **Plan** family contributes the remaining three, and they are reachable by ordinary valid selections that every earlier bound admits: `nativeContextDigests` has limit1024 (FX-CAP-1) and carries one member per DISTINCT admitted native context, so an inventory-only or otherwise narrow capability selection keeps `requestedCapabilities` far below its own11264 while1025 distinct admitted contexts cannot be expressed in the Plan, including when colocated language units share selected workspace roots; identical full context descriptors still deduplicate; `importIds` has limit256 while the selecting field `semantic-configuration.evidence.importIds` admits1024, so257 imported evidence records are an admitted configuration and an unrepresentable Plan; and `semanticClosures` has limit128 while `policyPackIds` admits128 packs that need not share closures. Those three are accounted **before plan2 is minted**, on the PROSPECTIVE Plan assembled from an already-admitted request, so a refusal mints no Plan and no Run for the refused step. They are **not** a retained-record check: an externally retained Plan over its bound is a corrupt or malformed retained record and keeps its schema-first refusal and this section's origin-dependent routing, because re-deriving a caller's remedy from already-committed bytes would misreport a corrupt store as an oversized request. Only an ACTUAL array over its bound refuses on cardinality; a missing field, null, boolean, number, string or object is a shape the schema owns and reaches it untouched. When more than one array of an assembled prospective Plan overflows at once the subject is the first in the published `$defs/plan` declaration order \u2014 `semanticClosures`, `nativeContextDigests`, `importIds` \u2014 so one request always yields one subject and narrowing makes deterministic progress. That order governs the prospective-Plan boundary, among the fields present there. It is not an ordering claim about the whole request: `nativeContextDigests` is also accounted at the earlier producer boundary that computes, admission-validates and deduplicates the native contexts, and an overflow refuses there before any prospective Plan is assembled. Native ScopeRefusal retains detail, D9 and observed bound fields for the host's closed termination projection, and is already field-generic, so all seven fields use one law and one code: the remedy class is identical \u2014 narrow the selection explicitly, nothing was truncated \u2014 which is why widening it across the further record families adds no public code, while each field keeps its OWN remedy wording because what a caller narrows differs per field. The effective schema sources for FX-CAP-1 are the exact complete copies schemas/identity-schemas.v3.json, schemas/common.schema.json and schemas/evaluator3-common.schema.json under docs/implementation/fallow-expansion/capacity-r2/. They replace only the stated four numeric maxima and carry the selected major-law/notice annotations; every other effective schema value is retained. Numeric maxima are selected through these copies, not text-value JSON Pointer overrides. Implementation generation materializes these selected copies under existing source URIs in one reviewed schema unit; no caller can substitute an earlier source as a current minting schema.\n\nS-B extends the same public bounded-request projection to the following producer-owned quantities. Every proven crossing is `request-rejected` (exit2), code `REQUEST.UNSATISFIABLE`, public detail `PROJECT.SCOPE_LIMIT`, without truncation or splitting. The observed quantity and published limit remain in the owner's typed refusal. Count subjects have the existing `field:count>limit` form, with `count` and `limit` replaced by exact decimal observed value and ceiling. Byte subjects use the same numeric form with an explicitly byte-labelled field, not JSON string length, framing or ledger charge. The public field labels below identify producer quantities; they add no schema members, parser coercions, new wire cause, mint list or changed schema bound.\n\n| Typed refusal | Public field | Quantity | Ceiling | Population and boundary |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| SnapshotBound | snapshot.sourceInventory | rows | 100000 | Complete captured source inventory |\n| SnapshotBound | snapshot.descriptorBytes | canonical bytes | 4194304 | Full canonical snapshot descriptor |\n| SnapshotBound | snapshot.totalFileBytes | content bytes | 8589934592 | Entire retained snapshot, shared by every universe; smallest applicable bound |\n| DependencySetBound | dependency-source-set.packages | packages | 4096 | Final admitted, activated, non-path set |\n| DependencySetBound | dependency-source-set.files | logical files | 1000000 | Sum of committed fileCount over that same final set |\n| DependencySetBound | dependency-source-set.totalBytes | content bytes | 8589934592 | Sum of committed totalBytes over that same final set |\n| DependencyAcquisitionBound | dependency-acquisition.sources | candidates | 16384 | Full acquisition candidate population, before activation |\n| DependencyAcquisitionBound | dependency-acquisition.files | logical files | 4000000 | Decoded/read candidate regular files, before activation |\n| DependencyAcquisitionBound | dependency-acquisition.totalBytes | content bytes | 34359738368 | Decoded/read candidate content, before activation |\n| Host subject-scope bound | subject-scope.subjects | subjects | 100000 | Complete host-derived subject census, after execution at H admission |\n| Host subject-scope bound | subject-scope.descriptorBytes | canonical bytes | 4194304 | Full canonical host-derived subject-scope descriptor, after execution |\n\nThe snapshot remedy is \"add ignorePaths or select a narrower project root\". Dependency and acquisition remedies narrow the project selection whose complete required populations exceed the ceiling; they never delete required package rows, manufacture activation, partially materialize a candidate or reinterpret rejected packages as admitted ones. The subject-scope remedy narrows explicit project/capability selection before retry; it never truncates, partitions or fabricates the host's complete census.\n\nThe set check occurs once after activation and route admission, before descriptor/wrapper/transport. Inactive candidates and rejected archives do not count toward the final set, even when acquired; their acquisition work retains its separate population. A single oversized candidate has no shortcut: acquire to completion subject to acquisition and per-archive decoder limits, admit or reject under its own route, materialize whole only when admitted, then decide activation and the final set bound. Per-archive decoder D1-D4 crossings remain archive-profile breaches and missing/undecodable routes; they are never DependencySetBound or DependencyAcquisitionBound. Ledger exhaustion and adapter scratch/output budgets keep their own existing routes.\n\nAcquisition's first crossing is the owner's deterministic canonical order: candidates ascending by UTF-8 (name, version, sourceId), archive file order or tree ascending logical path within a candidate; a simultaneous crossing is sources, then files, then content. S-B adds no priority across independent producer boundaries. Snapshot, final-set and subject-scope refusal selection remain their owners' typed observations rather than a new guessed tie-break rule.\n\nSnapshot/dependency/acquisition refusals occur before Plan minting and mint no Plan or Run for the refused step. Subject-scope refuses after provider execution at H's atomic admission boundary; S-B does not undo an existing Plan, relabel provider source custody, invent an earlier refusal, or mint an admitted Run from refused assembly. H/J's existing effect, settlement and termination composition continue to govern. Malformed inputs and corrupt retained records retain schema-first origin-dependent refusal routes; the count/byte projection applies only to a proven owner-computed quantity, never a length coerced from null, boolean, string, object or malformed array.\n\nThe original seven selection-array fields, their FX-CAP-1 capacities, prospective-Plan declaration order and earlier native-context boundary remain unchanged. No context, feature, dependency, snapshot, prepared-output, request, source-variant, record-byte, frame, stage or ledger bound is widened here. C1a/C3a/H5/J consumer code and real controls remain separately gated; text/arithmetic projection controls are not producer completeness, runtime admission, O7 execution or product qualification.\n", "before":