ity selection keeps `requestedCapabilities` far below its own11264 while1025 distinct admitted contexts cannot be expressed in the Plan, including when colocated language units share selected workspace roots; identical full context descriptors still deduplicate; `importIds` has limit256 while the selecting field `semantic-configuration.evidence.importIds` admits1024, so257 imported evidence records are an admitted configuration and an unrepresentable Plan; and `semanticClosures` has limit128 while `policyPackIds` admits128 packs that need not share closures. Those three are accounted **before plan2 is minted**, on the PROSPECTIVE Plan assembled from an already-admitted request, so a refusal mints no Plan and no Run for the refused step. They are **not** a retained-record check: an externally retained Plan over its bound is a corrupt or malformed retained record and keeps its schema-first refusal and this section's origin-dependent routing, because re-deriving a caller's remedy from already-committed bytes would misreport a corrupt store as an oversized request. Only an ACTUAL array over its bound refuses on cardinality; a missing field, null, boolean, number, string or object is a shape the schema owns and reaches it untouched. When more than one array of an assembled prospective Plan overflows at once the subject is the first in the published `$defs/plan` declaration order \u2014 `semanticClosures`, `nativeContextDigests`, `importIds` \u2014 so one request always yields one subject and narrowing makes deterministic progress. That order governs the prospective-Plan boundary, among the fields present there. It is not an ordering claim about the whole request: `nativeContextDigests` is also accounted at the earlier producer boundary that computes, admission-validates and deduplicates the native contexts, and an overflow refuses there before any prospective Plan is assembled. Native ScopeRefusal retains detail, D9 and observed bound fields for the host's closed termination projection, and is already field-generic, so all seven fields use one law and one code: the remedy class is identical \u2014 narrow the selection explicitly, nothing was truncated \u2014 which is why widening it across the further record families adds no public code, while each field keeps its OWN remedy wording because what a caller narrows differs per field. The effective schema sources for FX-CAP-1 are the exact complete copies schemas/identity-schemas.v3.json, schemas/common.schema.json and schemas/evaluator3-common.schema.json under docs/implementation/fallow-expansion/capacity-r2/. They replace only the stated four numeric maxima and carry the selected major-law/notice annotations; every other effective schema value is retained. Numeric maxima are selected through these copies, not text-value JSON Pointer overrides. Implementation generation materializes these selected copies under existing source URIs in one reviewed schema unit; no caller can substitute an earlier source as a current minting schema.", "parent": {"bytes": 329013, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/native-evidence.md", "sha256": "83b99783893bec4bcca76bc043310e1d33305fc41ef85e012fbcb19e5b222ca0"}, "selector": {"line": 4276}, "supersedes": {"parent": {"bytes": 329013, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/native-evidence.md", "sha256": "83b99783893bec4bcca76bc043310e1d33305fc41ef85e012fbcb19e5b222ca0"}, "record": {"bytes": 30391, "path": "docs/implementation/fallow-expansion/capacity-r2/successor.json", "sha256": "9f7de1a2a27414d24834ee41ddbd8fa7017580e70c75a526fa4fa55540c51d16"}, "selector": {"line": 4276}}}], "selected": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/s-b-r1/successor.json", "sha256": "d57f3bc513b0e066a7e25c2aa4eab782c48127f375a5b9b5ff55bdd6ff3fb5a6"}, {"inputs": [{"bytes": 2777, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/host-pipeline-j/j-bs-runtime-pins-r1/README.md", "sha256": "6eea0023292665e05fa58793a40fcfef203f4141906c1efaffabab0057a0d670"}, {"bytes": 2215, "path": "docs/implementa