 "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/vcs-1-r1/CONTRACT.md", "sha256": "62e59de74e7e20512f972696ca7258872a5406fcbeb7d0731ad622497aedcf41"}, {"bytes": 1335, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/vcs-1-r1/README.md", "sha256": "6c2cf79caafb23c41ee3e52654d02c0d2015efada5d20678a1da805a4436fcd0"}, {"bytes": 1708, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/vcs-1-r1/source.json", "sha256": "0f5cff3a415dd878665cab0cbed9133b5c580baf7d8acc69371694d764714c25"}], "passageOverrides": [{"after": "runtime-to-source correspondence.\n\n**M3 meaning of `dirty` (VCS-1, accepted M3-C item4).** At M3, `dirty` means that cleanliness has not been established. For a Git observation the host reads no Git object and cannot compare the working tree with HEAD's tree, so it records `dirty: true`; this is not a claim that a particular path changed. It must not record `dirty: false` from Git index stat data or infer a change set from this flag. `NoRepository` keeps `kind: none`, `commitId: null`, and `dirty: false`. The flag does not prove a clean Git revision, and an M3 Git observation cannot satisfy a `vcs-revision` correspondence requiring a clean commit. At M3 no runtime, test or history evidence import consumes such a correspondence.\n\nThe same Git `dirty` meaning applies to each admitted D15 member's schema-3 observation under B-S2 and its accepted successors. The schema-3 root remains `kind: none`, `commitId: null`, and `dirty: false`; it supplies no member correspondence. Schema-2 and schema-3 fields, admission, member order, canonical bytes, inventory-digest meaning and historical retained observations are unchanged. This passage changes no observed flag or retained identity by reinterpretation. It publishes no Git object reader, per-path change set, new correspondence mapping, new record member, schema version, launch authority or C1b implementation. C1b, its native reads and all dependent M3 units retain their accepted gates.\n", "before": "runtime-to-source correspondence.", "parent": {"bytes": 135448, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/identity-and-evidence.md", "sha256": "c82404f3a0cf56fa6cc02e99cc3ebbd5356fedc3b36aeb38f9ef284077fbd31f"}, "selector": {"line": 546}}], "passageSupersessions": [], "selected": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/vcs-1-r1/successor.json", "sha256": "dcca6976860869e2da1730c1b4f03fa0b7d015650229a0fdba126e5866027e3e"}, {"inputs": [{"bytes": 13644, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/c2pm-1-r2/CONTRACT.md", "sha256": "58d285ca4de164465e41ade0c89400aa81a41eeb0acfed4a0de2b57de55fbc63"}, {"bytes": 2714, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/c2pm-1-r2/README.md", "sha256": "0e9c21febaa7ae230b22308d9756fcfc902735f7f849ae555cc805d09477919c"}, {"bytes": 1075, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/c2pm-1-r2/STATUS-DELTA.md", "sha256": "f273bb2ef3977f6a86cf65d8acdb9cf4e19c1febbaca623844c51756ce5b2f2d"}, {"bytes": 8437, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/c2pm-1-r2/closure-protocol-routes.json", "sha256": "047972c68a5ab8fbe7b2fdceb8c19e80af78019be2479ab5c1cc1108c94ccf2c"}, {"bytes": 55259, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/c2pm-1-r2/completion/design/common.schema.json", "sha256": "a410ea3467cf19f86800aac31db7ab87d0e6e86f39c9af92d35f1f62881b2ba5"}, {"bytes": 25247, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/c2pm-1-r2/completion/manifest-schema.completed.v1.json", "sha256": "80254f9e1fd9a3014f5bba73715f1c882d0d511293cc154cc2b59cfbf9d37353"}, {"bytes": 55259, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/c2pm-1-r2/completion/product/common-v1.schema.json", "sha256": "a410ea3467cf19f86800aac31db7ab87d0e6e86f39c9af92d35f1f62881b2ba5"}, {"bytes": 64840, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/c2pm-1-r2/completion/product/common-v3.schema.json", "sha256": "7c53a967a2d2c039963bf8cfff7f403d618c67595125cd1a647b0e1fc755c060"}, {"bytes": 65359, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/c2pm-1-r2/completion/product/common-v4.schema.json", "sha256": "6bcabe43609116a58b46e78586b91ec44097808919c20a0ed65d68b42850ebc6"}, {"bytes": 59998, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/c2pm-1-r2/completion/public-detail-registry.v1.json", "sha256": "6f1fa56fe07107b13b44f220b1d9fe7fb73c629cf8e98e87d51bfbb03da7d14e"}, {"bytes": 50314, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/c2pm-1-r2/evidence/audit.json", "sha256": "45d1dd608a9a3b9423994f14136af96fe50581ef503eef4a05598cd1d9953e95"}, {"bytes": 10327, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/c2pm-1-r2/evidence/check.py", "sha256": "836b0b5778d91f0c6fedef3a52fb6793f2488b2eb9ce53cf6f762cf2359b26ab"}, {"bytes": 833, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/c2pm-1-r2/evidence/dependencies.json", "sha256": "44204f549a68dc6b6cf435a3d813a8572135d5b45e1519f5b63a0e0e3067993b"}, {"bytes": 3051, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/c2pm-1-r2/evidence/verify_scratch.py", "sha256": "bea03636d56415a7e9bc2f26bfbcd046d2c6b430a59ffa102a03510ac1ffb71d"}, {"bytes": 1315, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/c2pm-1-r2/manifest-field-extension.json", "sha256": "22a686f86d4f20faac9f446744bc01aad4001c38ae9784077334af8061bb9c79"}, {"bytes": 8823, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/c2pm-1-r2/materialization-map.json", "sha256": "1f31305ac2cba7631a32ad4f37415eeb86962dd9489ecc0ef31fa64aa3f6f833"}, {"bytes": 3142, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/c2pm-1-r2/public-detail-extension.json", "sha256": "466f75b191b30a21bc527446c6c4167eb9822afff18721854936b4da317e350d"}, {"bytes": 5960, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/c2pm-1-r2/source.json", "sha256": "667be69aff2824c2331e2b867111f14161f0bcf9e33ffa236993aba223218199"}], "passageOverrides": [{"after": "- **`closure2.protocolMajor`.** No accepted text says which manifest field supplies it for any role. CR-1 changes no compatibility field, and C2a's projection applies one rule to every role. Historical CR-1 left this source undecided. C2PM-1 prospectively selects signed top-level closureProtocolMajor and the exact validation/projection/support/refusal/migration duties in its reviewed CONTRACT.md; no CR-1 historical byte or closure standing is reinterpreted.", "before": "- **`closure2.protocolMajor`.** No accepted text says which manifest field supplies it for any role. CR-1 changes no compatibility field, and C2a's projection applies one rule to every role.", "parent": {"bytes": 35706, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/cr-1/README.md", "sha256": "1898c3a2ddc72e7866bf0c77f51db5157e714b681dbe32791d55293bbde5bc40"}, "selector": {"line": 288}}, {"after": "Exact product bytes for unit C2a (law M3-C r7 item 7; C2a depends on CR-1): it copies the candidate to its product path, re-pins tools/security/inputs/sources.json and regenerates crates/security/src/generated/component_manifest_shape.rs through tools/generate_security_tables.py (the role node becomes the five-way enum, the root node reads commands only when present and gains a one-of term, and two branch nodes are added; every keyword is inside the generator's allowed set, so the generator itself is unchanged). C2a also implements the table and the closure-only rules in the manifest owner and closure admission, which this map does not fix. Independent review and root assent are required before selection. C2PM-1 prospectively supersedes this schema-copy selection for its new declaration: C2a uses docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/c2pm-1-r2/materialization-map.json and its exact successor completion, preserving all CR-1 role/command/name/trust duties; this older map and pins remain historical bytes.", "before": "Exact product bytes for unit C2a (law M3-C r7 item 7; C2a depends on CR-1): it copies the candidate to its product path, re-pins tools/security/inputs/sources.json and regenerates crates/security/src/generated/component_manifest_shape.rs through tools/generate_security_tables.py (the role node becomes the five-way enum, the root node reads commands only when present and gains a one-of term, and two branch nodes are added; every keyword is inside the generator's allowed set, so the generator itself is unchanged). C2a also implements the table and the closure-only rules in the manifest owner and closure admission, which this map does not fix. Independent review and root assent are required before selection.", "parent": {"bytes": 4602, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/cr-1/materialization-map.json", "sha256": "1e4c31cffd5d2da3051cfff4933f1ec69311bb66d4df553e467110ad70b40630"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/standing"}}, {"after": "SRC-02 (at 1811c682), 'One component architecture' section, verbatim: 'A manifest eventually binds commands, capabilities, dependencies/prerequisites, compatibility, exact immutable tree and entrypoint, publisher/provenance, SBOM/attestations, permissions, configuration classification, state/migration, and update data. Concrete schemas remain DR-103.' Every member of that enumeration appears below either as a designed field group or as an explicitly reserved binding point; none is silently dropped. C2PM-1 additionally publishes the optional signed body field closureProtocolMajor and scoped semantic consistency in docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/c2pm-1-r2/manifest-field-extension.json and its exact completed schema. Optional M2 absence stays valid; new C2a projection separately requires authenticated presence. Present analyzer declaration must equal same-body compatibility.providerProtocol in both validation paths. Passive role declarations are not provider/control/state compatibility epochs.", "before": "SRC-02 (at 1811c682), 'One component architecture' section, verbatim: 'A manifest eventually binds commands, capabilities, dependencies/prerequisites, compatibility, exact immutable tree and entrypoint, publisher/provenance, SBOM/attestations, permissions, configuration classification, state/migration, and update data. Concrete schemas remain DR-103.' Every member of that enumeration appears below either as a designed field group or as an explicitly reserved binding point; none is silently dropped.", "parent": {"bytes": 139492, "path": "docs/coop/artifacts/component-manifest-schemas.v11.json", "sha256": "1c0b8868444a097256aaa7d9caf8ebaa1c6f73fb071dbb4dd712334abb17a005"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/manifestSchema/coverageObligation"}}, {"after": "Structural invalidity, including populating a RESERVED BINDING POINT (stateMigration/updateData/treeRootDigest/trustMetadata/deprecation.doctorRemediation) before its owning surface closes - reserved means reserved - and including a DUPLICATE JSON KEY anywhere in manifest, index, or lock bytes: admission parses with a duplicate-rejecting pairs hook and the refusal NAMES the duplicated key. Warrant for the class, measured at SRC-FRZ section 7.5 (493 checker executions, nothing sampled): last-key-wins parsing lets a document say one thing to a reader and another to every instrument while its bytes - and therefore its admission digest, the sha256 of exact bytes - stay unchanged; for an exact-byte admission cell that means the custody join would verify while the PARSED identity, name set, tree entries, or declarations diverge between the admitting host and any later consumer, and section 7.5's own finding that naming the key is part of the fix is carried into the refusal shape. Malformed inputs remain REPRESENTABLE for diagnosis (the prototype's raw-vs-admitted pattern, PB-1 RawToolPluginManifest) but are never admitted. C2PM-1 explicitly extends this rule ONLY with scoped semantic subcode CLOSURE_PROTOCOL_MISMATCH for present analyzer closureProtocolMajor unequal to same-body compatibility.providerProtocol; exact predicate/private category and origin-sensitive host carrier are its field extension and route registry. This is a new scoped consistency duty, not an inferred structural rule. Optional absence is not MISSING_REQUIRED_FIELD; no other semantic predicate or refusal mapping changes.", "before": "Structural invalidity, including populating a RESERVED BINDING POINT (stateMigration/updateData/treeRootDigest/trustMetadata/deprecation.doctorRemediation) before its owning surface closes - reserved means reserved - and including a DUPLICATE JSON KEY anywhere in manifest, index, or lock bytes: admission parses with a duplicate-rejecting pairs hook and the refusal NAMES the duplicated key. Warrant for the class, measured at SRC-FRZ section 7.5 (493 checker executions, nothing sampled): last-key-wins parsing lets a document say one thing to a reader and another to every instrument while its bytes - and therefore its admission digest, the sha256 of exact bytes - stay unchanged; for an exact-byte admission cell that means the custody join would verify while the PARSED identity, name set, tree entries, or declarations diverge between the admitting host and any later consumer, and section 7.5's own finding that naming the key is part of the fix is carried into the refusal shape. Malformed inputs remain REPRESENTABLE for diagnosis (the prototype's raw-vs-admitted pattern, PB-1 RawToolPluginManifest) but are never admitted.", "parent": {"bytes": 139492, "path": "docs/coop/artifacts/component-manifest-schemas.v11.json", "sha256": "1c0b8868444a097256aaa7d9caf8ebaa1c6f73fb071dbb4dd712334abb17a005"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/rejectionRules/rules/5/trigger"}}, {"after": "`native-evidence.schemas.v2.json#/x-opensip-public-route-registry`.\n\n**C2PM-1 exact additive projection-route extension.** For the newly published signed closureProtocolMajor, merge the four exact internal keys/predicates/origin mappings of `docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/c2pm-1-r2/closure-protocol-routes.json` with the existing closed registry, preserving all old rows. The exact encoding materialization duty is C2PM-1 materialization-map.json. Missing declaration, analyzer inequality, unsupported role major and manifest/descriptor major mismatch follow CONTRACT.md at their originating admission boundary: authenticated release or known external retained/supplied input rejects2/REQUEST.PRECONDITION_FAILED; a host bug minting its own invalid internal layer fails4/SYSTEM.OUTCOME.ILLEGAL_STATE/faultCause host-invariant. The host retains actual provenance, no caller origin/default/historical bypass. domainDetail is absent, internal keys stay in diagnostic records, and exactly one public detail member native.closure-protocol-invalid is selected through the reviewed public-detail registry/common copies, while D9 error/class/fault enums stay unchanged. Existing handshake, context/resource, Run-closure and other-boundary routes stay owned by their laws; these failing new pre-Plan admissions mint no identity, Plan, Run or worker.\n", "before": "`native-evidence.schemas.v2.json#/x-opensip-public-route-registry`.", "parent": {"bytes": 329013, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/native-evidence.md", "sha256": "83b99783893bec4bcca76bc043310e1d33305fc41ef85e012fbcb19e5b222ca0"}, "selector": {"line": 3558}}, {"after": "PROPOSED single closed public domain-detail registry; existing D9 class/code/exit remains unchanged C2PM-1 prospectively selects its exact completion/public-detail-registry.v1.json: exactly one new record native.closure-protocol-invalid for known external component closure-protocol guards. Every inherited record/owner is unchanged; host bugs use existing HOST.INVARIANT_VIOLATED, D9 vocabularies unchanged. Common schema copies and code-keyed remedies/envelope errors must agree.", "before": "PROPOSED single closed public domain-detail registry; existing D9 class/code/exit remains unchanged", "parent": {"bytes": 59547, "path": "docs/coop/design-corrections/public-detail-registry.v1.json", "sha256": "2702e6ca97b6d8095cbcef0b0a219048b1ffdc434e0d8bb8c4bff247cda70e68"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/standing"}}, {"after": "Generated/drift-checked from docs/coop/design-corrections/public-detail-registry.v1.json. Every owner joins the same closed registry; unknown names refuse. These details do not create D9 codes. C2PM-1 selects exactly one additional closed public detail native.closure-protocol-invalid, sourced from its exact successor registry/common completion copies and public-detail-extension.json; existing members and every isolated common3/common4 addition are preserved. It supplies required failure-envelope errors when termination.domainDetail is absent. Internal closure guard keys are not enum members. No D9 class/error/fault member changes.", "before": "Generated/drift-checked from docs/coop/design-corrections/public-detail-registry.v1.json. Every owner joins the same closed registry; unknown names refuse. These details do not create D9 codes.", "parent": {"bytes": 55042, "path": "docs/coop/design-corrections/workflows/schemas/common.schema.json", "sha256": "b7b25d5e7c2bf2c8d3496a2f222c31bcc87360f3e2541fbdb551136358f7d39c"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/$defs/DomainDetailCode/description"}}], "passageSupersessions": [{"after": "as `verify_envelope` already-accepted signers, not that delivery selector.\n\n**Component roles and closure kinds (contract successor CR-1, 2026-10-04).** The `closure2` `kind` of a component-manifest closure is fixed by one closed host table keyed by the admitted manifest's `role`. DR-103 makes the role vocabulary host-owned and its extension a host decision, not a manifest right; this table is that decision:\n\n| Manifest `role` | `closure2` `kind` | Command tree (`commands`) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `analyzer` | `provider` | required, non-empty |\n| `toolchain` | `toolchain` | none: absent |\n| `stdlib` | `stdlib` | none: absent |\n| `rust-dev-llvm` | `rust-dev-llvm` | none: absent |\n| `grammar` | `grammar` | none: absent |\n\nThe kind is never asserted by a caller, inferred from a path or file name, or taken from any other manifest field. A closure whose role maps to a kind other than the one its selecting field requires (identity `closureKinds.byField`) refuses. `evaluator`, `detector` and `adapter` are never manifest roles, and `core` is never a role or an identity closure kind: the core evaluator, detector, provider and adapter closures are projections of the authenticated core inventory (identity \u00a73, contract successors EC1 and CRC-1), never component-manifest closures. `analyzer` is the only role whose component the host launches, as a provider session. The other four roles are **closure-only**: the host never launches such a component through its manifest, and reads its tree only as retained closure bytes. A closure-only manifest therefore declares no capability (`capabilities` is `[]`, since a capability declaration is an analyzer role subprotocol) and requests no permission (`permissions` is `[]`); its platform `entrypoint` names a committed regular-file entry of that platform's tree, never a directory or symlink, under RJ-3's path and presence rules, but need not be executable, and the host never executes it; and it carries no command tree: `commands` is absent, so it asks for no mount and claims no root command. **The D4 join.** D4's EE-5a root-command check at R10a (M3-D r3 item 24) examines every manifest that declares a command tree. For a closure-only role any declared tree is such a claim: it is refused by the closed manifest schema at admission (RJ-6), and at D4 as an EE-5a root-command claim by that existing route. How EE-5a judges an `analyzer` manifest's required tree is M3-D's and is unchanged here. A tool that the host launches from a closure-only tree is launched under its own launch law, never as a component session. Every other DR-103 manifest rule applies to every role alike. A violation refuses at manifest admission through the manifest owner's existing schema, capability, permission and path refusals; no code is added.\n\n**C2PM-1 signed closure-major source and scoped refusal selection.** The exact prospective CONTRACT.md, completed manifest schema, manifest-field-extension.json, closure-protocol-routes.json and materialization-map.json under `docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/c2pm-1-r2/` select the previously unresolved declaration and its owning duties. Every new component closure projection uniformly reads mandatory top-level signed `closureProtocolMajor`, integer1..9223372036854775807; historical M2 body validation preserves optional absence. Present analyzer declarations equal the same body compatibility.providerProtocol in both shared validation paths and again at new projection, with the scoped RJ6 semantic extension; passive role interfaces independently support the explicitly selected1 and gain no launch/ABI authority. Existing analyzer support, compiler/resource joins, platform/tree/trust/role/field checks, core projection and identity recipes remain owned by their existing laws. The exact four internal route predicates preserve external-precondition versus host-invariant origins and use real StepTermination vocabularies with domainDetail absent plus required full failure-envelope errors: native.closure-protocol-invalid for known external origins, existing HOST.INVARIANT_VIOLATED for host bugs, exact code-keyed remedies, no internal key used as a public code. Old readers are not reverse compatible; no backfill, caller historical switch, version inference, unsigned replacement or historical reinterpretation is allowed. C2PM-1 acceptance is design selection, not full C2a implementation, L effect, native admission/activation or qualification.\n", "before": "as `verify_envelope` already-accepted signers, not that delivery selector.\n\n**Component roles and closure kinds (contract successor CR-1, 2026-10-04).** The `closure2` `kind` of a component-manifest closure is fixed by one closed host table keyed by the admitted manifest's `role`. DR-103 makes the role vocabulary host-owned and its extension a host decision, not a manifest right; this table is that decision:\n\n| Manifest `role` | `closure2` `kind` | Command tree (`commands`) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `analyzer` | `provider` | required, non-empty |\n| `toolchain` | `toolchain` | none: absent |\n| `stdlib` | `stdlib` | none: absent |\n| `rust-dev-llvm` | `rust-dev-llvm` | none: absent |\n| `grammar` | `grammar` | none: absent |\n\nThe kind is never asserted by a caller, inferred from a path or file name, or taken from any other manifest field. A closure whose role maps to a kind other than the one its selecting field requires (identity `closureKinds.byField`) refuses. `evaluator`, `detector` and `adapter` are never manifest roles, and `core` is never a role or an identity closure kind: the core evaluator, detector, provider and adapter closures are projections of the authenticated core inventory (identity \u00a73, contract successors EC1 and CRC-1), never component-manifest closures. `analyzer` is the only role whose component the host launches, as a provider session. The other four roles are **closure-only**: the host never launches such a component through its manifest, and reads its tree only as retained closure bytes. A closure-only manifest therefore declares no capability (`capabilities` is `[]`, since a capability declaration is an analyzer role subprotocol) and requests no permission (`permissions` is `[]`); its platform `entrypoint` names a committed regular-file entry of that platform's tree, never a directory or symlink, under RJ-3's path and presence rules, but need not be executable, and the host never executes it; and it carries no command tree: `commands` is absent, so it asks for no mount and claims no root command. **The D4 join.** D4's EE-5a root-command check at R10a (M3-D r3 item 24) examines every manifest that declares a command tree. For a closure-only role any declared tree is such a claim: it is refused by the closed manifest schema at admission (RJ-6), and at D4 as an EE-5a root-command claim by that existing route. How EE-5a judges an `analyzer` manifest's required tree is M3-D's and is unchanged here. A tool that the host launches from a closure-only tree is launched under its own launch law, never as a component session. Every other DR-103 manifest rule applies to every role alike. A violation refuses at manifest admission through the manifest owner's existing schema, capability, permission and path refusals; no code is added.", "parent": {"bytes": 119915, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/security-and-lifecycle.md", "sha256": "a319da39b9f90aff4483a295af3fe004540c71861fb2bd34e84c7e1318bad9d6"}, "selector": {"line": 70}, "supersedes": {"parent": {"bytes": 119915, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/security-and-lifecycle.md", "sha256": "a319da39b9f90aff4483a295af3fe004540c71861fb2bd34e84c7e1318bad9d6"}, "record": {"bytes": 10694, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/cr-1/successor.json", "sha256": "0f1e187b0af676bb3face09a5d0055a75a77a0d898071763a2952d487a3ce9f8"}, "selector": {"line": 70}}}], "selected": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/c2pm-1-r2/successor.json", "sha256": "f75f9cbb3561826d128ad1bbbcd7f179a31c0ef24614202eb68f27d7bd71b446"}, {"inputs": [{"bytes": 10480, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/nij-1-r1/CONTRACT.md", "sha256": "6c7d15a347b90ca27888dfbf83160470873b65dced5fad2a088a2a65c5d00f74"}, {"bytes": 1199, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/nij-1-r1/README.md", "sha256": "15a3a4bab87c2aada4c78bf5ffd26b93c6082010ce4fa08b0df8230f9c14f987"}, {"bytes": 55459, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/nij-1-r1/completion/design/common.schema.json", "sha256": "1705531a120037c1331935da2cacd261d2b4e95bc94002814a4c7b95dc6c904b"}, {"bytes": 280476, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/nij-1-r1/completion/native.v2.schema.json", "sha256": "c0e39cfc0f9052f513c6a291ce976ea2867f0d9088e1074dc70508f279269cac"}, {"bytes": 55459, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/nij-1-r1/completion/product/common-v1.schema.json", "sha256": "1705531a120037c1331935da2cacd261d2b4e95bc94002814a4c7b95dc6c904b"}, {"bytes": 65040, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/nij-1-r1/completion/product/common-v3.schema.json", "sha256": "d6af487e0fb147fe1db67dd6384383d96dd8e0ac49193be0d31ba3e8179e468c"}, {"bytes": 65559, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/nij-1-r1/completion/product/common-v4.schema.json", "sha256": "bbc2d31ecd870e8c9ed8ab2482ce263e1f1da7a5680fc88a76b0d5813dc38474"}, {"bytes": 60309, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/nij-1-r1/completion/public-detail-registry.v1.json", "sha256": "98f837f4302abbb70c1979dc648817991c89d22b877fbadb593a853315359bf2"}, {"bytes": 1991, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/nij-1-r1/evidence/carriers.json", "sha256": "09dc02835aeb00db46ac4fa08c98bfed79766bac61b6fbfc770c83db78f0e717"}, {"bytes": 2679, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/nij-1-r1/evidence/carriers.py", "sha256": "8632eb33d31365ac413187a0ef8019d031fa5c2b4ff9d0abf51ab42fa879810c"}, {"bytes": 4321, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/nij-1-r1/evidence/check.py", "sha256": "56ddfe6b5029e0d614864ca313c66a02a5d3316c0e1d88385a6d90b5a58fb4b7"}, {"bytes": 2143, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/nij-1-r1/evidence/controls.json", "sha256": "01bce4e4313213fd672a537085b891c3369e728cd23de01113c7be90558e5134"}, {"bytes": 833, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/nij-1-r1/evidence/dependencies.json", "sha256": "44204f549a68dc6b6cf435a3d813a8572135d5b45e1519f5b63a0e0e3067993b"}, {"bytes": 2474, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/nij-1-r1/evidence/join-specification.json", "sha256": "80105683ac84a59a8ed96274cc62eaabb1a7faf358bb2f224482b986fd04f71a"}, {"bytes": 2852, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/nij-1-r1/materialization-map.json", "sha256": "c6400963ad323cf68af659a496395a19fa039aa8617a94591173e80c6f3f4c08"}, {"bytes": 8642, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/nij-1-r1/native-import-routes.json", "sha256": "d3850e5507020683cf8cc28dbe218355d6c89c3ca4ce8799f8335cd420b2cfe7"}, {"bytes": 2917, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/nij-1-r1/public-detail-extension.json", "sha256": "d081bca564e9aafe38e6acb32fd89e86da00794e6b37ed003144a4bcc56ac2e4"}, {"bytes": 394, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/nij-1-r1/scope-check.json", "sha256": "8da6f31628bdacc6bd4f832e0152a6e6747ae44aa30bcff60ec98db34e03e992"}, {"bytes": 13158, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/nij-1-r1/source.json", "sha256": "30583eddd41b2cf38500d341e81922c7698faea1ac375f358293f2b24dd3f73f"}], "passageOverrides": [{"after": "| `dependency` | `native/native-evidence.schemas.v2.json` | `#/$defs/DependencySourcePayloadV1` = `{payloadDomain, set: DependencySourceSetV1, acquisitionSourcePath, tarballDigests[]}` | `native.import-payload.dependency-source.v1` | native |\n\n**NIJ-1 prospective native dependency/prepared import assembly and identity join.** The exact CONTRACT.md, native schema completion, native-import-routes.json, public-detail-extension.json and materialization-map.json under docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/nij-1-r1/ publish item13's native-owner successor. Newly admitted M3 dependency payloads require literal null acquisitionSourcePath; the closed schema adds null while preserving historical bounded strings for their old selected readers/replay. Historical records are never rewritten or caller-relabelled; old readers reject new null. The source acquisition argument stays in the existing custody procedure and never enters another content identity as a substitute. All newly admitted M3 acquisition.descriptorId/preparation.importId values are null before set hashing, no second set projection. CRC1 authenticated producer/adapter projections and the existing exact six raw auxiliary digests/observation records remain. The context identity joins select exactly one admitted wrapper of the right kind and rederived set identity, zero/two distinct imports refuse, and unselected retained imports are never consumed. Final Plan importIds is the existing canonical union of ordinary resolved evidence and lawful context-selected native imports; configuration cannot name dependency/prepared imports. Retained Run closure independently rederives the same exact joins with no filesystem/provider operation and actual retained boundary provenance. Exact five internal predicates and one new public detail native.input-import-invalid publish full origin-sensitive refusal/envelope/remedy composition; existing missing/corrupt custody, schema/identity/signature/CRC1 and correspondence owners retain their precedence. R3/owner membership, package/path codecs, effectiveL/fullC3a and actual product implementation remain owed.\n", "before": "| `dependency` | `native/native-evidence.schemas.v2.json` | `#/$defs/DependencySourcePayloadV1` = `{payloadDomain, set: DependencySourceSetV1, acquisitionSourcePath, tarballDigests[]}` | `native.import-payload.dependency-source.v1` | native |", "parent": {"bytes": 329013, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/native-evidence.md", "sha256": "83b99783893bec4bcca76bc043310e1d33305fc41ef85e012fbcb19e5b222ca0"}, "selector": {"line": 2671}}, {"after": "At Run closure, every Plan-selected import re-admits its retained canonical SourceCorrespondence. An exact-snapshot import must name the Plan snapshot. VCS-revision imports require a retained, canonical admitted SourceMappingV1 to that snapshot, with inventory-matching source digests and the current clean revision; an unmapped/stale intake artifact may remain available for inspection but cannot be selected as an analyzed Plan input. Intake storage does not establish this source join. The authenticated importer/evaluator still owns payload-schema, observation-scope and polarity admission; rehashing an opaque import is insufficient. The reference closure reuses the same workflow import/mapping schema admission functions so the source law has one implementation.\n\n**NIJ-1 native input joins.** For dependency/prepared native imports, docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/nij-1-r1/CONTRACT.md additionally requires the rederived context/set/Plan-selected-wrapper join exactly once per imported set; final Plan importIds includes these lawful context-resolved wrappers, never names them through configured evidence.importIds. The same joins are independently rederived at retained Run closure, consuming only selected admitted records. CRC1 projections, exact raw auxiliary digest preimages, source correspondence and prior custody/admission ordering remain unchanged. No self-referencing ids, alternate set projection, unselected lookup fallback or provider/filesystem action is introduced. NIJ-1 defines prospective null acquisitionSourcePath and its reader/migration boundary, preserving historical retained bytes.\n", "before": "At Run closure, every Plan-selected import re-admits its retained canonical SourceCorrespondence. An exact-snapshot import must name the Plan snapshot. VCS-revision imports require a retained, canonical admitted SourceMappingV1 to that snapshot, with inventory-matching source digests and the current clean revision; an unmapped/stale intake artifact may remain available for inspection but cannot be selected as an analyzed Plan input. Intake storage does not establish this source join. The authenticated importer/evaluator still owns payload-schema, observation-scope and polarity admission; rehashing an opaque import is insufficient. The reference closure reuses the same workflow import/mapping schema admission functions so the source law has one implementation.", "parent": {"bytes": 135448, "path": "docs/v2/contracts/product-v1/identity-and-evidence.md", "sha256": "c82404f3a0cf56fa6cc02e99cc3ebbd5356fedc3b36aeb38f9ef284077fbd31f"}, "selector": {"line": 1835}}, {"after": "Closed Draft 2020-12 records for the native evidence contract. Exact typed admission (foundation/canonical.py) precedes validation: integers are exact JSON integer literals, booleans are not integers, no float/exponent/-0, no duplicate keys, no lone surrogates. The import2 wrapper itself is foundation/identity-schemas.v2.json#/$defs/import; these are its native typed payloads plus native context/coverage/protocol records. Revision after Codex review v2: per-language co-located units (WorkspaceUnitV2/UnitMembershipV1), inert generated-file rows with a read-only virtual OUT_DIR, workflow canonical import payloads with native adapter-input shapes, ImportRegistryRowV1, preparedResolution, principal join, StageAuthorityV1. Blind-consumer corrections: the closed TypeScript native context (TypeScriptNativeContextV2 and family, domain native.context.typescript.v2, carrying typescriptStdlibMerkleRoot) and the subject-scope commitment / coverage-admission records. NIJ-1 prospectively selects its exact schema completion for dependency acquisitionSourcePath: historical bounded string OR null, with mandatory null at new-M3 semantic admission. Every other base shape remains unchanged; apply as a merge preserving all accepted overlays and selected route extensions, never overwrite newer sources. See docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/nij-1-r1/materialization-map.json; old records retain exact selected historical document digest and bytes.", "before": "Closed Draft 2020-12 records for the native evidence contract. Exact typed admission (foundation/canonical.py) precedes validation: integers are exact JSON integer literals, booleans are not integers, no float/exponent/-0, no duplicate keys, no lone surrogates. The import2 wrapper itself is foundation/identity-schemas.v2.json#/$defs/import; these are its native typed payloads plus native context/coverage/protocol records. Revision after Codex review v2: per-language co-located units (WorkspaceUnitV2/UnitMembershipV1), inert generated-file rows with a read-only virtual OUT_DIR, workflow canonical import payloads with native adapter-input shapes, ImportRegistryRowV1, preparedResolution, principal join, StageAuthorityV1. Blind-consumer corrections: the closed TypeScript native context (TypeScriptNativeContextV2 and family, domain native.context.typescript.v2, carrying typescriptStdlibMerkleRoot) and the subject-scope commitment / coverage-admission records.", "parent": {"bytes": 280357, "path": "docs/implementation/m1/source-selection-v2/schemas/sources/native.v2.schema.json", "sha256": "e5834d37aebd96d77d352975878da349033f8633ecbd83322ad0fbea461f7773"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/description"}}, {"after": "PROPOSED single closed public domain-detail registry; existing D9 class/code/exit remains unchanged C2PM-1 adds exactly native.closure-protocol-invalid for its external projection guards; existing records unchanged, D9 vocabulary unchanged. NIJ-1 prospectively extends this selected registry with exactly one native.input-import-invalid record in its exact completion, preserving every inherited record/alias. Its origin-sensitive routes never add context-free aliases. Historical selected bytes stay unchanged.", "before": "PROPOSED single closed public domain-detail registry; existing D9 class/code/exit remains unchanged C2PM-1 adds exactly native.closure-protocol-invalid for its external projection guards; existing records unchanged, D9 vocabulary unchanged.", "parent": {"bytes": 59998, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/c2pm-1-r2/completion/public-detail-registry.v1.json", "sha256": "6f1fa56fe07107b13b44f220b1d9fe7fb73c629cf8e98e87d51bfbb03da7d14e"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/standing"}}, {"after": "Generated/drift-checked from docs/coop/design-corrections/public-detail-registry.v1.json. Every owner joins the same closed registry; unknown names refuse. These details do not create D9 codes. C2PM-1 additionally selects native.closure-protocol-invalid under its exact successor public-detail registry. Existing isolated additions and all D9 meanings stay unchanged. NIJ-1 prospectively selects its corresponding exact completion with native.input-import-invalid appended once, all inherited enum values/order and other shapes retained; no class/error/fault member is added.", "before": "Generated/drift-checked from docs/coop/design-corrections/public-detail-registry.v1.json. Every owner joins the same closed registry; unknown names refuse. These details do not create D9 codes. C2PM-1 additionally selects native.closure-protocol-invalid under its exact successor public-detail registry. Existing isolated additions and all D9 meanings stay unchanged.", "parent": {"bytes": 55259, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/c2pm-1-r2/completion/design/common.schema.json", "sha256": "a410ea3467cf19f86800aac31db7ab87d0e6e86f39c9af92d35f1f62881b2ba5"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/$defs/DomainDetailCode/description"}}, {"after": "Generated/drift-checked from docs/coop/design-corrections/public-detail-registry.v1.json. Every owner joins the same closed registry; unknown names refuse. These details do not create D9 codes. C2PM-1 additionally selects native.closure-protocol-invalid under its exact successor public-detail registry. Existing isolated additions and all D9 meanings stay unchanged. NIJ-1 prospectively selects its corresponding exact completion with native.input-import-invalid appended once, all inherited enum values/order and other shapes retained; no class/error/fault member is added.", "before": "Generated/drift-checked from docs/coop/design-corrections/public-detail-registry.v1.json. Every owner joins the same closed registry; unknown names refuse. These details do not create D9 codes. C2PM-1 additionally selects native.closure-protocol-invalid under its exact successor public-detail registry. Existing isolated additions and all D9 meanings stay unchanged.", "parent": {"bytes": 55259, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/snapshot-plan-c/c2pm-1-r2/completion/product/common-v1.schema.json", "sha256": "a410ea3467cf19f86800aac31db7ab87d0e6e86f39c9af92d35f1f62881b2ba5"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/$defs/DomainDetailCode/description"}}, {"after": "Generated/drift-checke