5e9cd7c17bbc"}], "passageOverrides": [{"after": "M3-B r9 items 19 to 24 (units B3-b2b1 and B3-b2b2b, B3-b plan r2 amendments A2 and A4): the D15 readers (cargo-config-patch@1, npm-workspaces-members@1) and membership decision core, B3-b0 r4 discover without SLM passes over a WorkspaceObservation (entry, markers, children, bytes, custody-captured entries, and item 6b outcome per candidate with its index gitlinks): carriers and custody, the parsers, entry shapes and globs over lawfully visible repositories, placement, candidate failures, the 64-member cap; branch A decided before the caller one walk (the array gate returning the size or first-grammar refusal, then member decisions in path order stopping at the first refusal, then the admitted members and gitlinks for the walk, and links only after an accepted walk) and branch B; root-in-repository, entries at or below a gitlink, links and declaration rows. Pure; composed by custody/workspace_discovery.rs.", "before": "M3-B r9 items 19 to 24 (unit B3-b2b1, B3-b plan r2 amendment A2): the D15 readers (cargo-config-patch@1, npm-workspaces-members@1) and membership decision core, B3-b0 discover without SLM passes over a WorkspaceObservation (entry, markers, children, bytes, per-candidate item 6b verdict): carriers and custody, the parsers, entry shapes and globs over lawfully visible repositories, placement, candidate failures, the 64-member cap, branches A (two phases around the trial walk, short-circuit in path order) and B, root-in-repository, links and declaration rows. Pure; no consumer yet (B3-b2b2).", "parent": {"bytes": 703195, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v206.json", "sha256": "496c1631bea889f9e075b19b93ccd0828c3bbaff79337dc3c81899b957888aa6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/617/description"}}, {"after": "M3-B units B3-b2b1 and B3-b2b2b controls: every workspace member vector reproduced, with branch A walk members and gitlinks equal to the reference ones, item 6b asked exactly where the reference consulted it, and the core looking up, reading or listing only paths the B3-b0 r4 reference did, never touching the checker unobserved paths and never reading its unread ones; a forbidden lookup caught without an output change; branch A stopping at the first refusal with no later candidate custody or item 6b verdict observed; TOML resource bounds as the existing parse disposition where tomllib reads; colliding Cargo entries keeping the first in document order.", "before": "M3-B unit B3-b2b1 controls: every workspace member vector reproduced with the core touching only paths the B3-b0 reference looked up, read or listed and none of the checker forbidden paths; a forbidden lookup caught without an output change; branch A stopping at the first refusal with no later member observed; TOML resource bounds as the existing parse disposition where tomllib reads; colliding Cargo entries keeping the first in document order.", "parent": {"bytes": 703195, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v206.json", "sha256": "496c1631bea889f9e075b19b93ccd0828c3bbaff79337dc3c81899b957888aa6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/618/description"}}, {"after": "M3-B units B3-b2b1 and B3-b2b2b workspace member vectors, generated by tools/security/workspace_member_vectors.py: B3-b0 r4 (contract successor 169) decisions over its checker D15 cases and the discovery cases (observation with item 6b verdicts and gitlinks, facts, branch A walk members, gitlinks and status, the expected outcome including array-gate and walk refusals, the reference access record, the item 6b consultations, and the forbidden lookups and reads) and labelled TOML and JSON parser controls; the non-string arrays a product array cannot hold are listed by id. Design evidence, not qualification.", "before": "M3-B unit B3-b2b1 workspace member vectors, generated by tools/security/workspace_member_vectors.py: B3-b0 (contract successor 168) decisions over its checker D15 cases and the discovery cases (observation, facts, trial, expected outcome, the reference access record and forbidden paths) and labelled TOML and JSON parser controls. Design evidence, not qualification.", "parent": {"bytes": 703195, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v206.json", "sha256": "496c1631bea889f9e075b19b93ccd0828c3bbaff79337dc3c81899b957888aa6"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/622/description"}}], "passageSupersessions": [], "selected": "docs/implementation/m3/config-discovery-b/b3-b-descriptions/successor.json", "sha256": "13db54befccd29d3126014e104ae1b0234d67a0e39400f087c6730d8c28067e0"}, {"inputs": [{"bytes": 7066, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/config-discovery-b/b2-d-reference/README.md", "sha256": "1333add3c4ad6645dabf3d72e6e2b7e8c9f63c782822cd72b94878c01225f524"}, {"bytes": 22882, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/config-discovery-b/b2-d-reference/evidence/check.py", "sha256": "9cd9c05f1a1cce33bf5c4a2dfcbd5f96c350d6bfb36fd17a0c5c70fcc11f788d"}, {"bytes": 12752, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/config-discovery-b/b2-d-reference/evidence/checks.json", "sha256": "6924ab952d3882ab6c700036df8ae727e12bb8c48032243b283b2f11a1684d36"}, {"bytes": 4220, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/config-discovery-b/b2-d-reference/evidence/mutants.json", "sha256": "16b99524298e7d0d0576687ddd893b70866568347d923c5b2b3da1d9b111ddc6"}, {"bytes": 4780, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/config-discovery-b/b2-d-reference/evidence/mutants.py", "sha256": "2f080bbeeab9f4aaad732954f58854d76b7b4c6c44054a81198c0f7034550702"}, {"bytes": 19933, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/config-discovery-b/b2-d-reference/reference/framework_recognition_reference.py", "sha256": "ed9858b0de1035ac95cde38743066a2e7a6df1740c3a30523021c715255b6a89"}], "passageOverrides": [], "passageSupersessions": [], "selected": "docs/implementation/m3/config-discovery-b/b2-d-reference/successor.json", "sha256": "a2a37d2dfe7bead07822348f1ef68049ce5e802af5f0b20cef996db61e98b01a"}, {"inputs": [{"bytes": 5024, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/config-discovery-b/s10b-remedy/README.md", "sha256": "b93c9416dc1a6441bbfa701b90932d1dbc71c108e275319ad47a1b49f5dde7d8"}, {"bytes": 10189, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/config-discovery-b/s10b-remedy/evidence/check.py", "sha256": "99a418f7b281141ce9a94309783806e1ea249871302bbfd532eb03ffb4c08411"}, {"bytes": 4764, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/config-discovery-b/s10b-remedy/evidence/checks.json", "sha256": "0955e52ba9527316eebdce49c65ed1f05ab5d6b1e08f2bd0c639cdc5dc0e0158"}, {"bytes": 322096, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/config-discovery-b/s10b-remedy/reference/native_evidence_model.py", "sha256": "bdfd8373355e82c99057fa3235284acd64f28a03efcebf94b9c540c16e515b2b"}, {"bytes": 321976, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/config-discovery-b/s10b-remedy/reference/native_evidence_model.v2.py", "sha256": "d645540782a6291ae6416f882bee5c6216c785ffb8f83c24099ade7b3e13b4bf"}], "passageOverrides": [], "passageSupersessions": [], "selected": "docs/implementation/m3/config-discovery-b/s10b-remedy/successor.json", "sha256": "ec78b77d7ab0e779aabba7d3e196b266f1f27dc6e9daa2e834f44e6d4b3d9691"}, {"inputs": [{"bytes": 1794, "path": "docs/implementation/m3/config-discovery-b/b2-c2b-descriptions/README.md", "sha256": "5641e186adda0fb2d8ca36d41618da8d1ebed994f4fb2d0666545b7dc26313bf"}], "passageOverrides": [{"after": "M3-B r8 item 14a as r9 and r10 amend it (units B2-c2 and B2-c2b): the pre-Plan TypeScript configuration extends graph and effective allowJs: the closed specifier grammar, lexical normalization and containment, path and package candidate orders, canonical node identity with cycle, 32-node and 64 KiB checks and the r10 record bounds (at most 64 extends entries per file, repetitions included, and at most 4,096 characters per node path), reads only through a custody source (every link unresolved before C1), NE:527-545 inheritance evaluated once per node in postorder, the four internal keys with the S10 alias rows, and the composition with U-1. Pure; no production caller (J1, C1).", "before": "M3-B r8 item 14a as r9 amends it (unit B2-c2): the pre-Plan TypeScript configuration extends graph and effective allowJs: the closed specifier grammar, lexical normalization and containment, path and package candidate orders, canonical node identity with cycle, 32-node and 64 KiB checks, reads only through a custody source (every link unresolved before C1), NE:527-545 inheritance evaluated once per node in postorder, the four internal keys with the S10 alias rows, and the composition with U-1. Pure; no production caller (J1, C1).", "parent": {"bytes": 716592, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v212.json", "sha256": "b42e7f5f8555aeb8096d6cb29f337efcd7e591e3c06399e6423d65a6a166be17"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/202/description"}}, {"after": "M3-B units B2-c2 and B2-c2b controls: every item 14a law control replays with its options, graph, refusal key and path, source reads and linear evaluation work, and every admitted graph is admitted by the embedded TypeScriptConfigGraphV1 schema; the r10 bounds equal that schema extendsResolved maxItems and CanonicalPath maxLength, admitted at each bound and refused by the schema one past it; the seven units-config-effective-allowjs cases from configuration bytes; the composition reads only surviving configuration markers; the alias map equals the S10 registry rows; M3-J1 r7 row 59 projects the D9 row, the S10b remedy and the bounded subject; deep nesting at the file bound.", "before": "M3-B unit B2-c2 controls: every item 14a law control replays with its options, graph, refusal key and path, source reads and linear evaluation work; the seven units-config-effective-allowjs cases from configuration bytes; the composition reads only surviving configuration markers; the alias map equals the S10 registry rows; M3-J1 r7 row 59 projects the S10 D9 row, remedy and bounded subject; deep nesting at the file bound.", "parent": {"bytes": 716592, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v212.json", "sha256": "b42e7f5f8555aeb8096d6cb29f337efcd7e591e3c06399e6423d65a6a166be17"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/203/description"}}, {"after": "M3-B units B2-c2 and B2-c2b TypeScript configuration vectors, generated by tools/host/typescript_config_vectors.py: the hand-written item 14a law controls (as r9 and r10 amend it) with the reference result, graph, source reads and work; graphs admitted by the S10b native model copy (contract successor 172); the units cases through the B2-c0 reference (contract successor 166); the S10 alias rows, the D9 row, the S10b remedy and bounded subjects. Design evidence, not qualification.", "before": "M3-B unit B2-c2 TypeScript configuration vectors, generated by tools/host/typescript_config_vectors.py: the hand-written item 14a law controls with the reference result, graph, source reads and work; graphs admitted by the S10 native model copy (contract successor 167); the units cases through the B2-c0 reference (contract successor 166); the alias rows, D9 row, remedy and bounded subjects. Design evidence, not qualification.", "parent": {"bytes": 716592, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v212.json", "sha256": "b42e7f5f8555aeb8096d6cb29f337efcd7e591e3c06399e6423d65a6a166be17"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/204/description"}}, {"after": "M3-B units B2-c2 and B2-c2b: generates or checks crates/host/src/typescript_config_vectors.json from hand-written M3-B r8 item 14a controls (as r9 and r10 amend it), an independent Python reading of the law that must give each expected result, the S10b native model copy, the S10 registry copy and the B2-c0 reference at pinned architecture bytes. No network, process or environment input.", "before": "M3-B unit B2-c2: generates or checks crates/host/src/typescript_config_vectors.json from hand-written M3-B r8 item 14a controls (as r9 amends it), an independent Python reading of the law that must give each expected result, the S10 native model copy and the B2-c0 reference at pinned architecture bytes. No network, process or environment input.", "parent": {"bytes": 716592, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v212.json", "sha256": "b42e7f5f8555aeb8096d6cb29f337efcd7e591e3c06399e6423d65a6a166be17"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/1171/description"}}], "passageSupersessions": [], "selected": "docs/implementation/m3/config-discovery-b/b2-c2b-descriptions/successor.json", "sha256": "4f20ee6629af942a3205d08520897199b111cea58576dcfe5bae31c080802a6c"}], "generationSources": {"executedGeneratorCode": false, "productQualification": false, "sourcesVerified": 41}, "inputsVerified": 46, "inventoryPassageInheritance": [{"after": "Coordinate explicit maintenance scripts without npm workspace hoisting; select npm11.13.0 with independent per-package locks and Node24.16.0.", "before": "Declare TypeScript workspace membership and common script names; package manager and its lockfile remain to be selected.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/1002/description"}}, {"after": "Retain exact selected urn:opensip:product-v1:workflows:imported-evidence bytes for binding generation. Pure retained source correspondence and parameter selection belong to crates/evaluator/src/import_joins.rs; closed import payload admission belongs to the evaluator. Host crates/host/src/imports.rs owns import workflow I/O, not pure semantic admission.", "before": "Retain exact selected urn:opensip:product-v1:workflows:imported-evidence bytes for binding generation; semantic admission belongs to crates/host/src/imports.rs.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/1085/description"}}, {"after": "Check an X9 crash-matrix run set (law X9 r1 item 7) and scan a release binary (item 2, guard 4); read-only, standard library only. check (law X9 r10 and r16, unit X9-6) is the two-target M2 exit gate: it takes storage's and host's reviewed required-runs.v1.json, each with its pair of lead run sets, exactly once each, and refuses unless, for each target, both lead run sets list exactly that file's rows (a row's optional unit member, X9-2 to X9-6, admitted; law X9 r15 and r16), each run and matrix.json is canonical JSON of the opensip.x9 v1 shapes, every verdict is PASS with labels, units, script and expected values equal to the required row, the reviewed clean commit and dev crash-matrix profile, synthetic-signed-v2 and BASELINE-ATTESTED host labels, process-death exactly when the script kills with a signal-9 child held there, a census of registered scopes with the right durability whose derived kill set equals the recorded one, a passed release absence, limits L1 to L11 (law X9 r8, unit X9-2), and agreement of the two repetitions on normalizedSha256, trace digests and census; every run labelled scripted-clock with the run set's clock (the required runs' clockEpoch, script x9-ordinal-3600) and its children's ordinals in spawn order (law X9 r2 item 3, unit X9-1); timingGuard {monotonicMs, limitMs 5000} within its limit on exactly the runs whose script arms x4.observer.tick, not compared between repetitions (law X9 r12 and r13, unit X9-3); one clockEpoch in both files; and every point of the union census's kill set (by point name, each at its largest occurrence count, its durability equal in both) killed by a process-death run of either target, in each repetition. Only the two targets together report matrixPass. check-unit (law X9 r4 item 7, unit X9-2) applies the per-run check and repetition agreement to one unit's subset (X9-2 to X9-5: the rows whose case is in the unit's list; r15: a row with a unit member is in that unit's subset only, and an X9-6 row in none) with the stated base commit, a worktree that may be dirty and no full coverage, only every killed point inside the derived kill set. coverage (unit X9-6) runs nothing and reads no run set: from each target's census and required runs it reports every union kill-set point no row kills and every killed point outside it. release-absence reports whether OPENSIP_X9_ or any scope name from the barrier's registry block occurs in a binary. It runs no matrix and qualifies nothing.", "before": "Check an X9 crash-matrix run set (law X9 r1 item 7) and scan a release binary (item 2, guard 4); read-only, standard library only. check refuses unless both lead run sets list exactly the reviewed required-runs.v1.json rows, each run and matrix.json is canonical JSON of the opensip.x9 v1 shapes, every verdict is PASS with labels, units, script and expected values equal to the required row, the reviewed clean commit and dev crash-matrix profile, synthetic-signed-v2 and BASELINE-ATTESTED host labels, process-death exactly when the script kills with a signal-9 child held there, a census of registered scopes with the right durability whose derived kill set equals the recorded one and is fully killed, a passed release absence, limits L1 to L10, and agreement of the two repetitions on normalizedSha256, trace digests and census. release-absence reports whether OPENSIP_X9_ or any scope name from the barrier's registry block occurs in a binary. It runs no matrix and qualifies nothing.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/1122/description"}}, {"after": "M3-B units B2-c2 and B2-c2b: generates or checks crates/host/src/typescript_config_vectors.json from hand-written M3-B r8 item 14a controls (as r9 and r10 amend it), an independent Python reading of the law that must give each expected result, the S10b native model copy, the S10 registry copy and the B2-c0 reference at pinned architecture bytes. No network, process or environment input.", "before": "M3-B unit B2-c2: generates or checks crates/host/src/typescript_config_vectors.json from hand-written M3-B r8 item 14a controls (as r9 amends it), an independent Python reading of the law that must give each expected result, the S10 native model copy and the B2-c0 reference at pinned architecture bytes. No network, process or environment input.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/1192/description"}}, {"after": "Run the real built opensip doctor with no override (law X10 items 5 and 7): a development build ends at CORE.NO_EMBEDDED_RELEASE, exit 2, with a command-envelope v7 schema-valid kind failure envelope in JSON and the matching human lines, and the account's real installation untouched; doctor takes no flags and the agent format is not applicable; help and completion list doctor. Source pins keep every environment, home, feature, test or debug selector out of the binary, the doctor ingress and the request path, require exactly one producer call in the ingress, and refuse a features table on apps/cli and reporting and, on host, security and storage, any feature but X9's crash-matrix and X8's scenario-fixtures, a default included (law X10 r4 item 5, unit X9-1).", "before": "Run the real built opensip doctor with no override (law X10 items 5 and 7): a development build ends at CORE.NO_EMBEDDED_RELEASE, exit 2, with a command-envelope v7 schema-valid kind failure envelope in JSON and the matching human lines, and the account's real installation untouched; doctor takes no flags and the agent format is not applicable; help and completion list doctor. Source pins keep every environment, home, feature, test or debug selector out of the binary, the doctor ingress and the request path, require exactly one producer call in the ingress, and refuse a features table on the crates of that path.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/12/description"}}, {"after": "M3-B units B2-b4a to B2-b4c: the T2 discovery census. Re-verifies each lead-readable T2 store against its pinned gitTree and QD-22 digest from stored bytes, no-follow; checks every modeled walk against the B2-b0 reference population; converts it to objects, edges and bytes (affine in the root spelling length, sizes from a closed inventory) by an abstract walk mirroring the redesigned walker, with unknowns at their dearer branch and constants read by anchors from a whole-pinned cost closure; censuses workspaces as composed checkouts; writes the census record and the soundness vectors. Its --held-out-blind mode (B2-b4 plan r6 section 2.5) measures each held-out store through one guarded join and writes only a closed-format receipt of margins or error classes, bound to its inputs; the ordinary mode never opens a held-out store and composes held-out rows only from a current receipt.", "before": "M3-B unit B2-b4a: the T2 discovery census. Re-verifies each lead-readable T2 store against its pinned gitTree and QD-22 digest from stored bytes, no-follow, never opening a held-out entry; checks every modeled walk against the B2-b0 reference population; converts it to objects and edges by an abstract walk mirroring the walker, with unknowns at their dearer branch and constants read by anchors from a whole-pinned cost closure; censuses workspaces as composed checkouts; writes the census record and the soundness vectors.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/1234/description"}}, {"after": "Exercise tools/check_crash_matrix.py on a disposable synthetic run pair against the repository's real scope registry: the registry read, kill-set derivation and point grammar; a complete agreeing pair passing; missing and extra runs; each run-field corruption (verdicts, labels, clock epoch and script, child ordinals, expected, script, commit, dirty worktree, profile, fixture labels, unverified kills, keys, postState); a claimed process death; noncanonical bytes, floats and duplicate keys; matrix corruptions (run bytes, kill set, durability, unregistered census point, release absence, limits, product); an uncovered new durability point; disagreeing repetitions; required runs without clockEpoch or the scripted-clock label (unit X9-1); check-unit (unit X9-2) passing an uncommitted unit that check refuses, taking only its cases without full coverage, refusing a kill outside the kill set, keeping the per-run check and repetition agreement, and refusing an unknown unit; a row's unit member, X9-4's moved F14 row in that unit's subset only and still required by check, an invalid unit refused (unit X9-4), and an X9-6 row in no unit's subset, required by check and refused as a check-unit unit (unit X9-6); the timing guard required and bounded where the observer tick is armed and refused elsewhere (unit X9-3); the release-absence scan; and X9-6's two-target gate over synthetic host sets (the union kill set covered from either target, a union point no target kills, a larger occurrence count in the other target, one clock epoch and one name per target, each target's per-run check and repetition agreement, and the command line needing both targets) and coverage reporting the uncovered union points before any run.", "before": "Exercise tools/check_crash_matrix.py on a disposable synthetic run pair against the repository's real scope registry: the registry read, kill-set derivation and point grammar; a complete agreeing pair passing; missing and extra runs; each run-field corruption (verdicts, labels, expected, script, commit, dirty worktree, profile, fixture labels, unverified kills, keys, postState); a claimed process death; noncanonical bytes, floats and duplicate keys; matrix corruptions (run bytes, kill set, durability, unregistered census point, release absence, limits, product); an uncovered new durability point; disagreeing repetitions; and the release-absence scan.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/1255/description"}}, {"after": "Host's crash-matrix support surface (law X9 r1 item 6, unit X9-1), doc(hidden). Compiled only under the test-only crash-matrix feature, which no manifest enables from a dependency table and which the crate refuses in any build without debug assertions (law X9 r1 item 2), so it is absent from every release build. It forwards storage's surface, which forwards security's, unchanged; X3d-3's synthetic run candidate reaches it there, as storage's own item (law X3d r8 item 13, X9 r7). Host's own items (law X9 r10 and r11, unit X9-5) include two runners: finalize_commit, which reads the on-disk run candidate and calls host's finalize with the operation entry as its admission and the fixed delivery phase, so that replay runs before any custody, and reports an unreadable candidate on the host I/O row with no entry; and store_gc, which runs host's maintenance::run over the settlement_sweep entry. Each makes the process's one entry, a second runner call refusing on the invariant row without effect, and returns a value report only: FinalizeReport (the outcome with any retained RunId, remedy, namespace and requested-binding flag, the end-path, end-step and rollover disclosures, the optional failure and the delivered bytes) or GcReport (each namespace's sweep or busy skip with its row, and the ending row). Host also supplies the opensip.x9.run-candidate.v1 file's writer and reader, inputs only, and the fixed delivery phase, one fixed response with exit 0 whose failure comes only from an armed x7.delivery point. Nothing here takes a receipt, gate, guard, session, ProjectOperation, ReplayedRun or delivery phase from its caller, or sleeps, polls or waits on a timer. Apart from the forwarded driver entries, nothing here returns an authority type.", "before": "Host's crash-matrix support surface (law X9 r1 item 6, unit X9-1), doc(hidden). Compiled only under the test-only crash-matrix feature, which no manifest enables from a dependency table and which the crate refuses in any build without debug assertions (law X9 r1 item 2), so it is absent from every release build. It forwards storage's surface, which forwards security's, unchanged; item 6's synthetic run candidate for the evaluator's replay joins it with X9-5. Nothing here returns an authority type.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/140/description"}}, {"after": "The opensip doctor ingress (law X10 r3), beside the metadata ingress under the same process-custody request authority; doctor writes nothing durable. It makes exactly one producer call, observe_installation_for_doctor, the process's one attempt, read receipt and observation session, with the native account and the authenticated release and profile producers as its only inputs, and passes doctor's other checks as empty. A produced report or a report that cannot be produced is a kind doctor envelope carrying the assembled DoctorResult unchanged; an unreachable installation is a kind failure envelope with its law 468 item 6 row as termination and its one detail in errors (HOST.IO_FAILURE for the host I/O row). Exit codes derive from the class only. Remedies are the selected golden's where one exists and otherwise one fixed text per row. The STATE.SCHEMA_UNSUPPORTED row (law X3a item 5), which has no registered remedy, takes its own fixed text. The same kind failure envelope projects law X12 r3 item 7's policy-selection rows, whose CONFIG.INVALID and POLICY.IMPERATIVE_KEY_REFUSED details take the configuration module's remedies, and law X5 r3 item 5's replay-join rows, whose evidence.missing, evidence.regeneration-mismatch and EVALUATION.INPUT_REFUSED details take fact_admission's route remedies (item 5d, unit X5a); a detail with no remedy is a projection error.", "before": "The opensip doctor ingress (law X10 r3), beside the metadata ingress under the same process-custody request authority; doctor writes nothing durable. It makes exactly one producer call, observe_installation_for_doctor, the process's one attempt, read receipt and observation session, with the native account and the authenticated release and profile producers as its only inputs, and passes doctor's other checks as empty. A produced report or a report that cannot be produced is a kind doctor envelope carrying the assembled DoctorResult unchanged; an unreachable installation is a kind failure envelope with its law 468 item 6 row as termination and its one detail in errors (HOST.IO_FAILURE for the host I/O row). Exit codes derive from the class only. Remedies are the selected golden's where one exists and otherwise one fixed text per row.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/151/description"}}, {"after": "Check the doctor ingress from every value the security layer produces (law X10 items 4, 5 and 7): native-shaped complete and incomplete installations with no other checks (every finding kind, law X3a item 5's core and current-store findings included), explicitly synthetic report-bound inputs (one other defect plus the note, 255 plus the note versus 256, partial 256 versus 257) and every law 468 item 6 row, with the invariant row and law X3a item 5's STATE.SCHEMA_UNSUPPORTED row. Each envelope is validated against the selected command-envelope v7 source schema, its exit derives from its class, and its human output equals the lines its JSON facts imply, the complete termination included; an unproducible envelope without or with an altered nested doctor kind fails validation.", "before": "Check the doctor ingress from every value the security layer produces (law X10 items 4, 5 and 7): native-shaped complete and incomplete installations with no other checks, explicitly synthetic report-bound inputs (one other defect plus the note, 255 plus the note versus 256, partial 256 versus 257) and every law 468 item 6 row. Each envelope is validated against the selected command-envelope v7 source schema, its exit derives from its class, and its human output equals the lines its JSON facts imply, the complete termination included; an unproducible envelope without or with an altered nested doctor kind fails validation.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/152/description"}}, {"after": "The doctor report assembler (law 458c r6 items 7, 10 and 12, owner section 5 Diagnostics). A complete installation adds the informational INSTALLATION.DURABILITY_NOT_CHECKED entry with the owner's fixed text, which takes one of the 256 slots: 255 actual defects plus the note is a report, 256 is not. A report without the note keeps the 256-entry bound. defectsFound counts actual defects only, and DOCTOR.DEFECTS_FOUND is set only when that count is positive. A report that cannot be produced is HOST.IO_FAILURE / DOCTOR.REPORT_NOT_PRODUCIBLE, exit 4, and latches the report session; nothing is truncated and no envelope member is added. Doctor's installation check projects an unreachable installation onto its law 468 item 6 row with no report, and each structural finding of a reachable incomplete installation (a missing member, the pair, marker, store id, node or chain, and law X3a item 5's core and current-store findings) onto one CONFIG.CUSTODY_REFUSED entry with its installation-incomplete subject and fixed remedy. A positive DOCTOR.DEFECTS_FOUND carries the selected golden doctor-defects-found remedy. The doctor ingress (law X10) emits it through doctor_installation.", "before": "The doctor report assembler (law 458c r6 items 7, 10 and 12, owner section 5 Diagnostics). A complete installation adds the informational INSTALLATION.DURABILITY_NOT_CHECKED entry with the owner's fixed text, which takes one of the 256 slots: 255 actual defects plus the note is a report, 256 is not. A report without the note keeps the 256-entry bound. defectsFound counts actual defects only, and DOCTOR.DEFECTS_FOUND is set only when that count is positive. A report that cannot be produced is HOST.IO_FAILURE / DOCTOR.REPORT_NOT_PRODUCIBLE, exit 4, and latches the report session; nothing is truncated and no envelope member is added. Doctor's installation check projects an unreachable installation onto its law 468 item 6 row with no report, and each structural finding of a reachable incomplete installation onto one CONFIG.CUSTODY_REFUSED entry with its installation-incomplete subject and fixed remedy. Library only: nothing emits it yet.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/153/description"}}, {"after": "Own host admission of provider candidates, Coverage and occupancy joins against the selected source/context before evaluation; protocol framing alone grants no fact authority. Its M2 slice is the replay join alone (law X5 r3 item 1, unit X5a); the M3 syntax, context, occupancy and Coverage joins arrive here. RunCandidateInputs is the inert candidate Run, exactly what RetainedInputs::new borrows plus the claimed RunId, and claims nothing. replay_candidate calls the evaluator's replay_run once and returns its opaque ReplayedRun unchanged, or a ReplayRefusal carrying the claimed RunId and the evaluator's refusal unchanged (item 2); replay is pure, with no I/O, lock or ledger charge, so host finalization (X7a), its one production caller, runs it before any write or project admission, fence, lease or attempt row (item 3). REPLAY_LIMITS holds item 5e's local replay bounds, the value the one production call passes (item 5d). replay_termination maps each refusal onto one existing operational-failed exit 4 row in two steps (item 5): a promised retained byte the inputs lack, wherever the evaluator nested it, is HOST.IO_FAILURE evidence.missing naming the reference; otherwise an exhaustive match over ReplayError with no wildcard makes a structure, capture, law, input or evaluation failure PROVIDER.PROTOCOL_VIOLATION EVALUATION.INPUT_REFUSED and a mismatch HOST.IO_FAILURE evidence.regeneration-mismatch naming the claimed RunId. replay_remedy gives each route its item 5d remedy (identity's EvidenceUnavailable and RegenerationMismatch remedies verbatim, and the provider-return route's); the doctor ingress's row_remedy takes the same three remedies for those details.", "before": "Own host admission of provider candidates, Coverage and occupancy joins against the selected source/context before evaluation; protocol framing alone grants no fact authority.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/156/description"}}, {"after": "Check X5a (law X5 r3 item 8, host side), included as fact_admission.rs's cfg(test) module, on the synthetic replay corpus with no scratch installation: all 101 cases go through replay_candidate with REPLAY_LIMITS, the lawful ones returning the claimed RunId, the forged-output ones evidence.regeneration-mismatch with the claimed RunId as subject, and the corpus missing-blob case evidence.missing with its digest although the evaluator reports it under the walk; removing each member of one lawful case's claimed closure, one at a time, is evidence.missing naming exactly that reference, and removing an ambient member still replays; schema and identity failures are EVALUATION.INPUT_REFUSED on the provider-return route; every ReplayError variant maps to its row and remedy, each rendered through the shared failure envelope valid against command-envelope v7 with no run member; the remedies equal the fault-contract routes and identity carriers byte for byte; REPLAY_LIMITS holds item 5e's values, each of its nine dimensions admits the corpus at its measured exact need, at most the constant, and refuses one below on the structural row, and the capture-entry bound holds at the constant itself with ambient padding; and source pins show the join calls replay_run exactly once, mints no ReplayedRun, RunId or verdict, wires no check_plan_pack and reaches no custody, storage, ledger or I/O, and that every production caller passes REPLAY_LIMITS and there is exactly one (finalization.rs, unit X7a), with a self-check refusing caller-chosen limits.", "before": "Check X5a (law X5 r3 item 8, host side), included as fact_admission.rs's cfg(test) module, on the synthetic replay corpus with no scratch installation: all 101 cases go through replay_candidate with REPLAY_LIMITS, the lawful ones returning the claimed RunId, the forged-output ones evidence.regeneration-mismatch with the claimed RunId as subject, and the corpus missing-blob case evidence.missing with its digest although the evaluator reports it under the walk; removing each member of one lawful case's claimed closure, one at a time, is evidence.missing naming exactly that reference, and removing an ambient member still replays; schema and identity failures are EVALUATION.INPUT_REFUSED on the provider-return route; every ReplayError variant maps to its row and remedy, each rendered through the shared failure envelope valid against command-envelope v7 with no run member; the remedies equal the fault-contract routes and identity carriers byte for byte; REPLAY_LIMITS holds item 5e's values, each of its nine dimensions admits the corpus at its measured exact need, at most the constant, and refuses one below on the structural row, and the capture-entry bound holds at the constant itself with ambient padding; and source pins show the join calls replay_run exactly once, mints no ReplayedRun, RunId or verdict, wires no check_plan_pack and reaches no custody, storage, ledger or I/O, and that every production caller passes REPLAY_LIMITS (vacuous until X7a), with a self-check refusing caller-chosen limits.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/157/description"}}, {"after": "Coordinate full evaluator replay and live security admission, then pass opaque ReplayedRun and CommitSession to guarded storage publication; project success only after PublishedCommit. finalize is the host's one commit coordinator (law X7 r5, units X7a and X7b), in this order: X5's replay_candidate with REPLAY_LIMITS before any custody, a refusal ending the invocation on the replay join's row and remedy; the caller's X1 write and X2 project admission, which yields the one ProjectOperation; CommitSession::open, prepare_commit and publish; StoppedSession::finish on every end path; and only then, for a Committed Run not latched after admission, the caller's delivery phase at X9's x7.delivery required and optional points, which reads nothing from the store and takes no lease, receipt or read session. It projects exactly one outcome through exhaustive matches with no wildcard arm (item 3): a delivered commit is authoritative with its exit 0, 1 or 3 and any optional failure beside it; a failed or latched delivery is DELIVERY.REQUIRED_FAILED with the RunId retained; CommitUndetermined is the durability row with the ExecutionId as subject and the namespace beside it; a refusal is its own row; ExistingAttempt is the invariant row with the ExecutionId as subject and the whole requested binding disclosed (law X6 r3 item 6, unit X6b); the undetermined and existing-attempt rows take a later invocation's read-only recovery remedy. Only authoritative_run, which takes &PublishedCommit and nothing else, writes the authoritative label and RunId (DR-G27); an ephemeral analysis's label is ephemeral with no RunId. A typed CarrierCapacityExhausted is finished like any other attempt: X3d's finish carries it into X3b's end step, which runs the grant-generation rollover under the fence that step holds (X3b r10 item 13); finalization runs no rollover, projects item 6a's busy row naming the namespace, and discloses beside it, through SessionEnd's accessors, the rollover's outcome and any end-step failure, as it discloses a failed end-path settlement beside any outcome, none of which rewrites the row (item 6, unit X7b); storage never calls lifecycle. Finalization charges no ledger, never recovers its own outcome or retries a commit, and takes no admission, gate or fence of its own. The retained analysis projection after publication is the caller's delivery phase. Library only: no CLI command is wired.", "before": "Coordinate full evaluator replay and live security admission, then pass opaque ReplayedRun and CommitSession to guarded storage publication; project success only after PublishedCommit. Route typed CarrierCapacityExhausted through cleanup and operation-lease release to lifecycle rollover under its fence; storage never calls lifecycle. After publication, use outcomes.rs for the retained analysis projection and separately validate delegated attempt attribution, detail and authority under their owners.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/158/description"}}, {"after": "Declare pinned browser report build dependencies: TypeScript6.0.3 and esbuild0.28.2, with native DOM modules and an offline self-contained deliverable.", "before": "Declare report build dependencies and scripts; frontend framework and bundler remain undecided.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/16/description"}}, {"after": "Check X7a and X7b (law X7 r5 item 10, host side), included as finalization.rs's cfg(test) module, with no scratch installation, home or lock: every row of item 3's table through injected X3d outcomes (a delivered commit is authoritative with its RunId and the delivered exit 0, 1 or 3, with any optional failure disclosed beside it; a failed required delivery and a latch after admission are both DELIVERY.REQUIRED_FAILED, delivery-required, DELIVERY.RENDERER_FAILED_AFTER_COMMIT, exit 4, with the RunId retained; CommitUndetermined is DURABILITY.COMMIT_FAILED with the ExecutionId as subject, the namespace beside it, no RunId and the read-only recovery remedy; a refusal is its X3d item 9 row unchanged; ExistingAttempt is the invariant row with its ExecutionId as subject, the binding's namespace and its other three members (store generation digest, carrier digest and operation reference; law X6 r3 item 6, unit X6b) beside it and the later read-only recovery remedy, with no recovery in the writer's invocation; CarrierCapacityExhausted is item 6a's busy row naming the namespace); every caller-constructible CommitOutcome and NotPrepared joins one attempt, the requested binding carried through; a failed end-path settlement is disclosed beside the outcome and never rewrites it; through injected finish disclosures (unit X7b), each rollover outcome (rolled, already rolled, skipped) is disclosed beside the unchanged busy row, a rollover refusal keeps its own row (busy, host I/O, quarantine, invariant and budget) and an uncertain append is DURABILITY.COMMIT_FAILED with no subject, neither rewriting the attempt's row, and an entered end step's failure is disclosed on its own row beside the outcome and any rollover outcome; the delivery phase over a scripted renderer and sink (a latched commit starts nothing, a renderer, exit, write or flush failure fails once with no retry and no optional effect, an optional failure never changes the exit); over the synthetic replay corpus, a replay refusal ends before any admission on the replay join's row and remedy, and admission runs once after a successful replay with its refusal on its own row; an ephemeral label is never authoritative and has no RunId; and source pins show finalization is the host's only commit coordinator, its order (replay, admission, open, prepare, publish, finish, then delivery), the one authoritative literal inside authoritative_run(&PublishedCommit), no recovery, admission, gate, fence, ledger, lifecycle, retry or wildcard arm of its own, no 458c read entry, receipt or read session, X2 lease or storage reader (the delivery phase reads nothing from the store; only the commit facade's two exact use lines, the security one also naming the inert RequestedBinding and the end path's RolloverDisclosure and SessionEnd), X9's two x7.delivery points, and that it reads the end path only through SessionEnd's three accessors, once each, naming no rollover operation, end step, carrier record or lease (unit X7b). A ProjectOperation cannot be built from the host's tests before X9-1's support surface or X8b's scenario seam (law X9 r1 gap G1), so the session-level integration rows are not here. Synthetic fixtures; no compiler qualification.", "before": "Check X7a (law X7 r5 item 10, host side), included as finalization.rs's cfg(test) module, with no scratch installation, home or lock: every row of item 3's table through injected X3d outcomes (a delivered commit is authoritative with its RunId and the delivered exit 0, 1 or 3, with any optional failure disclosed beside it; a failed required delivery and a latch after admission are both DELIVERY.REQUIRED_FAILED, delivery-required, DELIVERY.RENDERER_FAILED_AFTER_COMMIT, exit 4, with the RunId retained; CommitUndetermined is DURABILITY.COMMIT_FAILED with the ExecutionId as subject, the namespace beside it, no RunId and the read-only recovery remedy; a refusal is its X3d item 9 row unchanged; ExistingAttempt is the invariant row with its ExecutionId as subject, the binding's namespace beside it and the later read-only recovery remedy, with no recovery in the writer's invocation; CarrierCapacityExhausted is item 6a's busy row naming the namespace); every caller-constructible CommitOutcome and NotPrepared joins one attempt; a failed end-path settlement is disclosed beside the outcome and never rewrites it; the delivery phase over a scripted renderer and sink (a latched commit starts nothing, a renderer, exit, write or flush failure fails once with no retry and no optional effect, an optional failure never changes the exit); over the synthetic replay corpus, a replay refusal ends before any admission on the replay join's row and remedy, and admission runs once after a successful replay with its refusal on its own row; an ephemeral label is never authoritative and has no RunId; and source pins show finalization is the host's only commit coordinator, its order (replay, admission, open, prepare, publish, finish, then delivery), the one authoritative literal inside authoritative_run(&PublishedCommit), no recovery, admission, gate, fence, ledger, lifecycle, retry or wildcard arm of its own, no 458c read entry, receipt or read session, X2 lease or storage reader (the delivery phase reads nothing from the store; only the commit facade's two exact use lines), and X9's two x7.delivery points. A ProjectOperation cannot be built from the host's tests before X9-1's support surface or X8b's scenario seam (law X9 r1 gap G1), so the session-level integration rows are not here. Synthetic fixtures; no compiler qualification.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/160/description"}}, {"after": "Retain the selected pair, every immutable lineage node and the physically opened selected endpoint store marker under the ordinary binding owner. Admit complete node ancestry without requiring reclaimed intermediate physical stores; recheck all contributing original descriptors and latch failures under one operation budget. Separate observation-only from durable/write capabilities; structural values alone grant no namespace/current authority.", "before": "Retain the selected pair, every lineage node and each corresponding store marker under one native installation fence; recheck every contributing descriptor before consumption and latch failures. Provisional structural evidence only; namespace, operation-wide budget, transition authorization and full binding remain separate.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/165/description"}}, {"after": "Host-owned provisional pair/marker bundle over the read session's selected store endpoint (law X3a r5 item 4, unit X3a-2): the pair through the host's provisional selection and the endpoint marker through storage's provisional marker, bound to the pair's S, both from the session's one read and never captured again. One full session recheck covers both and compares their full samples; the contributing samples are I's, the fence carrier's and the marker's, then the pair's, each after a full recheck and on H's filesystem. The bundle is no namespace, project, profile, active-slot or current authority admission.", "before": "Host-owned provisional pair/marker bundle. S equality is not full S/G/K, namespace/lineage, core/profile, active-slot or current authority admission.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/166/description"}}, {"after": "Host-owned provisional selection (law X3a r5 item 4, unit X3a-2): the decoded selection pair of the read session's selected store endpoint, taken through the session's endpoint admission from its one read and never captured again. Its recheck, run before the pair is handed out, is the session's full recheck and compares the pair's full sample; its filesystem sample follows that recheck, with the pair reopened by name under the retained I, its full sample unchanged and on H's filesystem. A pair the session cannot read or decode refuses the fence, never an invitation to create I. Grants no namespace, project, profile, active slot, current authority or initialization permission.", "before": "Host-owned provisional selection capture. Does not admit a selected store, core, profile, active slot, current authority or initialization permission.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/167/description"}}, {"after": "Project each law 468 item 6 row onto its one public D9 termination: class, exit, error code, fault cause, domain detail and subject, exactly as the 468a diagnostic routes publish them plus law 468 r5's incomplete-installation row. It also projects the rows later units added to the closed table, each with existing codes and the class S12 fixes for it: law X3a item 5's STATE.SCHEMA_UNSUPPORTED row (request-rejected, exit 2, REQUEST.SCHEMA_MAJOR_UNSUPPORTED); the fenced trust admission's rows of law X4T r9 item 10 and the live guards' TRUST.COMPONENT_REVOKED_DURING_OPERATION and OBSERVER.FAIL_STOP rows (law X4 r7 item 8, unit X4a), where each ROOT.* chain detail projects to its own code and only ROOT.SCHEMA_UNSUPPORTED leaves the admission class; and the commit facade's LEDGER.CORRUPT, MIGRATION.CORRUPT, DURABILITY.COMMIT_FAILED and project-owner rows (law X3d r6 item 9, unit X3d-1). The invariant row is SYSTEM.OUTCOME.ILLEGAL_STATE with HOST.INVARIANT_VIOLATED, exit 4. The match is exhaustive, so a new row cannot compile without its termination. InstallationTerminationV1 is the termination the doctor ingress emits for an unreachable installation (law X10), and the shape law X12 r3 item 7's policy-selection terminations take.", "before": "Project each law 468 item 6 row onto its one public D9 termination: class, exit, error code, fault cause, domain detail and subject, exactly as the 468a diagnostic routes publish them plus law 468 r5's incomplete-installation row. The match is exhaustive, so a new row cannot compile without its termination. Nothing emits it yet: no CLI command is wired.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/168/description"}}, {"after": "Coordinate store status, authorized purge/backup/recovery and pin-impact decisions through lifecycle/security/storage owners; never use cache deletion as evidence deletion. Its M2 content is store-gc's per-namespace settlement sweep step (law X6 r3 items 1, 7 and 8, unit X6c, under commit-recovery-readonly.v3.md section 4 and F53), routing only: sweep_store admits security's SettlementSweep once (X1's ordinary writer admission, the endpoint, I/stores/S and the registry, under the installation fence), and run takes each listed namespace in order under X2's EXCLUSIVE lease, runs storage's sweep_namespace under it and drops the lease before the next, skipping and retaining a busy lease and ending the sweep on a lease refusal's X2 row, then releases the fence last. Item 8's rows per namespace: an unreadable ledger is the host I/O row and the sweep continues, a refused charge the budget row, and a settle whose COMMIT reports failure DURABILITY.COMMIT_FAILED, never retried; a busy or missing ledger and a one-sided or contradictory attempt (F23) write nothing and report no row, and MIGRATION.CORRUPT is on no row. Orphan objects are left in place. No CLI command calls it; the crash matrix's store_gc runner (unit X9-5) calls run over its settlement_sweep entry.", "before": "Coordinate store status, authorized purge/backup/recovery and pin-impact decisions through lifecycle/security/storage owners; never use cache deletion as evidence deletion.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/176/description"}}, {"after": "M3-B r8 item 14a as r9 and r10 amend it (units B2-c2 and B2-c2b): the pre-Plan TypeScript configuration extends graph and effective allowJs: the closed specifier grammar, lexical normalization and containment, path and package candidate orders, canonical node identity with cycle, 32-node and 64 KiB checks and the r10 record bounds (at most 64 extends entries per file, repetitions included, and at most 4,096 characters per node path), reads only through a custody source (every link unresolved before C1), NE:527-545 inheritance evaluated once per node in postorder, the four internal keys with the S10 alias rows, and the composition with U-1. Pure; no production caller (J1, C1).", "before": "M3-B r8 item 14a as r9 amends it (unit B2-c2): the pre-Plan TypeScript configuration extends graph and effective allowJs: the closed specifier grammar, lexical normalization and containment, path and package candidate orders, canonical node identity with cycle, 32-node and 64 KiB checks, reads only through a custody source (every link unresolved before C1), NE:527-545 inheritance evaluated once per node in postorder, the four internal keys with the S10 alias rows, and the composition with U-1. Pure; no production caller (J1, C1).", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/215/description"}}, {"after": "M3-B units B2-c2 and B2-c2b controls: every item 14a law control replays with its options, graph, refusal key and path, source reads and linear evaluation work, and every admitted graph is admitted by the embedded TypeScriptConfigGraphV1 schema; the r10 bounds equal that schema extendsResolved maxItems and CanonicalPath maxLength, admitted at each bound and refused by the schema one past it; the seven units-config-effective-allowjs cases from configuration bytes; the composition reads only surviving configuration markers; the alias map equals the S10 registry rows; M3-J1 r7 row 59 projects the D9 row, the S10b remedy and the bounded subject; deep nesting at the file bound.", "before": "M3-B unit B2-c2 controls: every item 14a law control replays with its options, graph, refusal key and path, source reads and linear evaluation work; the seven units-config-effective-allowjs cases from configuration bytes; the composition reads only surviving configuration markers; the alias map equals the S10 registry rows; M3-J1 r7 row 59 projects the S10 D9 row, remedy and bounded subject; deep nesting at the file bound.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/216/description"}}, {"after": "M3-B units B2-c2 and B2-c2b TypeScript configuration vectors, generated by tools/host/typescript_config_vectors.py: the hand-written item 14a law controls (as r9 and r10 amend it) with the reference result, graph, source reads and work; graphs admitted by the S10b native model copy (contract successor 172); the units cases through the B2-c0 reference (contract successor 166); the S10 alias rows, the D9 row, the S10b remedy and bounded subjects. Design evidence, not qualification.", "before": "M3-B unit B2-c2 TypeScript configuration vectors, generated by tools/host/typescript_config_vectors.py: the hand-written item 14a law controls with the reference result, graph, source reads and work; graphs admitted by the S10 native model copy (contract successor 167); the units cases through the B2-c0 reference (contract successor 166); the alias rows, D9 row, remedy and bounded subjects. Design evidence, not qualification.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/217/description"}}, {"after": "Verify malformed provider joins, identity-valid but replay-invalid Runs, revoked authority and stale fences cannot produce acknowledged authoritative commits. It also holds the opaque API refusal suite's compile-fail driver (law X8 r3, unit X8a): a census of the cases under crates/host/tests/refusal/cases, each row naming its file, owning crate, type, owner unit, group and category (groups I, K and L from unit X8a, group D's AdmissionPermit, FinalGate and OperationGuard rows from unit X4a, the session types' rows of groups D, E, F and G from unit X3d-1, storage's facade rows of groups A to E, G and H for prepare_commit, PreparedCommit and PublishedCommit from unit X3d-2, group C's unnameable RunCandidateInputs and group E's ReplayedRun reuse from unit X5a, the authoritative projection's rows of groups A and B and AuthoritativeRun's capability rows of groups D, E and G from unit X7a, and group J's unresolved scenario modules, category TestSeam, from unit X8b), checked before anything compiles; a nested cargo check of opensip-host's release surface; each case compiled twice by the pinned rustc 1.95.0, the control compiling and the misuse under --cfg x8_misuse failing with exactly its one annotated error; the must-reject self-tests under crates/host/tests/refusal/selftest each rejected for the reason it names; the annotation grammar; the scenario-fixtures source pin over the workspace manifests, with its refusals; and law X8 r3 item 3b's source pin confining security's two-phase adapter traits, begin_journal_txn and seal_under_append_lock to security's defining module (with its root re-export) and storage's commit.rs, with its self-check (unit X3d-2). The behavioural cases that verify the first sentence are unit X8c's. They are in this file (law X8 r4 item 5, macOS only): B0 to B8, each a synthetic_ test on its own ScenarioHome driving the public facade (scenario::operation, CommitSession::open, replay_run, prepare_commit, publish and finish; B8 replay_run alone), each refusal from B1 to B6 asserting its outcome variant, exact row and public projection, that no PublishedCommit exists, and the carrier and evidence-ledger census after finish: B0, the lawful control, commits; B1 another Run's replay and B2 another evaluator closure are refused on the invariant row; B3 a replaced project marker or writer lease file and B5 a lineage node naming another generation are refused on the custody row at publish's first checkpoint; B4 a planted attempt row for the session's ExecutionId is ExistingAttempt with the requested binding and stays unchanged; B6 a helper process's live revocation of the core closure is TRUST.COMPONENT_REVOKED_DURING_OPERATION, while B7's unrelated revocation still commits; and B8 an altered claimed output never replays and leaves no namespace, carrier, lease, ledger or marker. Beside them are item 5a's revocation helper entry, storage's synthetic run candidate included by path (law X3d r8 item 13), and the run candidate's registry pin (host's and storage's schema sources list the same 48 files, in order).", "before": "Verify malformed provider joins, identity-valid but replay-invalid Runs, revoked authority and stale fences cannot produce acknowledged authoritative commits.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/224/description"}}, {"after": "The crash matrix's host rows (law X9 r15 item 12, unit X9-5, with r10 to r13): F01, F12's and F40's caller route, F16, F17, F32 with X3b item 13's rollover crash table, F39's delivery half and F53's store-gc step, run through host's own commit coordinator and store-gc step in fresh processes. Built only with an explicit --features crash-matrix (required-features), and whole-file under the support predicate. Its children are this binary re-executed by X9-0's driver under the scripted wall clock: fixture; candidate (r10's host order: one operation entry, CommitSession::open, X3d-3's candidate and r12's distinct variant written to disk with the session's core closure, then the session's refused end); finalize (host's finalize_commit runner, which replays before any custody); store-gc (host's store_gc runner); recover; and a publisher that revokes the session's core closure under the installation fence. The parent transcribes nothing: it runs each reviewed host required row's script step by step by blocking record reads, records r12's timing guard on the parent's monotonic clock for runs that arm the observer tick, captures the post state, runs item 8's ladder through host where a row leaves an attempt, and writes one run record per run and the run set's matrix.json with r11's three-run host census. x9_5_matrix runs the file's rows that carry no unit member; x9_6_matrix, X9-6's host run set (law X9 r16), runs every row, X9-6's four F16 and F17 kills at the x7.delivery points included, over the same census. On every child whose outcome is an authoritative commit it asserts item 8's guard order from that child's own trace (law X9 r16, unit X9-6): acquisition at the journal's level 3, then the ledger's level 3, then level 4; release at the evidence COMMIT, level 4, then level 3, then the writer lease, then the end fence, with an owed REV or CLN append's level 4 and level 3 released before the lease; a miss fails the run. Nothing sleeps, polls or waits on a timer. Synthetic; no compiler qualification.", "before": "The crash matrix's host rows (law X9 r15 item 12, unit X9-5, with r10 to r13): F01, F12's and F40's caller route, F16, F17, F32 with X3b item 13's rollover crash table, F39's delivery half and F53's store-gc step, run through host's own commit coordinator and store-gc step in fresh processes. Built only with an explicit --features crash-matrix (required-features), and whole-file under the support predicate. Its children are this binary re-executed by X9-0's driver under the scripted wall clock: fixture; candidate (r10's host order: one operation entry, CommitSession::open, X3d-3's candidate and r12's distinct variant written to disk with the session's core closure, then the session's refused end); finalize (host's finalize_commit runner, which replays before any custody); store-gc (host's store_gc runner); recover; and a publisher that revokes the session's core closure under the installation fence. The parent transcribes nothing: it runs each reviewed host required row's script step by step by blocking record reads, records r12's timing guard on the parent's monotonic clock for runs that arm the observer tick, captures the post state, runs item 8's ladder through host where a row leaves an attempt, and writes one run record per run and the run set's matrix.json with r11's three-run host census. Nothing sleeps, polls or waits on a timer. Synthetic; no compiler qualification.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/225/description"}}, {"after": "Host's reviewed required runs of the crash matrix (law X9 r10 and r16: host's rows only, a file separate from storage's), opensip.x9.required-runs.v1 canonical JSON with storage's clockEpoch. Unit X9-5 transcribes its 94 rows (F01 4, F12 2, F16 1, F17 1, F32 76, F39 1, F40 3, F53 6) from law X9 r15 before any lead run: F32's kill points and their crash-table windows from the unarmed host census's exhausted-carrier run, and every expected value from the law's row or the owning law's outcome it names, never read back from a run. Unit X9-6 appends law X9 r16's four host kill-set coverage rows before any run, for 98: process-death kills after r12's distinct R2 at x7.delivery.required.before and .after (F16) and x7.delivery.optional.before and .after (F17), each at #1, expecting the finalize child killed and the committed Run kept (R1 committed-historically:pendingSettlement, R2 authoritative:0, R3 committed, R4 committed-historically); each carries the unit member X9-6, which keeps it out of every unit's check-unit subset. Each row names its case, variant, owning units, labels, a step-by-step script (candidate variants, the reserved-slot plant, arms, runs, awaits with kill, revocation or live-writer actions, mutations, and r12's distinct R2) and expected per-step values. Host's crash-matrix target reads it; tools/check_crash_matrix.py check-unit --unit X9-5 checks run sets against it, and X9-6's check takes it beside storage's file, as does the coverage command. Synthetic; no compiler qualification.", "before": "Host's reviewed required runs of the crash matrix (law X9 r10: X9-5's rows only, a file separate from storage's), opensip.x9.required-runs.v1 canonical JSON with storage's clockEpoch. Unit X9-5 transcribes its 94 rows (F01 4, F12 2, F16 1, F17 1, F32 76, F39 1, F40 3, F53 6) from law X9 r15 before any lead run: F32's kill points and their crash-table windows from the unarmed host census's exhausted-carrier run, and every expected value from the law's row or the owning law's outcome it names, never read back from a run. Each row names its case, variant, owning units, labels, a step-by-step script (candidate variants, the reserved-slot plant, arms, runs, awaits with kill, revocation or live-writer actions, mutations, and r12's distinct R2) and expected per-step values. Host's crash-matrix target reads it; tools/check_crash_matrix.py check-unit --unit X9-5 checks run sets against it, and X9-6's check takes it beside storage's file. Synthetic; no compiler qualification.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/229/description"}}, {"after": "Own alloc-only full-triple lineage keys, the one relative node spelling transitions/lineage/S/G/K.node that every crate joins, closed canonical 4096-byte node decoding and bounded iterative supplied-chain inspection. Callers assemble OS paths from those components; the spelling is not resolution. SuppliedChain::into_nodes yields the inspected chain by value, selected node to root, so that the write gate's one read and the observation session's member reads keep it for the selected store endpoint (law X3a items 2 and 3, unit X3a-1). Decoded chains do not prove native existence, publication, complete operation budget or authority.", "before": "Own alloc-only full-triple lineage keys, closed canonical4096-byte node decoding and bounded iterative supplied-chain inspection. Keep OS-path assembly in lifecycle; decoded chains do not prove native existence, publication, complete operation budget or authority.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/411/description"}}, {"after": "Expose wall and sleep-inclusive monotonic observations; do not decide trust time. Under the test-only crash-matrix feature only, observe_clock takes its wall reading from the crash barrier's scripted wall clock when the process is a matrix child with a clock script (law X9 r2 item 3, unit X9-1); its monotonic readings and boot identity stay native, and without the script or the feature the wall reading is the OS's.", "before": "Expose wall and sleep-inclusive monotonic observations; do not decide trust time.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/507/description"}}, {"after": "The crash barrier module of law X9 r1 items 1 to 5 (unit X9-0), compiled only under the test-only crash-matrix feature, which no manifest enables and which src/lib.rs refuses without debug assertions. It holds the scope registry fixed by item 5 (with a read-only flag for x4.observer and x6.recover, and two x9.selftest scopes only in this crate's test build), the point grammar <scope>[.segments][/<primitive-step>.<before|after>] with per-name 1-based occurrences, the kinds step, fallible, write, gate and draw and the actions each admits, and the OPENSIP_X9_ARMS parser (<full name>#<k|*>=hold|fail-before|fail-after|torn|inject-id:exec1_..., inject-id only at x3d.session.execution-draw). It also holds law X9 r2 item 3's scripted wall clock (unit X9-1): OPENSIP_X9_CLOCK=<E>:<k>, read once (E a whole second, k the process's ordinal, with no sign, space or leading zero), and scripted_wall, which only observe_clock's wall reading calls: in a matrix child with a script, reading n (from 0) is E + 3600\u00b7k + n seconds with zero nanoseconds, and in any other process there is none, so the OS's wall clock is read. Without OPENSIP_X9_ARMS every point is one OnceLock read and a return. In a matrix child (OPENSIP_X9_CHILD set) every reached point writes one tagged stderr record X9|pid|thread|n|name#k|event|payload in a single write(2) of at most 512 bytes, numbered under one lock; hold registers the point, writes held and blocks on a stdin read until resume (bare when exactly one thread holds) or resume <name>#<k>, one held thread reading for all and handing lines to their targets by condvar; fail-before and fail-after return EIO from a primitive or the caller's error at a protocol site without or after the effect; torn writes the first half of the bytes with the same write, then holds, and a resumed torn write fails with EIO; a gate's armed rendezvous replaces the product's timed wait; the draw records its payload or returns an injected ExecutionId. An unadmitted action, a primitive outside any scope, an unknown scope or a malformed arming is a harness-error record and exit status 86, as are a clock script outside a matrix child or malformed and a process's scripted wall reading n = 3600. It never sleeps, polls or waits on a timer. Test-only; confers no authority, qualification or production seam.", "before": "The crash barrier module of law X9 r1 items 1 to 5 (unit X9-0), compiled only under the test-only crash-matrix feature, which no manifest enables and which src/lib.rs refuses without debug assertions. It holds the scope registry fixed by item 5 (with a read-only flag for x4.observer and x6.recover, and two x9.selftest scopes only in this crate's test build), the point grammar <scope>[.segments][/<primitive-step>.<before|after>] with per-name 1-based occurrences, the kinds step, fallible, write, gate and draw and the actions each admits, and the OPENSIP_X9_ARMS parser (<full name>#<k|*>=hold|fail-before|fail-after|torn|inject-id:exec1_..., inject-id only at x3d.session.execution-draw). Without OPENSIP_X9_ARMS every point is one OnceLock read and a return. In a matrix child (OPENSIP_X9_CHILD set) every reached point writes one tagged stderr record X9|pid|thread|n|name#k|event|payload in a single write(2) of at most 512 bytes, numbered under one lock; hold registers the point, writes held and blocks on a stdin read until resume (bare when exactly one thread holds) or resume <name>#<k>, one held thread reading for all and handing lines to their targets by condvar; fail-before and fail-after return EIO from a primitive or the caller's error at a protocol site without or after the effect; torn writes the first half of the bytes with the same write, then holds, and a resumed torn write fails with EIO; a gate's armed rendezvous replaces the product's timed wait; the draw records its payload or returns an injected ExecutionId. An unadmitted action, a primitive outside any scope, an unknown scope or a malformed arming is a harness-error record and exit status 86. It never sleeps, polls or waits on a timer. Test-only; confers no authority, qualification or production seam.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/508/description"}}, {"after": "The parent driver of law X9 r1 item 3 and item 5's census (unit X9-0), under the test-only crash-matrix feature. MatrixChild re-executes the current test binary as current_exe() --exact <entry> --nocapture --test-threads=1 with a cleared environment holding only OPENSIP_X9_CHILD, OPENSIP_X9_INPUT, OPENSIP_X9_ARMS (absent for an untraced child), OPENSIP_X9_CLOCK (law X9 r2 item 3, unit X9-1: <E>:<k> from the ChildSpec's ScriptedClock, the run set's epoch and the child's ordinal; absent without one, so the child reads the OS's wall clock) and TMPDIR, validates the arming and the clock script before spawning, reads the child's stderr records on a reader thread and drains stdout, awaits a held or other event by blocking reads, writes addressed resumes on stdin, and kills with SIGKILL; dropping an unfinished child kills it. The per-run watchdog (300 s; shorter only in this crate's tests) is the one timed wait: it kills the child and the run can only be a HARNESS-ERROR. Exit verifies a finished child (exit 0, the entry ran, no harness-error, every arm reached) and item 3's death at a point (signal 9, the holding thread's last record is that held, and no later record names a durability point), and gives the outcome and the trace without the process id. Census takes a traced child with nothing armed into points with contiguous occurrences and durability, and derives the kill set: each durability point at #1 and, when repeated, at (n + 1) / 2 and n. Test-only; confers no authority.", "before": "The parent driver of law X9 r1 item 3 and item 5's census (unit X9-0), under the test-only crash-matrix feature. MatrixChild re-executes the current test binary as current_exe() --exact <entry> --nocapture --test-threads=1 with a cleared environment holding only OPENSIP_X9_CHILD, OPENSIP_X9_INPUT, OPENSIP_X9_ARMS (absent for an untraced child) and TMPDIR, validates the arming before spawning, reads the child's stderr records on a reader thread and drains stdout, awaits a held or other event by blocking reads, writes addressed resumes on stdin, and kills with SIGKILL; dropping an unfinished child kills it. The per-run watchdog (300 s; shorter only in this crate's tests) is the one timed wait: it kills the child and the run can only be a HARNESS-ERROR. Exit verifies a finished child (exit 0, the entry ran, no harness-error, every arm reached) and item 3's death at a point (signal 9, the holding thread's last record is that held, and no later record names a durability point), and gives the outcome and the trace without the process id. Census takes a traced child with nothing armed into points with contiguous occurrences and durability, and derives the kill set: each durability point at #1 and, when repeated, at (n + 1) / 2 and n. Test-only; confers no authority.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/509/description"}}, {"after": "X9-0's self-tests (law X9 r1 item 12), compiled only for this crate's tests with the crash-matrix feature. A matrix_child test entry returns at once in the parent's own run; each test re-executes it as a child over a /tmp scratch root and drives it: a kill at replace's rename.after with the new bytes visible, at write.before leaving the empty staging file no destructor removed, and at lock.after with the flock busy until the death, each verified by SIGKILL and the trace; hold then resume to the lawful outcome and tree, with the bare resume when one thread holds; fail-before and fail-after at rename, file-barrier and directory-barrier returning EIO on the primitive's own stage and visibility, with the state each leaves, and at an accounted barrier; torn writes leaving half the bytes, killed or resumed into a failed write; an untraced child writing no record and matching the featureless outcome and tree, a traced unarmed child doing the same and recording the pinned 42-point census and 44-point kill set with a reproducible trace; armed but unreached points and actions a point's kind does not admit as harness errors; a primitive outside any scope; addressed resumes across two held threads, read-only points of another thread admitted after a kill point and a durability point refused; the watchdog ending a never-resumed child as a harness error; gate, protocol fallible and draw points with inject-id; and the arming grammar, point names, durability and kill-set derivation. X9-1's clock self-tests (law X9 r2 item 3) run an untraced child that reads observe_clock: under a ScriptedClock, ordinals 0 to 2 each read E + 3600\u00b7k, + 1 and + 2 seconds with zero nanoseconds while the monotonic readings move forward, and no record is written; without a script the wall reading lies within 60 s of /bin/date +%s readings taken before and after the child (unit F7); 3600 readings finish and a 3601st is a harness error; and the clock script grammar.", "before": "X9-0's self-tests (law X9 r1 item 12), compiled only for this crate's tests with the crash-matrix feature. A matrix_child test entry returns at once in the parent's own run; each test re-executes it as a child over a /tmp scratch root and drives it: a kill at replace's rename.after with the new bytes visible, at write.before leaving the empty staging file no destructor removed, and at lock.after with the flock busy until the death, each verified by SIGKILL and the trace; hold then resume to the lawful outcome and tree, with the bare resume when one thread holds; fail-before and fail-after at rename, file-barrier and directory-barrier returning EIO on the primitive's own stage and visibility, with the state each leaves, and at an accounted barrier; torn writes leaving half the bytes, killed or resumed into a failed write; an untraced child writing no record and matching the featureless outcome and tree, a traced unarmed child doing the same and recording the pinned 42-point census and 44-point kill set with a reproducible trace; armed but unreached points and actions a point's kind does not admit as harness errors; a primitive outside any scope; addressed resumes across two held threads, read-only points of another thread admitted after a kill point and a durability point refused; the watchdog ending a never-resumed child as a harness error; gate, protocol fallible and draw points with inject-id; and the arming grammar, point names, durability and kill-set derivation.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/510/description"}}, {"after": "Non-authority bounded object/edge/byte accounting borrowed across one host operation, with permanent failure, unwind closure and precharged effect/postcheck reservations; prepaid work inside a postcheck reservation draws only from its carved allowance, failing ReservedPostcheck before the work it covers (law 467 item 6). Each instance may take one settlement reserve (law X3d r6 item 8, unit X3d-0): reserve_settlement charges it to used at once and never refunds it, and settle spends a SettlementReserve this instance issued once, even after the ledger has failed, with every charge inside drawing only from its allowance; outside settle a failed ledger still refuses everything. Retained evidence identity/cache and native authority remain with their owners; constructor alone cannot establish same-attempt discipline. Includes focused accounting regressions and the compile_fail doctests that keep SettlementReserve uncloneable, unconstructible and out of WorkScope and ReservedPostchecks.", "before": "Non-authority bounded object/edge/byte accounting borrowed across one host operation, with permanent failure, unwind closure and precharged effect/postcheck reservations. Retained evidence identity/cache and native authority remain with their owners; constructor alone cannot establish same-attempt discipline. Includes focused accounting regressions.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/542/description"}}, {"after": "Exercise the public settlement reserve of the work ledger (law X3d r6 items 8 and 13, unit X3d-0): a reserve charged to used at once and never refunded; a settlement still spendable after a nested failure, an unwind, a swallowed failure, a budget overrun or a postcheck overrun has closed the ledger, which still refuses every scope, charge, run, record and effect outside it; charges inside it, including nested run, effect, ReservedPostchecks and prepaid, drawing only from the allowance with used unchanged, even past exhausted limits; an overrun inside it refused ReservedPostcheck before the work, and an Err, a swallowed failure or an unwind inside it latching both the settlement and the ledger, so no second step runs; a successful settlement leaving an open ledger open; a second reservation, before or after the spend, and a reservation on a closed ledger or beyond the limits refused on existing BudgetFailure variants, taking nothing and latching; a reserve from another instance refused Closed before its action; another ledger unchanged; and a source pin (unit X3d-1) that, outside comments and _tests.rs files, the production sources naming reserve_settlement or SettlementReserve are exactly the end-path chain work_ledger.rs, lib.rs, initial_installation.rs, read_premise.rs, operation_handoff.rs and commit_session.rs, that apart from work_ledger.rs only initial_installation.rs calls reserve_settlement, once, and that lib.rs names SettlementReserve once and reserve_settlement never. Pure accounting and borrow tests; no journal, carrier, native effect or end-path qualification.", "before": "Exercise the public settlement reserve of the work ledger (law X3d r6 items 8 and 13, unit X3d-0): a reserve charged to used at once and never refunded; a settlement still spendable after a nested failure, an unwind, a swallowed failure, a budget overrun or a postcheck overrun has closed the ledger, which still refuses every scope, charge, run, record and effect outside it; charges inside it, including nested run, effect, ReservedPostchecks and prepaid, drawing only from the allowance with used unchanged, even past exhausted limits; an overrun inside it refused ReservedPostcheck before the work, and an Err, a swallowed failure or an unwind inside it latching both the settlement and the ledger, so no second step runs; a successful settlement leaving an open ledger open; a second reservation, before or after the spend, and a reservation on a closed ledger or beyond the limits refused on existing BudgetFailure variants, taking nothing and latching; a reserve from another instance refused Closed before its action; another ledger unchanged; and a source pin that no production source names reserve_settlement or SettlementReserve until X3d-1. Pure accounting and borrow tests; no journal, carrier, native effect or end-path qualification.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/548/description"}}, {"after": "The private final commit-admission primitive, not a CommitSession constructor: it is handed no custody, grant, generation, writer or journal guard and no replay evidence of its own. FinalGate is one SeqCst two-bit atomic (0 preparing, 1 admitted, 2 latched before admission, 3 admitted then latched) whose compare-exchange 0 to 1 is the only admission and whose fetch-OR 2 is the latch, never reset. OperationGate (law X4 r7 items 2 and 3, unit X4a) is an operation's one gate, created at the lease-free point before any prepared value, not Clone, with its StopObserver to latch and observe it; its admit binds the prepared value and mints the one AdmissionPermit, or hands the value back with the gate's state on a refusal, so there is no second permit. PreparedAttempt is the same gate created with its prepared value. An AdmissionPermit is consumed once, and a later latch records a stop without relabelling its result. It places law X9 r1 G4's x4.gate.admit.after and x4.gate.latch.after points. PreparedJournalSeal (law X3d r6 item 4 step 3.3, unit X3d-1) binds the SEAL record that seal_under_append_lock is about to append to the caller's borrowed evaluator-minted ReplayedRun by run id, refusing another record type, a missing or fixture run id and a mismatched run; it establishes no live custody, guard, generation or durable commit. CommitSession, JournalWriteTxn and JournalSealBinding are custody/commit_session.rs's, and the S6 checkpoint is custody/operation_guard.rs's. Its tests check every bounded admit, latch and observe trace exhaustively, a latch before and after admission, a dropped permit never reopening admission, a concurrent stop never lost and no effect duplicated, and the seal binding's refusals.", "before": "Mint opaque live CommitSession from actual project/store custody, operational grants and writer guard; bind exact operation/generation and perform final S6 checkpoint under the journal lock. Own the session-consuming JournalWriteTxn, opaque JournalSealBinding and atomic final commit-admission gate; accept only evaluator-minted ReplayedRun for SEAL targets.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/567/description"}}, {"after": "X9-1's pin of every source site that names a test-only feature (law X9 r1 item 6, law X8 r3 item 4b), in every security test build: it scans every cfg and cfg! in crates/*/src and crates/*/tests and requires exactly the pinned table, the shared fixture gates under the one joint predicate any(test, feature = crash-matrix, feature = scenario-fixtures) (Image::Injected with the injected image and synthetic V2 profile set, HomeSource::Fixture with the creator path on a scratch H and the gate's test constructor, X4T-0 and its accessor, and the fenced state.v1 publisher, each with the closure it needs, and the fifth gate law X3d r8 item 13 adds by name (unit X3d-3), CommitSession::core_evaluator_closure_preimages with its two hops through ProjectOperation and the write receipt), the crash-matrix-only carrier fixtures, each crate's guard and support module, the census tests, security's and storage's scenario modules under scenario-fixtures alone (law X8 r3 item 4a, unit X8b), storage's and host's crash-matrix targets (tests/commit_tests.rs, unit X9-2; tests/commit_matrix_tests.rs, unit X9-5), each guarded whole-file by the support predicate, and X9-0's and X9 r2's platform sites; with a scanner self-test, a no-sleep pin over X9-1's sources, storage's run candidate and both matrix targets, which admits in each target at most one line, the timing guard's type GuardClock = std::time::Instant (law X9 r12 item 11; units X9-3 and X9-5), and tests of the shared producers (accepted trust installed, the fenced publisher advancing the revocation version and refusing without writing while another holder has the fence, a writer admitted) and of the inherited carrier fixture.", "before": "X9-1's pin of every source site that names a test-only feature (law X9 r1 item 6, law X8 r3 item 4b), in every security test build: it scans every cfg and cfg! in crates/*/src and crates/*/tests and requires exactly the pinned table, the shared fixture gates under the one joint predicate any(test, feature = crash-matrix, feature = scenario-fixtures) (Image::Injected with the injected image and synthetic V2 profile set, HomeSource::Fixture with the creator path on a scratch H and the gate's test constructor, X4T-0 and its accessor, and the fenced state.v1 publisher, each with the closure it needs), the crash-matrix-only carrier fixtures, each crate's guard and support module, the census tests and X9-0's and X9 r2's platform sites; with a scanner self-test, a no-sleep pin over X9-1's sources, and tests of the shared producers (accepted trust installed, the fenced publisher advancing the revocation version and refusing without writing while another holder has the fence, a writer admitted) and of the inherited carrier fixture.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/570/description"}}, {"after": "Security's synthetic crash-matrix support surface (law X9 r1 item 6, unit X9-1), doc(hidden). Compiled only under the test-only crash-matrix feature, which no manifest enables from a dependency table and which the crate refuses in any build without debug assertions (law X9 r1 item 2), so it is absent from every release build. It produces inputs on disk and returns paths, values and outcomes, and apart from its three driver entries never an authority type: SyntheticInstallation (create, or open by paths in a matrix child) through the real creator path with InitialCore's injected loaded image and the synthetic V2 profile set; X4T-0's signed accepted store written in place; publish_revocation, a thin caller of the shared fenced state.v1 publisher, returning the revocation version before and after, and refusing without writing in a process that made a driver entry; the inherited format-1 and format-2 carrier fixture; X3b-2's reserved-slot technique; and the FIXTURE and PROFILE_STANDING labels every run records. Every producer is a thin caller of a site on the pinned shared fixture-gate list. Law X9 r4 item 6's three driver entries (unit X9-2) are the one exception to returning no authority type: operation, the production chain to a root's ProjectOperation under an APPEND-WRITE lease (X1's ordinary writer with the gate over HomeSource::Fixture, X2's root admission and tracking with the first registration when the root is unregistered, and begin_operation); recovery_admission, a read receipt for the installation's H, recovery's own allocated ledger and recovery_admission::admit_on over HomeSource::Fixture; and settlement_sweep, X1's writer and then settlement_sweep::admit_on. Each takes only the synthetic installation, a root path or an inert RecoveryRequest and returns only that composition's result or refusal, and a process makes at most one call to any of the three (X1 items 1 and 7): the module's own flag makes a second refuse on the invariant row without effect. Synthetic; no compiler qualification.", "before": "Security's synthetic crash-matrix support surface (law X9 r1 item 6, unit X9-1), doc(hidden). Compiled only under the test-only crash-matrix feature, which no manifest enables from a dependency table and which the crate refuses in any build without debug assertions (law X9 r1 item 2), so it is absent from every release build. It produces inputs on disk and returns paths, values and outcomes, never an authority type: SyntheticInstallation (create, or open by paths in a matrix child) through the real creator path with InitialCore's injected loaded image and the synthetic V2 profile set; X4T-0's signed accepted store written in place; publish_revocation, a thin caller of the shared fenced state.v1 publisher, returning the revocation version before and after; the inherited format-1 and format-2 carrier fixture; X3b-2's reserved-slot technique; and the FIXTURE and PROFILE_STANDING labels every run records. Every producer is a thin caller of a site on the pinned shared fixture-gate list. Synthetic; no compiler qualification.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/571/description"}}, {"after": "Law X9 r1 item 7's post-state (unit X9-1), under the test-only crash-matrix feature: raw (every regular file under a scratch root by relative path, size and SHA-256, but a file the owner cannot read, which is never read and which logical's unreadable member records by path, size and mode instead, for rows F24 and F53's mode-000 mutations, unit X9-3), logical (every SQLite database dumped table by table from a private copy of its file and -wal, a BLOB holding UTF-8 text as that text (unit X9-2), the rows of a table without rowid ordered by their unnumbered shape and tied rows by how each numbers against the rest of the dump (unit X9-3), and a file with SQLite's header that SQLite cannot read recorded as undumpable (unit X9-3), the operational witness and floor files decoded, the object set, recorded by each name's shape, sorted, because some of its content-digest names derive from drawn values (unit X9-2), and trustState, the trust store reduced to one digest over its files normalized one by one) and normalizedSha256 (logical with every drawn value, ExecutionId, op- and req1_ token, staging and temporary-name nonce, drawn identifier, digest over drawn content and timestamp, replaced by a placeholder numbered in order of first appearance, members ordered by their unnumbered shape, and every native clock sample or wall-clock datum replaced by its member name alone, law X9 r2 item 7). Capturing opens nothing under the root for writing, and locks, checkpoints and creates nothing there.", "before": "Law X9 r1 item 7's post-state (unit X9-1), under the test-only crash-matrix feature: raw (every regular file under a scratch root by relative path, size and SHA-256), logical (every SQLite database dumped table by table from a private copy of its file and -wal, the operational witness and floor files decoded, the object set, and trustState, the trust store reduced to one digest over its files normalized one by one) and normalizedSha256 (logical with every drawn value, ExecutionId, op- and req1_ token, staging and temporary-name nonce, drawn identifier, digest over drawn content and timestamp, replaced by a placeholder numbered in order of first appearance, members ordered by their unnumbered shape, and every native clock sample or wall-clock datum replaced by its member name alone, law X9 r2 item 7). Capturing opens, locks, checkpoints and creates nothing under the root.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/572/description"}}, {"after": "Law X9 r1 item 7's run writer (unit X9-1, with law X9 r2 items 3 and 6), under the test-only crash-matrix feature: one canonical opensip.x9.run.v1 record per run, runs/<case>-<variant>.json, never replacing an existing file, carrying the product, the host with the synthetic-signed-v2 fixture and BASELINE-ATTESTED standing, the run set's clock (epoch and the x9-ordinal-3600 script), each child's role, ordinal, exit, lastHeld point, trace record count and digest (X9-0's trace lines without the process id) and outcome, the post-state, the ladder, the transcribed expected value and the caller's verdict, with the optional ladderEnd and notApplicable and, for a run that arms the observer tick, law X9 r1 item 11's timingGuard (r12 and r13, unit X9-3): the caller's measured monotonic milliseconds and the 5,000 ms limit, TIMING_GUARD_LIMIT_MS; and a census with its kill set in matrix.json's shape. It records and decides no verdict.", "before": "Law X9 r1 item 7's run writer (unit X9-1, with law X9 r2 items 3 and 6), under the test-only crash-matrix feature: one canonical opensip.x9.run.v1 record per run, runs/<case>-<variant>.json, never replacing an existing file, carrying the product, the host with the synthetic-signed-v2 fixture and BASELINE-ATTESTED standing, the run set's clock (epoch and the x9-ordinal-3600 script), each child's role, ordinal, exit, lastHeld point, trace record count and digest (X9-0's trace lines without the process id) and outcome, the post-state, the ladder, the transcribed expected value and the caller's verdict; and a census with its kill set in matrix.json's shape. It records and decides no verdict.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/573/description"}}, {"after": "The security side of the CommitSession storage facade (law X3d r6 items 1 to 4, 7, 8 and 9; unit X3d-1, with X4c's end path merged), custody.rs's macOS module beside X2e's operation_handoff and X4a's operation_guard. CommitSession::open consumes one ProjectOperation, draws the ExecutionId (exec1_) and the operationRef (op-) from 16 host-CSPRNG bytes each on the write receipt's attempt ledger (law X9 r1's x3d.session.execution-draw draw point), and binds N, (S, G, K), the carrier digest SHA-256(N) and the receipt's selected core closure, with a read-only execution_id. core_evaluator_closure is the receipt's core evaluator closure (law X3d r8 item 13, unit X3d-3; contract successor EC1), derived from the same authenticated core inventory as the selected core closure and never read from a Run, which a ReplayedRun's evaluator closure must equal; core_evaluator_closure_preimages, doc(hidden) and compiled only under the joint predicate (test, crash-matrix or scenario-fixtures) on X8 r3 item 4b's site list, returns that closure's descriptor, the TR-CORE-signed inventory body and each tree member's bytes for the synthetic run candidate, and no production path calls it. Two read-only hooks serve storage's commit (unit X3d-2): project_id, the ProjectId of N's ACTIVE row that a Run must name, and store_root, the selected store's directory I/stores/S for item 3 steps 4 to 6, opened no-follow from the retained I as one charged step on the attempt ledger, holding the marker the endpoint admitted and named by its absolute spelling, returned as the exported StoreRoot (the retained handle, that spelling and the invoking user; not authority), any failure latching the ledger. reserve_end_path is item 3 step 0: the attempt ledger's one settlement reserve (X3d-0), taken through the attempt, write receipt and operation (one production caller each, source-pinned), sized exactly at one REV and one CLN append at their bounded maximum bodies by X3b-2's own cost functions (writer open, witness read and append cost at the largest generation and the last ordinary sequence, with no literal), held only in a private end-path value. capacity is item 3 step 3 over seal_fits on the tail the start proved. begin_journal_txn takes the journal's level 3 (refusing a session without the reserve) and JournalWriteTxn keeps it detached across storage's evidence-ledger BEGIN IMMEDIATE, with a consuming abort. seal_under_append_lock runs item 4 step 3 with the security-owned two-phase adapter traits (CommitAdapter staging, StagedCommit COMMIT, EvidenceCommit) inside one attempt-ledger charge: level 4, X4's checkpoint, the SEAL built and bound to the caller's ReplayedRun through PreparedJournalSeal, its witnessed append, the repeated checkpoint, staging, the final checkpoint ending in FinalGate::admit, the evidence COMMIT only through the one AdmissionPermit, level 4's release; a certain stop rolls the evidence transaction back, then releases level 4 and any journal level 3, appends nothing and latches the gate; JournalSealBinding is read-only evidence of the SEAL. Every certain refusal before admission latches the operation's one gate (0 to 2). An uncertain journal commit or barrier, an undetermined attempt-admission COMMIT and an undetermined evidence COMMIT forfeit the reserve where classified. StoppedSession::finish owes a REV for a durable SEAL without its evidence commit, a latched gate or an observed revocation (reason from a closed set: trust-revoked, policy, observer-fail-stop, stale-guard, operation-stopped; no trustEpochObserved) and a CLN for F38's fixed residual pair, appends them in one settle of the reserve as fresh level-3-then-level-4 appends with no checkpoint, each refused on the invariant row before any effect if its draft exceeds its kind's body bound, stopping at the first failure (no CLN after a failed REV), then releases the lease and runs X3b's end step unless an outcome was uncertain or the attempt ledger is closed. SessionEnd's end_step_failure gives an entered end step's own failure on its row, and its rollover gives the capacity rollover's outcome when the end step ran it, as the public value RolloverDisclosure (Rolled with the opened generation, AlreadyRolled, Skipped, Refused on its row, Undetermined), a disclosure that grants nothing and never rewrites the session's outcome (law X7 r6 item 6, unit X7b). SessionRefusal projects every composition row through InstallationTermination. It places law X9 r1's x3d.session, x3d.publish (after-staging, before-final-checkpoint, commit-returned) and x3d.finish (settle.before, settle.after) points, and runs the SEAL's journal transaction (level 3 through level 4's release) and each end-path REV and CLN append inside law X9 r1 item 5's x3b.append.seal, x3b.append.rev and x3b.append.cln scopes. Library only: no command commits.", "before": "The security side of the CommitSession storage facade (law X3d r6 items 1 to 4, 7, 8 and 9; unit X3d-1, with X4c's end path merged), custody.rs's macOS module beside X2e's operation_handoff and X4a's operation_guard. CommitSession::open consumes one ProjectOperation, draws the ExecutionId (exec1_) and the operationRef (op-) from 16 host-CSPRNG bytes each on the write receipt's attempt ledger (law X9 r1's x3d.session.execution-draw draw point), and binds N, (S, G, K), the carrier digest SHA-256(N) and the receipt's selected core closure, with a read-only execution_id. reserve_end_path is item 3 step 0: the attempt ledger's one settlement reserve (X3d-0), taken through the attempt, write receipt and operation (one production caller each, source-pinned), sized exactly at one REV and one CLN append at their bounded maximum bodies by X3b-2's own cost functions (writer open, witness read and append cost at the largest generation and the last ordinary sequence, with no literal), held only in a private end-path value. capacity is item 3 step 3 over seal_fits on the tail the start proved. begin_journal_txn takes the journal's level 3 (refusing a session without the reserve) and JournalWriteTxn keeps it detached across storage's evidence-ledger BEGIN IMMEDIATE, with a consuming abort. seal_under_append_lock runs item 4 step 3 with the security-owned two-phase adapter traits (CommitAdapter staging, StagedCommit COMMIT, EvidenceCommit) inside one attempt-ledger charge: level 4, X4's checkpoint, the SEAL built and bound to the caller's ReplayedRun through PreparedJournalSeal, its witnessed append, the repeated checkpoint, staging, the final checkpoint ending in FinalGate::admit, the evidence COMMIT only through the one AdmissionPermit, level 4's release; a certain stop rolls the evidence transaction back, then releases level 4 and any journal level 3, appends nothing and latches the gate; JournalSealBinding is read-only evidence of the SEAL. Every certain refusal before admission latches the operation's one gate (0 to 2). An uncertain journal commit or barrier, an undetermined attempt-admission COMMIT and an undetermined evidence COMMIT forfeit the reserve where classified. StoppedSession::finish owes a REV for a durable SEAL without its evidence commit, a latched gate or an observed revocation (reason from a closed set: trust-revoked, policy, observer-fail-stop, stale-guard, operation-stopped; no trustEpochObserved) and a CLN for F38's fixed residual pair, appends them in one settle of the reserve as fresh level-3-then-level-4 appends with no checkpoint, each refused on the invariant row before any effect if its draft exceeds its kind's body bound, stopping at the first failure (no CLN after a failed REV), then releases the lease and runs X3b's end step unless an outcome was uncertain or the attempt ledger is closed. SessionRefusal projects every composition row through InstallationTermination. It places law X9 r1's x3d.session, x3d.publish (after-staging, before-final-checkpoint, commit-returned) and x3d.finish (settle.before, settle.after) points. Library only: no command commits.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/575/description"}}, {"after": "Check X3d-1 through X2e's real handoff on scratch homes (a creator-published P0 with X4T-0's signed accepted store, ordinary writers over 462's signed test trees, a scripted monitor clock, observer ticks on request, a test adapter standing in for storage's staging and COMMIT; no real home or installation), included under operation_handoff_tests.rs's cfg(test) module: open's ids, grammar and binding; a lawful commit appending one SEAL, no REV or CLN, and the end step copying the floor; after each certain refusal that closes the attempt ledger (a publication-reserve overrun, busy at the evidence ledger's BEGIN IMMEDIATE with the consuming abort, a staging I/O error, a failed final checkpoint on a stall), the REV (and the CLN after a durable SEAL) appended from the settlement with its closed reason and the end step taking no fence; a busy carrier failing the REV, with no CLN and the busy row disclosed; a refused step 0 holding no reserve and appending nothing; an unfunded journal transaction and a second reserve on the invariant row; nothing appended and no end step after an uncertain SEAL commit, an undetermined evidence COMMIT and an undetermined attempt admission; an exhausted generation returned with the ledger open and rolled over; the reserve equal to one bounded REV and one bounded CLN, the closed reasons, and a draft one byte over its bound refused; a real REV and CLN at the last two ordinary slots charging exactly the cost function; the settlement chain's single production caller per link; the x3d crash points' names; X3d-2's hooks (the session's project_id the ACTIVE row's ProjectId, its store root the endpoint's I/stores/S by identity and spelling, and a replaced store directory refused as required-files-changed with the attempt ledger latched); and X7b's disclosure through SessionEnd's accessors (law X7 r6 item 6): through finish on a real exhausted carrier, the rollover Rolled to generation 2, Skipped under a held readers.lease and Refused busy at a busy level 3, each with no end-step failure, and an end step refused on a moved namespace before the rollover disclosing its failure and no rollover; and, at X3b-4's test points, an undetermined rollover, one short of its reservation on the budget row, an I/O-refused rollover beside its copy's host I/O failure, and a failed copy after Rolled as an end-step failure.", "before": "Check X3d-1 through X2e's real handoff on scratch homes (a creator-published P0 with X4T-0's signed accepted store, ordinary writers over 462's signed test trees, a scripted monitor clock, observer ticks on request, a test adapter standing in for storage's staging and COMMIT; no real home or installation), included under operation_handoff_tests.rs's cfg(test) module: open's ids, grammar and binding; a lawful commit appending one SEAL, no REV or CLN, and the end step copying the floor; after each certain refusal that closes the attempt ledger (a publication-reserve overrun, busy at the evidence ledger's BEGIN IMMEDIATE with the consuming abort, a staging I/O error, a failed final checkpoint on a stall), the REV (and the CLN after a durable SEAL) appended from the settlement with its closed reason and the end step taking no fence; a busy carrier failing the REV, with no CLN and the busy row disclosed; a refused step 0 holding no reserve and appending nothing; an unfunded journal transaction and a second reserve on the invariant row; nothing appended and no end step after an uncertain SEAL commit, an undetermined evidence COMMIT and an undetermined attempt admission; an exhausted generation returned with the ledger open and rolled over; the reserve equal to one bounded REV and one bounded CLN, the closed reasons, and a draft one byte over its bound refused; a real REV and CLN at the last two ordinary slots charging exactly the cost function; the settlement chain's single production caller per link; and the x3d crash points' names.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/576/description"}}, {"after": "B2-b4a T2 census record, regenerated through B2-b4b3 (the caps M3-B r7 item 12 records): per lead-readable entry and composed workspace, the integrity-verified pins, counts, and modeled objects, edges and bytes (affine in the root spelling length, reported at three root lengths) with per-mille margins against the discovery profile limits; the failing entries and the caps state (recorded-margin-holds, or returned-to-law when any entry fails); workspace rows walker-only for the full ledger; held-out rows outstanding, stale, or composed from a current B2-b4c blind receipt, with the held-out state. Design evidence, not qualification.", "before": "B2-b4a T2 census record: per lead-readable entry and composed workspace, the integrity-verified pins, counts and modeled objects and edges against the discovery profile limits; the failing entries and the caps returned to the law; held-out entries outstanding. Design evidence, not qualification.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/578/description"}}, {"after": "Check the T2 census on scratch trees: the walker uses no more objects and edges than the census cost model predicts on every walk vector and composed-workspace vector; the census pins the cost closure and size inventory as compiled; each censused entry is classified against the discovery profile limits in objects, edges and bytes at the largest root length by charging a fresh ledger, and no lead-readable entry fails; composed held-out rows are classified by their blind margins, with coverage and the held-out state checked, and only a holding state meets the gate.", "before": "Check the T2 census on scratch trees: the walker uses no more objects and edges than the census cost model predicts on every walk vector and composed-workspace vector; the census pins the cost closure as compiled; each censused entry is classified against the discovery profile limits by charging a fresh ledger, and no margin is claimed for a failing entry.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/580/description"}}, {"after": "First registration of a project root (law X2 r8 item 6, unit X2c) on the law 468 durable write gate, under its held fence and on its one ledger, with the write-gate entries for project admission and item 6a tracking. Preconditions before any effect: a FirstUseCandidate with the marker positively absent, the admission's own recheck, the tracking observation rechecked, no entry beside P0's lineage under transitions (the active-transition-recovery gate), a present host or projects judged private, up to eight independent ProjectId and UUIDv4 namespace candidates checked against all history and a no-follow occupancy lookup, and capacity (rows, the RESERVED and ACTIVE documents and the worst later terminal spelling within 4 MiB, and the all-registered wrapper, within 4096 rows; PROJECT.SCOPE_LIMIT with S12 subjects). The registry replacement primitive, used for RESERVED and ACTIVE: reconfirm the current owner R by its retained descriptor and full sample and by name, with project-registry.v1 absent; build the complete canonical document and validate it with the same decoder; write a fresh exclusive private temporary name in I with F_FULLFSYNC, confirmed by identity and exact bytes; rename it over the registry; I's barrier and a name recheck by identity. R advances R0 to R1 to R2 only through these confirmed publications, and the gate's retained registry sample with it. The namespace I/host/projects/N is published whole from a private stage holding exactly two empty private lease files, by an exclusive rename with its parent's barrier; host and projects are created or admitted as 465 item 3 does; .opensip is created or admitted under S3 custody with item 1's premise; the 92-byte marker is created no-replace with its file and directory barriers. Every current owner is rechecked before ACTIVE, ACTIVE is built from R1's document, and R2 must classify the root Eligible. Each effect reserves its confirmation work first; any refusal spends the gate, with no retry and no deletion, and leftovers are the registry owner's explicit-recovery cases. Each refusal names its item 8 row; the public projection is the composition owner's. A cfg(test) seam stops at each step and scripts candidate draws. Later units build on it: namespace_lease reuses its owner checks, costs and rows, and its refusals add the Lease step, the busy row for a project lease held elsewhere and the write-gate refusal's namespace arm (law X2 r8 item 7, unit X2d); the registered project's recheck takes a namespace footprint, Exact (item 6's two empty leases only) or Started (after X3b's carrier start: N private and both leases present, private and empty, with no closed entry scan), beside the Handoff step and I/trust as an owner (law X2 r8 item 7a, unit X2e); and recheck_registered_owners rechecks only a registered project's original owners after the fence is released: the chain, the root's incarnation, .opensip by name and its custody, and the marker by descriptor and by name (law X4 r7 item 4, unit X4a). Library only: no lease (X2d) and no handoff (X2e).", "before": "First registration of a project root (law X2 r8 item 6, unit X2c) on the law 468 durable write gate, under its held fence and on its one ledger, with the write-gate entries for project admission and item 6a tracking. Preconditions before any effect: a FirstUseCandidate with the marker positively absent, the admission's own recheck, the tracking observation rechecked, no entry beside P0's lineage under transitions (the active-transition-recovery gate), a present host or projects judged private, up to eight independent ProjectId and UUIDv4 namespace candidates checked against all history and a no-follow occupancy lookup, and capacity (rows, the RESERVED and ACTIVE documents and the worst later terminal spelling within 4 MiB, and the all-registered wrapper, within 4096 rows; PROJECT.SCOPE_LIMIT with S12 subjects). The registry replacement primitive, used for RESERVED and ACTIVE: reconfirm the current owner R by its retained descriptor and full sample and by name, with project-registry.v1 absent; build the complete canonical document and validate it with the same decoder; write a fresh exclusive private temporary name in I with F_FULLFSYNC, confirmed by identity and exact bytes; rename it over the registry; I's barrier and a name recheck by identity. R advances R0 to R1 to R2 only through these confirmed publications, and the gate's retained registry sample with it. The namespace I/host/projects/N is published whole from a private stage holding exactly two empty private lease files, by an exclusive rename with its parent's barrier; host and projects are created or admitted as 465 item 3 does; .opensip is created or admitted under S3 custody with item 1's premise; the 92-byte marker is created no-replace with its file and directory barriers. Every current owner is rechecked before ACTIVE, ACTIVE is built from R1's document, and R2 must classify the root Eligible. Each effect reserves its confirmation work first; any refusal spends the gate, with no retry and no deletion, and leftovers are the registry owner's explicit-recovery cases. Each refusal names its item 8 row; the public projection is the composition owner's. A cfg(test) seam stops at each step and scripts candidate draws. Library only: no lease (X2d) and no handoff (X2e).", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/594/description"}}, {"after": "Check X2c on scratch homes with a creator-published P0, an ordinary writer over 462's signed test trees, and scratch projects (no real home, installation or Git): canonical UUIDv4 candidates, exact document lengths and later spellings and R0's exact re-encoding, and the S12 scope-limit and identity rows; a whole registration (one ACTIVE random row binding the root's path and incarnation, private host and projects, a namespace with exactly two empty private leases, a 0700 .opensip and the exact 0600 marker, no temporary left, the gate's and the project's rechecks passing, a read session then classifying Eligible, and a second registration refused); a reused .opensip and existing parents admitted and never changed; Eligible, RESERVED and one-sided roots refused with nothing written; a VCS change after the observation, a transition entry and a non-private host refused before any effect; eight colliding ProjectId draws, eight occupied namespace paths and failed entropy as host I/O; 4097 rows, a RESERVED document over 4 MiB and a worst later terminal spelling over 4 MiB as PROJECT.SCOPE_LIMIT before any effect; a stop at each of the eleven steps leaving its durable prefix, spending the writer and classifying as the registry owner's recovery cases; a registry rewritten or a marker replaced before ACTIVE refused without ACTIVE; a later registry change seen by the registered project's recheck; and the two namespace footprints (unit X2e): an N populated by X3b's carrier start refused as changed under the Exact footprint and admitted under Started, and a lease that is no longer empty, or missing, refused under Started.", "before": "Check X2c on scratch homes with a creator-published P0, an ordinary writer over 462's signed test trees, and scratch projects (no real home, installation or Git): canonical UUIDv4 candidates, exact document lengths and later spellings and R0's exact re-encoding, and the S12 scope-limit and identity rows; a whole registration (one ACTIVE random row binding the root's path and incarnation, private host and projects, a namespace with exactly two empty private leases, a 0700 .opensip and the exact 0600 marker, no temporary left, the gate's and the project's rechecks passing, a read session then classifying Eligible, and a second registration refused); a reused .opensip and existing parents admitted and never changed; Eligible, RESERVED and one-sided roots refused with nothing written; a VCS change after the observation, a transition entry and a non-private host refused before any effect; eight colliding ProjectId draws, eight occupied namespace paths and failed entropy as host I/O; 4097 rows, a RESERVED document over 4 MiB and a worst later terminal spelling over 4 MiB as PROJECT.SCOPE_LIMIT before any effect; a stop at each of the eleven steps leaving its durable prefix, spending the writer and classifying as the registry owner's recovery cases; a registry rewritten or a marker replaced before ACTIVE refused without ACTIVE; and a later registry change seen by the registered project's recheck.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/595/description"}}, {"after": "The owner \u00a75 durable write gate a creator invocation passes after its creator act ends (law 468 r5 items 1 to 5). One failure-latching ledger with the owner's limits, one admission per process. Step 0 is a charged, retained, no-follow walk from the root through H, Library, Application Support, OpenSIP and I with the 460 and 465 custody predicates; InitialPlatform's omission premise applies only to the root-to-H prefix, Library and Application Support, through its own fstatfs, and OpenSIP and I must be private. Step 1 opens lifecycle.fence through the retained I handle and takes one nonblocking exclusive lock; busy latches. Under the held fence it rechecks the account, the retained chain, the I-parent name and I's identity, custody of I and its parent, H's filesystem, the locked fence, and the required files of a complete I (fence, registry, pair, endpoint marker, node chain, trust current record) by owner, mode, link and ACL; then I's barrier and I's parent's barrier, receipts checked against their handles, and the recheck set again, reserved with the barriers and run even after a failed barrier. The first recheck is the session's one read of each required file (law X3a item 2, unit X3a-1): it keeps each file's full metadata sample and the decoded pair, marker, node chain (at most 64 nodes; past that, the budget row) and trust current record, whose C.store must name the pair's (S, G, K); the second recheck, and every later gate recheck, compare those samples and read nothing again, so an in-place rewrite is a change. It borrows only the sealed InitialPlatform qualification and reuses no creator handle, lock or receipt. Typed refusals follow law 468 item 6, with law X3a item 5's STATE.SCHEMA_UNSUPPORTED; the public mapping is separate. After admission the gate's own ledger charges each further step for the installation it admitted (charged, scope, recheck) and stays spent after any refusal (spend). DurableInstallation lends the retained I, H's spelling as walked, the account's UID and the endpoint values; WriterFence, its held fence as the trust readers' HeldFence (law X4 r7 item 2, unit X4a); current_trust, the trust current owner's inputs from the one state.v1 read, and observe_private_file, that owner's no-follow metadata recheck of one required file (law X4T r9 item 7). The retained registry and state.v1 samples advance only from the predecessor a confirmed publication reconfirmed (advance_registry, unit X2c; advance_current, unit X4T-b), and into_handoff moves the installation's owners into the operation handoff with the fence lock still held (unit X2e). Library only.", "before": "The owner \u00a75 durable write gate a creator invocation passes after its creator act ends (law 468 r5 items 1 to 5). One failure-latching ledger with the owner's limits, one admission per process. Step 0 is a charged, retained, no-follow walk from the root through H, Library, Application Support, OpenSIP and I with the 460 and 465 custody predicates; InitialPlatform's omission premise applies only to the root-to-H prefix, Library and Application Support, through its own fstatfs, and OpenSIP and I must be private. Step 1 opens lifecycle.fence through the retained I handle and takes one nonblocking exclusive lock; busy latches. Under the held fence it rechecks the account, the retained chain, the I-parent name and I's identity, custody of I and its parent, H's filesystem, the locked fence, and the required files of a complete I (fence, registry, pair, endpoint marker, node chain, trust current record) by owner, mode, link and ACL; then I's barrier and I's parent's barrier, receipts checked against their handles, and the same recheck reserved with the barriers and run even after a failed barrier. It borrows only the sealed InitialPlatform qualification and reuses no creator handle, lock or receipt. Typed refusals follow law 468 item 6; the public mapping is separate. Library only.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/600/description"}}, {"after": "Check the durable write gate (law 468 r5) over scratch account homes after a real scratch publication, with InitialCore and InitialPlatform built from signed on-disk test trees: the step order under a held fence, the required files, the busy fence, first and second recheck refusals, a replaced required file, scripted barrier failures with the recheck still run, short ledgers, the premise present or absent at an omitted-ACL prefix, OpenSIP or I with an omitted ACL refusing even with a premise, a deep home well inside the owner's budget, and one gate per process whose only input is the platform qualification. It also checks law X3a's one read (unit X3a-1): the endpoint values retained from step 2 with the ledger kept for one more full recheck, an in-place rewrite between the rechecks as required-files-changed, an undecodable trust current record as incomplete, a node chain past the 64-node limit or unended after 64 nodes on the budget row, and a 64-node chain on a deep home read once within the owner's caps; and that a confirmed trust publication is the only advance of the retained state.v1 sample, only from its reconfirmed predecessor and leaving the registry sample untouched, an unadvanced publication failing the recheck (unit X4T-b), with two confirmed publications of one hold followed in order and the second before the first refused as changed (unit X4B-b). Nothing runs on the real account's I. Compiled only for tests; confers no qualification.", "before": "Check the durable write gate (law 468 r5) over scratch account homes after a real scratch publication, with InitialCore and InitialPlatform built from signed on-disk test trees: the step order under a held fence, the required files, the busy fence, first and second recheck refusals, a replaced required file, scripted barrier failures with the recheck still run, short ledgers, the premise present or absent at an omitted-ACL prefix, OpenSIP or I with an omitted ACL refusing even with a premise, a deep home well inside the owner's budget, and one gate per process whose only input is the platform qualification. Nothing runs on the real account's I. Compiled only for tests; confers no qualification.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/601/description"}}, {"after": "Doctor's installation check (law 458c r6 items 7 and 12). It observes I through the same read receipt and observation session as every read command, and differs only at item 7's end: a reachable I that is incomplete or contradictory is not a refusal for doctor, and each structural finding is returned in member order, never collapsed. Every refusal before or at a recheck, and every ordinary read failure, ends on its law 468 item 6 row exactly as for any read command. The fence is released before the result is returned. Its findings include law X3a item 5's core finding (the pair names a core other than the selected core) and current-store finding (the trust current record is over its cap, does not decode, or names another store). observe_installation_for_doctor is the one producer call of the host's doctor ingress (law X10).", "before": "Doctor's installation check (law 458c r6 items 7 and 12). It observes I through the same read receipt and observation session as every read command, and differs only at item 7's end: a reachable I that is incomplete or contradictory is not a refusal for doctor, and each structural finding is returned in member order, never collapsed. Every refusal before or at a recheck, and every ordinary read failure, ends on its law 468 item 6 row exactly as for any read command. The fence is released before the result is returned. Library only.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/602/description"}}, {"after": "Check doctor's installation check on scratch account homes with a creator-published P0: a complete installation with the fence released; every missing member its own finding in member order; an undecodable pair, marker or node, and a missing node, as their findings; and a missing home, a fence busy through the wait, and a member refused custody each ending on its row with no report. It also checks the security layer's part of law X10 item 5: a node from another store's lineage as the chain finding, and an account refusal, no premise, an exhausted session ledger and a positively absent I ending on the account, omitted-ACL custody, budget and not-initialized rows; and an undecodable trust current record, or one still naming G 0 after the pair is reselected at G 1, as the current-store finding (law X3a item 5, unit X3a-1).", "before": "Check doctor's installation check on scratch account homes with a creator-published P0: a complete installation with the fence released; every missing member its own finding in member order; an undecodable pair, marker or node, and a missing node, as their findings; and a missing home, a fence busy through the wait, and a member refused custody each ending on its row with no report.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/603/description"}}, {"after": "Define the held-fence view that installation captures borrow (law 458c item 8): the held I, the actor, a charged recheck of the held fence and a sample of the fence carrier's filesystem, and the carrier name lifecycle.fence. The read session implements it for every production reader. The supplied and native installation fences (one nonblocking exclusive attempt with native root provenance and pre/post custody checks, busy distinguished from missing or invalid state) remain for supplied-root tests only; no production installation reader calls them. No descriptor escape, creation or authority.", "before": "Acquire and retain the existing installation fence with native root provenance and pre/post custody checks; distinguish busy from missing or invalid state.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/604/description"}}, {"after": "Check the initial creator's stage, validation and publication (law 467) over scratch account homes, with InitialCore and InitialPlatform built from signed on-disk test trees: the exact private P0 tree and its 44 barriers, the fence held through the act and free after, reads through the read session and the native trust read session, the lost race, a moved stage, a scripted indeterminate rename, scripted barrier failures, ledgers short of a reservation, validation refusals, an owner change before the rename, two concurrent creators and NotPristine. Nothing runs on the real account's chain. Compiled only for tests; confers no qualification.", "before": "Check the initial creator's stage, validation and publication (law 467) over scratch account homes, with InitialCore and InitialPlatform built from signed on-disk test trees: the exact private P0 tree and its 44 barriers, the fence held through the act and free after, reads through the existing installation fence and native trust read session, the lost race, a moved stage, a scripted indeterminate rename, scripted barrier failures, ledgers short of a reservation, validation refusals, an owner change before the rename, two concurrent creators and NotPristine. Nothing runs on the real account's chain. Compiled only for tests; confers no qualification.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/608/description"}}, {"after": "The read session for installation readers (law 458c r5 item 8). It keeps a complete I that the observation session admitted: the held fence, the retained chain, the account and the read receipt, with the observation session's one ledger. Every later capture and recheck is charged to that ledger before it runs (item 6). A capture opens prefixes and a leaf only through the retained I and its retained descendants: each directory judged private by the owner's predicate on a charged capture and named exactly, the file judged a private regular file, bounded, named, on H's filesystem, read, judged again and reopened by name. Its failures are values, so the full recheck (item 5 step 4) runs after every capture, over the fence, the observation's required files and every file captured since; any refusal latches the session. Between full rechecks, the held-fence check samples the carrier's name and identity, and a capture's own recheck samples its edges and file. It lends the trust readers a held-fence view and charges their retained budget to the same ledger. Captures never walk from the root again; admit_project_root alone walks from / to the launch or explicit path and the selected project root (law X2 r5, unit X2b-1), charged on the session ledger. Each of the session's admissions runs on its ledger under the held fence, then one full recheck, and any refusal latches it: store_endpoint admits the selected store endpoint from the observation's one read against the read receipt's selected core, reading no file (law X3a items 1 and 2, unit X3a-1); admit_project_root selects and admits the root with its registry classification and rechecks it; observe_project_tracking observes the root's Git tracking, reading the process environment only to refuse and the fixed system sources as refusal-only evidence (law X2 r7 item 6a, unit X2b-2); and admit_namespace makes the session the namespace fence holder for an Eligible root, for a SHARED-READ lease only (law X2 item 7, unit X2d). Never a write capability.", "before": "The read session for installation readers (law 458c r5 item 8). It keeps a complete I that the observation session admitted: the held fence, the retained chain, the account and the read receipt, with the observation session's one ledger. Every later capture and recheck is charged to that ledger before it runs (item 6). A capture opens prefixes and a leaf only through the retained I and its retained descendants: each directory judged private by the owner's predicate on a charged capture and named exactly, the file judged a private regular file, bounded, named, on H's filesystem, read, judged again and reopened by name. Its failures are values, so the full recheck (item 5 step 4) runs after every capture, over the fence, the observation's required files and every file captured since; any refusal latches the session. Between full rechecks, the held-fence check samples the carrier's name and identity, and a capture's own recheck samples its edges and file. It lends the trust readers a held-fence view and charges their retained budget to the same ledger. Nothing walks from the root again. Never a write capability.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/609/description"}}, {"after": "Test support for installation readers on scratch account homes: publish a real P0 with the creator composition under a scratch H, then hold read fences on it through the observation session with the read receipt over signed on-disk test trees, and write further files below I as private creations. It also extends the published store's node chain and reselects the pair at a given generation, or rewrites the root node to name an unread predecessor (law X3a item 3's chains); replaces the P0 trust store with the signed accepted store for the same S and K, in place or as another writer would by atomic replacement with state.v1 renamed last, giving every new trust file the zero-rights owner allow (unit X4a); and restarts a creation or session refused only for churn in a shared ancestor above the scratch parent, at most eight attempts (F4). churned runs a whole test again on fresh fixtures only when it panicked in an attempt whose ancestor walks refused one or more captures as changed, every one above the private scratch parent by installation_root's record, at most eight attempts with every assertion strict (F5). It is the shared installation fixture of the HomeSource::Fixture gate, compiled under the joint predicate any(test, crash-matrix, scenario-fixtures) (law X8 r3 item 4b, unit X9-1), with its read-session helpers, trust rewrites and churned still cfg(test) inside it. ReadFixture::new_in puts the scratch root under the caller's scratch parent (law X8 r3 item 4c, unit X8b). InstallationAt names a published synthetic installation by its paths, for a matrix child or helper process that did not create it, and owns and removes nothing (law X9 r1 item 6, unit X9-1); ReadFixture::at gives a fixture's own. Its producers: writer, an ordinary writer on I by X1's composition (the write receipt over a signed test tree on its own test attempt, then the write gate over the scratch H by the production walk), and admitted_writer, the same keeping the refusal's stage and 468 item 6 row (unit X8b), which the crash-matrix settlement-sweep entry also admits (unit X9-2); current_trust_values, state.v1's K and revocation version read by name; install_accepted_trust, X4T-0's signed accepted store for S and K written in place with no fence, each fresh directory and file given the zero-rights owner allow; and the shared fenced state.v1 publisher publish_accepted_trust_fenced, under which a write gate takes I's fence by the production walk, the store is published by the trust owner's protocol, the gate's retained owner advances to the confirmed state.v1 and the fence is released, with publish_trust_fenced keeping the refusal by stage as FencedRefusal and its 468 item 6 row (unit X8b); both support surfaces call the last three. InstallationAt::operation is the one production-chain composition to a ProjectOperation that both support surfaces' operation entries call (law X8 r3 item 4c, unit X8b): X1's ordinary writer, X2b's root admission and tracking, X2c's first registration for a first-use candidate, then begin_operation (X2d's namespace and lease, X3a's endpoint, X4's lease-free first read and monitor, X3b's floor step and carrier start, X2e's handoff), with its RequestId and ExecutionId drawn from the host CSPRNG, each refusal its own row through X3d r6 item 9's maps, and the writer, with the fence, released on every refusal. It never opens the real account's I.", "before": "Test support for installation readers on scratch account homes: publish a real P0 with the creator composition under a scratch H, then hold read fences on it through the observation session with the read receipt over signed on-disk test trees, and write further files below I as private creations. It never opens the real account's I.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/610/description"}}, {"after": "Resolve the fixed installation root from the actual account database and retain its existing or missing-path evidence; do not derive authority from environment variables. It also holds the creator's read half of owner \u00a71a step 5 (law 460): observe_installation_parent, on the attempt's one ledger with that attempt's actor and premise, retains and judges root to H and then each existing fixed ancestor (Library, Application Support, OpenSIP) with charged opens, ACL captures and name checks, and reports a missing ancestor, preview-v1 positively absent through the retained OpenSIP, or preview-v1 present; every directory through Application Support must pass the external ancestor check, an omitted ACL only through InitialPlatform's Evidence B premise, OpenSIP must be private, names and the actor are rechecked at the end, any refusal latches the attempt, and nothing is created. That walk (observe_parent, which law 465's preparation runs with OpenSIP retained by name only), the external ancestor judgment of one capture (judge_ancestor, judge_captured_ancestor) and the ParentRefusal rows are shared by the parent preparation, the durable write gate's chain walk and recheck, and the project chain walk. A cfg(test) per-thread record of each capture judge_ancestor refused as changed is what F5's churned reads.", "before": "Resolve the fixed installation root from the actual account database and retain its existing or missing-path evidence; do not derive authority from environment variables.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/612/description"}}, {"after": "The read side's observation path (law 458c r5 items 5, 6, 7 and 11). One session per process on one failure-latching ledger with the owner's limits; one observation per session. It reaches I through the durable write gate's own step 0 and step 1 code, shared rather than copied, with no barrier: a charged, retained, no-follow walk from the root to I under the read receipt's sealed lending, a positive absence only when the first missing suffix component is observed absent under its retained parent, and lifecycle.fence opened through the retained I. The lock wait repeats one nonblocking attempt on that same descriptor, paced at 25 ms, for at most 5 s and 201 attempts, all reserved before the first; busy retries, any other failure stops on the host I/O row, and a lock still busy at the bound is the busy row. Under the held fence it runs the receipt's rechecks and the full recheck set, reads the required members charged before they run (the fixed members up front, each lineage node before its own read), records missing, undecodable or misbound members as structural findings, and runs the same full recheck over every captured file, also after a failed read. The receipt's rechecks run again after that final recheck. Within the same one read (law X3a items 1 to 5, unit X3a-1): the selection pair must name the read receipt's selected core, or it is the core finding; the trust current record trust/stores/S/state.v1 is read once under CURRENT_STATE_CAP and decoded, and a record over its cap, undecodable or naming a store other than the pair's is the current-store finding; a lineage chain longer than its generation allows is a finding, while the session's own node limit is the budget row; and a complete I keeps the endpoint values (the pair, the marker, the node chain and the current record) for the selected store endpoint. retain (unit 458c-b2) requires I complete and keeps the observation, the receipt and the session's ledger in the ReadSession. Every refusal takes exactly the write gate's row. Never a write capability. Its consumers are the read session behind InstallationReadFence::acquire and doctor's installation check. Read-only recovery's admission takes only its member reads (law X6 r3 item 3 step 3, unit X6b): read_recovery_members reads them through the retained I with no fence, the fence member neither opened nor judged and every other member read as here, each charged before it runs, and returns every captured file with its full sample and the endpoint values, a structural finding on the incomplete row and an ordinary failure on its own row. Its one wired CLI path is opensip doctor (law X10), through doctor's installation check.", "before": "The read side's observation path (law 458c r5 items 5, 6, 7 and 11). One session per process on one failure-latching ledger with the owner's limits; one observation per session. It reaches I through the durable write gate's own step 0 and step 1 code, shared rather than copied, with no barrier: a charged, retained, no-follow walk from the root to I under the read receipt's sealed lending, a positive absence only when the first missing suffix component is observed absent under its retained parent, and lifecycle.fence opened through the retained I. The lock wait repeats one nonblocking attempt on that same descriptor, paced at 25 ms, for at most 5 s and 201 attempts, all reserved before the first; busy retries, any other failure stops on the host I/O row, and a lock still busy at the bound is the busy row. Under the held fence it runs the receipt's rechecks and the full recheck set, reads the required members charged before they run (the fixed members up front, each lineage node before its own read), records missing, undecodable or misbound members as structural findings, and runs the same full recheck over every captured file, also after a failed read. Every refusal takes exactly the write gate's row. Never a write capability. Library only: no consumer uses it yet.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/615/description"}}, {"after": "Check the observation path on scratch account homes, with P0 published by the creator composition and the read receipt built from signed on-disk test trees, and an injected wait clock: a complete I observed under the fence with no barrier; positive absence at each suffix component, and a symlink, a file or a custody refusal at a suffix name not taken for absence; a missing H on the write gate's row; no premise refusing at the root; a busy fence retried on the same descriptor to 5 s or 201 attempts, freed during the wait, or ending on a wait error; the wait reserved before its first attempt; a required file's mode, link count or identity changed before the final recheck; every finding of an incomplete I; a deep home well within the caps; a failed member capture after which the final recheck still runs and the capture failure stays the reported cause; and no ordinary fence acquire in the source. It also checks law X3a's one read (unit X3a-1): a complete observation retaining the endpoint values of its one read and every required file's full sample; an undecodable or oversized trust current record as the current-store finding with no endpoint values, every read but doctor's ending on the incomplete row; a node chain past the session limit, or unended after 64 nodes at G 63, on the budget row as an ordinary failure, not a finding; and a 64-node chain on a deep home read once within the owner's caps, its trust current record still naming G 0 as the current-store finding.", "before": "Check the observation path on scratch account homes, with P0 published by the creator composition and the read receipt built from signed on-disk test trees, and an injected wait clock: a complete I observed under the fence with no barrier; positive absence at each suffix component, and a symlink, a file or a custody refusal at a suffix name not taken for absence; a missing H on the write gate's row; no premise refusing at the root; a busy fence retried on the same descriptor to 5 s or 201 attempts, freed during the wait, or ending on a wait error; the wait reserved before its first attempt; a required file's mode, link count or identity changed before the final recheck; every finding of an incomplete I; a deep home well within the caps; and no ordinary fence acquire in the source.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/616/description"}}, {"after": "Namespace admission and leases (law X2 r8 item 7, unit X2d) under a held installation fence, on the fence holder's one ledger: the ordinary writer's law 468 gate (APPEND-WRITE or EXCLUSIVE) or the 458c read session (SHARED-READ only). For an Eligible root with its item 6a tracking observation (N from R0's ACTIVE row) or a project the same writer has just registered (N from R2's ACTIVE row, which must still classify Eligible), never from a caller: the root admission's recheck (chain, incarnation, marker, registry owner, v1 absence) with the tracking recheck, or the registered project's recheck; then I/host, I/host/projects and N confirmed private, exactly named and on their parents' device, and writer.lease and readers.lease opened no-follow and judged private, all retained, with no project lock taken. That NamespaceTarget is the seam where X3b's floor step runs (X3b r8 items 1 and 3, composed by X3b-3): fence held, R current, no project lock. The lease rechecks the subject, the directories and both retained carriers again, then, as one effect reserved before the first lock, takes S7's locks without blocking (SHARED-READ readers.lease LOCK_SH|LOCK_NB; APPEND-WRITE writer.lease LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB; EXCLUSIVE writer.lease then readers.lease, both LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB) on the retained carriers and rechecks each locked carrier's name binding and the directories, then the holder's full recheck. A busy carrier is the busy row; a busy or failed lock or any later refusal releases every lock already taken, in reverse order, while the fence is still held, and spends the gate or latches the session. Absent namespace objects are the incomplete row, custody is 468's custody row, a change is required-files-changed, and FirstUseCandidate is a broken composition. The result, FencedNamespace (the held lease, the ACTIVE row snapshot and the root admission), borrows the fence holder, has no upgrade and never releases the fence: it can be rechecked or dropped, which releases its lease, until item 7a's handoff (X2e). For X2e it lends the lease-free point (NamespaceTarget::lease_free: the holder, the retained N and N's id, where X4a's fenced trust admission runs before X3b's floor step) and moves its owners out (FencedNamespace::into_parts to NamespaceParts, the lease still held, nothing copied). After the fence's release (law X4 r7 items 4 and 8, unit X4a), recheck_namespace_at rechecks host, projects and N by name binding and privacy under the retained I, and HeldLease::recheck_held rechecks each held carrier's name binding and that its lock is still held: a fresh nonblocking EXCLUSIVE attempt on a new descriptor must be refused, and a granted probe is dropped at once and refused as S7's busy row, never a changed file. A cfg(test) seam releases the locks while keeping each carrier's judged sample (the lost-lock row). No fence-free lease (item 7's recovery exception is X6's). For X6 it lends its own carrier, lock and recheck primitives (X2d's open_carrier, required_directory, lock, recheck_binding and effect_lease, X4a's recheck_namespace_at, and their costs) and the two HeldLease values built outside take_lease: shared_reader, readers.lease alone locked shared, which read-only recovery's admission takes without the fence (unit X6b), and exclusive, writer.lease then readers.lease locked exclusive with no subject, which the settlement sweep takes under the held fence (law X6 r3 item 7, unit X6c), its drop releasing readers.lease before writer.lease; try_lock is lock for that sweep, a busy carrier returned as no lock so that nothing latches. Library only.", "before": "Namespace admission and leases (law X2 r8 item 7, unit X2d) under a held installation fence, on the fence holder's one ledger: the ordinary writer's law 468 gate (APPEND-WRITE or EXCLUSIVE) or the 458c read session (SHARED-READ only). For an Eligible root with its item 6a tracking observation (N from R0's ACTIVE row) or a project the same writer has just registered (N from R2's ACTIVE row, which must still classify Eligible), never from a caller: the root admission's recheck (chain, incarnation, marker, registry owner, v1 absence) with the tracking recheck, or the registered project's recheck; then I/host, I/host/projects and N confirmed private, exactly named and on their parents' device, and writer.lease and readers.lease opened no-follow and judged private, all retained, with no project lock taken. That NamespaceTarget is the seam where X3b's floor step runs (X3b r8 items 1 and 3, composed by X3b-3): fence held, R current, no project lock. The lease rechecks the subject, the directories and both retained carriers again, then, as one effect reserved before the first lock, takes S7's locks without blocking (SHARED-READ readers.lease LOCK_SH|LOCK_NB; APPEND-WRITE writer.lease LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB; EXCLUSIVE writer.lease then readers.lease, both LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB) on the retained carriers and rechecks each locked carrier's name binding and the directories, then the holder's full recheck. A busy carrier is the busy row; a busy or failed lock or any later refusal releases every lock already taken, in reverse order, while the fence is still held, and spends the gate or latches the session. Absent namespace objects are the incomplete row, custody is 468's custody row, a change is required-files-changed, and FirstUseCandidate is a broken composition. The result, FencedNamespace (the held lease, the ACTIVE row snapshot and the root admission), borrows the fence holder, has no upgrade and never releases the fence: it can be rechecked or dropped, which releases its lease, until item 7a's handoff (X2e). No fence-free lease (item 7's recovery exception is X6's). Library only.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/618/description"}}, {"after": "The live security guards of one ProjectOperation (law X4 r7 items 2 to 8; unit X4a), custody.rs's macOS module beside X2e's operation_handoff. LeaseFree creates the operation's one FinalGate (commit_authority's OperationGate, never reset) and its FreshnessMonitor first, at X2e's lease-free point under the held fence and before any lease, and runs the monitor's first read with the counter receiving that read's own opening sample (X4B r5 item 1). OperationGuard is built inside the handoff from that gate, monitor and admitted view with the trust store's retained handles, and moves into ProjectOperation, which starts its one observer thread: every 5 s (in tests, only on request) it performs one observation through the shared monitor, whose one history sits behind one mutex shared with every checkpoint so their reads serialize and a waiting read's wait is inside its bound; the observer never takes the fence, appends nothing, and charges only its own per-observation ledger; it stops when the guard is dropped. A revoking observation latches the gate with trust-revoked or policy; an unrelated update or a policy change keeping every required grant (none for any M2 writer) is recorded as drift; any other failure is a fail-stop. Checkpoint, lent by ProjectOperation::journal inside the write receipt's attempt-ledger charge and requiring a JournalAppendHeld borrow of the operation's own append lock (level 4), runs item 3's steps inside the x4.checkpoint scope: item 4's mandatory guard rechecks on the attempt ledger (the write receipt's actor, core and platform; the held lease by name binding and by a fresh nonblocking EXCLUSIVE probe on a new descriptor, a lost lock being S7's busy row; the original project root, .opensip and marker; the namespace; the store marker and lineage nodes by full sample; and the operation joins N, S, G, K and the append lock's N), the final monitored observation and S6's predicate against the immutable start epoch, the latch check, and the admission-boundary check (one clock sample, no I/O, boot unchanged, within 10 s of the final read's earliest instant), with the monitor held from the observation through admission; then FinalGate::admit binds the prepared value and mints the one AdmissionPermit (handing the value back on a refusal), or the effect's own intent append follows. Every failure latches the gate and records the first stop cause (revoked, fail-stop with its reason, or a stale guard with that guard's own row). OperationGuard::stop (law X3d r6 item 3, unit X3d-1) latches the gate for a certain refusal (the fetch-OR 2: 0 to 2 before admission, 1 to 3 after it, never relabelling the admitted outcome), recording no cause. trust_termination maps X4T r9 item 10's fenced admission rows onto new InstallationTermination variants on existing details. It places law X9 r1's points x4.observer.tick (a gate replacing the observer's timed wait when armed), x4.observer.after-observation and x4.checkpoint.before-observation, .after-observation and .before-admit. cfg(test) only: a scripted monotonic clock, a manual cadence and observer ticks on request. Library only.", "before": "The live security guards of one ProjectOperation (law X4 r7 items 2 to 8; unit X4a), custody.rs's macOS module beside X2e's operation_handoff. LeaseFree creates the operation's one FinalGate (commit_authority's OperationGate, never reset) and its FreshnessMonitor first, at X2e's lease-free point under the held fence and before any lease, and runs the monitor's first read with the counter receiving that read's own opening sample (X4B r5 item 1). OperationGuard is built inside the handoff from that gate, monitor and admitted view with the trust store's retained handles, and moves into ProjectOperation, which starts its one observer thread: every 5 s (in tests, only on request) it performs one observation through the shared monitor, whose one history sits behind one mutex shared with every checkpoint so their reads serialize and a waiting read's wait is inside its bound; the observer never takes the fence, appends nothing, and charges only its own per-observation ledger; it stops when the guard is dropped. A revoking observation latches the gate with trust-revoked or policy; an unrelated update or a policy change keeping every required grant (none for any M2 writer) is recorded as drift; any other failure is a fail-stop. Checkpoint, lent by ProjectOperation::journal inside the write receipt's attempt-ledger charge and requiring a JournalAppendHeld borrow of the operation's own append lock (level 4), runs item 3's steps inside the x4.checkpoint scope: item 4's mandatory guard rechecks on the attempt ledger (the write receipt's actor, core and platform; the held lease by name binding and by a fresh nonblocking EXCLUSIVE probe on a new descriptor, a lost lock being S7's busy row; the original project root, .opensip and marker; the namespace; the store marker and lineage nodes by full sample; and the operation joins N, S, G, K and the append lock's N), the final monitored observation and S6's predicate against the immutable start epoch, the latch check, and the admission-boundary check (one clock sample, no I/O, boot unchanged, within 10 s of the final read's earliest instant), with the monitor held from the observation through admission; then FinalGate::admit binds the prepared value and mints the one AdmissionPermit (handing the value back on a refusal), or the effect's own intent append follows. Every failure latches the gate and records the first stop cause (revoked, fail-stop with its reason, or a stale guard with that guard's own row). trust_termination maps X4T r9 item 10's fenced admission rows onto new InstallationTermination variants on existing details. It places law X9 r1's points x4.observer.tick (a gate replacing the observer's timed wait when armed), x4.observer.after-observation and x4.checkpoint.before-observation, .after-observation and .before-admit. cfg(test) only: a scripted monotonic clock, a manual cadence and observer ticks on request. Library only.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/620/description"}}, {"after": "The checked operation handoff (law X2 r8 item 7a, unit X2e) composed with the grant journal's floor step, carrier start and end step (law X3b r10 items 1, 3, 3a and 4, unit X3b-3) and with X4a's live guards (law X4 r7 items 2 and 6), on one ordinary writer's law 468 gate under one held installation fence. In order: the selected store endpoint joined from the gate's one read (law X3a r5); the subject's namespace admitted (X2d, no lock); at the lease-free point, with R current and no project lock held, first X4a's gate and monitor created and X4T's fenced admission run as the monitor's first read on the gate's ledger, with X4B-b's bootstrap acceptance (from a BootstrapSource the write receipt lends) and its one confirming admission inside that read on F absent, the gate following each confirmed publication, and the trust store's handles retained; then I/trust confirmed private and N's absolute spelling admitted only if a no-follow status finds the retained N, then X3b's floor step on the write receipt's attempt ledger (X3b's operation ledger) followed by the gate's and the receipt's rechecks; the APPEND-WRITE or EXCLUSIVE lease (X2d); the join, rechecking the subject, the namespace, each locked carrier's binding and the gate, and deriving owner \u00a78's five-field binding {schemaVersion 1, N, S, G, K} from the ACTIVE row and the admitted endpoint; the transfer of the owners out of the fenced gate, never copied; X3b's carrier start on the attempt ledger (INIT creation when the floor step found INIT, the start against the floor step's observation, the append lock), then every owner rechecked again with a registered subject's namespace held to the started footprint; OperationGuard built under the fence and moved into the operation, starting its observer; and only then the fence's release, ending the gate's ledger. Any refusal spends the gate and releases the append lock, the lease and then the fence. ProjectOperation is not Clone, exported only because the public CommitSession::open takes it (law X3d r6 item 2, unit X3d-1), and cannot be built or read outside security. It holds X4a's OperationGuard, the started carrier and start tail, the held lease, the binding and the ACTIVE row snapshot, the subject's owners, the carrier place, the endpoint values and required files' full samples with the retained chain to I (no fence), and the write receipt, whose attempt ledger charges the operation's journal steps, each lent X4's Checkpoint by journal; charge, reserve_end_path_settlement and settle_end_path serve X3d-1's commit session (a step with no checkpoint; the one settlement reserve, spent with no checkpoint even on a closed ledger). It lends the receipt's core evaluator closure beside selected_core as a value (law X3d r8 item 13, unit X3d-3), with the joint-predicate read of its preimages behind CommitSession's test-support accessor. open_store_root, which CommitSession::store_root calls (law X3d r6 item 3 steps 4 to 6, unit X3d-2), opens I/stores/S as one charged step on the attempt ledger: stores and S each present, private, exactly named and on I's device, the store-instance.v1 marker under it the endpoint's admitted marker by device and inode, and its absolute spelling from H as walked naming the same directory, a change or a gone directory being required-files-changed; it is read-only and takes no fence. Its steps, store_root_in, run on any caller's ledger and are shared with read-only recovery's admission (unit X6b), which also shares namespace_spelling, and with the settlement sweep (unit X6c). Its end path drops the guard (its observer stops) and the append lock and releases the lease; the end step is then not entered when the owner reports an uncertain journal outcome or when the attempt ledger is closed (law X3b r10 item 4): ProjectOperation::end returns NotEntered, with no fence walk, read or copy and nothing disclosed; otherwise end_entered, on the attempt ledger, retakes the fence by the shared charged walk, no-follow fence open and S7's bounded wait, requires the walked I to be the retained I and trust, host, projects and N still bound by name, runs, when the owner reports X3d's CarrierCapacityExhausted with every journal outcome certain, X3b-4's rollover (law X3b r10 item 13) under its own EXCLUSIVE lease, then the end step's probe, read and floor copy, and releases the fence; a failure is a value that never rewrites the operation's outcome, and a rollover outcome is returned for disclosure on its own row. It places x3d.finish's lease-release and end-step points, and the end step's fence wait and release run inside law X9 r1 item 5's x2.fence.end scope. CarrierPlace, built only here, is the one input of the carrier location's production constructor for an operation (CarrierLocation::admitted; read-only recovery's location is built from X6b's own place). Rows: the endpoint's 468c row, X2's item 8 rows, X3b's item 8 carrier rows, the budget row, X4T's trust rows through trust_termination, X4's fail-stop row and the observer's host I/O row. No read-session handoff. Library only.", "before": "The checked operation handoff (law X2 r8 item 7a, unit X2e) composed with the grant journal's floor step, carrier start and end step (law X3b r10 items 1, 3, 3a and 4, unit X3b-3), on one ordinary writer's law 468 gate under one held installation fence. In order: the selected store endpoint joined from the gate's one read (law X3a r5); the subject's namespace admitted (X2d, no lock); at the lease-free point, with R current and no project lock held, I/trust confirmed private and N's absolute spelling admitted only if a no-follow status finds the retained N, then X3b's floor step on the write receipt's attempt ledger (X3b's operation ledger) followed by the gate's and the receipt's rechecks; the APPEND-WRITE or EXCLUSIVE lease (X2d); the join, rechecking the subject, the namespace, each locked carrier's binding and the gate, and deriving owner \u00a78's five-field binding {schemaVersion 1, N, S, G, K} from the ACTIVE row and the admitted endpoint; the transfer of the owners out of the fenced gate, never copied; X3b's carrier start on the attempt ledger (INIT creation when the floor step found INIT, the start against the floor step's observation, the append lock), then every owner rechecked again with a registered subject's namespace held to the started footprint; and only then the fence's release, ending the gate's ledger. Any refusal spends the gate and releases the append lock, the lease and then the fence. ProjectOperation is private and not Clone: the started carrier and start tail, the held lease, the binding and the ACTIVE row snapshot, the subject's owners, the carrier place, the endpoint values and required files' full samples with the retained chain to I (no fence), and the write receipt, whose attempt ledger charges the operation's journal steps. Its end path drops the append lock and releases the lease; the end step is then not entered when the owner reports an uncertain journal outcome or when the attempt ledger is closed (law X3b r10 item 4): ProjectOperation::end returns NotEntered, with no fence walk, read or copy and nothing disclosed; otherwise, on the attempt ledger, it retakes the fence by the shared charged walk, no-follow fence open and S7's bounded wait, requires the walked I to be the retained I and trust, host, projects and N still bound by name, runs, when the owner reports X3d's CarrierCapacityExhausted with every journal outcome certain, X3b-4's rollover (law X3b r10 item 13) under its own EXCLUSIVE lease, then the end step's probe, read and floor copy, and releases the fence; a failure is a value that never rewrites the operation's outcome, and a rollover outcome is returned for disclosure on its own row. CarrierPlace, built only here, is the one input of the carrier location's production constructor. Rows: the endpoint's 468c row, X2's item 8 rows, X3b's item 8 carrier rows and the budget row. OperationGuard (X4a) is a seam not built here; no read-session handoff. Library only.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/622/description"}}, {"after": "Check X2e with X3b-3 on scratch homes with a creator-published P0 whose trust store is replaced by X4T-0's signed accepted store for the same S and K, ordinary writers over 462's signed test trees and a registered scratch project (no real home, installation or Git), each handoff run through a begin_test adapter on a scripted guard seam (a scripted monitor clock, observer ticks on request, a fixed invocation), other holders simulated by nonblocking flocks on separate descriptors and S7's wait run on a scripted clock: a fresh registration's APPEND-WRITE handoff (fence released, writer.lease held and readers.lease free, the INIT floor, the created carrier and its COMMITTED 0 witness, owner \u00a78's binding equal to the ACTIVE row's N and the endpoint's S, G and K, then an end step leaving the floor unchanged and nothing locked); an Eligible root's EXCLUSIVE handoff, a certain append leaving the floor until the end step copies it forward, and the next writer's start at that tail; the floor step written before the lease when EXCLUSIVE then refuses busy, with no carrier created; a busy writer.lease skipping the floor step and refusing busy with nothing written; a floorLost quarantine refused before any lease with nothing written; a budget cut at the handoff's last charge, after the carrier start, releasing the lease and the fence, with the next writer starting on what it left; an uncertain outcome releasing the lease with no fence taken and no copy, and the next writer's floor step copying forward; a busy fence at the end waited for within S7's bound, then disclosed as the busy row with the lease already released; a closed attempt ledger not entering the end step (no walk while the fence is held elsewhere, no copy, nothing disclosed), and the next writer's floor step copying forward; a moved namespace refusing the end copy as required-files-changed with the fence released; a capacity exhaustion reported at an exhausted tail rolling the generation over under the retaken fence (TERMINAL closing generation 1, witness COMMITTED at generation 2) and copying the floor to generation 2's empty tail, with the next writer starting there; the rollover's outcome returned whatever the attempt-ledger charge returned, with the copy's result beside it: a failed reservation (Refused on the budget, no copy), a writer.lease taken by another writer before the rollover (Skipped, then the copy Skipped), a floor I/O refusal (Refused on the host I/O row, then the copy's host I/O failure), an undetermined OPEN at X3b-4's test point (Undetermined on the durability row, no copy), and a copy failure after Rolled (Rolled kept, generation 2 open, the copy's host I/O failure); and a source pin that carrier_floor builds a carrier location only in its cfg(test) constructor and that the production constructor for an operation (admitted) takes this module's carrier place. It also includes operation_live_tests.rs (X4a through this handoff) and commit_session_tests.rs (X3d-1) as child modules that reuse its scratch-home helpers.", "before": "Check X2e with X3b-3 on scratch homes with a creator-published P0, ordinary writers over 462's signed test trees and a registered scratch project (no real home, installation or Git), other holders simulated by nonblocking flocks on separate descriptors and S7's wait run on a scripted clock: a fresh registration's APPEND-WRITE handoff (fence released, writer.lease held and readers.lease free, the INIT floor, the created carrier and its COMMITTED 0 witness, owner \u00a78's binding equal to the ACTIVE row's N and the endpoint's S, G and K, then an end step leaving the floor unchanged and nothing locked); an Eligible root's EXCLUSIVE handoff, a certain append leaving the floor until the end step copies it forward, and the next writer's start at that tail; the floor step written before the lease when EXCLUSIVE then refuses busy, with no carrier created; a busy writer.lease skipping the floor step and refusing busy with nothing written; a floorLost quarantine refused before any lease with nothing written; a budget cut at the handoff's last charge, after the carrier start, releasing the lease and the fence, with the next writer starting on what it left; an uncertain outcome releasing the lease with no fence taken and no copy, and the next writer's floor step copying forward; a busy fence at the end waited for within S7's bound, then disclosed as the busy row with the lease already released; a closed attempt ledger not entering the end step (no walk while the fence is held elsewhere, no copy, nothing disclosed), and the next writer's floor step copying forward; a moved namespace refusing the end copy as required-files-changed with the fence released; a capacity exhaustion reported at an exhausted tail rolling the generation over under the retaken fence (TERMINAL closing generation 1, witness COMMITTED at generation 2) and copying the floor to generation 2's empty tail, with the next writer starting there; the rollover's outcome returned whatever the attempt-ledger charge returned, with the copy's result beside it: a failed reservation (Refused on the budget, no copy), a writer.lease taken by another writer before the rollover (Skipped, then the copy Skipped), a floor I/O refusal (Refused on the host I/O row, then the copy's host I/O failure), an undetermined OPEN at X3b-4's test point (Undetermined on the durability row, no copy), and a copy failure after Rolled (Rolled kept, generation 2 open, the copy's host I/O failure); and a source pin that the carrier location's only production constructor takes this module's carrier place.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/623/description"}}, {"after": "Check X4a through X2e's real handoff on scratch homes (a creator-published P0 whose trust store is replaced by X4T-0's signed accepted store for the same S and K except in the P0 acceptance case, ordinary writers over 462's signed test trees, a scripted monitor clock, observer ticks on request, other holders as flocks on separate descriptors; no real home or installation): the first read admitting the start view at the lease-free point and the write-ahead publication completing the handoff, then a tick with no drift; a P0 installation accepted inside the monitor's one first read (law X4B r5 item 1, unit X4B-b): the read's bracket opening on P0 and closing on the accepted state.v1, both under the fence and before any lease, the anchor the opening sample's projection and evalHighWater its wall time, revision 2 (the confirming admission wrote nothing) with the four carried roles ST-TRUSTED, the handoff completing and a tick reading the same store, and a next operation writing nothing; an effect checkpoint then its RA, the commit admission's one permit, a revoking tick after admission taking the gate from 1 to 3 without relabelling the admitted result, and a second admission refused; a pause beyond the bound after the first read fail-stopping before any effect and closing the attempt ledger; ticks and checkpoints sharing one history; a pause after the final observation failing the admission boundary; a boot change; another operation's level 4 refused as a broken caller; an unrelated revocation update continuing as drift while another holder keeps the fence; a revoking update latching through the observer and through the checkpoint's own observation; a newer view whose revocation body is missing failing unreadable, and a pointer back below the start view's counters failing as a rollback; a state.v1 replacement during the first attempt absorbed inside one monitored read and a second one failing as mixed; the stale guards (a lost lease lock on the busy row, a replaced lease file, a substituted marker, a replaced or missing store marker, a namespace no longer private, a substituted project root), each refusing before any effect on its own row; an unrelated registration not invalidating the operation; a repeated checkpoint after journal work latching on a guard gone stale; a read waiting on the shared monitor counting its wait; and the end path stopping the observer and releasing everything.", "before": "Check X4a through X2e's real handoff on scratch homes (a creator-published P0 whose trust store is replaced by X4T-0's signed accepted store for the same S and K, ordinary writers over 462's signed test trees, a scripted monitor clock, observer ticks on request, other holders as flocks on separate descriptors; no real home or installation): the first read admitting the start view at the lease-free point and the write-ahead publication completing the handoff, then a tick with no drift; a P0 installation refusing TRUST.NO_ADMITTED_TIME_CONTEXT before any lease with nothing written; an effect checkpoint then its RA, the commit admission's one permit, a revoking tick after admission taking the gate from 1 to 3 without relabelling the admitted result, and a second admission refused; a pause beyond the bound after the first read fail-stopping before any effect and closing the attempt ledger; ticks and checkpoints sharing one history; a pause after the final observation failing the admission boundary; a boot change; another operation's level 4 refused as a broken caller; an unrelated revocation update continuing as drift while another holder keeps the fence; a revoking update latching through the observer and through the checkpoint's own observation; a newer view whose revocation body is missing failing unreadable, and a pointer back below the start view's counters failing as a rollback; a state.v1 replacement during the first attempt absorbed inside one monitored read and a second one failing as mixed; the stale guards (a lost lease lock on the busy row, a replaced lease file, a substituted marker, a replaced or missing store marker, a namespace no longer private, a substituted project root), each refusing before any effect on its own row; an unrelated registration not invalidating the operation; a repeated checkpoint after journal work latching on a guard gone stale; a read waiting on the shared monitor counting its wait; and the end path stopping the observer and releasing everything.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/624/description"}}, {"after": "The ordinary writer's admission (law X1 r1 items 2 to 7). A writer that is not a creator produces the write receipt (the same producer composition as the read side's, typed Write), rechecks it, enters the law 468 durable write gate with the receipt's sealed DurableBarrierQualification as the gate's only input, and rechecks the receipt again while the gate's fence is held; a failed second recheck releases the fence and returns nothing. OrdinaryWriteAdmission holds the admitted DurableInstallation (the held fence and confirmed durability), the write receipt and the gate that admitted it, kept for its one ledger; it has no serialized form and no conversion into a read session. An absent I is INSTALLATION.NOT_INITIALIZED through the gate's positive absence; I is never created. The admission's refusals are their law 468 item 6 rows through 468c's maps. Further standing comes only through its steps, each under the held fence and followed by the gate's full recheck and the receipt's recheck, any refusal spending the gate: admit_store_endpoint validates the selected store endpoint from the gate's one read against the receipt's selected core and yields StoreAdmittedWriter, which grants only the endpoint (law X3a items 1 and 2, unit X3a-1; a refusal releases the fence); admit_project_root, observe_project_tracking, register_first_use and recheck_registered run on the gate's ledger with the write receipt's premise (law X2 items 2 to 6a, unit X2c); admit_namespace makes the writer the namespace fence holder for an APPEND-WRITE or EXCLUSIVE lease (law X2 item 7, unit X2d); and begin_operation consumes the writer into the checked operation handoff, taking the floor publication's TrustInvocation and carrying X4's live guard (law X2 r8 item 7a, unit X2e; law X4 r7, unit X4a). For the handoff it lends, crate-internally, join_store_endpoint, attempt_step on the write receipt's attempt ledger, gate_step on the gate's own ledger at the lease-free point (the installation lent mutably so a confirmed trust publication can advance state.v1, and the receipt's bootstrap source for the fenced first read's F-absent branch, law X4B r5 item 2, unit X4B-b), selected_closure and into_parts. Library only: no command is wired.", "before": "The ordinary writer's admission (law X1 r1 items 2 to 7). A writer that is not a creator produces the write receipt (the same producer composition as the read side's, typed Write), rechecks it, enters the law 468 durable write gate with the receipt's sealed DurableBarrierQualification as the gate's only input, and rechecks the receipt again while the gate's fence is held; a failed second recheck releases the fence and returns nothing. OrdinaryWriteAdmission grants exactly what DurableInstallation grants: the held fence and confirmed durability, with no store, trust, project, id, grant or commit standing, no serialized form and no conversion into a read session. An absent I is INSTALLATION.NOT_INITIALIZED through the gate's positive absence; nothing is created. Every refusal is its law 468 item 6 row through 468c's maps. Library only: no command is wired.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/625/description"}}, {"after": "Check the ordinary writer's admission on scratch account homes with a creator-published P0 and 462's signed test trees: admission with the fence held, a recheck and release; a development build at CORE.NO_EMBEDDED_RELEASE; an absent I as INSTALLATION.NOT_INITIALIZED with nothing created, and a non-directory at a suffix name not taken as absence; a step 2 recheck refusal that never enters the gate and a step 4 refusal that releases the held fence; busy and budget rows; one attempt and one gate per process; and type-level and source pins that each purpose's receipt lends only its own sealed qualification and that only InitialPlatform implements either. It also checks the writer's selected store endpoint (law X3a items 1 and 2, unit X3a-1): admitted from the gate's one read with S, G 0 and one node, still under the held fence until release; and an in-place rewrite after that read refusing the admission as required-files-changed with the fence freed.", "before": "Check the ordinary writer's admission on scratch account homes with a creator-published P0 and 462's signed test trees: admission with the fence held, a recheck and release; a development build at CORE.NO_EMBEDDED_RELEASE; an absent I as INSTALLATION.NOT_INITIALIZED with nothing created, and a non-directory at a suffix name not taken as absence; a step 2 recheck refusal that never enters the gate and a step 4 refusal that releases the held fence; busy and budget rows; one attempt and one gate per process; and type-level and source pins that each purpose's receipt lends only its own sealed qualification and that only InitialPlatform implements either.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/626/description"}}, {"after": "Walk and judge the project chain (law X2 r5 items 1 to 3 and 9, and the custody half of item 4): one retained, no-follow, charged walk from / to a selected project root. The root must be strictly below H and outside H/Library/Application Support/OpenSIP, judged lexically before any open. / through the directory above the root takes the shared 468 step 0 external-ancestor judgment with the invocation's omission premise; the root takes S3 directory custody (owner the invoking user or root, no other-write, group-write and ACL write grants only for explicit trust groups, ACL readable or omitted under the premise); every directory from H down must be on H's own volume. That S3 judgment, from one bounded ACL capture with the premise applied to an omitted ACL through the object's own fstatfs, is judge_project_object, which also judges .opensip and the S3 config file (as a configuration file: regular, one link, at most 4 MiB) for project admission and first registration, and the Git evidence for tracking. The root's native birth is sampled with the existing sampler, charged first, with no mtime or ctime substitute. ProjectChain is private and not Clone, holds the retained chain, spelling and birth, rechecks names, custody, volume and birth for the held-fence recheck set, and grants nothing; it lends its retained path and H's index, and the policy its H-filesystem check, to X2b-2's tracking. Each refusal names its item 8 row and subject; selection, the registry, ProjectRootAdmission, tracking and public projection are X2b's. Nothing is created. Library only.", "before": "Walk and judge the project chain (law X2 r5 items 1 to 3 and 9, and the custody half of item 4): one retained, no-follow, charged walk from / to a selected project root. The root must be strictly below H and outside H/Library/Application Support/OpenSIP, judged lexically before any open. / through the directory above the root takes the shared 468 step 0 external-ancestor judgment with the invocation's omission premise; the root takes S3 directory custody (owner the invoking user or root, no other-write, group-write and ACL write grants only for explicit trust groups, ACL readable or omitted under the premise); every directory from H down must be on H's own volume. The root's native birth is sampled with the existing sampler, charged first, with no mtime or ctime substitute. ProjectChain is private and not Clone, holds the retained chain, spelling and birth, rechecks names, custody, volume and birth for the held-fence recheck set, and grants nothing. Each refusal names its item 8 row and subject; selection, the registry, ProjectRootAdmission, tracking and public projection are X2b's. Nothing is created. Library only.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/632/description"}}, {"after": "Produce the read side's premise receipt (law 458c r5 items 1 to 4): the process's one attempt, the actor, InitialCore and InitialPlatform, exactly the creator's producers, with no intent, storage choice, disclosure, effect or fence. Law X1 r1 items 1 and 2 type it by purpose: the one producer composition makes PlatformReceipt<Read> (ReadPremiseReceipt, produce_read_platform) or PlatformReceipt<Write> (WritePlatformReceipt, produce_write_platform); Read and Write are sealed, neither receipt converts into the other, and a receipt whose actor, core or platform belongs to another attempt is the invariant row. The read receipt is private and not Clone, holds its own attempt, and lends only the sealed ReadPremiseQualification that InitialPlatform alone implements: the check that a filesystem is H's and the omission premise with 465 item 4's scope, no barrier policy. The write receipt is likewise private, not Clone and holds its own attempt; its lending is the durable write gate's sealed DurableBarrierQualification, beside which it lends the running core's embedded bootstrap source to the fenced first read alone (law X4B r5 item 2, unit X4B-b), charge, one charged step on its attempt ledger with that lending (unit X2e), charge_guarded, the same step with the actor, core and platform rechecks lent beside it for the checkpoint's guard (law X4 r7 item 4, unit X4a), and the attempt ledger's one end-path settlement reserve and its single spend, on the commit session's way only (law X3d r6 item 8, unit X3d-1). Both purposes expose recheck, the actor, core and platform rechecks on the receipt's own attempt, and selected_core, the selected core's closure and state writer K as values only (law X3a item 1). Both also expose core_evaluator_closure, the selected core's evaluator closure (contract successor EC1) derived from the same authenticated inventory, as a value only (law X3d r8 item 13, unit X3d-3), and, only under the joint predicate (test, crash-matrix or scenario-fixtures), the read of its preimages behind CommitSession's test-support accessor. Every refusal ends in its law 468 item 6 row through 468c's maps. InstallationReadFence::acquire and doctor's installation check pass it to the observation session. The ordinary writer's admission consumes the write receipt (law X1), and the operation handoff carries it (unit X2e). Its one wired CLI path is opensip doctor (law X10), through doctor's installation check; no CLI command reaches a write receipt.", "before": "Produce the read side's premise receipt (law 458c r5 items 1 to 4): the process's one attempt, the actor, InitialCore and InitialPlatform, exactly the creator's producers, with no intent, storage choice, disclosure, effect or fence. The receipt is private and not Clone, holds its own attempt, and lends only the sealed ReadPremiseQualification that InitialPlatform alone implements: the check that a filesystem is H's and the omission premise with 465 item 4's scope, no barrier policy. Every refusal ends in its law 468 item 6 row through 468c's maps. Library only: no read path uses it yet.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/634/description"}}, {"after": "M3-B r9 items 19 to 24 (units B3-b2b1 and B3-b2b2b, B3-b plan r2 amendments A2 and A4): the D15 readers (cargo-config-patch@1, npm-workspaces-members@1) and membership decision core, B3-b0 r4 discover without SLM passes over a WorkspaceObservation (entry, markers, children, bytes, custody-captured entries, and item 6b outcome per candidate with its index gitlinks): carriers and custody, the parsers, entry shapes and globs over lawfully visible repositories, placement, candidate failures, the 64-member cap; branch A decided before the caller one walk (the array gate returning the size or first-grammar refusal, then member decisions in path order stopping at the first refusal, then the admitted members and gitlinks for the walk, and links only after an accepted walk) and branch B; root-in-repository, entries at or below a gitlink, links and declaration rows. Pure; composed by custody/workspace_discovery.rs.", "before": "M3-B r9 items 19 to 24 (unit B3-b2b1, B3-b plan r2 amendment A2): the D15 readers (cargo-config-patch@1, npm-workspaces-members@1) and membership decision core, B3-b0 discover without SLM passes over a WorkspaceObservation (entry, markers, children, bytes, per-candidate item 6b verdict): carriers and custody, the parsers, entry shapes and globs over lawfully visible repositories, placement, candidate failures, the 64-member cap, branches A (two phases around the trial walk, short-circuit in path order) and B, root-in-repository, links and declaration rows. Pure; no consumer yet (B3-b2b2).", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/643/description"}}, {"after": "M3-B units B3-b2b1 and B3-b2b2b controls: every workspace member vector reproduced, with branch A walk members and gitlinks equal to the reference ones, item 6b asked exactly where the reference consulted it, and the core looking up, reading or listing only paths the B3-b0 r4 reference did, never touching the checker unobserved paths and never reading its unread ones; a forbidden lookup caught without an output change; branch A stopping at the first refusal with no later candidate custody or item 6b verdict observed; TOML resource bounds as the existing parse disposition where tomllib reads; colliding Cargo entries keeping the first in document order.", "before": "M3-B unit B3-b2b1 controls: every workspace member vector reproduced with the core touching only paths the B3-b0 reference looked up, read or listed and none of the checker forbidden paths; a forbidden lookup caught without an output change; branch A stopping at the first refusal with no later member observed; TOML resource bounds as the existing parse disposition where tomllib reads; colliding Cargo entries keeping the first in document order.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/644/description"}}, {"after": "M3-B units B3-b2b1 and B3-b2b2b workspace member vectors, generated by tools/security/workspace_member_vectors.py: B3-b0 r4 (contract successor 169) decisions over its checker D15 cases and the discovery cases (observation with item 6b verdicts and gitlinks, facts, branch A walk members, gitlinks and status, the expected outcome including array-gate and walk refusals, the reference access record, the item 6b consultations, and the forbidden lookups and reads) and labelled TOML and JSON parser controls; the non-string arrays a product array cannot hold are listed by id. Design evidence, not qualification.", "before": "M3-B unit B3-b2b1 workspace member vectors, generated by tools/security/workspace_member_vectors.py: B3-b0 (contract successor 168) decisions over its checker D15 cases and the discovery cases (observation, facts, trial, expected outcome, the reference access record and forbidden paths) and labelled TOML and JSON parser controls. Design evidence, not qualification.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/648/description"}}, {"after": "Own the initial creator's pre-installation attempt: one per process, the only work ledger for the whole act and a failure latch, with private non-Clone receipts bound to the attempt. The same attempt, with its one ledger, is also the process's one attempt behind the read receipt and the ordinary writer's write receipt (laws 458c and X1). Mint the InitialActor (equal real and effective UID, a plain absolute home spelling that fits I within 4096 bytes and 256 components) and recheck it; classify storage (constant UNKNOWN), apply the first-creation backup choice, write and flush the first-write disclosure, and mint, recheck and consume the single-use CreationIntent with its request and execution ids at StepId 0. recheck_actor_in rechecks the actor on a borrowed scope (law 467 item 6; also the write receipt's guard, law X4 r7 item 4), and recheck_storage_in and recheck_handoff_storage rebuild I and re-classify a consumed intent's target (law 467 item 2). reserve_end_path_settlement takes the attempt ledger's one settlement reserve, refused Closed once the attempt is latched, and settle_end_path spends it once whether or not the attempt is latched (law X3d r6 item 8, unit X3d-1); they are the only production callers of WorkLedger::reserve_settlement and settle, reached only on the write receipt's way to the commit session's end-path step and StoppedSession::finish. It opens no installation path, creates nothing, and does not enable the creator.", "before": "Allocate the one pre-installation attempt per process, owning the single work ledger and a failure latch, and mint the attempt-bound InitialActor from the charged account observation: equal real and effective UID and a plain absolute home spelling that fits I within 4096 bytes and 256 components. It opens no path, creates nothing, and does not enable the creator.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/659/description"}}, {"after": "The production installation reader API (law 458c r5 items 5 and 8). InstallationReadFence::acquire produces the read receipt, runs the observation session on the account's I and requires I complete; every refusal is its law 468 item 6 row, and a development build ends at CORE.NO_EMBEDDED_RELEASE. A bounded leaf or descendant capture opens only through the held I by the read session, is charged to its one ledger, and rechecks the held fence, every retained edge and the file before its bytes are consumed; a request refused before any native step (an invalid leaf, depth or bound) has no row. Provisional observations only: no selected store, core/profile admission, current authority, durability or creation permission. Cooperative first-party exclusion and the existing kernel/loader TCB apply.", "before": "Provisional observations under a native installation fence. No selected store, core/profile admission, current authority, durability or creation permission. Cooperative first-party exclusion and the existing kernel/loader TCB apply.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/662/description"}}, {"after": "The closed vocabulary of law 468 item 6: one variant per public row of creator and existing-root admission refusals, the platform decision's NT-TCB detail, and an invariant row for refusals only a broken caller or a broken internal invariant reaches, such as a second F absent after a confirmed trust acceptance (unit X4B-b). Later units extend the table, each row with existing codes: law X3a item 5's STATE.SCHEMA_UNSUPPORTED row (unit X3a-1); the fenced trust admission's rows of law X4T r9 item 10, with RootDetail's nine S5 ROOT.* chain details, and the live guards' revoked-during-operation and observer fail-stop rows (law X4 r7 item 8, unit X4a); and the commit facade's journal and ledger quarantine, carrier migration, durability-undetermined and project-owner rows (law X3d r6 item 9, unit X3d-1). A diagnostic classification for the host's D9 projection, never authority.", "before": "The closed vocabulary of law 468 item 6: one variant per public row of creator and existing-root admission refusals, the platform decision's NT-TCB detail, and an invariant row for refusals only a broken caller reaches. A diagnostic classification for the host's D9 projection, never authority.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/664/description"}}, {"after": "Own grant journal append/witness/high-water admission and versioned SEAL carrier mapping; preserve append-only sequence/cap/restore rules and keep this carrier distinct from lifecycle transition journals; own grant-generation succession (law X3b r8, unit X3b-4): per-generation prefix admission in which a TERMINAL closes its generation and only cause grantGenerationClosure justifies the next, the SEAL ceiling predicate seal_fits, and, in the macOS carrier_floor child, the predecessor check, the successor rule and the rollover; re-export the carrier's operation-handoff (X3b-3 with X2e) and commit-session (X3d-1) surface and its private file protocol, X6a's read-only carrier recovery capture (recover_carrier and RecoveryQuery) for the recovery admission and the public, inert CarrierObservation and CarrierKind that storage's recover reads (law X6 r3, unit X6b), and, under cfg(test) or the test-only crash-matrix feature only, X9-1's carrier fixtures (law X9 r1 item 6).", "before": "Own grant journal append/witness/high-water admission and versioned SEAL carrier mapping; preserve append-only sequence/cap/restore rules and keep this carrier distinct from lifecycle transition journals.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/665/description"}}, {"after": "The grant-journal append protocol (law X3b r6 items 5, 6, 8 and 9; unit X3b-2; X3b r8 item 5a's capacity window, unit X3b-4), as carrier_floor.rs's macOS child module append, under the operation lease the caller holds. JournalAppendLock (level 4) is one in-process mutex per carrier, made only from the CarrierStart it consumes, holding the committed tail its appends follow and two latches that are never reset: REV (after which no SEAL or RA is appended, S6) and undetermined. Level 3: begin opens the carrier read-write through the retained namespace (the same file before and after), sets the WAL and fullfsync writer pragmas with busy_timeout 0, takes BEGIN IMMEDIATE (busy is LEDGER.BUSY_TIMEOUT at once and holds nothing, F06), re-admits the exact definitions, the format row and the SHA-256(N) binding in that transaction, and confirms the tail is the lock's and the witness names it (COMMITTED at the tail, or a PENDING beyond it that a certain failure left; on a closed generation, item 4a's effective tail), refusing while level 4 is held or after an undetermined outcome. Level 4 is only tried after level 3, never waited on; the held value is the borrow X4's checkpoint receives, and dropping it releases level 4 and then rolls back any open level 3. One append, with both witness publications, their confirmations and the commit reserved before the first effect: build the record at tail + 1 (SEAL, REV, CLN and RA as closed schema-3 bodies through the existing parser, TERMINAL as the frozen recordSchema-1 body with cause grantGenerationClosure, built only through append_terminal, which only the rollover calls; the physical operationRef grammar, the domain-framed body hash, chain_law 1 from the genesis value), check item 5a's capacity table before any effect (nothing after a TERMINAL, a SEAL only while seal_fits holds, RA, REV and CLN refused as GenerationFull at tail 9007199254740990, a TERMINAL only in its window), witness PENDING by the file protocol, INSERT and COMMIT, witness COMMITTED. A SEAL keeps level 4 held after its commit until released (r5); other records release it on return. Failures before visibility are certain; a failure after the PENDING rename, a failed or lost COMMIT, and any failure after the commit are durability-undetermined: the lock latches and refuses every further effect, nothing follows in the operation, and the next writer's floor step and start reconcile (X3b r9 item 5). Refusals name item 8's rows, GenerationFull the busy row, and a broken composition (wrong carrier, lock order, after REV, capacity, the terminal slot, the SEAL ceiling, an unbuildable record) the invariant row. Nothing here takes the fence or a lease or writes trust state; grant-generation rollover itself is carrier_rollover.rs's. Its own crash and fault hooks (AppendStep, Fault) exist only under cfg(test); law X9's barrier points beside them in the caller's x3b.append.<kind> scope (begin, level-four, built, the witness-pending and witness-committed publications, insert, the fallible commit standing in for a COMMIT that fails before or after it lands, and the two releases; law X9 r1 item 5, unit X9-1) exist only under the test-only crash-matrix feature, and without it a point compiles to nothing and a scope or fallible point to the code it wraps. Library only.", "before": "The grant-journal append protocol (law X3b r6 items 5, 6, 8 and 9; unit X3b-2), as carrier_floor.rs's macOS child module append, under the operation lease the caller holds. JournalAppendLock (level 4) is one in-process mutex per carrier, made only from the CarrierStart it consumes, holding the committed tail its appends follow and two latches that are never reset: REV (after which no SEAL or RA is appended, S6) and undetermined. Level 3: begin opens the carrier read-write through the retained namespace (the same file before and after), sets the WAL and fullfsync writer pragmas with busy_timeout 0, takes BEGIN IMMEDIATE (busy is LEDGER.BUSY_TIMEOUT at once and holds nothing, F06), re-admits the exact definitions, the format row and the SHA-256(N) binding in that transaction, and confirms the tail is the lock's and the witness names it (COMMITTED at the tail, or a PENDING beyond it that a certain failure left), refusing while level 4 is held or after an undetermined outcome. Level 4 is only tried after level 3, never waited on; the held value is the borrow X4's checkpoint receives, and dropping it releases level 4 and then rolls back any open level 3. One append, with both witness publications, their confirmations and the commit reserved before the first effect: build the record at tail + 1 (SEAL, REV, CLN and RA as closed schema-3 bodies through the existing parser, TERMINAL as the frozen recordSchema-1 body with cause grantGenerationClosure only at the reserved slot; the physical operationRef grammar, the domain-framed body hash, chain_law 1 from the genesis value, ordinary records refused at the reserved slot), witness PENDING by the file protocol, INSERT and COMMIT, witness COMMITTED. A SEAL keeps level 4 held after its commit until released (r5); other records release it on return. Failures before visibility are certain; a failure after the PENDING rename, a failed or lost COMMIT, and any failure after the commit are durability-undetermined: the lock latches, refuses every further effect, and reconciles through X3b-1b's reconcile_after_uncertain, writing only the witness. Refusals name item 8's rows, and a broken composition (wrong carrier, lock order, after REV, capacity, the terminal slot, an unbuildable record) the invariant row. Nothing here takes the fence or a lease or writes trust state; grant-generation rollover is not here. Crash and fault hooks exist only under cfg(test). Library only.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/668/description"}}, {"after": "Check X3b-2 and X3b-4's capacity window on scratch installations: SEAL, REV, CLN, RA and TERMINAL bodies, the domain-framed hash, the genesis and chain_law 1 previous values, and each build refusal (operationRef grammar, fixture or non-run3 Run, non-object epoch, overlong reason, malformed wall clock, a missing previous hash); every protocol step in order, the floor untouched, no temporary witness left, and the reader's own prefix admission accepting the appended RA, SEAL, REV and CLN; a SEAL holding level 4 until released, no level 3 under it and no second record, then level 4 released before level 3; a stop before the record releasing both and committing nothing; a busy journal refused within a second holding nothing; no SEAL or RA after a REV while CLN and REV follow; a crash after each step reconciling by the next floor step and start to OK, REVERT, REVERT, REVERT, ADVANCE and OK; failures before visibility certain with the next append completing, and failures after it undetermined, latching every further effect, the end step copying nothing, and the next writer's floor step and start reconciling to the stated outcome; item 5a's window at each boundary: a SEAL taking 9007199254740988 and refused above the ceiling, RA, REV and CLN taking 9007199254740990 and refused there as GenerationFull on the busy row, a TERMINAL refused below its window and on an empty tail and admitted after tails 9007199254740988 to 9007199254740990 as the frozen recordSchema-1 body, and nothing after it; a source pin that only carrier_rollover.rs calls append_terminal and RecordDraft has no TERMINAL; a moved tail, a foreign witness and another carrier refused before any write; the reservation refusing before the first effect and covering a measured witness publication; and a source pin that the module never waits, retries, writes trust state or stores a quarantine marker.", "before": "Check X3b-2 on scratch installations: SEAL, REV, CLN, RA and TERMINAL bodies, the domain-framed hash, the genesis and chain_law 1 previous values, and each build refusal (operationRef grammar, fixture or non-run3 Run, non-object epoch, overlong reason, malformed wall clock, a missing previous hash); every protocol step in order, the floor untouched, no temporary witness left, and the reader's own prefix admission accepting the appended RA, SEAL, REV and CLN; a SEAL holding level 4 until released, no level 3 under it and no second record, then level 4 released before level 3; a stop before the record releasing both and committing nothing; a busy journal refused within a second holding nothing; no SEAL or RA after a REV while CLN and REV follow; a crash after each step reconciling by the next floor step and start to OK, REVERT, REVERT, REVERT, ADVANCE and OK; failures before visibility certain with the next append completing, and failures after it undetermined, latching every further effect and reconciling to the stated tail that the end step alone copies; TERMINAL only at the reserved slot, an ordinary record refused there, and nothing after it; a moved tail, a foreign witness and another carrier refused before any write; the reservation refusing before the first effect and covering a measured witness publication; and a source pin that the module never waits, retries, writes trust state or stores a quarantine marker.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/669/description"}}, {"after": "Dispatch observed carrier schema names and exact definitions within one read-only SQLite snapshot; historical classification does not authorize migration or repair. The captured dispatch also reports whether its snapshot holds an inherited format-1 or format-2 grant_journal beside or instead of the current objects (inherited_present), which X6a's read-only recovery capture uses to tell the lawful {A, B} migration prefix from an unpublished fresh footprint (law X6 r2 item 1, unit X6a).", "before": "Dispatch observed carrier schema names and exact definitions within one read-only SQLite snapshot; historical classification does not authorize migration or repair.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/670/description"}}, {"after": "The grant-journal carrier floor step and floor-first carrier creation (law X3b r4 items 2, 3, 3a and 5; unit X3b-1a). The private file protocol publishes a witness or floor by a fresh exclusive private temporary name, write and read-back with F_FULLFSYNC, rename, directory barrier, and a no-follow reopen confirming the same file and bytes. The floor step probes writer.lease without waiting and releases it at once (busy skips and writes nothing), observes the floor under trust/carrier-floors, the carrier and the witness read-only, classifies the carrier by format before any floor decision (inherited, footprint and project-binding refusals), decides by the floor table (INIT, resume, copy forward never down, floorLost, uncertainTailLoss, regression, and reconcile_witness as a read-only decision), and writes only the floor, with no project lock held. Creation re-observes INIT, creates the database exclusively and private, sets the WAL and fullfsync writer pragmas, applies the DDL and publishes the format row in one BEGIN IMMEDIATE transaction, takes the file and namespace barriers, and writes the witness COMMITTED 0. Generation succession (X3b r8 item 4a; unit X3b-4) is here too: the committed tail carries its TERMINAL flag, the tail query every writer open uses checks that a newer generation's predecessor is a TERMINAL of the generation before, succession applies the successor rule over one tail and one witness (OPEN on a closed generation, NoSuccessor at the last generation), and the floor follows its copy tail with the open-successor exception. Each refusal names its item 8 row, and the composition owner maps it. Its macOS child modules are start (carrier_start.rs: the carrier start and the end step), append (carrier_append.rs: the append protocol), rollover (carrier_rollover.rs: grant-generation rollover), operation (carrier_operation.rs: the journal half of the operation handoff, X3b-3 with X2e), recovery_capture (recovery_capture.rs: the read-only carrier recovery capture, law X6 r2 item 1, unit X6a, which reuses this file's tail query, successor rule and floor comparison and writes nothing) and recovery_location (recovery_location.rs: the recovery side of that capture and its public CarrierObservation, law X6 r3, unit X6b). The operation child's CarrierLocation::admitted, built from X2e's confirmed CarrierPlace (item 11), and the recovery_location child's CarrierLocation::recovered, built only from the RecoveryPlace a recovery admission confirmed (law X6 r3 item 12), are CarrierLocation's only production constructors. It also exports publish_private_file, through which the trust current pointer is replaced (law X4T r9 item 7), and project_key_digest for X3d-1's commit session. Library only.", "before": "The grant-journal carrier floor step and floor-first carrier creation (law X3b r4 items 2, 3, 3a and 5; unit X3b-1a). The private file protocol publishes a witness or floor by a fresh exclusive private temporary name, write and read-back with F_FULLFSYNC, rename, directory barrier, and a no-follow reopen confirming the same file and bytes. The floor step probes writer.lease without waiting and releases it at once (busy skips and writes nothing), observes the floor under trust/carrier-floors, the carrier and the witness read-only, classifies the carrier by format before any floor decision (inherited, footprint and project-binding refusals), decides by the floor table (INIT, resume, copy forward never down, floorLost, uncertainTailLoss, regression, and reconcile_witness as a read-only decision), and writes only the floor, with no project lock held. Creation re-observes INIT, creates the database exclusively and private, sets the WAL and fullfsync writer pragmas, applies the DDL and publishes the format row in one BEGIN IMMEDIATE transaction, takes the file and namespace barriers, and writes the witness COMMITTED 0. Each refusal names its item 8 row; the public mapping, the carrier start's witness writes, the end step and the append are later units. CarrierLocation has no production constructor until X3b-3. Library only.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/671/description"}}, {"after": "The grant journal's half of the operation handoff (law X3b r10 items 1, 3, 3a, 4 and 11; unit X3b-3, composed with X2e): the carrier location's writer-side production constructor, which takes only the carrier place X2e's handoff confirms (recovery_location.rs holds the recovery side's, unit X6b); the floor step at X2's lease-free point, keeping the committed tail the start must confirm; inside the handoff under the fence and the writer lease, item 3a's creation when the floor step found INIT, item 4's carrier start against that observation and item 6's append lock from it, with a skipped floor step (busy writer.lease) refusing on the busy row if the lease was nevertheless taken; and the end step's probe, read and forward-only floor copy after certain outcomes, or, after a capacity exhaustion, the end step from its step 3 (X3b-4's rollover, then the copy). It adds no carrier semantics and composes only X3b-1's, X3b-2's and X3b-4's steps. It also passes on the append surface others name through journal_store: JournalAppendHeld, the level-4 borrow X4's checkpoint receives (law X4 r7 item 3, unit X4a), and, for X3d-1's commit session, the end path's records, the append outcome, the detached level 3 and the end path's exact cost and body bounds. Under cfg(test) or the test-only crash-matrix feature only, one helper plants a committed tail with X3b-2's reserved-slot technique (also for the composition owner's and the commit session's tests) and another an inherited format-1 or format-2 carrier (InheritedFormat): the crash matrix's carrier fixtures (law X9 r1 item 6, unit X9-1); cfg(test) re-exports carry X3b-2's append test points and X3b-4's rollover test points, and a cfg(test) seam lends the started lock to install an append hook (unit X3d-1). Library only.", "before": "The grant journal's half of the operation handoff (law X3b r10 items 1, 3, 3a, 4 and 11; unit X3b-3, composed with X2e): the carrier location's production constructor, which takes only the carrier place X2e's handoff confirms; the floor step at X2's lease-free point, keeping the committed tail the start must confirm; inside the handoff under the fence and the writer lease, item 3a's creation when the floor step found INIT, item 4's carrier start against that observation and item 6's append lock from it, with a skipped floor step (busy writer.lease) refusing on the busy row if the lease was nevertheless taken; and the end step's probe, read and forward-only floor copy after certain outcomes, or, after a capacity exhaustion, the end step from its step 3 (X3b-4's rollover, then the copy). It adds no carrier semantics and composes only X3b-1's, X3b-2's and X3b-4's steps. A cfg(test) helper plants a committed tail with X3b-2's reserved-slot technique, and cfg(test) re-exports carry X3b-4's rollover test points, for the composition owner's tests. Library only.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/673/description"}}, {"after": "Grant-generation rollover (law X3b r10 item 13, with items 4a, 5, 5a, 8 and 9; unit X3b-4), as carrier_floor.rs's macOS child module rollover, and the end step's exhaustion input (item 4 step 3). end_step_after_exhaustion takes X3d's CarrierCapacityExhausted {grantGeneration, provenTailSeq} and the released operation's operationRef, runs the rollover, then X3b-1b's floor copy (steps 4 and 5), which after an undetermined rollover copies nothing and is not entered on an attempt ledger the rollover closed (r10); the composition owner holds the fence around it with no project lock held. The rollover refuses an input on which a SEAL still fits (seal_fits) as a broken composition, then makes one fixed reservation on the attempt ledger before any lease (leases, classification with the predecessor and tail queries, witness and floor reads, the entropy draw and clock sample, step 3's one REVERT or ADVANCE witness write, one TERMINAL append at item 5's cost, and OPEN's witness write); a failed reservation takes no lease. It takes EXCLUSIVE as writer.lease then readers.lease LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB, never waiting, and on either busy releases what it took and returns Skipped. Under the lease it observes the carrier start's state (classification, binding, predecessor check, the committed tail, the witness) and the floor (with item 4a's open-successor exception), and decides by item 13's table in order: any quarantine or floor regression refuses; a later generation or an open successor is AlreadyRolled; a closing TERMINAL the witness names OK or ADVANCE is OPEN only; an open generation 9223372036854775807 refuses on the invariant row; a tail below the proven tail refuses as floor regression or uncertainTailLoss; an open in-window tail at or above the proof closes. Closing mints the TERMINAL's operationRef (op- and 16 host-CSPRNG bytes as hex, once per attempt, never the released operation's, no redraw) and one UTC clock sample in the frozen YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ shape before any write, writes a REVERT or ADVANCE witness as the start does, makes its own JournalAppendLock, appends the TERMINAL with cause grantGenerationClosure through item 5 inside item 5a's window, then OPENs G+1 by the witness COMMITTED (G+1, 0, null) alone, drops the lock, and releases readers.lease then writer.lease. It writes no floor (the end step's copy writes (G+1, 0, null) after the lease is released), no carrier row but the TERMINAL, and no carrier_capacity_pause or quarantine row. After a failure after visibility nothing follows in the operation (no reconciliation, carrier read, witness write or floor copy; r9 item 5): the lock is dropped, the lease released, and the outcome disclosed as DURABILITY.COMMIT_FAILED, leaving a crash-table state the next writer's floor step and start reconcile. Decision refusals leave the attempt ledger open for the end step's copy. Outcomes are Skipped, AlreadyRolled, Rolled, Refused and Undetermined, each with item 8's row. Its own crash and fault hooks, and injectable entropy and clock, exist only under cfg(test); law X9's barrier points (law X9 r1 item 5, unit X9-1) exist only under the test-only crash-matrix feature: the x3b.rollover scope with a point at each RolloverStep place and scopes around the lease takes and releases and the witness writes, the TERMINAL append's x3b.append.terminal and the end step's x3b.end.floor; without the feature a point compiles to nothing and a scope to the code it wraps. Library only.", "before": "Grant-generation rollover (law X3b r10 item 13, with items 4a, 5, 5a, 8 and 9; unit X3b-4), as carrier_floor.rs's macOS child module rollover, and the end step's exhaustion input (item 4 step 3). end_step_after_exhaustion takes X3d's CarrierCapacityExhausted {grantGeneration, provenTailSeq} and the released operation's operationRef, runs the rollover, then X3b-1b's floor copy (steps 4 and 5), which after an undetermined rollover copies nothing and is not entered on an attempt ledger the rollover closed (r10); the composition owner holds the fence around it with no project lock held. The rollover refuses an input on which a SEAL still fits (seal_fits) as a broken composition, then makes one fixed reservation on the attempt ledger before any lease (leases, classification with the predecessor and tail queries, witness and floor reads, the entropy draw and clock sample, step 3's one REVERT or ADVANCE witness write, one TERMINAL append at item 5's cost, and OPEN's witness write); a failed reservation takes no lease. It takes EXCLUSIVE as writer.lease then readers.lease LOCK_EX|LOCK_NB, never waiting, and on either busy releases what it took and returns Skipped. Under the lease it observes the carrier start's state (classification, binding, predecessor check, the committed tail, the witness) and the floor (with item 4a's open-successor exception), and decides by item 13's table in order: any quarantine or floor regression refuses; a later generation or an open successor is AlreadyRolled; a closing TERMINAL the witness names OK or ADVANCE is OPEN only; an open generation 9223372036854775807 refuses on the invariant row; a tail below the proven tail refuses as floor regression or uncertainTailLoss; an open in-window tail at or above the proof closes. Closing mints the TERMINAL's operationRef (op- and 16 host-CSPRNG bytes as hex, once per attempt, never the released operation's, no redraw) and one UTC clock sample in the frozen YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ shape before any write, writes a REVERT or ADVANCE witness as the start does, makes its own JournalAppendLock, appends the TERMINAL with cause grantGenerationClosure through item 5 inside item 5a's window, then OPENs G+1 by the witness COMMITTED (G+1, 0, null) alone, drops the lock, and releases readers.lease then writer.lease. It writes no floor (the end step's copy writes (G+1, 0, null) after the lease is released), no carrier row but the TERMINAL, and no carrier_capacity_pause or quarantine row. After a failure after visibility nothing follows in the operation (no reconciliation, carrier read, witness write or floor copy; r9 item 5): the lock is dropped, the lease released, and the outcome disclosed as DURABILITY.COMMIT_FAILED, leaving a crash-table state the next writer's floor step and start reconcile. Decision refusals leave the attempt ledger open for the end step's copy. Outcomes are Skipped, AlreadyRolled, Rolled, Refused and Undetermined, each with item 8's row. Crash and fault hooks, and injectable entropy and clock, exist only under cfg(test). Library only.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/675/description"}}, {"after": "The grant-journal carrier start and the end step (law X3b r6 item 4; unit X3b-1b; item 4a's successor rule, X3b r9, unit X3b-4; X3d r3 item 7 relies on them), as carrier_floor.rs's macOS child module start. The start, under the fence and the operation lease, opens and classifies the carrier (format, SHA-256(N) binding and the predecessor check), reads the tail and the witness, confirms the tail equals the floor step's observation before any write, then runs item 4a's reconciliation: OK writes nothing, REVERT, ADVANCE and INIT write only the witness COMMITTED at the committed tail by the private file protocol, OPEN on a generation a TERMINAL closed writes only the witness COMMITTED (G+1, 0, null), and QUARANTINE and OPEN at the last generation refuse writing nothing; it writes no floor and returns the effective tail (the successor's empty tail after OPEN) for X4's epoch binding. X3b r9 item 5 withdrew X3b-1b's reconciliation after an uncertain outcome: nothing follows such an outcome in the operation, and the next writer's floor step and start reconcile. The end step, under the fence with no project lock, does nothing at all after an uncertain outcome; otherwise it probes writer.lease without waiting (busy skips), reads the tail and the witness while the probe is held, releases the probe, and copies the floor forward to item 4a's copy tail only if higher, a floor already at the witnessed successor's empty tail being unchanged; a missing floor is floorLost and a floor above the copy tail is regression, each writing nothing. Step 3's rollover (carrier_rollover.rs's end_step_after_exhaustion) runs before it. Taking the fence and lease is the composition owner's (X3b-3). Library only.", "before": "The grant-journal carrier start, the end step and the post-uncertainty reconciliation (law X3b r6 item 4, item 5's uncertain outcomes; unit X3b-1b; X3d r3 item 7 relies on them). The start, under the fence and the operation lease, opens and classifies the carrier (format and SHA-256(N) binding), reads the tail and the witness, confirms the tail equals the floor step's observation before any write, then runs reconcile_witness: OK writes nothing, REVERT, ADVANCE and INIT write only the witness COMMITTED at the committed tail by the private file protocol, and QUARANTINE refuses writing nothing; it writes no floor and returns the start tail for X4's epoch binding. reconcile_after_uncertain, still under the lease, reopens and reconciles, writing only the witness on REVERT, ADVANCE or INIT, and yields a reconciled tail only on OK, REVERT or ADVANCE (INIT stays non-copyable). The end step, under the fence with no project lock, probes writer.lease without waiting (busy skips), reads the tail to copy while the probe is held (the committed tail, or after an uncertain outcome only the reconciled tail, or nothing), releases the probe, and copies the floor forward only if higher; a missing floor is floorLost and a floor above the tail is regression, each writing nothing. Taking the fence and lease is the composition owner's (X3b-3). Library only.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/677/description"}}, {"after": "Check X3b-1b, with X3b r9's uncertain-outcome rule (unit X3b-4), on scratch installations: the floor step reports the tail the start must confirm (INIT, skipped, current); a consistent start writes nothing and returns the start tail; a moved tail or a missing carrier refuses before any write; REVERT, ADVANCE and INIT write only the witness and never the floor; QUARANTINE at start (witnessless restore, malformed witness, protocol violation) refuses and writes nothing; after an uncertain outcome the end step returns before its probe and any carrier, witness or floor read, charging no work and writing nothing, in REVERT, ADVANCE, INIT and witnessless-restore states alike; the end step copies the committed tail forward and never down, releases its probe, refuses regression and a lost floor without writing; a busy writer lease skips the copy; after an uncertain outcome the next writer reconciles (the floor step copying the committed tail and the start writing COMMITTED there on REVERT and ADVANCE, the floor step resuming INIT and the start writing COMMITTED 0, and a witnessless restore refused at the floor step writing nothing); and a floor bound to another namespace refuses at end.", "before": "Check X3b-1b on scratch installations: the floor step reports the tail the start must confirm (INIT, skipped, current); a consistent start writes nothing and returns the start tail; a moved tail or a missing carrier refuses before any write; REVERT, ADVANCE and INIT write only the witness and never the floor; QUARANTINE at start (witnessless restore, malformed witness, protocol violation) refuses and writes nothing; reconciliation after an uncertain outcome is copyable only on OK, REVERT or ADVANCE, writing the witness on REVERT, ADVANCE and INIT, with INIT left non-copyable so the end step copies nothing; the end step copies the committed tail forward and never down, releases its probe, refuses regression and a lost floor without writing; a busy writer lease skips the copy; after an uncertain outcome only the reconciled tail is copied and without one nothing runs; and a floor bound to another namespace refuses at end.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/678/description"}}, {"after": "Judge private-descendant ACL evidence and create private objects. Omission and the NOACL sentinel are unavailable, not an empty ACL; a present ACL must grant no group or other access, and mode must be exactly 0700 or 0600 for the invoking user. Charged helpers create one private file or directory, append the zero-rights owner allow of a fresh private creation when the sample omitted the ACL, observe a private directory, and spend an existing reservation for the creator's OpenSIP step. This grants no profile qualification, parent admission or creator authority.", "before": "Judge one already captured ACL sample for a private descendant. Omission and the NOACL sentinel are unavailable, not an empty ACL. A present ACL must grant no group or other access, including read and search, and mode must be exactly 0700 or 0600 for the invoking user. This predicate does not capture, qualify a profile, or create custody or creator authority.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/692/description"}}, {"after": "Private fail-stop freshness monitor under S6's 10-second OBSERVATION_BOUND; a caller-supplied counter reader remains a custody/authentication obligation. FreshnessMonitor, shared one per operation by law X4 r7 (unit X4a), brackets each monitored read with native clock samples (Sample: monotonic interval, boot id and wall clock) and latches the attempt's StopObserver on an unavailable or malformed clock, a boot change or regression, an unreadable counter, a stall beyond the bound or an unwind; read_sampled_with hands the counter the read's own opening sample (law X4B r5 item 1), and boundary_with is item 3 step 4's one-sample admission-boundary check with no I/O. StopReason::subject names OBSERVER.FAIL_STOP's subject (item 8). No polling thread, journal append, cancellation or operational authority is supplied here; the S6 trust-epoch predicate is root_payload's observe_revocation.", "before": "Observe trust epochs and enforce effect/SEAL checkpoints under the required journal ordering.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/693/description"}}, {"after": "Store-data custody for the per-project store directories and files (law X3c r6 items 1 and 2), under a supplied retained store directory and the caller's namespace writer lease, or, for read-only recovery's reads, its shared readers.lease (unit X6b). create_or_admit_store_directory applies 465 item 3: a charged exclusive 0700 create with the zero-rights owner allow, or, only on a raw EEXIST, a fresh no-follow admission of the existing entry judged private; then the exact name, the parent's filesystem, the directory's own barrier and its parent's barrier. A link or non-directory at the name is a kind refusal; an existing mode or ACL is never changed. create_store_file makes one private 0600 file exclusively after a no-follow absence sample, and open_store_file opens an existing regular file no-follow and judges it private. admit_existing_store_directory is read-only recovery's admission of an existing store directory (law X6 r3 item 4, unit X6b): nothing is created and no barrier is taken; the entry is opened no-follow if present, judged private, bound to its exact name and on its parent's filesystem, and a positively absent name is Ok(None), which the caller never reads as absence of history. Store data, not trust state; it selects no store, admits no namespace and grants nothing. Library only.", "before": "Store-data custody for the per-project store directories and files (law X3c r6 items 1 and 2), under a supplied retained store directory and the caller's namespace writer lease. create_or_admit_store_directory applies 465 item 3: a charged exclusive 0700 create with the zero-rights owner allow, or, only on a raw EEXIST, a fresh no-follow admission of the existing entry judged private; then the exact name, the parent's filesystem, the directory's own barrier and its parent's barrier. A link or non-directory at the name is a kind refusal; an existing mode or ACL is never changed. create_store_file makes one private 0600 file exclusively after a no-follow absence sample, and open_store_file opens an existing regular file no-follow and judges it private. Store data, not trust state; it selects no store, admits no namespace and grants nothing. Library only.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/702/description"}}, {"after": "Generate a signed accepted trust store for tests only (law X4T r4 item 13, X4T-0), declared solely under the shared fixture gates' one joint predicate any(test, feature = crash-matrix, feature = scenario-fixtures) (law X8 r3 item 4b, unit X9-1); its one accessor outside test code is root_payload's accepted_store_files, through which the shared installation fixture installs or publishes the store. It builds a real P0 publication with the product initial_publication producer, then one retained publication at revision 2. On macOS, wherever one acceptance reaches the whole requested six-role assignment (law X4B r5 item 9, unit X4B-c), that publication is X4B-a's producer's: Knobs::for_roles runs role_machine::bootstrap_sequence, the producer's own item 4 dispatch, over the store-wide root-expired, TR-INDEX catalog-expired and stale-list inputs with Revoked set per role (TR-PROFILE and TR-REPAIR uncarried; TR-BUNDLE, TR-CORE and TR-INDEX carried; TR-COMPONENT carried only with a component manifest); the module signs the payload with the public test-only quorum seeds (the root chain, the final root expired when required; the list, the caller's entries plus the third signer of each revoked role's document, issued more than 90 days before tEval when stale; a TR-INDEX catalog, expired when required, with the component manifest's release row when one is carried; 463h's component manifest under TR-COMPONENT unless that role is uncarried; a TR-BUNDLE bootstrap manifest; a synthetic TR-CORE inventory binding only the embedded bootstrap pair; and root 1's embedded binding), and every retained-phase record, the descriptor and state.v1 are build_over_p0's over that P0, placed where publish writes them (each file at its TrustWrite::path, state.v1 by name). A non-head chain, replaced signers, no reaching inputs (QuorumLost, an accepted TR-PROFILE or TR-REPAIR, an uncarried TR-BUNDLE, TR-CORE or TR-INDEX, a time state no store-wide input gives), a producer refusal or a role ending elsewhere (a caller's list entry revoking a role requested Trusted) leaves the module's own construction, as on every other target: a recorded root chain of n roots (default one; each later root rotates ROOT to the next public key set and is signed by both, and the head is root n), and a revocation list, catalog and payload manifest signed under the head with the public test-only quorum seeds; the payload metadata closure, a bootstrap operation, an S4 evaluation input as time evidence with a signed time source; ordinary root and catalog/revocation admission records and a list revocation history; an S4 clock-write event and, per role, an accepted EV-PRESENT-PAYLOAD role event plus one conditioning event for an expired, stale-revocation, quorum-lost or revoked target, with their role-change and publication-event rows; and the retained-phase capsule and descriptor. AcceptedStore::produced says which route built the store. Spec also chooses the store id S and the list's revocation version (X4a), and, for X4T-a3's negative cases, substitute root or list signers and a closure rootChain that ends on another envelope of the head, is short or is empty. Every record is canonically encoded and admitted by its closed shape before it is written (except the empty closure rootChain, written past its shape check, whose refusal is the shape's); files are content-addressed in the trust locators. No production path reaches it; X4B-a's trust_bootstrap is the product producer of these kinds.", "before": "Generate a signed accepted trust store for tests only (law X4T r4 item 13, X4T-0), declared solely under cfg(test). It builds a real P0 publication with the product initial_publication producer, then one retained publication at revision 2: a root, revocation, catalog and payload manifest signed with the public test-only quorum seeds; the payload metadata closure, a bootstrap operation, an S4 evaluation input as time evidence with a signed time source; ordinary root and catalog/revocation admission records and a list revocation history; an S4 clock-write event and, per role, an accepted EV-PRESENT-PAYLOAD role event plus one conditioning event for an expired, stale-revocation, quorum-lost or revoked target, with their role-change and publication-event rows; and the retained-phase capsule and descriptor. Every record is canonically encoded and admitted by its closed shape before it is written; files are content-addressed in the trust locators. It is the only constructor of these kinds and no production path reaches it; X4B owns the real producers.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/704/description"}}, {"after": "Check the signed accepted-store generator: every immutable file is canonical and addressed by its digest and each kind passes its closed shape; the root, catalog and revocation envelopes carry real ed25519 quorum signatures over their bodies by their owning role; current_record_bindings::bind, publication_events::bind_events (from P0's roles and event head) and bind_retained_head (through the capsule clock) accept the generated store for state schemas 1 and 2; a recorded rotation of two and three roots lists the whole chain with the head last, each link signed by both key sets and the list signed under root n, and the binders accept it; chosen role states publish with their accepted standing, INDEX-only catalog and revoked or quorum-lost evidence; capture_p2 accepts the written store through a supplied native fence on a scratch installation; a store with no accepted role is not offered; on macOS, ten requests one acceptance reaches (the default, no component manifest, an expired catalog, an expired root, a stale list, an expired catalog with a stale list, a revoked core, an expired root with component and core revoked, a revoked bundle on a two-root chain, and a successor list on another store at schema 2) are built by X4B's producer, each role's events exactly its item 4 sequence in order, accepted.by its EV-PRESENT-PAYLOAD, revokedBy its EV-REVOKE or null, uncarried roles blank and the binders accepting the store, with the default store's evalHighWater W and lastAccepted A (law X4B r5 items 9 and 10, unit X4B-c); ten requests no acceptance reaches (among them QuorumLost, all six roles trusted, a lone expired core, an uncarried index, the X4T-a3 negative stores and a list revoking the running core) keep the construction in their requested states; a source pin limits build_over_p0's callers to trust_bootstrap.rs and this generator; and a source pin shows the constructor is declared only under the shared fixture gates' one joint predicate any(test, feature = crash-matrix, feature = scenario-fixtures) (law X8 r3 item 4b, unit X9-1) and named by no other source file except X9-1's site pin crash_matrix_sites.rs, X4T-a's current_trust_admission_tests.rs and X4T-b's floor_publication_tests.rs, the two test files each checked to be included only under cfg(test).", "before": "Check the signed accepted-store generator: every immutable file is canonical and addressed by its digest and each kind passes its closed shape; the root, catalog and revocation envelopes carry real ed25519 quorum signatures over their bodies by their owning role; current_record_bindings::bind, publication_events::bind_events (from P0's roles and event head) and bind_retained_head (through the capsule clock) accept the generated store for state schemas 1 and 2; chosen role states publish with their accepted standing, INDEX-only catalog and revoked or quorum-lost evidence; capture_p2 accepts the written store through a supplied native fence on a scratch installation; a store with no accepted role is not offered; and a source pin shows the constructor is declared only under cfg(test) and named by no other source file.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/705/description"}}, {"after": "Validate retained core inventory syntax, paths, DAG, semantic versions and bootstrap members before anchor authentication. From the bound descriptor it also derives the release's closure2: identity and its core evaluator closure (contract successor EC1, law X3d r8 item 13, unit X3d-3): closure2: over the same descriptor with kind set to evaluator and every other member unchanged (evaluator_descriptor), which InitialCore lends for a Run's evaluator closure check; its tests reproduce EC1's vector byte for byte and check every accepted core-inventory318 case.", "before": "Validate retained core inventory syntax, paths, DAG, semantic versions and bootstrap members before anchor authentication.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/724/description"}}, {"after": "Admit the installation's current trust read-only (law X4T r5, unit X4T-a), as a child of the retained-head composition in ordinary_targets. A pre-acceptance capsule is refused as F absent (TRUST.NO_ADMITTED_TIME_CONTEXT) before any read. Otherwise it binds the capsule, descriptor and event chain with current_record_bindings::bind (capture_p2 on the native path), bounds the descriptor at 32 events, checks each accepted role's accepted.by as an accepted role event of that role, from the loaded chain or opened by its reference (one read, at most six, in this store and earlier than the current publication; history is never walked), takes the role machine's continuation standing before authentication, binds the root head with bind_retained_head, opens the root admission record, and, with heads.root as the signing root, takes the accepted root as the closure's first rootChain document (the chain must end on exactly heads.root.document) and authenticates through the existing retained-head shared authentication at a 16-link, 16 MiB chain budget. It opens the catalog and revocation heads (body, envelope, admission), history and timeEvidence itself with Budget::load, bounds the closure's stored bytes at 40 MiB outside the chain, checks the capsule's floors against the time evidence (trust-rollback), reverifies the catalog envelope and verifies the revocation list with verify_revocation over exactly those bytes, refuses an entry naming a closure component (release, signing keyId, namespace or catalog snapshot) on the continuation row, then requires the catalog's and the list's quorums with the list's keyIds excluded, merges the empty global policy (and a project policy when supplied) for Effective::digest(), evaluates S4 time on the fenced read only (report-only proposes no writes; a reread admits none). It returns AdmittedCurrentTrust (standing, S6 epoch, time outcome, revoked subjects, the capsule's floors, the closure components and admitted revocation evidence for X4's S6 predicate, and a fenced non-report read's S4 proposal for the write-ahead) or a refusal naming its law 468 item 6 row (TrustRow, including the host invariant row). The admission itself writes nothing. The file declares its macOS child modules floor_publication (X4T-b's write-ahead floor publication), live_observation (X4a's fenced operation read, retained handles and observation) and trust_bootstrap (X4B's first trust acceptance and its F-absent branch), and re-exports ConfirmedCurrent, fenced_operation_read, the live-observation types and BootstrapSource.", "before": "Admit the installation's current trust read-only (law X4T r5, unit X4T-a), as a child of the retained-head composition in ordinary_targets. A pre-acceptance capsule is refused as F absent (TRUST.NO_ADMITTED_TIME_CONTEXT) before any read. Otherwise it binds the capsule, descriptor and event chain with current_record_bindings::bind (capture_p2 on the native path), bounds the descriptor at 32 events, checks each accepted role's accepted.by against the loaded events of that role, binds the root head with bind_retained_head, opens the root admission record, and authenticates the accepted state from the accepted root through the existing retained-head shared authentication at a 16-link, 16 MiB chain budget. It opens the catalog and revocation heads (body, envelope, admission), history and timeEvidence itself with Budget::load, reverifies the catalog envelope and verifies the revocation list with verify_revocation over exactly those bytes, merges the empty global policy (and a project policy when supplied) for Effective::digest(), evaluates S4 time on the fenced read only (report-only proposes no writes; a reread admits none), and takes the role machine's continuation standing. It returns AdmittedCurrentTrust (standing, S6 epoch, time outcome, revoked subjects) or a refusal naming its law 468 item 6 row. It writes nothing; the write-ahead floor is X4T-b's and consumption is X4a's.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/728/description"}}, {"after": "Check the read-only current-trust admission over X4T-0's signed store: an accepted store is admitted for state schemas 1 and 2 with its epoch, empty-policy digest and install-gate standing; a creator-only capsule is F absent before any read; each continuation refusal names its role and state under CONTINUE-CORE-NOT-TRUSTED; an expired or stale index is admitted as existing-only; time is admitted only on the fenced read, report-only proposes no writes, and the payload-future, beyond-horizon and in-session refusals take their rows; the policy digest is equal on both reads; accepted.by must name a loaded event of that role; each member X4T-a opens is required; the measured view cost is pinned within TRUST_VIEW_COST; the event, chain and ledger bounds refuse on the budget row; a recorded rotation of one to three roots is admitted from its first root with heads.root as the signing root, at the final root's epoch and rootVersion floor on the fenced read and a reread; a closure rootChain ending on another envelope of the head or short is incomplete (FinalIdentity) and an empty one is refused by its shape; a first root below its own threshold, also once the list's keyIds are excluded, is not admissible; a failing link takes its S5 row (ROOT.CHAIN_OLD_THRESHOLD or ROOT.CHAIN_NEW_THRESHOLD) naming the link; a list signed under the signing root is admitted with its entries, and one signed only by root 1's keys is refused (law X4T r10 item 12 and, for the list, r11 item 4, unit X4T-a3); and the native read through a supplied fence admits the written store and refuses a replaced record.", "before": "Check the read-only current-trust admission over X4T-0's signed store: an accepted store is admitted for state schemas 1 and 2 with its epoch, empty-policy digest and install-gate standing; a creator-only capsule is F absent before any read; each continuation refusal names its role and state under CONTINUE-CORE-NOT-TRUSTED; an expired or stale index is admitted as existing-only; time is admitted only on the fenced read, report-only proposes no writes, and the payload-future, beyond-horizon and in-session refusals take their rows; the policy digest is equal on both reads; accepted.by must name a loaded event of that role; each member X4T-a opens is required; the measured view cost is pinned within TRUST_VIEW_COST; the event, chain and ledger bounds refuse on the budget row; and the native read through a supplied fence admits the written store and refuses a replaced record.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/729/description"}}, {"after": "Perform the fenced first read's S4 write-ahead floor publication (law X4T r9 items 7 and 8; unit X4T-b), as a child of the current-trust admission, under the installation fence with no project lock and before any lease. The retained trust current owner is the session's one read of trust/stores/S/state.v1 (its bytes, the capsule decoded from them and its full custody sample), with trust/stores/S bound by a metadata recheck, never a second content read; any change but a confirmed publication is required-files-changed. The read admits the owner's capsule through X4T-a, applies rollback check 3 against the floors of every evaluated or retained capsule this fence hold retained before (checks 1 and 2 run in every admission), and, unless report-only, publishes when F rises, L advances or the anchor changes: the before image, the S4EvaluationInputV2 from proposed_time_input's assembler, a host-trust-admission operation with its continue input for the running core, the clock-write event, the by-predecessor descriptor and the successor capsule with every other field unchanged, verified first by successor_record_bindings and current_record_bindings. The publication protocol, shared with X4B-a, reserves its writes and post-publication confirmation before the first effect (each exact reread at the platform's read_bounded_cost with the read session's attempt bound, so nothing is charged after the first effect), writes each dependency through the private-file producer with its file and directory barriers (admitting an existing record only when its bytes are equal), replaces state.v1 last through X3b's private file protocol, rereads and samples it, and advances the owner, minting ConfirmedCurrent for the gate's advance_current; the owner keeps every confirmed publication of its hold in order (confirmations, unit X4B-b). Nothing is retried or deleted; failures take the host I/O, budget, incomplete or custody rows. Beside it, publish_store_fenced is the shared fenced state.v1 publisher (law X9 r1 item 6, law X8 r3 items 4b and 5a; unit X9-1), declared only under the shared fixture gates' one joint predicate any(test, feature = crash-matrix, feature = scenario-fixtures) and with no production caller: under a held fence it binds the retained owner to the write gate's one read of state.v1, turns each generated file into its records, objects, events or publications write (refusing a path its locator does not give back exactly), and runs that protocol with state.v1 last, returning the ConfirmedCurrent; the shared installation fixture is its one caller. Library only.", "before": "Perform the fenced first read's S4 write-ahead floor publication (law X4T r9 items 7 and 8; unit X4T-b), as a child of the current-trust admission, under the installation fence with no project lock and before any lease. The retained trust current owner is the session's one read of trust/stores/S/state.v1 (its bytes, the capsule decoded from them and its full custody sample), with trust/stores/S bound by a metadata recheck, never a second content read; any change but a confirmed publication is required-files-changed. The read admits the owner's capsule through X4T-a, applies rollback check 3 against the floors of every evaluated or retained capsule this fence hold retained before (checks 1 and 2 run in every admission), and, unless report-only, publishes when F rises, L advances or the anchor changes: the before image, the S4EvaluationInputV2 from proposed_time_input's assembler, a host-trust-admission operation with its continue input for the running core, the clock-write event, the by-predecessor descriptor and the successor capsule with every other field unchanged, verified first by successor_record_bindings and current_record_bindings. The publication protocol, shared with X4B-a, reserves its writes and post-publication confirmation before the first effect (each exact reread at the platform's read_bounded_cost with the read session's attempt bound, so nothing is charged after the first effect), writes each dependency through the private-file producer with its file and directory barriers (admitting an existing record only when its bytes are equal), replaces state.v1 last through X3b's private file protocol, rereads and samples it, and advances the owner, minting ConfirmedCurrent for the gate's advance_current. Nothing is retried or deleted; failures take the host I/O, budget, incomplete or custody rows. Library only.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/736/description"}}, {"after": "Check X4T-a2 and X4T-b (law X4T r9 item 12) on ACL-scratch installations with X4T-0's signed store under a supplied fence: a floor advance is written ahead with F := tEval, the anchor rewritten and every other field, L and the time evidence unchanged, the descriptor in the predecessor's bucket, the pointer last and the retained owner advanced to the confirmed file; the next invocation's fenced read and the native reread admit the store with each accepted role's acceptance event opened by reference, at a pinned measured cost within TRUST_VIEW_COST; a floor publication on a store whose recorded chain rotates root 1 to root 2 keeps the heads and is followed by an admitted fenced read and native reread at root 2's epoch and rootVersion floor (law X4T r10 item 12, unit X4T-a3); accepted.by naming another role's event, a missing event, another store, a non-role event or the current chain's clock-write refuses as incomplete; a confirming admission on the same clock sample writes nothing; report-only and a beyond-horizon refusal write nothing; an in-place rewrite of state.v1 is required-files-changed; check 3 refuses below this hold's floors and an unevaluated predecessor is never compared; checks 1 and 2 over retained, evaluated and unevaluated before-clocks, the evaluation wall and an S4.5 epoch input; a whole-file restore of an older self-consistent state.v1 is admitted, pinning the stated limit; the protocol admits an equal content-addressed record and refuses a foreign one before the pointer; at each reread growth boundary up to the 131072-byte parser cap (16384, 20480, 32768, 53248, 65536, 118784, 131072) a publication beside an equal record of that length completes on an exactly sufficient ledger and refuses before any effect with one byte less; a short ledger writes nothing; and a reread mode is not a fenced first read. Compiled only for tests; confers no qualification.", "before": "Check X4T-a2 and X4T-b (law X4T r9 item 12) on ACL-scratch installations with X4T-0's signed store under a supplied fence: a floor advance is written ahead with F := tEval, the anchor rewritten and every other field, L and the time evidence unchanged, the descriptor in the predecessor's bucket, the pointer last and the retained owner advanced to the confirmed file; the next invocation's fenced read and the native reread admit the store with each accepted role's acceptance event opened by reference, at a pinned measured cost within TRUST_VIEW_COST; accepted.by naming another role's event, a missing event, another store, a non-role event or the current chain's clock-write refuses as incomplete; a confirming admission on the same clock sample writes nothing; report-only and a beyond-horizon refusal write nothing; an in-place rewrite of state.v1 is required-files-changed; check 3 refuses below this hold's floors and an unevaluated predecessor is never compared; checks 1 and 2 over retained, evaluated and unevaluated before-clocks, the evaluation wall and an S4.5 epoch input; a whole-file restore of an older self-consistent state.v1 is admitted, pinning the stated limit; the protocol admits an equal content-addressed record and refuses a foreign one before the pointer; at each reread growth boundary up to the 131072-byte parser cap (16384, 20480, 32768, 53248, 65536, 118784, 131072) a publication beside an equal record of that length completes on an exactly sufficient ledger and refuses before any effect with one byte less; a short ledger writes nothing; and a reread mode is not a fenced first read. Compiled only for tests; confers no qualification.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/737/description"}}, {"after": "Produce the attempt-bound InitialCore receipt of owner \u00a71a step 3 (law 463 r3\u2013r9). Join the kernel-validated running image to the entrypoint row of the embedded, authenticated CoreInventoryV3 through fixed no-follow opens under core-tree custody, an in-memory anchor store charged to the attempt's one ledger, the release's own revocation, the required code-signing flags and K from stateWriter. No listing, latest selection, record from I, or caller revoked set. For first trust acceptance (law X4B r5 item 2) it retains, as raw bytes with their references and unverified, the catalog pair (only when an envelope's stored digest admitted it), the component-manifest pairs in members.manifests order and the revocation pair (bootstrap_documents), and lends the anchor's embedded root binding, the bootstrap manifest and core inventory pairs and the root chain's pairs in chain order (bootstrap_release); it grants nothing for them. It also lends evaluator_closure, the core evaluator closure of contract successor EC1 (law X3d r8 item 13, unit X3d-3), from the same authenticated inventory as its closure on the running and the injected arm alike, which the write receipt, its operation and the commit session pass on as a value that a replayed Run's evaluator closure must equal (item 3 step 1).", "before": "Produce the attempt-bound InitialCore receipt of owner \u00a71a step 3 (law 463 r3\u2013r8). Join the kernel-validated running image to the entrypoint row of the embedded, authenticated CoreInventoryV3 through fixed no-follow opens under core-tree custody, an in-memory anchor store charged to the attempt's one ledger, the release's own revocation, the required code-signing flags and K from stateWriter. No listing, latest selection, record from I, or caller revoked set.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/740/description"}}, {"after": "Build a signed on-disk core tree with the public test-only quorum62 seeds and explicit ACLs, and check InitialCore end to end with an injected running-image observation and every refusal. It also holds InitialCore::evaluator_closure_preimages (law X3d r8 item 13, unit X3d-3), the read behind the shared test-support accessor for the synthetic run candidate: the core evaluator closure's descriptor (contract successor EC1), the TR-CORE-signed inventory body and each tree member's bytes by logical path, read through the core's retained tree root as a release file is judged and checked against the inventory's digest and length. Compiled for tests and, as shared fixture gate 1's module defining the injected image and the signed test tree, under the shared fixture gates' one joint predicate any(test, feature = crash-matrix, feature = scenario-fixtures) (law X8 r3 item 4b, unit X9-1), so absent from every release build; confers no release qualification.", "before": "Build a signed on-disk core tree with the public test-only quorum62 seeds and explicit ACLs, and check InitialCore end to end with an injected running-image observation and every refusal. Compiled only for tests; confers no release qualification.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/741/description"}}, {"after": "Sign SYNTHETIC profile sets at run time with the public test-only quorum62 seeds, including a V2 measured row built from this host's observed identity, and check InitialPlatform end to end over the signed on-disk core tree: both authentication paths, the tier, Evidence B and every refusal. It also provides the receipt producers: receipts_at (InitialCore and InitialPlatform on one attempt, for law 465's tests) and read_producers (for law 458c's read composition: InitialCore through the injected image of a signed test tree, and InitialPlatform with H at a chosen home, under PM with the premise or the BASELINE-ATTESTED P469 without one), which the crash-matrix and scenario support surfaces also hold. Compiled for tests and, as part of shared fixture gate 1 (the synthetic V2 profile set and its receipt producers), under the shared fixture gates' one joint predicate any(test, feature = crash-matrix, feature = scenario-fixtures) (law X8 r3 item 4b, unit X9-1), with F6's churn retry in the test build only; checks in no measured row and confers no qualification.", "before": "Sign SYNTHETIC profile sets at run time with the public test-only quorum62 seeds, including a V2 measured row built from this host's observed identity, and check InitialPlatform end to end over the signed on-disk core tree: both authentication paths, the tier, Evidence B and every refusal. Compiled only for tests; checks in no measured row and confers no qualification.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/743/description"}}, {"after": "The operation's trust side (law X4 r7 items 2, 4 and 5 over law X4T r9 items 7 to 9; unit X4a; with law X4B r5 item 1, unit X4B-b), as a macOS child of the current-trust admission. fenced_operation_read is the lease-free point's fenced first read: X4T-b's retained state.v1 owner bound to the write gate's one read (its bytes and sample, no new content read), X4T-b's fenced_first_read with its write-ahead and the S4 observation the caller projects from the monitor's opening sample, and on F absent only, X4B-b's acceptance from the write receipt's BootstrapSource and the one confirming admission on the same observation (trust_bootstrap's bootstrapping_first_read), returning the view and every confirmed publication in order for the gate to follow one by one with advance_current; TrustInvocation is the floor publication's invocation identity (req1_ and exec1_ with 32 lowercase hex, a step id). RetainedTrustHandles retains trust/stores/S, its events, and objects, records and publications, each bound no-follow by exact name to its retained parent and judged private and on H's filesystem under the fence. LiveObservation is one unfenced observation through those handles only, on a fresh per-observation ledger of two views at TRUST_VIEW_COST (256 objects, 4096 edges, 224 MiB): state.v1 opened by name through the store handle, X4T-a's reread over the closure with every file opened by name through its collection's retained handle (the native readers' own locator), state.v1 reopened and its identity and full sample compared, and all of it once more if they differ; a second change is mixed. A reread refusal naming a revoked closure component is the revoking observation; any other refusal, an unreadable record, a budget overrun or a view below the start view's floors or counters (never reaching the predicate) is a fail-stop with its reason. Then S6's observe_revocation runs against the immutable start epoch and closure subjects (X4T item 4's components, from the start view), with empty required permission pairs. The observation never takes the fence, writes nothing and grants nothing. Library only.", "before": "The operation's trust side (law X4 r7 items 2, 4 and 5 over law X4T r9 items 7 to 9; unit X4a), as a macOS child of the current-trust admission. fenced_operation_read is the lease-free point's fenced first read: X4T-b's retained state.v1 owner bound to the write gate's one read (its bytes and sample, no new content read), X4T-b's fenced_first_read with its write-ahead and the S4 observation the caller projects from the monitor's opening sample, returning the view and any confirmed publication for the gate's advance_current; TrustInvocation is the floor publication's invocation identity (req1_ and exec1_ with 32 lowercase hex, a step id). RetainedTrustHandles retains trust/stores/S, its events, and objects, records and publications, each bound no-follow by exact name to its retained parent and judged private and on H's filesystem under the fence. LiveObservation is one unfenced observation through those handles only, on a fresh per-observation ledger of two views at TRUST_VIEW_COST (256 objects, 4096 edges, 224 MiB): state.v1 opened by name through the store handle, X4T-a's reread over the closure with every file opened by name through its collection's retained handle (the native readers' own locator), state.v1 reopened and its identity and full sample compared, and all of it once more if they differ; a second change is mixed. A reread refusal naming a revoked closure component is the revoking observation; any other refusal, an unreadable record, a budget overrun or a view below the start view's floors or counters (never reaching the predicate) is a fail-stop with its reason. Then S6's observe_revocation runs against the immutable start epoch and closure subjects (X4T item 4's components, from the start view), with empty required permission pairs. It never takes the fence, writes nothing and grants nothing. Library only.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/745/description"}}, {"after": "Capture the complete structural successor census at fixed installation-relative locations under a borrowed held-fence view and shared budget.", "before": "Capture the complete structural successor census at fixed installation-relative locations under the retained native fence and shared budget.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/749/description"}}, {"after": "Capture the original current capsule file, compare its independently parsed store claim, and retain current/dependency observations under one budget. It also owns the trust current record's own read bound, CURRENT_STATE_CAP, and decoder, decode_current_store, which returns C.store's (S, G, K) for the write gate's and the observation session's single read (law X3a r3 item 2), and parts_mut hands the capsule, its descriptor, the bindings and the capture's native store to the read-only current trust admission (law X4T r5 item 2).", "before": "Capture the original current capsule file, compare its independently parsed store claim, and retain current/dependency observations under one budget.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/750/description"}}, {"after": "Own all original current and successor observations of one borrowed InstallationReadFence under one cumulative failure-latching budget charged to that read session's one ledger (law 458c item 6); lend the trust captures its held-fence view, run the read session's full recheck after each capture, and recheck every consumer without exposing reset or raw handles.", "before": "Own all original current and successor observations under one cumulative failure-latching budget and borrowed native fence; recheck every consumer without exposing reset or raw handles.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/753/description"}}, {"after": "Resolve full typed record locations under a borrowed held-fence view, retain every prefix/file and preserve native failure diagnostics across logical decoding.", "before": "Resolve full typed record locations under the retained native fence, retain every prefix/file and preserve native failure diagnostics across logical decoding.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/754/description"}}, {"after": "Produce first trust acceptance from the running core's embedded bootstrap payload (law X4B r5 items 2 to 6, unit X4B-a; item 1, unit X4B-b), as a macOS child of the current-trust admission, under the held installation fence on the retained P0 state.v1 owner. The payload is exactly what InitialCore retains (law 463 r9): the root chain, the revocation pair, the bootstrap manifest (TR-BUNDLE), the core inventory (TR-CORE), the catalog pair (TR-INDEX) and the component-manifest pairs (TR-COMPONENT). Every signature is verified again: the chain from the embedded root binding within ChainBudget{16 links, 16 MiB}, the list under the final root, re-filtering every link, the index-0 root and its own quorum; each document's envelope at its role's threshold with the list's keyIds excluded. A bootstrap without a signed catalog, or any document that fails, refuses PAYLOAD-NOT-ADMISSIBLE before any write. S4 step 2 runs on the caller's one clock sample (F := max(W, A), L := A, the anchor, expiry from the presented documents). Each carried role's sequence goes through role_machine::decide: PresentOrdinary, then Clock when expired or stale, then Revoke when the payload's own list names its signer, namespace, release or catalog snapshot. Every record (objects, payload closure, operation, time evidence with the payload's signed time source, root and metadata admissions, revocation history, clock-write and role events with their role changes, the revision-2 descriptor and the retained capsule) is canonically encoded, admitted by its closed shape and bound by current_record_bindings, successor_record_bindings and publication_events before floor_publication's shared protocol publishes it, state.v1 last, advancing the retained owner from the confirming reread. That record builder is build_over_p0, over a P0 capsule and its exact bytes (build_acceptance passes the retained owner's); it writes and grants nothing, and its only other caller is X4T-0's test-only store generator, which places its files where publish writes them (law X4B r5 item 9, unit X4B-c). bootstrapping_first_read is the body of the operation monitor's single first read at the lease-free point: X4T-b's fenced_first_read on the retained owner, then, only on F absent (TRUST.NO_ADMITTED_TIME_CONTEXT) and only for a fenced read that is not report-only, acceptance of the payload from BootstrapSource (the write receipt's running core, copied and charged only on that branch) on the read's own clock sample under the same fence hold, and the one confirming admission on the confirmed state.v1, whose view is returned. An acceptance refusal ends the read, and a second F absent is the host invariant row (TrustRow::Invariant); the order lives in first_read_sequence. It grants nothing: the confirming admission is X4T's own.", "before": "Produce first trust acceptance from the running core's embedded bootstrap payload (law X4B r5 items 2 to 6; unit X4B-a), as a macOS child of the current-trust admission, under the held installation fence on the retained P0 state.v1 owner. The payload is exactly what InitialCore retains (law 463 r9): the root chain, the revocation pair, the bootstrap manifest (TR-BUNDLE), the core inventory (TR-CORE), the catalog pair (TR-INDEX) and the component-manifest pairs (TR-COMPONENT). Every signature is verified again: the chain from the embedded root binding within ChainBudget{16 links, 16 MiB}, the list under the final root, re-filtering every link, the index-0 root and its own quorum; each document's envelope at its role's threshold with the list's keyIds excluded. A bootstrap without a signed catalog, or any document that fails, refuses PAYLOAD-NOT-ADMISSIBLE before any write. S4 step 2 runs on the caller's one clock sample (F := max(W, A), L := A, the anchor, expiry from the presented documents). Each carried role's sequence goes through role_machine::decide: PresentOrdinary, then Clock when expired or stale, then Revoke when the payload's own list names its signer, namespace, release or catalog snapshot. Every record (objects, payload closure, operation, time evidence with the payload's signed time source, root and metadata admissions, revocation history, clock-write and role events with their role changes, the revision-2 descriptor and the retained capsule) is canonically encoded, admitted by its closed shape and bound by current_record_bindings, successor_record_bindings and publication_events before floor_publication's shared protocol publishes it, state.v1 last, advancing the retained owner from the confirming reread. Not wired to the fenced first read (X4B-b); grants nothing.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/790/description"}}, {"after": "Check X4B-a and X4B-b (law X4B r5 item 10) on ACL-scratch installations holding 467's P0 publication under a supplied fence, with test-signed releases (public quorum62 seeds) produced into an InitialCore through 463's injected-image constructor: P0 is accepted with TR-BUNDLE, TR-COMPONENT, TR-CORE and TR-INDEX trusted from one EV-PRESENT-PAYLOAD each and the one confirming admission admits the store without writing; X4T-a's native reread and every record's canonical content address accept it; the fresh-install time rules (tEval = max(W, A), payload-future, beyond-horizon and its boundary) write nothing on refusal; an expired catalog, an expired root, a stale list, a revoked signer whose quorum survives and expired-and-revoked give their event sequences, accepted.by and revokedBy, and X4T's continuation row or ExistingOnly; inactive and rejected entries record no event; a revoked counted signer that leaves a catalog or component manifest below threshold writes nothing; a missing catalog or a document signed outside its role writes nothing; no component manifest leaves TR-COMPONENT unbootstrapped; a crash before the pointer and a rerun; a changed state.v1; a short ledger; a non-P0 predecessor and a foreign root binding; and a two-root chain recorded in full, which the confirming admission admits at root 2's epoch and floor (X4T-a3). Through the first read (X4B-b): F absent is accepted inside it and the confirming view returned with exactly one confirmed publication and F, L and the anchor from the read's own sample, and a later first read enters no branch and writes nothing; a report-only or reread mode never bootstraps; a missing catalog or a beyond-horizon sample ends the read with nothing written and no confirming admission, and a missing component manifest leaves one publication that the confirming admission refuses; a first refusal other than F absent runs no acceptance; the stub sequence accepts once, admits at most twice and makes a second F absent the host invariant row; and source pins show only the write receipt lends a BootstrapSource, only the lease-free read reaches bootstrapping_first_read, and the read side names neither entry point.", "before": "Check X4B-a (law X4B r5 item 10) on ACL-scratch installations holding 467's P0 publication under a supplied fence, with test-signed releases (public quorum62 seeds) produced into an InitialCore through 463's injected-image constructor: P0 is accepted with TR-BUNDLE, TR-COMPONENT, TR-CORE and TR-INDEX trusted from one EV-PRESENT-PAYLOAD each and the one confirming admission admits the store without writing; X4T-a's native reread and every record's canonical content address accept it; the fresh-install time rules (tEval = max(W, A), payload-future, beyond-horizon and its boundary) write nothing on refusal; an expired catalog, an expired root, a stale list, a revoked signer whose quorum survives and expired-and-revoked give their event sequences, accepted.by and revokedBy, and X4T's continuation row or ExistingOnly; inactive and rejected entries record no event; a revoked counted signer that leaves a catalog or component manifest below threshold writes nothing; a missing catalog or a document signed outside its role writes nothing; no component manifest leaves TR-COMPONENT unbootstrapped; a crash before the pointer and a rerun; a changed state.v1; a short ledger; a non-P0 predecessor and a foreign root binding; and a two-root chain recorded in full, with the reader's current FirstIdentity refusal pinned as a stated gap.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/791/description"}}, {"after": "Assemble command-scoped host services lazily; help/version must not open a project, store or component session or access the network. For version metadata, read the build-embedded metadata covered by the enclosing signed host/release artifact and its exact declared closure IDs without opening a component/project/store session; do not claim these are currently loaded closures.", "before": "Assemble command-scoped host services lazily; help/version must not open a project, store or component session or access the network. For version metadata, read the build-embedded signed release descriptor and its exact declared closure IDs without opening a component/project/store session; do not claim these are currently loaded closures.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/8/description"}}, {"after": "Check X3d-2's storage half, included as commit.rs's cfg(test) module, on a scratch private I/stores/S under the per-process scratch parent, with binding values from a real session, never from the Run under test (law X3d r8 item 13, unit X3d-3): bound() takes the project and the core evaluator closure from a CommitSession opened once per process over X8b's scenario-fixtures ProjectOperation on a synthetic installation under the scratch parent, and the evaluator's real replay is of the synthetic run candidate built for that session (crash_matrix_support/run_candidate.rs, included by path): the plan declares exactly the retained typed objects (each frame its identity's H preimage, published under its H digest) and raw blobs, once each, and the commit inventory lists them; a Run of another project or another evaluator closure does not bind; the pinned corpus Run that security's SEAL tests replay, rebound to the session's project, is refused on its evaluator closure, and through prepare_commit on a real session on the invariant row with no namespace directory in the store and the end path's REV; the session's core closure is not its core evaluator closure, and a candidate renamed to name the core closure does not replay while one renamed to another evaluator closure does; the store generation digest is store-instance-lineage v1's recipe; a prepared commit publishes every frame and blob, stages the receipt (sequence 1, replayable, the unsigned local-custody signer), the thirteen-field association, the Run material, generation-0 retained availability and no pins with nothing visible to another connection until its one COMMIT, after which the pair joins the admitted attempt with settlement pending; the receipt counter orders stored canonical decimals by length then bytes (2, 9, 10, 100 give 101) and refuses a full u64 on the invariant row; a SEAL binding whose RunId, carrier digest or operationRef does not join stages nothing; an error from the evidence COMMIT is undetermined with the attempt still admitted; prepare_commit and publish compose end to end on a real session, the first real commit: Committed at sequence 1 and not latched, the end path appending no REV or CLN, the carrier holding one SEAL, and the store holding exactly the receipt, the attempt row still admitted and every retained frame and blob, the core evaluator closure's inventory body and tree included; the publication reserve equals exactly what attempt admission and the objects charge, and one byte short refuses on the budget row before the attempt row and any object; a reused ExecutionId is refused before any object; every ledger row maps to its existing termination; and x3d.publish.published is placed once, after the one PublishedCommit construction. Its child modules recover (recover_tests.rs, unit X6b) and settlement_sweep (sweep_tests.rs, unit X6c) run recovery's recover_from and the settlement sweep's sweep_from over the stores these steps commit.", "before": "Check X3d-2's storage half without a session (storage cannot build a CommitSession: security's fixtures are cfg(test) there and law X8 r3 item 4's scenario-fixtures seam is X8b's), on a scratch private I/stores/S under the per-process scratch parent, with the evaluator's real replay of the corpus Run that security's SEAL tests replay, included as commit.rs's cfg(test) module: the plan declares exactly the retained typed objects (each frame its identity's H preimage, published under its H digest) and raw blobs, once each, and the commit inventory lists them; a Run of another project or another evaluator closure does not bind; the store generation digest is store-instance-lineage v1's recipe; a prepared commit publishes every frame and blob, stages the receipt (sequence 1, replayable, the unsigned local-custody signer), the thirteen-field association, the Run material, generation-0 retained availability and no pins with nothing visible to another connection until its one COMMIT, after which the pair joins the admitted attempt with settlement pending; the receipt counter orders stored canonical decimals by length then bytes (2, 9, 10, 100 give 101) and refuses a full u64 on the invariant row; a SEAL binding whose RunId, carrier digest or operationRef does not join stages nothing; an error from the evidence COMMIT is undetermined with the attempt still admitted; the publication reserve equals exactly what attempt admission and the objects charge, and one byte short refuses on the budget row before the attempt row and any object; a reused ExecutionId is refused before any object; every ledger row maps to its existing termination; and x3d.publish.published is placed once, after the one PublishedCommit construction.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/951/description"}}, {"after": "Storage's crash-matrix support surface (law X9 r1 item 6, unit X9-1), doc(hidden). Compiled only under the test-only crash-matrix feature, which no manifest enables from a dependency table and which the crate refuses in any build without debug assertions (law X9 r1 item 2), so it is absent from every release build. It forwards security's surface unchanged, law X9 r4 item 6's three driver entries included; the shared fixture gates live in security, so storage adds no second site. Its one item of its own is the child module run_candidate, the synthetic run candidate, which needs the evaluator (law X3d r8 item 13, unit X3d-3; law X9 r7 item 6), re-exported with its label, its replay limits and the distinct variant (law X9 r12, unit X9-3); it reaches security only through CommitSession's public getters and the shared test-support accessor, and returns inputs only, never a ReplayedRun. Apart from the forwarded driver entries, nothing here returns an authority type.", "before": "Storage's crash-matrix support surface (law X9 r1 item 6, unit X9-1), doc(hidden). Compiled only under the test-only crash-matrix feature, which no manifest enables from a dependency table and which the crate refuses in any build without debug assertions (law X9 r1 item 2), so it is absent from every release build. It forwards security's surface unchanged; the shared fixture gates live in security, so storage adds no second site. Nothing here returns an authority type.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/952/description"}}, {"after": "The synthetic run candidate (law X3d r8 item 13, unit X3d-3; law X9 r7 item 6), a child of storage's crash-matrix support surface, doc(hidden), and included as the same source file by storage's unit tests (commit_tests.rs) and, on macOS, by host's admission_tests.rs for unit X8c's behavioural refusal cases and by host's commit_matrix_tests.rs (under crash-matrix) for unit X9-5's F01 substituted variants. As part of the support surface it is compiled only under the test-only crash-matrix feature, which no manifest enables from a dependency table and which the crate refuses in any build without debug assertions (law X9 r1 item 2), so it is absent from every release build. Inputs only: from a live CommitSession it starts from the pinned corpus Run security's SEAL tests replay (journal-seal-cases.json, packet-0-original), rewrites the snapshot's projectId to CommitSession::project_id and the evaluator closure to CommitSession::core_evaluator_closure (contract successor EC1), never core_closure, retaining that closure's descriptor with its manifest blob (the TR-CORE inventory body) and its tree blobs from security's shared test-support accessor, recomputes every dependent input identity and blob digest by a reference rewrite to a fixpoint (arrays kept in canonical order), re-derives the outputs with the evaluator's public derive_evaluation, and builds the semantic evidence, seal and Run as replay_run checks them. It returns retained objects, blobs and the claimed RunId with the synthetic label, never a ReplayedRun: replay_run stays the only mint. synthetic_run_candidate_distinct (law X9 r12, unit X9-3) builds the same candidate for the same session, except that the snapshot's source file extensionless gets same-length salted bytes before the fixpoint rewrite, so every dependent id and digest, the re-derived outputs and the RunId differ, for a next writer whose project already committed the candidate's Run (rows F13, F14 and F15). Test-only helpers (cfg(test)) rebind only the project, and rename the evaluator closure to a given descriptor, for the mismatch and core-closure tests. Synthetic; no compiler qualification.", "before": "The synthetic run candidate (law X3d r8 item 13, unit X3d-3; law X9 r7 item 6), a child of storage's crash-matrix support surface, doc(hidden), and included by storage's unit tests (commit_tests.rs) as the same source file. As part of the support surface it is compiled only under the test-only crash-matrix feature, which no manifest enables from a dependency table and which the crate refuses in any build without debug assertions (law X9 r1 item 2), so it is absent from every release build. Inputs only: from a live CommitSession it starts from the pinned corpus Run security's SEAL tests replay (journal-seal-cases.json, packet-0-original), rewrites the snapshot's projectId to CommitSession::project_id and the evaluator closure to CommitSession::core_evaluator_closure (contract successor EC1), never core_closure, retaining that closure's descriptor with its manifest blob (the TR-CORE inventory body) and its tree blobs from security's shared test-support accessor, recomputes every dependent input identity and blob digest by a reference rewrite to a fixpoint (arrays kept in canonical order), re-derives the outputs with the evaluator's public derive_evaluation, and builds the semantic evidence, seal and Run as replay_run checks them. It returns retained objects, blobs and the claimed RunId with the synthetic label, never a ReplayedRun: replay_run stays the only mint. Test-only helpers (cfg(test)) rebind only the project, and rename the evaluator closure to a given descriptor, for the mismatch and core-closure tests. Synthetic; no compiler qualification.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/953/description"}}, {"after": "Publish one commit's objects and prepare, stage and commit its evidence-ledger transaction (law X3c r7 items 4 to 7 and 9 to 11; unit X3c-2), as project_ledger.rs's child module commit. Objects: publication takes the durably admitted attempt; every declared object's length and SHA-256 are checked, and every byte reserved on the operation ledger, before the first write, so a commit that cannot be reserved refuses on the budget row and is never truncated; each object is published into objects/sha256 through blob_store (private temporary file, file barrier, exclusive link, directory barrier) or, only after an exclusive-link collision with unchanged visibility, confirmed byte for byte with its own barriers; an unequal, short or long file, a second link, a shared-writable mode or a non-regular entry at the name is LEDGER.CORRUPT and never overwritten; a failure stops publication and nothing is deleted, renamed or reused. PreparedLedger: BEGIN IMMEDIATE with busy_timeout 0 on the admitted, whole-schema-verified ledger, taken only with the published set (so after every barrier); busy is LEDGER.BUSY_TIMEOUT and holds nothing; owned, single use, not Clone, no SQL surface, and Drop only rolls back; its next_commit_sequence allocates the next receipt counter of (store, N) in that open transaction through the ledger's one counter accessor, charged as one indexed read, before the receipt that carries it is built (unit X3d-2). Staging in the open transaction, charged with the COMMIT before the first insert: the exact receipt and association through stage_recovery_pair, the Run material, whose inventory's blob digests together with its typed objects' H digests must equal the published objects exactly, a blob equal to a frame counting once (unit X3d-2), the initial availability record, and the Run's pins as a first publication under the attempt's operationRef; any failure drops the transaction, so all rows or none. The owned PreparedLedgerCommit adapter's one consuming method performs only COMMIT: success is Committed, an error or lost connection is Undetermined (DURABILITY.COMMIT_FAILED with the ExecutionId retained), never retried or read back, and the attempt row is never settled. X3d holds the journal transaction, level 4 and the AdmissionPermit around these calls. X3c-2's object crash points and COMMIT fault hook exist only under cfg(test); law X9's barrier points here (an x3c.object scope around each object's publication, and x3c.evidence's begin, one point after each of the four stagings, and a fallible COMMIT whose injected fail-before or fail-after is the undetermined outcome) have a body only under the test-only crash-matrix feature (law X9 r1 item 5, unit X9-1). attempt_and_objects_cost is exactly what attempt admission (the verified open and the attempt transaction) and the objects' publication charge, for the facade's one publication reserve (law X3d r6 item 3 step 2, unit X3d-2), and h_digest gives a typed object identity's H digest, the raw SHA-256 of its H preimage frame, which the facade's plan and recovery's object reads also use. The storage facade (commit.rs, unit X3d-2) composes these calls, which project_ledger.rs re-exports to it, on the production location ProjectStoreLocation::selected.", "before": "Publish one commit's objects and prepare, stage and commit its evidence-ledger transaction (law X3c r7 items 4 to 7 and 9 to 11; unit X3c-2), as project_ledger.rs's child module commit. Objects: publication takes the durably admitted attempt; every declared object's length and SHA-256 are checked, and every byte reserved on the operation ledger, before the first write, so a commit that cannot be reserved refuses on the budget row and is never truncated; each object is published into objects/sha256 through blob_store (private temporary file, file barrier, exclusive link, directory barrier) or, only after an exclusive-link collision with unchanged visibility, confirmed byte for byte with its own barriers; an unequal, short or long file, a second link, a shared-writable mode or a non-regular entry at the name is LEDGER.CORRUPT and never overwritten; a failure stops publication and nothing is deleted, renamed or reused. PreparedLedger: BEGIN IMMEDIATE with busy_timeout 0 on the admitted, whole-schema-verified ledger, taken only with the published set (so after every barrier); busy is LEDGER.BUSY_TIMEOUT and holds nothing; owned, single use, not Clone, no SQL surface, and Drop only rolls back. Staging in the open transaction, charged with the COMMIT before the first insert: the exact receipt and association through stage_recovery_pair, the Run material whose blob digests must equal the published objects exactly, the initial availability record, and the Run's pins as a first publication under the attempt's operationRef; any failure drops the transaction, so all rows or none. The owned PreparedLedgerCommit adapter's one consuming method performs only COMMIT: success is Committed, an error or lost connection is Undetermined (DURABILITY.COMMIT_FAILED with the ExecutionId retained), never retried or read back, and the attempt row is never settled. X3d holds the journal transaction, level 4 and the AdmissionPermit around these calls. Crash points and a COMMIT fault hook exist only under cfg(test). Only tests construct a location.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/959/description"}}, {"after": "Check X3c-2 on X3c-1's scratch location with a held test writer lease: objects published new (0600, one link, both barriers, no residue) then confirmed byte for byte by a second attempt without rewriting; a wrong length or digest or a repeated digest refused as the invariant row before any write; unequal, short and long bytes, a directory, a link, a shared-writable file and a second hard link at an object name refused as LEDGER.CORRUPT and left unchanged, with earlier objects kept; a commit short of budget refused before the first object; a crash at each object step (temporary write, before the file barrier, after the link, and before the directory barrier with the link lost) keeping earlier objects and the residue, the attempt row admitted and nothing staged, with the next attempt completing; a busy ledger refused at once holding nothing, and a held PreparedLedger busy to other writers; another attempt's objects refused; a staged commit invisible until COMMIT, then every row present and joined as recovery reads it with the attempt still admitted; a failure at each stager (pair collision, blob set, availability Run and generation, pin Run, a reused pin operation) and an operation mismatch committing nothing; a dropped ledger or adapter committing nothing; an injected COMMIT error classified undetermined with nothing committed or retried; staging short of budget; the item 10 rows; and a source pin that the module deletes, settles, waits on and reads back nothing. Under the test-only crash-matrix feature on macOS it declares the child module census (project_commit_census.rs): the census of storage's ledger path and verified kills at its points, in matrix children (law X9 r1 items 5 and 12, unit X9-1).", "before": "Check X3c-2 on X3c-1's scratch location with a held test writer lease: objects published new (0600, one link, both barriers, no residue) then confirmed byte for byte by a second attempt without rewriting; a wrong length or digest or a repeated digest refused as the invariant row before any write; unequal, short and long bytes, a directory, a link, a shared-writable file and a second hard link at an object name refused as LEDGER.CORRUPT and left unchanged, with earlier objects kept; a commit short of budget refused before the first object; a crash at each object step (temporary write, before the file barrier, after the link, and before the directory barrier with the link lost) keeping earlier objects and the residue, the attempt row admitted and nothing staged, with the next attempt completing; a busy ledger refused at once holding nothing, and a held PreparedLedger busy to other writers; another attempt's objects refused; a staged commit invisible until COMMIT, then every row present and joined as recovery reads it with the attempt still admitted; a failure at each stager (pair collision, blob set, availability Run and generation, pin Run, a reused pin operation) and an operation mismatch committing nothing; a dropped ledger or adapter committing nothing; an injected COMMIT error classified undetermined with nothing committed or retried; staging short of budget; the item 10 rows; and a source pin that the module deletes, settles, waits on and reads back nothing.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/961/description"}}, {"after": "Place, create and open the per-(S, N) evidence ledger and admit the attempt (law X3c r6 items 1 to 3, 9 and 10; unit X3c-1). Owner201's spellings: I/stores/S/projects/N/ledger.sqlite with its -wal and -shm, and objects under projects/N/objects/sha256. projects/, projects/N/, objects/ and objects/sha256/ are created or admitted through security's store custody. A new ledger is created exclusively, WAL is selected, then one BEGIN IMMEDIATE runs the selected DDL fragments in their pinned order and COMMITs under synchronous=FULL and fullfsync, the namespace directory is barriered, and the file is reopened through open_existing with a whole-schema equality against the same DDL. An empty file with no -wal is the only resumable creation state; every other stored state is LEDGER.CORRUPT, and nothing is migrated or adopted. The attempt_custody row (admitted, this namespace) is committed in its own non-waiting transaction before any object; a reused ExecutionId is the invariant row and a failed COMMIT is durability-undetermined. Every step is charged. Refusals name their item 10 row; the host projection is X3d's. admit_attempt returns a non-Clone AdmittedAttempt, which object publication requires. Object publication, the prepared ledger transaction, staging and the commit with its durability classification are the child module commit (project_commit.rs, unit X3c-2), whose ObjectDeclaration and StagingMismatch refusals take the invariant row and CommitUndetermined the durability row here. Nothing here settles an attempt or reads an outcome back. The production location is ProjectStoreLocation::selected (law X3d r6 item 3 steps 4 to 6, unit X3d-2), which the commit facade builds from the selected store directory I/stores/S that the commit session opened and confirmed, its absolute spelling, the session's (S, N) and the invoking user (X8b's test-only scenario-fixtures seam also builds one); tests construct one through a cfg(test) constructor. The file re-exports the commit child's entries crate-privately for the storage facade (unit X3d-2) and declares two further children: recovery_read (recovery_read.rs), read-only recovery's one ledger snapshot and object reads with no creation, barrier or write (law X6 r3, unit X6b), and sweep_settle (sweep_settle.rs), the authorized settlement sweep's one ledger snapshot and its settle write under the sweep's EXCLUSIVE lease (law X6 r3 item 7, unit X6c).", "before": "Place, create and open the per-(S, N) evidence ledger and admit the attempt (law X3c r6 items 1 to 3, 9 and 10; unit X3c-1). Owner201's spellings: I/stores/S/projects/N/ledger.sqlite with its -wal and -shm, and objects under projects/N/objects/sha256. projects/, projects/N/, objects/ and objects/sha256/ are created or admitted through security's store custody. A new ledger is created exclusively, WAL is selected, then one BEGIN IMMEDIATE runs the selected DDL fragments in their pinned order and COMMITs under synchronous=FULL and fullfsync, the namespace directory is barriered, and the file is reopened through open_existing with a whole-schema equality against the same DDL. An empty file with no -wal is the only resumable creation state; every other stored state is LEDGER.CORRUPT, and nothing is migrated or adopted. The attempt_custody row (admitted, this namespace) is committed in its own non-waiting transaction before any object; a reused ExecutionId is the invariant row and a failed COMMIT is durability-undetermined. Every step is charged. Refusals name their item 10 row; the host projection is X3d's. No object publication, staging, commit, settlement or read-back. Only tests construct a location.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/962/description"}}, {"after": "Read and join immutable manifest and receipt-inventory material from the recovery ledger snapshot, and stage a Run's manifest and commit inventory into commit_run_material inside an open write transaction (WriteTransaction::stage_run_material, law X3c r7 item 6, unit X3c-2) under the same schema check and body admission recovery uses, after the receipt staged in that transaction, returning the inventory's typed object identities and its blob digests, both of which the commit publishes (unit X3d-2), and committing nothing; signatures, live retention, pins and full replay remain separate owners.", "before": "Read and join immutable manifest and receipt-inventory material from the recovery ledger snapshot; signatures, live retention, pins and full replay remain separate owners.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/965/description"}}, {"after": "Check X6b's storage half without an admission (storage's ordinary test build cannot build security's RecoveryAdmission, which security's crash-matrix support surface supplies only under the test-only feature, law X9 r4 item 6, unit X9-2; X9 composes the two in fresh processes), included as commit_tests.rs's child module so that it runs recover_from over scratch I/stores/S stores that X3d-2's own storage steps committed into, replaying commit_tests.rs's synthetic run candidate under a real session's binding values (unit X3d-3), with a scripted carrier verdict, checking every time that recovery leaves the ledger bytes unchanged: a confirmed commit committed-historically with pending settlement disclosed, then without it once settled committed, and the carrier asked exactly the association's carrier half; the settlement matrix (admitted with no receipt unknown-attempt-open and no row unknown-attempt-unobserved, both without asking the carrier; settled refused with both absent the only negative; settled committed without a receipt and settled refused beside one unknown custody); each carrier verdict its own standing, a budget refusal RecoveryFailure; F34's requested binding equal giving the standing and differing in operation, store generation, namespace or carrier, or with no attempt row, binding-unusable before the carrier is asked; no projects directory, a missing ledger file, a non-database and a drifted schema unknown custody; a deleted and a flipped committed object evidence.missing and evidence.corrupt in committed-availability-degraded, and evidence.purged under a purged availability generation; a short ledger the budget row; ExistingAttempt's requested binding equal to the plan's and refused on operation against another attempt; and source pins that recover.rs and recovery_read.rs hold no SQL write, transaction, creation, barrier, publication, settlement or sleep and place after-ledger-snapshot once, after the snapshot.", "before": "Check X6b's storage half without an admission (storage cannot build security's RecoveryAdmission; X9 composes the two in fresh processes), included as commit_tests.rs's child module so that it runs recover_from over scratch I/stores/S stores that X3d-2's own storage steps committed into, with a scripted carrier verdict, checking every time that recovery leaves the ledger bytes unchanged: a confirmed commit committed-historically with pending settlement disclosed, then without it once settled committed, and the carrier asked exactly the association's carrier half; the settlement matrix (admitted with no receipt unknown-attempt-open and no row unknown-attempt-unobserved, both without asking the carrier; settled refused with both absent the only negative; settled committed without a receipt and settled refused beside one unknown custody); each carrier verdict its own standing, a budget refusal RecoveryFailure; F34's requested binding equal giving the standing and differing in operation, store generation, namespace or carrier, or with no attempt row, binding-unusable before the carrier is asked; no projects directory, a missing ledger file, a non-database and a drifted schema unknown custody; a deleted and a flipped committed object evidence.missing and evidence.corrupt in committed-availability-degraded, and evidence.purged under a purged availability generation; a short ledger the budget row; ExistingAttempt's requested binding equal to the plan's and refused on operation against another attempt; and source pins that recover.rs and recovery_read.rs hold no SQL write, transaction, creation, barrier, publication, settlement or sleep and place after-ledger-snapshot once, after the snapshot.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/974/description"}}, {"after": "Storage-owned marker syntax over the read session's selected store endpoint (law X3a r5 item 4, unit X3a-2): the endpoint marker bytes of the session's one read, never captured again, decoded by the shared store-marker codec and bound to the requested S, so a request for any other store refuses on the binding row; an invalid requested S refuses before any native step. Its recheck is the session's full recheck, which compares the marker's full sample; its samples follow that recheck and are I's, the fence carrier's and the marker's, the marker reopened by name under the retained I with its full sample unchanged, each on H's filesystem. A matched marker identifies S only. No full G/K, profile or current authority.", "before": "Storage-owned marker syntax on the original native-fenced descendant capture. A matched marker identifies S only. No full G/K, profile or current authority.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/981/description"}}, {"after": "Check X6c's storage step without a lease (storage's ordinary test build cannot build security's SweepLease, which security's crash-matrix support surface supplies through settlement_sweep only under the test-only feature, law X9 r4 item 6, unit X9-2; X9 composes the two in fresh processes), included as commit_tests.rs's child module so that sweep_from runs over scratch I/stores/S stores that X3d-2's own storage steps committed into, replaying commit_tests.rs's synthetic run candidate under a real session's binding values (unit X3d-3), read back by recovery's recover_from: a committed-but-unsettled attempt settled committed and a crashed one settled refused, with no receipt, association or object written, the reader moving from committed-historically with pending settlement and unknown-attempt-open to committed-historically without it and terminal-not-committed, and a second sweep listing and writing nothing; a one-sided ledger (receipt or association removed behind its reinstalled trigger, X9's F23 mutation) reported one-sided and left admitted while the crashed attempt beside it settles; a missing projects directory or ledger file no ledger, a non-database, a drifted schema and a non-private ledger file unreadable with nothing written; a foreign holder of the ledger's write lock stopping the namespace busy with every row left admitted until the next sweep settles each once; an admitted row under another binding never listed; a short ledger the budget row with nothing written; and source pins that the sweep's only write is the one settle UPDATE in ledger_store, guarded on admitted and never undetermined, called only from sweep_settle.rs, and that sweep.rs and sweep_settle.rs hold no other SQL write, staging, publication, creation, barrier, SEAL, witness, sleep or retry and place after-snapshot once between the snapshot and the first settle and settle.commit once after the settle statement.", "before": "Check X6c's storage step without a lease (storage cannot build security's SweepLease; X9 composes the two in fresh processes), included as commit_tests.rs's child module so that sweep_from runs over scratch I/stores/S stores that X3d-2's own storage steps committed into, read back by recovery's recover_from: a committed-but-unsettled attempt settled committed and a crashed one settled refused, with no receipt, association or object written, the reader moving from committed-historically with pending settlement and unknown-attempt-open to committed-historically without it and terminal-not-committed, and a second sweep listing and writing nothing; a one-sided ledger (receipt or association removed behind its reinstalled trigger, X9's F23 mutation) reported one-sided and left admitted while the crashed attempt beside it settles; a missing projects directory or ledger file no ledger, a non-database, a drifted schema and a non-private ledger file unreadable with nothing written; a foreign holder of the ledger's write lock stopping the namespace busy with every row left admitted until the next sweep settles each once; an admitted row under another binding never listed; a short ledger the budget row with nothing written; and source pins that the sweep's only write is the one settle UPDATE in ledger_store, guarded on admitted and never undetermined, called only from sweep_settle.rs, and that sweep.rs and sweep_settle.rs hold no other SQL write, staging, publication, creation, barrier, SEAL, witness, sleep or retry and place after-snapshot once between the snapshot and the first settle and settle.commit once after the settle statement.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/983/description"}}, {"after": "Exercise the actual carrier at each object/journal/ledger/acknowledgement fault point, lock order, revoked sessions and fresh-process recovery. It is storage's crash-matrix target (law X9 r1 items 2, 3 and 7 to 9; units X9-2, X9-3, X9-4 and X9-6), declared with required-features crash-matrix and compiled only under cfg(all(feature = crash-matrix, target_os = macos)), so a plain cargo test skips it. Every process of a run is a matrix child of this test binary under X9 r2 item 3's scripted wall clock, numbered in spawn order: fixture (ordinal 0: the synthetic installation, X4T-0's accepted store and a project root); commit and competitor-writer (X9 r4 item 6's operation, CommitSession::open, X3d-3's synthetic run candidate or r12's distinct variant replayed by replay_run, prepare_commit, publish and finish, or F42's forgotten stopped session, or X9-4's refused end with nothing staged); recover and reader (recovery_admission with a plain or binding-carrying request, then recover); sweep (settlement_sweep, then X6c's sweep_namespace for each namespace); revoke (publish_revocation of the session's core closure in a process of its own); and entries (one driver entry per process). The parent transcribes nothing: x9_2_matrix, x9_3_matrix, x9_4_matrix and x9_6_matrix each run, only when OPENSIP_X9_RUN_SET is set, their unit's rows of the reviewed required-runs.v1.json (X9-6: every storage row) after that unit's census, each driver part run twice and equal and the parts unioned by point name at the largest count (X9-2: the commit driver, with r9's reference run for transcription; X9-3: commit, recover and sweep; X9-4: the lawful commit and the refused end; X9-6: X9-3's three and the refused end). They arm, await, resume and kill by blocking record reads, in X9-2's single-commit form or X9-3's step form of named overlapping children (with X9-4's foreign SQLite holder, @m injected ids, requested bindings and before-and-after comparisons), apply parent mutations, capture the post state between processes, run item 8's ladder R1 to R4 (R1's stateUnchanged compared) and F36's R5, make a run whose timing guard on the parent's monotonic GuardClock exceeds 5,000 ms a HARNESS-ERROR (law X9 r12 to r15), digest each child's trace normalized per thread with r14's object-publication group order, fail a run whose Committed child breaks law X9 r16 item 8's guard order (journal level 3, ledger level 3, level 4; evidence COMMIT, level 4, level 3, writer lease, end fence, an owed REV or CLN released before the lease), and write one run record per run and matrix.json with the census, kill set, release-absence record and limits L1 to L11. Lane tests check one driver entry per process, the commit driver's census equal across two runs and in the guard order, and the release-order condition over synthetic traces. Nothing here sleeps, polls or waits on a timer; X9-0's watchdog is the one timed wait.", "before": "Exercise the actual carrier at each object/journal/ledger/acknowledgement fault point, lock order, revoked sessions and fresh-process recovery; API compile-fail fixtures require the selected harness.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/984/description"}}, {"after": "The reviewed required runs of the crash matrix (law X9 r1 items 7 and 9; r4 item 7's per-unit subset), opensip.x9.required-runs.v1 canonical JSON with the run set's clockEpoch. Unit X9-2 transcribes its rows (F00, F02 to F05, F07 to F10, F20 to F22, F31, F46; 231 runs) from law X9 r9 before any run: the kill points and their order from the unarmed census of its commit driver, each X4T dependency occurrence's R2 from r9's unarmed reference run, and every expected value from the law's row, never read back from a run. Each row names its case, variant, owning units, labels (synthetic and scripted-clock always; process-death, injected or mutation as the run is), script and expected per-step values, and (law X9 r15 and r16) optionally the X9 unit that owns it. A script is X9-2's single-commit form (arm, await-and-kill, hold-copy-resume or mutate, with X9-3's r2, ladder, request and commit directives) or X9-3's step form of named overlapping children (spawn, await with hold, pass or kill, resume, finish, mutate and ladder of a child), with X9-4's sqlite-hold and sqlite-release steps and binding, unchanged and observe options. Storage's crash-matrix target reads it; tools/check_crash_matrix.py check (with host's required runs, law X9 r10) and check-unit check run sets against it, and coverage checks its kills against the union census. Later X9 units append their rows after the earlier ones, which stay unchanged: X9-3's 57 (F11 to F15, F23 to F25, F27 to F29, F33, F36, F42 to F45, F49, F52, F53; law X9 r14), X9-4's 47 (F06, F18, F19, F26, F30, F34, F38, F39's storage half, F40, F41, and F14's moved x3d.finish.settle.before kill, whose row names unit X9-4; r15) and X9-6's 46 storage rows of r16's 50 kill-set coverage rows, each naming unit X9-6, for 381 runs. Synthetic; no compiler qualification.", "before": "The reviewed required runs of the crash matrix (law X9 r1 items 7 and 9; r4 item 7's per-unit subset), opensip.x9.required-runs.v1 canonical JSON with the run set's clockEpoch. Unit X9-2 transcribes its rows (F00, F02 to F05, F07 to F10, F20 to F22, F31, F46; 231 runs) from law X9 r9 before any run: the kill points and their order from the unarmed census of its commit driver, each X4T dependency occurrence's R2 from r9's unarmed reference run, and every expected value from the law's row, never read back from a run. Each row names its case, variant, owning units, labels (synthetic and scripted-clock always; process-death, injected or mutation as the run is), script (arm, await-and-kill, or mutate) and expected per-step values. Storage's crash-matrix target reads it; tools/check_crash_matrix.py check and check-unit check run sets against it. Later X9 units append their rows. Synthetic; no compiler qualification.", "parent": {"bytes": 729876, "path": "docs/implementation/m2/repository-file-inventory.v218.json", "sha256": "7b3370c9f8e94e9194967c41b2e70535ef787c9fb2a7dcab03073ce5035e0fa2"}, "selector": {"jsonPointer": "/files/985/description"}}], "inventoryPassageSupersessions": 21, "inventorySuccessors": [{"addedFiles": 4, "parent": "docs/v2/architecture/repository-file-inventory.v1.json", "selected": "docs/implementation/m1/repository-file-inventory.v3.json", "sha256": "63027fb69f55b6fd65f3cb5baa89d355736239060bfa2750f3137e93cbb716ee"}, {"addedFiles": 44, "parent": "docs/implementation/m1/repository-file-inventory.v3.json", "selected": "docs/implementation/m1/repository-file-inventory.v4.json", "sha256": "c6c85fb8adfe6cdf50dc7b07b7f1e0816275e3e00eb7307e1acb8c1568fe84f5"}, {"addedFiles": 77, "parent": "docs/im